/*
 * OCR A-Level Economics (H460) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * Shape: { topicId: { name, questions: [{ q, marks, command, markScheme, diagram? }] } }
 * 8 topics; ids match economics-ocr.js and topics-config.js.
 *
 * WHY THESE TARIFFS. Every question here uses a tariff that actually appears on an
 * H460 paper — no invented mark values. Established from the June 2022 and June 2023
 * question papers and mark schemes published by OCR; the same papers are indexed in
 * this repo's papers-config.js.
 *
 *   Papers 1 (Microeconomics) and 2 (Macroeconomics), 80 marks in 2 hours:
 *     Section A (30) — stimulus material with parts worth
 *        2 marks  "Explain what is meant by the term…" / "identify two causes of…" /
 *                 "Describe the relationship between…" / "Calculate…"
 *        4 marks  "Explain, using an appropriate diagram, …"
 *        8 marks  "Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent
 *                 to which…"
 *       12 marks  "Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether…"
 *     Section B (25) — one of two: "Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s),
 *       whether…"
 *     Section C (25) — one of two: "Evaluate whether…"
 *   Paper 3 (Themes in Economics), 80 marks in 2 hours: 30 one-mark multiple choice
 *     (those live in economics-ocr.js) plus a Section B on three extracts with 2, 3,
 *     4, 8 and 15-mark parts.
 *
 * MIX PER TOPIC: 8 x 2, 3 x 3, 7 x 4, 7 x 8, 5 x 12, 4 x 15, 6 x 25, in that order,
 * less one 2-mark and one 4-mark in the topic that carries 38 items.
 *
 * MARK SCHEMES. OCR marks the 8, 12, 15 and 25-mark questions by LEVELS OF RESPONSE,
 * and the 12, 15 and 25-mark questions are asterisked on the paper, so they also
 * carry marks for the quality of extended response. Each markScheme gives indicative
 * content plus the ladder an examiner applies. Indicative content is NOT a checklist
 * — a response that makes other valid points well still reaches the top band.
 *
 *   2, 3 and 4-mark: point-marked.
 *    8-mark: L2 (5-8) good knowledge and strong analysis with evaluation;
 *            L1 (1-4) limited to reasonable knowledge and analysis.
 *   12-mark: L3 (9-12) / L2 (5-8) / L1 (1-4).
 *   15-mark: L3 (11-15) / L2 (6-10) / L1 (1-5).
 *   25-mark: L5 (21-25) / L4 (16-20) / L3 (11-15) / L2 (6-10) / L1 (1-5).
 *
 * Extracts and data tables labelled "written for this practice question" are
 * practice material with internally consistent figures, not claims about the world.
 * Dated statistics outside those extracts are real and carry their date.
 * The student self-marks against the mark scheme: Full / Partial / Missed.
 */

const ECONOMICS_OCR_PRACTICE = {
'1.1': {
    name: 'Economic Problem & Market Systems',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'opportunity cost\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `1 mark: the cost of a choice / what is given up when a choice is made.\n1 mark: development — it is the value of the NEXT BEST alternative forgone (not all alternatives), and it arises because resources are scarce relative to unlimited wants.\nCredit a correct supporting example (a government spending £1bn on a new hospital forgoes the schools that £1bn could have built) but the example alone earns nothing without the definition.`},

      { q: 'Identify two factors of production other than labour, and give one example of each.', marks: 2, command: 'Identify',
        markScheme: `1 mark for each correctly identified factor with a valid example, maximum 2 marks. Accept any two of:\n• Land — all natural resources: North Sea gas reserves, agricultural land, fish stocks, mineral deposits.\n• Capital — man-made goods used to produce other goods: a factory robot, a delivery van, a wind turbine, IT systems.\n• Enterprise — the entrepreneur who combines the other factors and bears the risk: a founder starting a business.\nNo mark for naming the factor without an example, and no mark for labour (excluded by the question).`},

      { q: 'The price of a product rises by 10% and the quantity demanded falls by 25%. Calculate the price elasticity of demand for the product. You are advised to show your working.', marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Correct answer: PED = percentage change in quantity demanded / percentage change in price = -25 / +10 = -2.5 (2 marks).\n1 mark for the correct formula or correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for an answer of 2.5 without the negative sign.\nNo further mark is available for interpretation, but a correct answer of -2.5 stated as \'elastic\' is not penalised.`},

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'derived demand\', using one example.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `1 mark: demand for a good or factor that arises not for its own sake but because it is needed to produce something else.\n1 mark: a valid example developed — demand for construction labour is derived from demand for housing, so a slump in housebuilding reduces demand for bricklayers even if bricklayers\' wages have not changed; demand for steel is derived from demand for cars.\nAccept demand for a factor of production generally.`},

      { q: 'A 5% rise in real incomes leads to a 15% rise in the quantity of restaurant meals demanded. Calculate the income elasticity of demand and state what type of good this is.', marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Correct answer: YED = percentage change in quantity demanded / percentage change in income = +15 / +5 = +3.0 (1 mark).\nCorrect identification: a normal good, and specifically a luxury (income-elastic) good because YED is positive and greater than 1 (1 mark).\nAllow the second mark for \'normal\' alone; \'luxury\' or \'income elastic\' earns it outright. An answer of -3 or 0.33 scores a maximum of 1 mark if the method is otherwise correct.`},

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'consumer surplus\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `1 mark: the difference between what consumers are willing (and able) to pay for a good and what they actually pay.\n1 mark: development — on a demand and supply diagram it is the area beneath the demand curve and above the market price, and it is one measure of the welfare consumers gain from a transaction.\nAccept an accurate diagrammatic description in place of the written development.`},

      { q: `Refer to the stimulus material below.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
UK strawberry market, summer weeks
  Week 1: price £2.40 per punnet ; quantity supplied 1 200 000 punnets
  Week 6: price £2.76 per punnet ; quantity supplied 1 260 000 punnets

Calculate the price elasticity of supply of strawberries between Week 1 and Week 6, to two decimal places. You are advised to show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: percentage change in price = (2.76 - 2.40) / 2.40 × 100 = +15%. percentage change in quantity supplied = (1 260 000 - 1 200 000) / 1 200 000 × 100 = +5%.\nPES = percentage change in quantity supplied / percentage change in price = 5 / 15 = +0.33 (to two decimal places).\n2 marks for +0.33. 1 mark for the correct formula and correct percentage changes with an arithmetic slip, or for an unrounded 0.3333 without rounding to two decimal places as instructed.\nA correct answer implies inelastic supply; no additional mark for saying so.`},

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'joint supply\', using one example.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `1 mark: where the production of one good necessarily results in the production of another, so the two are supplied together from the same process.\n1 mark: a developed example — beef and leather from cattle, so a fall in beef demand reduces herd sizes and therefore also cuts the supply of hides, raising leather prices; crude oil refining yields petrol and diesel together; sheep produce lamb and wool.\nDo not credit complements in DEMAND (printers and ink cartridges) — that is a different relationship.`},

      { q: 'Explain why the demand for salt is likely to be price inelastic.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Up to 3 marks for a developed chain of reasoning. Creditable points:\n1 mark — few or no close substitutes: nothing else performs the same seasoning and preserving function, so consumers cannot switch away when the price rises.\n1 mark — salt takes up a negligible proportion of household income, so even a large percentage price rise is a trivial absolute sum and consumers barely respond.\n1 mark — it is habitually purchased and often a necessity in cooking, and (development) the time period is short relative to any change in cooking habits, so quantity demanded changes proportionately less than price, giving a PED between 0 and -1.\nAny three creditable and distinct points, or two points with clear development, reaches 3.`},

      { q: 'Explain why the supply of housing is likely to be price inelastic in the short run.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Up to 3 marks. Creditable points:\n1 mark — the existing housing stock is fixed at any moment; the flow of new build is tiny relative to the stock, so a price rise can raise total supply only marginally in the short run.\n1 mark — long production lags: land acquisition, planning permission, site preparation and construction take years, so builders cannot respond quickly to a price signal.\n1 mark — factor immobility and capacity constraints: shortages of serviced land, skilled bricklayers and materials mean output cannot be scaled up quickly even when profit margins rise.\nA final mark is available for the consequence — a rise in demand therefore raises price far more than quantity, which is why demand-side subsidies to buyers tend to be capitalised into prices.`},

      { q: 'Explain why the price elasticity of supply of a good is usually higher in the long run than in the short run.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Up to 3 marks. Creditable points:\n1 mark — in the short run at least one factor of production is fixed, so output can be expanded only by adding variable factors to a fixed capital stock, and diminishing marginal returns make extra output increasingly costly.\n1 mark — in the long run all factors are variable: firms can build new plant, train or recruit labour and adopt new technology, so a given price rise calls forth a much larger increase in quantity supplied.\n1 mark — new firms can also enter the industry in the long run, adding to market supply; and stocks, spare capacity and factor mobility all improve with time.\nCredit the conclusion that the long-run supply curve is therefore flatter than the short-run one.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the effect of a sharp rise in the world price of wheat on the UK market for bread.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): correctly labelled axes (price on the vertical, quantity on the horizontal), downward-sloping D and upward-sloping S, initial equilibrium P1Q1, supply curve shifted LEFT/upward to S2, new equilibrium P2Q2 with P2 > P1 and Q2 < Q1.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): wheat is a key raw material in bread production, so a rise in its price raises firms\' costs of production at every output level; profit-maximising bakers are willing to supply less at any given price, shifting supply left. At the original price there is excess demand, so price is bid up and quantity traded falls until the market clears at P2Q2.\nDevelopment worth the fourth mark: because bread is a staple with inelastic demand, most of the cost increase passes through to consumers as a higher price with only a small fall in quantity.\nNo diagram, or an unlabelled one: maximum 2 marks.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the effect of imposing a specific indirect tax on a good, and identify the tax revenue raised.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): D and S with equilibrium P1Q1; supply shifted vertically upward by the amount of the tax to S + tax (a PARALLEL shift for a specific tax), new equilibrium price P2 and quantity Q2; the price received by producers after tax shown as P3, with the vertical gap P2 - P3 equal to the tax per unit.\nIdentification of revenue (1 mark): the rectangle (P2 - P3) × Q2 — that is, tax per unit multiplied by the new quantity traded.\nExplanation (1 mark): the tax raises the marginal cost of supplying each unit, so producers require a higher price to supply any given quantity; consumers pay P2 and bear the incidence (P2 - P1) while producers absorb (P1 - P3).\nCredit the development that the split of the incidence depends on relative elasticities — the more inelastic side of the market bears more of the tax.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the effect of a per-unit government subsidy on the market for new battery-electric cars.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): D and S with equilibrium P1Q1; supply shifted vertically DOWNWARD by the subsidy per unit to S + subsidy; new equilibrium price P2 < P1 and quantity Q2 > Q1; the price received by producers P3 shown above P2, with P3 - P2 equal to the subsidy per car.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): the subsidy is a payment to producers that lowers the effective marginal cost of supplying each car, so producers are willing to supply more at every price. The market price falls to P2 and the quantity traded rises to Q2 as consumers move DOWN the demand curve — an extension of demand, not a shift.\nDevelopment: the total cost to the taxpayer is (P3 - P2) × Q2, and the split of the benefit between buyers and sellers again depends on relative elasticities; with inelastic demand most of the subsidy is captured by producers as a higher net price rather than passed on as a lower pump price.\nA demand-curve shift instead of a supply-curve shift is a fundamental error: maximum 1 mark for the diagram.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the effect of a maximum price set below the free-market equilibrium price in the market for domestic energy.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): D and S with free-market equilibrium P1Q1; a horizontal line Pmax drawn BELOW P1; quantity demanded Qd read off the demand curve and quantity supplied Qs read off the supply curve at Pmax, with Qd > Qs and the horizontal distance between them labelled excess demand / shortage.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): the ceiling is binding only if set below equilibrium. At Pmax consumers extend demand along D while producers contract supply along S, because the price no longer covers the marginal cost of the most expensive units. The result is persistent excess demand of Qd - Qs, which must be rationed by queues, waiting lists, quality reduction or allocation by supplier rather than by price.\nDevelopment worth the fourth mark: consumers who still obtain the good gain consumer surplus, but those rationed out lose entirely, and the reduction in quantity traded from Q1 to Qs creates a deadweight welfare loss. Ofgem\'s domestic price cap, which stood at £1,723 a year for a typical dual-fuel direct-debit household for 1 October to 31 December 2026, is a cap on unit rates and standing charges rather than a total bill, and it is reset quarterly in line with wholesale costs, so it behaves less like a classic binding ceiling than the textbook case.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the possible effect on employment of a national minimum wage set above the market-clearing wage in a competitive labour market.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): labour market axes — wage rate on the vertical, quantity of labour on the horizontal; downward-sloping demand for labour (the MRP curve) and upward-sloping supply of labour; free-market equilibrium W1L1; a horizontal minimum wage line Wmin drawn ABOVE W1; quantity of labour demanded Ld and supplied Ls at Wmin, with Ls > Ld and the gap labelled excess supply of labour / unemployment.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): above the equilibrium wage, firms contract demand for labour because the wage now exceeds the marginal revenue product of the least productive workers, while more workers are attracted into the market. The excess supply Ls - Ld is the predicted rise in unemployment; note that the size of the effect depends on the elasticity of labour demand.\nContext: the UK National Living Wage for workers aged 21 and over rises to £12.71 an hour from April 2026, up from £12.21 in April 2025.\nDevelopment: in a MONOPSONISTIC labour market the same minimum wage can raise BOTH the wage and employment, because it removes the monopsonist\'s incentive to restrict hiring to keep the wage down — credit this as evaluation, but the diagram asked for is the competitive one.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how economic growth and opportunity cost are illustrated by a production possibility frontier.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): two goods on the axes (for example capital goods and consumer goods), a concave-to-the-origin PPF, a point ON the curve labelled as productively efficient, a point INSIDE labelled as inefficient/unemployed resources, a point OUTSIDE labelled unattainable, and an outward shift of the whole curve to PPF2 to show economic growth.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): every point on the frontier uses all resources fully, so producing more of one good requires producing less of the other — the gradient measures opportunity cost. The frontier is concave because factors of production are not perfectly substitutable between uses, so the opportunity cost of each extra unit rises. An outward shift represents an increase in productive capacity from more or better factors of production — net investment, immigration, education and training, or technological progress.\nDevelopment: choosing a point with a high ratio of capital to consumer goods sacrifices present consumption but produces a larger outward shift in future, which is the opportunity cost of growth stated over time.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how consumer surplus and producer surplus change when there is a fall in supply.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): D and S1 with equilibrium P1Q1; consumer surplus shaded as the triangle between D and P1 and producer surplus as the triangle between P1 and S1; supply shifted LEFT to S2 giving a new equilibrium P2Q2 with P2 > P1, Q2 < Q1, and the new, smaller surplus areas shown.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): the higher price and lower quantity unambiguously reduce consumer surplus, because every remaining buyer pays more and some buyers leave the market. Producer surplus is ambiguous: sellers gain on each unit sold from the higher price but lose the surplus on the units no longer traded, so the net effect depends on the price elasticity of demand.\nDevelopment worth the fourth mark: with inelastic demand the price rise dominates and producer surplus can rise — which is why a poor harvest can raise farmers\' aggregate revenue; total welfare (CS + PS) falls in either case because Q2 < Q1 means mutually beneficial trades are lost.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which the price mechanism on its own allocates resources efficiently in the UK housing market.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
English housing market, illustrative data
  Average house price to average earnings ratio: 4.1 in 2000 ; 7.8 in 2025
  Net additional dwellings completed: 234 000 in 2019-20 ; 199 000 in 2024-25
  Households on local authority waiting lists: 1.33 million in 2024
  Median private rent, England, year to March 2025: up 7.7% on the previous year`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The signalling and incentive functions clearly work in one direction: the earnings ratio rising from 4.1 to 7.8 between 2000 and 2025 in the stimulus is a powerful price signal that housing is scarce, and it has pulled land and capital into residential development and raised rents enough to attract build-to-rent investors.\n• But the rationing function is doing most of the work here, and it rations by ability to pay rather than by need: 1.33 million households on waiting lists are people for whom the market has simply cleared them out, which is an equity problem the price mechanism is not designed to solve.\n• The supply response is weak because PES is very low in the short run — planning delays, land banking, skills shortages. Completions FELL from 234 000 to 199 000 despite a 25-year price surge, which is evidence that the incentive function is being blocked, not that it is absent.\n• Market failures compound this: land use generates externalities (congestion, loss of green space), and the local nature of planning creates a classic free-rider/NIMBY problem where the costs of new supply are concentrated and the benefits diffuse.\nEvaluation:\n• Time period matters — supply is far more elastic over 10 years than 2, so evidence of a weak response over a five-year window may overstate the failure.\n• Much of the price rise is driven by cheap credit and land scarcity rather than by construction costs, so it is not obvious that the price mechanism has failed rather than that it has correctly signalled a genuine land shortage.\n• Intervention has its own costs: Help to Buy demand-side subsidy was substantially capitalised into prices given inelastic supply, and rent controls risk the excess demand described in the maximum-price analysis.\n• Judgement: the mechanism allocates the EXISTING stock reasonably efficiently but fails to deliver the socially optimal QUANTITY, and the binding constraint is the planning system rather than the price mechanism itself.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — the stimulus data is used explicitly to support a developed chain of reasoning, with evaluation that weighs the argument rather than listing counterpoints. L1 (1–4) — limited or no use of the stimulus, assertion rather than analysis, evaluation absent or bolted on.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which a rise in the National Living Wage will reduce employment in low-paying sectors.

Stimulus (written for this practice question, using published UK rates)
  National Living Wage (age 21+): £11.44 April 2024 ; £12.21 April 2025 ; £12.71 April 2026
  Illustrative hospitality firm: 40 staff, average 30 hours a week, previously paid £12.21
  Wage bill at £12.21: 40 x 30 x 52 x £12.21 = £761 904 a year
  Wage bill at £12.71: 40 x 30 x 52 x £12.71 = £793 104 a year`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Standard competitive analysis: the NLW is a wage floor above the market-clearing wage, so firms move up and left along the labour demand (MRP) curve. The stimulus firm faces an extra £31 200 a year — roughly a 4.1% rise in its wage bill — and in a sector with thin margins that is a real cost shock.\n• Employment adjustment need not be redundancies: firms cut HOURS, slow recruitment, reduce overtime, or substitute capital for labour (self-service ordering, kiosks). The measured employment effect is therefore often smaller than the true effect on labour input.\n• The size of the effect depends on the elasticity of demand for labour: it is higher where labour is a large share of costs, where capital substitutes exist, and where the firm cannot pass costs on because product demand is price elastic.\nEvaluation:\n• Monopsony: where employers have wage-setting power, a minimum wage can raise employment as well as pay, because the firm no longer needs to restrict hiring to hold the wage down. This is the standard theoretical reason why the empirical employment effects of the UK NLW have been smaller than early predictions.\n• Efficiency wage and turnover effects: higher pay can raise productivity, reduce quit rates and cut recruitment costs, partly self-financing the rise.\n• Demand-side offset: low-paid workers have a high marginal propensity to consume, so aggregate spending in exactly these sectors rises.\n• The rise from £12.21 to £12.71 is about 4.1% in cash terms, so the REAL increase depends on inflation over the year — a nominal rise that merely tracks prices imposes little new real burden.\n• Judgement: a supported conclusion should turn on the SIZE of the uprating relative to productivity growth and on how much monopsony power exists in the sector, rather than asserting a textbook result.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — stimulus figures used explicitly (the £31 200 / 4.1% calculation is the obvious route), developed chain of reasoning, genuine evaluation. L1 (1–4) — textbook diagram reproduced with no use of the data, or a list of points without weighing.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which knowledge of price elasticity of demand should determine a firm\'s pricing decision.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
Estimated PED for a rail operator\'s two ticket types
  Peak-time commuter season tickets: PED = -0.3
  Off-peak leisure advance tickets:  PED = -1.6
Current revenue: season tickets £48m ; advance tickets £22m`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The elementary result: where demand is inelastic (-0.3), raising price raises total revenue, because the proportionate fall in quantity is smaller than the proportionate rise in price; where demand is elastic (-1.6), a price CUT raises revenue. A profit-maximising operator would therefore raise season-ticket prices and cut advance fares — which is precisely why rail operators price-discriminate between the two segments.\n• Worked illustration: a 10% rise in season-ticket prices with PED -0.3 cuts volume by 3%, so revenue moves by roughly (1.10 x 0.97 - 1) = +6.7%, about £3.2m on a £48m base.\n• PED also tells the firm how much of a cost increase it can pass on, and how vulnerable it is to a competitor\'s price cut.\nEvaluation:\n• Revenue is not profit: the firm needs marginal COST as well. A price cut that raises revenue can still cut profit if the extra passengers cost more to carry than they pay at the margin.\n• PED estimates are backward-looking and assume ceteris paribus. Fuel prices, remote working, and a competitor\'s response all change the true elasticity; the 2020s shift to hybrid working has made commuter demand substantially more elastic than historic estimates suggest.\n• PED varies along a straight-line demand curve, so a single number is only valid for small changes near the current price.\n• Other objectives and constraints: regulated fare caps, political limits on commuter fare rises, brand and fairness perceptions, and long-run elasticity exceeding short-run elasticity as passengers change jobs or move house.\n• Judgement: PED is a necessary input but not a sufficient basis — a defensible conclusion is that it should determine the DIRECTION of a price change while costs, competitor reaction and the time horizon determine its size.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — both elasticity values used and contrasted, reasoning developed, evaluation weighed. L1 (1–4) — definitions of elastic and inelastic without application to the stimulus.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which subsidies are an effective way of increasing the take-up of electric vehicles.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
UK new car registrations by fuel type
  Petrol:            2024 = 610 000 ; 2025 = 556 000
  Battery electric:  2024 = 382 000 ; 2025 = 470 000
  Total:             2024 = 1 953 000 ; 2025 = 1 990 000
  Illustrative purchase grant: £3 750 per eligible vehicle`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Calculation from the data: the battery-electric share rose from 382/1953 = 19.56% in 2024 to 470/1990 = 23.62% in 2025 — a rise of just over 4 percentage points, consistent with a subsidy shifting supply right and lowering the effective price.\n• Mechanism: a per-unit grant shifts the supply curve vertically downward by the grant, cutting the price paid and extending demand along D. Justified on externality grounds — EVs generate a positive consumption externality by displacing tailpipe NOx and particulates, so the free market underprovides them relative to the social optimum.\n• A subsidy also builds the market to the scale at which economies of scale and charging-network network effects become self-sustaining, which is a dynamic argument a static diagram misses.\nEvaluation:\n• Incidence: with inelastic supply (battery constraints, waiting lists) much of the grant is captured by manufacturers as a higher net price rather than passed to buyers, so the take-up effect is smaller than the cost implies.\n• Deadweight cost and opportunity cost: a grant pays for purchases that would have happened anyway. Direct opportunity cost — the same money spent on charging infrastructure might shift demand further.\n• Distribution: new-car buyers are disproportionately higher-income, so a purchase grant is regressive in its incidence.\n• The counterfactual: the share was already rising sharply because of the ZEV mandate on manufacturers, falling battery costs and model availability, so attributing the whole 4-point rise to the subsidy is an error.\n• Regulation as an alternative: a quantity mandate on manufacturers achieves the target with no exchequer cost, though it raises prices of petrol cars to cross-subsidise.\n• Judgement: effective at the margin, but weaker than the raw share increase suggests once the counterfactual and incidence are allowed for.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — data used quantitatively (the share calculation is the expected route), reasoning developed, evaluation weighed. L1 (1–4) — describes what a subsidy is with no engagement with the figures.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which changes in the price of a complement explain changes in demand for a good.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
UK market for home coffee machines and coffee pods
  Average price of a pack of 30 pods: £7.50 in Year 1 ; £9.75 in Year 2
  Coffee machines sold: 1 400 000 in Year 1 ; 1 190 000 in Year 2
  Estimated cross elasticity of demand (machines with respect to pod price): -0.5
  Average real household disposable income over the same period: -1.2%`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Interpretation of XED: a negative value confirms the goods are complements — a rise in the price of pods reduces demand for machines, shifting the machine demand curve LEFT (a shift, not a movement along).\n• Quantitative test: pods rose by (9.75 - 7.50)/7.50 = +30%. With XED of -0.5 that predicts a 15% fall in machine demand. Actual fall = (1 400 000 - 1 190 000)/1 400 000 = -15%. So on these figures the complement relationship accounts for essentially the whole observed change.\n• Mechanism: the machine is close to worthless without pods, so consumers evaluate the total cost of ownership; a higher running cost lowers willingness to pay for the durable.\nEvaluation:\n• The fit is suspiciously exact. XED of -0.5 was itself estimated from past data, so using it to \'explain\' the same relationship is close to circular.\n• The stimulus gives a competing cause: real disposable incomes fell 1.2%, and coffee machines are a durable with positive and probably high YED, so part of the fall is an income effect, meaning the true XED effect must be smaller than -0.5 implies.\n• Ceteris paribus is violated in other ways too: substitutes (refillable pods, ground coffee, coffee shops), the replacement cycle for a durable good, and any change in tastes.\n• Elasticity values are only reliable for small changes; a 30% price move is large, and XED is unlikely to be constant over that range.\n• Judgement: the complement relationship is plausibly the dominant single explanation, but the data cannot isolate it from the income effect, so a confident attribution is not supportable.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — the XED value applied numerically to the stimulus, competing explanation recognised. L1 (1–4) — defines XED without applying it.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which low price elasticity of supply explains price volatility in agricultural markets.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
UK wheat market, two harvest years
  Year 1: harvest 14.0 million tonnes ; farmgate price £185 per tonne
  Year 2: harvest 11.2 million tonnes ; farmgate price £268 per tonne
  Estimated PED for wheat: -0.2   Estimated short-run PES for wheat: +0.1`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The data: output fell 20% ((14.0 - 11.2)/14.0) while price rose 44.9% ((268 - 185)/185). A proportionately small quantity change producing a far larger price change is the signature of inelastic demand AND supply.\n• Diagrammatic reasoning: a leftward shift of a steep (inelastic) supply curve along a steep (inelastic) demand curve produces a large vertical price movement for a small horizontal quantity movement. Both elasticities matter — it is the COMBINATION, not PES alone, that generates volatility.\n• Why PES is low: biological production lags of a full season, land committed at sowing, weather-determined yield, no way to increase this year\'s harvest once it is in the ground.\n• Why PED is low: wheat is a necessity with few substitutes and a small share of the final price of bread, so buyers absorb the price rise.\nEvaluation:\n• PES is not the only cause. The shift itself is weather-driven, so the immediate cause of volatility is the SUPPLY SHOCK; low PES only amplifies it. On these numbers PED of -0.2 is doing as much work as PES of +0.1.\n• Storage, buffer stocks and international trade dampen volatility considerably: domestic prices track world prices, and imports are far more elastic than domestic supply, so the effective PES facing UK buyers exceeds +0.1.\n• Speculation and futures markets can either dampen volatility (by moving stocks across time) or amplify it.\n• Longer run: PES rises as farmers reallocate land between crops, so price spikes are self-correcting over two or three seasons.\n• Judgement: low PES is a necessary but not sufficient explanation — it converts supply shocks into price shocks, but the shocks and inelastic demand are equally responsible.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — percentage changes computed from the stimulus and both elasticities used. L1 (1–4) — states that agricultural supply is inelastic without quantitative use of the data.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which consumers can be assumed to behave rationally.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
Findings from a survey of UK energy customers
  Households that had never switched supplier: 46%
  Households that said switching \'takes too much time to be worth it\': 61%
  Average annual saving available from switching to the cheapest fixed tariff: £180
  Households on default tariffs protected by the Ofgem price cap, October 2026: about 65%`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The stimulus is prima facie evidence against the rational-agent assumption: an unexploited £180 a year is money left on the table, and 46% never switching is inertia rather than optimisation. Behavioural explanations — status quo bias, default effects, loss aversion, choice overload from hundreds of tariff variants.\n• Bounded rationality: consumers have limited time and limited ability to process tariff structures made of unit rates, standing charges and exit fees, so they satisfice rather than maximise.\n• Asymmetric information and computational complexity make the comparison genuinely hard, which is a market failure as well as a behavioural one.\nEvaluation:\n• The behaviour may in fact BE rational once search costs are counted. If switching takes four hours of effort and mental cost, a household valuing its time at £45 an hour is rationally declining a £180 saving. The 61% who say it is not worth the time may be optimising correctly — the survey does not disprove rationality, it reveals the price of attention.\n• The saving quoted is an average and is uncertain: the cheapest fix today may be beaten by the capped default tariff later, so a risk-averse consumer choosing the capped default is making a defensible choice under uncertainty.\n• Rationality is an assumption chosen for TRACTABILITY, not a factual claim. It predicts aggregate market behaviour well even where individuals deviate, and deviations that are random cancel out in aggregate. The behavioural critique bites only where biases are SYSTEMATIC, which the default effect is.\n• Policy implication: if inertia is systematic, nudges (automatic switching, simplified tariffs, default caps) raise welfare at lower cost than information provision — which is the logic of the Ofgem cap itself.\n• Judgement: the evidence supports bounded rather than absent rationality, and the useful question is not whether consumers are rational but which biases are predictable enough to design policy around.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — stimulus figures used, both the behavioural reading and the search-cost defence developed. L1 (1–4) — lists biases with no use of the data.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether a maximum price is the most appropriate way of protecting households from rising energy bills.

Stimulus (written for this practice question, using published Ofgem figures)
  Ofgem domestic energy price cap, typical dual-fuel direct-debit household:
    July-September 2026: £1 663 a year
    October-December 2026: £1 723 a year (a rise of 4%, about £60)
  Ofgem stated the rise was driven by higher wholesale gas prices, partly offset by the
  government cutting VAT on domestic electricity from 5% to 0% for 1 October 2026 to 31 March 2027.
  About 35% of households (roughly 11 million) were on fixed tariffs and unaffected by the cap change.`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The case for a cap: energy is a necessity with very inelastic demand and low switching rates, so suppliers of default tariffs face weak competitive discipline. A cap limits the exploitation of that inertia and protects real incomes for the poorest households, for whom energy is a large and regressive share of spending.\n• Diagram: a binding maximum price below equilibrium creates excess demand Qd - Qs and a deadweight loss; students should then note that the Ofgem cap is NOT a fixed ceiling but a formula reset quarterly to track wholesale costs, so it rarely binds in the textbook sense — which is why Britain saw supplier exits in 2021-22 rather than persistent physical shortages.\n• Evidence from the stimulus: a 4% rise driven by wholesale gas shows the cap passing costs through rather than suppressing them; the VAT cut shows a SEPARATE instrument doing the work of actually reducing bills.\n• Alternative 1 — targeted income transfers (Warm Home Discount, cold weather payments, an expanded social tariff). Advantage: help reaches the fuel-poor without distorting the marginal price signal or subsidising high-consuming affluent households. Disadvantage: administrative cost, take-up failure, and it does nothing about supplier market power.\n• Alternative 2 — tax changes, as in the VAT cut in the stimulus. Advantage: immediate and certain pass-through. Disadvantage: untargeted and costly to the exchequer, and it weakens the price signal to reduce consumption at exactly the moment gas is scarce.\n• Alternative 3 — supply-side and efficiency policy: insulation grants, heat pumps, renewables deployment. Slow but it lowers the equilibrium price rather than just the price consumers see.\nEvaluation:\n• A cap that suppresses the marginal price weakens the incentive to reduce demand, which is perverse when scarcity is the underlying problem.\n• The cap reduced tariff variety and arguably weakened switching, because the capped default became a good enough deal to make search unattractive — a policy that entrenches the inertia it was designed to counteract. The stimulus shows 35% on fixes, so the cap is not the whole market.\n• The distributional point is decisive: a universal cap gives the largest cash benefit to the largest users, who are on average better off, so it is a poorly targeted anti-poverty instrument even though it is popular and administratively cheap.\n• Judgement: a cap is a defensible SHORT-RUN response to weak competition, but as protection against high bills it is inferior to targeted transfers combined with efficiency investment; a supported conclusion should distinguish the two objectives.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — sustained, well-supported argument using the stimulus, with alternatives genuinely weighed and a justified conclusion. L2 (5–8) — some analysis and evaluation, application uneven. L1 (1–4) — descriptive, little evaluation. Asterisked question: marks are also available for quality of extended response — a coherent structure and a conclusion that follows from the argument.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether an indirect tax is an effective way of reducing the consumption of a good.', marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Mechanism: a specific or ad valorem tax shifts supply left/upward, raising price and contracting quantity along the demand curve. Where the good generates a negative externality the tax can in principle internalise it, moving output towards the socially optimal level where MSB = MSC.\n• Evidence that it works on quantity: the Soft Drinks Industry Levy, charged at 20.8p per litre on drinks with 5g to under 8g of sugar per 100ml and 27.8p per litre at 8g or more (rates in force from 1 April 2026), cut sugar mainly through REFORMULATION rather than through consumers buying less — most manufacturers reformulated below the threshold. From 1 January 2028 the lower threshold falls to 4.5g per 100ml and milk-based drinks lose their exemption.\n• Revenue: the tax raises revenue that can hypothecate to related spending, and revenue is most reliable precisely where demand is inelastic.\nEvaluation:\n• PED is decisive. With inelastic demand (tobacco, fuel, alcohol for dependent drinkers) the tax raises a lot of revenue but changes quantity little, so it is effective as a revenue instrument and weak as a behavioural one. With elastic demand it changes behaviour but raises little.\n• Regressivity: demerit goods are disproportionately consumed by lower-income households, so a flat indirect tax takes a larger share of a poor household\'s income.\n• Setting the rate requires valuing the externality, which is extremely difficult; too low and it fails to correct, too high and it overcorrects and creates a new welfare loss.\n• Unintended consequences: cross-border shopping, illicit trade (a persistent issue with tobacco duty), and substitution to an untaxed good that may be no better.\n• The reformulation lesson cuts both ways — the SDIL succeeded because it was levied on a THRESHOLD producers could design around, not on quantity consumed, so it is evidence for well-designed taxes rather than for taxes in general.\n• Alternatives to weigh: minimum unit pricing (which raises the price of the cheapest units directly), regulation and bans, information campaigns, and nudges.\n• Judgement: effective where demand is elastic or where the tax is structured to trigger producer reformulation; largely a revenue device otherwise.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) / L2 (5–8) / L1 (1–4) as for all 12-mark questions. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether the price mechanism is the best way of allocating water resources in England and Wales.', marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Household water in England and Wales is not allocated by a free market: it is supplied by regional natural monopolies whose prices are set by Ofwat at each price review. In the PR24 final determinations, published in December 2024 and effective from 1 April 2025, average household bills were allowed to rise by about £157 (roughly 36%) before inflation over 2025-2030, with wide variation between companies.\n• The case for pricing: water is scarce, abstraction has real environmental opportunity costs, and metered volumetric charging gives households an incentive to economise. Unmetered charging by rateable value makes the marginal price of an extra litre zero, which guarantees over-use.\n• The case against leaving it to a market: water is a necessity with very inelastic demand and no substitute, distribution is a natural monopoly with vast sunk costs, abstraction and sewage discharge generate large negative externalities, and there is a strong merit-good and public-health argument for universal access.\nEvaluation:\n• Regulation is itself imperfect: RPI/CPIH-X price control creates an incentive to under-invest in assets whose failure is not immediately visible, which is one account of the sewage-discharge record; and the regulator suffers information asymmetry relative to the firms it regulates (regulatory capture risk).\n• Equity: a 36% real-terms bill rise falls hardest on low-income households, so social tariffs and cross-subsidy are needed, which weakens the allocative signal.\n• Supply-side alternatives — reservoirs, leakage reduction, transfer schemes — address the scarcity itself rather than rationing it, but have long lead times and their own environmental externalities.\n• Non-price rationing (hosepipe bans) is blunt, but it is administratively simple and avoids the equity problem of pricing during a drought.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that PRICING at the margin (universal metering) improves efficiency, while leaving the industry\'s structure and investment to an unregulated market would not, so the answer is a regulated price mechanism rather than either extreme.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) / L2 (5–8) / L1 (1–4). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether the government should subsidise the purchase of electric vehicles.', marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Efficiency case: replacing an internal-combustion vehicle with a battery-electric one removes tailpipe NOx and particulates and reduces CO2 emissions, so EV purchase generates external benefits. The free market therefore underconsumes relative to the social optimum, and a subsidy equal to the marginal external benefit moves output towards it.\n• Dynamic case: early subsidy builds the installed base needed for charging-network effects and lets manufacturers descend the experience curve, so the subsidy can be temporary and self-liquidating.\n• Diagram: MPB below MSB with the welfare gain triangle; the subsidy shifts supply down so that the private optimum coincides with the social optimum.\nEvaluation:\n• Incidence: with inelastic supply, much of the grant accrues to manufacturers rather than buyers.\n• Distribution: new-car buyers skew affluent, so a purchase grant is regressive, and the second-hand market — where lower-income buyers shop — is reached only with a lag.\n• The counterfactual: the ZEV mandate already obliges manufacturers to sell a rising share of zero-emission vehicles, and falling battery costs are driving adoption anyway, so a grant may pay for purchases that would have happened regardless.\n• Opportunity cost: the same money spent on charging infrastructure, grid capacity or public transport might reduce emissions more per pound; and EVs do not remove congestion, road wear or the externalities of battery mineral extraction.\n• Fiscal sustainability: fuel duty revenue falls as the fleet electrifies, so subsidising the transition compounds a revenue problem that road pricing would have to solve.\n• Judgement: the externality case is sound, but a purchase grant is a poorly targeted instrument for it; conclusions favouring infrastructure spending, a scrappage scheme aimed at the oldest vehicles, or tightening the mandate are all creditable if argued.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) / L2 (5–8) / L1 (1–4). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether elasticity estimates are a reliable basis for a firm\'s revenue strategy.', marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• What the elasticities offer: PED tells the firm the direction of the revenue response to a price change and how much of a cost increase can be passed on; YED tells it how sales will move over the cycle and guides portfolio choice between necessities and luxuries; XED measures exposure to rivals\' pricing and identifies complements worth bundling.\n• Applied use: price discrimination between segments with different PED (peak and off-peak travel, student and standard subscriptions) raises revenue above a single-price strategy, and requires reliable segment-level elasticity estimates to implement.\nEvaluation:\n• Measurement problems: estimates are derived from historical data under ceteris paribus conditions that no longer hold. Identification is genuinely hard, because observed price and quantity are jointly determined — a firm may mistake a demand shift for a movement along the curve.\n• PED is not constant along a demand curve, so a point estimate is valid only for small changes near the current price; large repricings extrapolate beyond the data.\n• Revenue is not profit. A revenue-maximising price is always below the profit-maximising price, because MR = 0 at the revenue maximum while MR = MC at the profit maximum. A firm optimising on elasticity alone ignores cost.\n• Competitor reaction is the largest omission: in oligopoly the effective elasticity facing the firm depends on whether rivals match the change, which elasticity estimates from a stable period cannot capture (see the kinked demand model).\n• Long run versus short run: elasticities rise with time as consumers find substitutes and adjust habits, so a price rise that looks revenue-enhancing this quarter can erode market share over years.\n• Judgement: reliable as a guide to DIRECTION and to segmentation, unreliable as a precise forecasting tool; the strongest answers argue the firm should treat estimates as a prior to be updated by experimentation (A/B pricing tests) rather than as a parameter.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) / L2 (5–8) / L1 (1–4). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram and the information below, the extent to which a rise in the National Living Wage raises the living standards of low-paid workers.

Extract 1 (written for this practice question, using published UK rates)
The National Living Wage for workers aged 21 and over rose to £12.21 an hour in April 2025 and to
£12.71 an hour in April 2026, an increase of about 4.1%. The Low Pay Commission recommends the
rate each year against a target expressed as a share of median hourly earnings. Around a fifth of
employees in hospitality and retail are paid at or close to the statutory floor. Employer National
Insurance contributions and the cost of pension auto-enrolment mean the total cost of employing a
worker is materially higher than the headline hourly rate.`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: a competitive labour market with the NLW as a wage floor above equilibrium, showing Ls > Ld and the excess supply of labour. The strongest answers ALSO draw the monopsony case — MC of labour above the supply curve, the monopsonist hiring where MRP = MCL and paying off the supply curve — and show that a minimum wage set between the monopsony wage and the competitive wage raises both pay and employment.\n• Gains: for workers who keep their hours, gross pay rises 4.1% in cash terms, which raises real living standards provided inflation is lower. Low-paid households have a high marginal propensity to consume, so the spending circulates locally. Narrowing wage differentials reduces in-work poverty and can cut in-work benefit spending.\n• Efficiency-wage effects: higher pay can lower turnover and absenteeism and raise effort and productivity, so part of the cost is recouped and the employment effect is muted.\nEvaluation:\n• Employment and hours: the competitive model predicts job losses concentrated among the youngest and least experienced. In practice UK employment effects have been smaller than early predictions, which is usually attributed to monopsony power, to firms cutting hours rather than heads, and to cost pass-through into prices.\n• Real versus nominal: a 4.1% rise only raises living standards if prices rise by less; if CPI inflation matches it, the worker is no better off. This is the single most important evaluative point and the question invites it directly.\n• Tax and benefit interaction: the Extract\'s reference to employer NICs matters for the cost side, but on the worker side income tax, National Insurance and benefit withdrawal mean the marginal retention rate on a pay rise can be well below 100%, so gross gains overstate net gains.\n• Price pass-through: firms in low-paying sectors raise prices, and low-paid workers are disproportionately consumers of those same goods, which partially offsets the real gain.\n• Coverage: it helps only those IN work at the floor; the unemployed, the self-employed and those on zero-hours contracts with reduced hours gain nothing or lose.\n• Judgement: a well-supported conclusion turns on the real (not nominal) uprating, on the degree of monopsony in the sector, and on whether the aim is poverty reduction — for which tax credits and benefits are better targeted, since many minimum-wage earners live in higher-income households.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — accurate, correctly labelled diagram used to drive the argument; sustained reasoning; evaluation integrated; supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present but partly used; analysis developed but one-sided or evaluation asserted. L1 (1–5) — no or incorrect diagram, descriptive. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram and the information below, the extent to which the price mechanism responds effectively to a sudden shortage.

Extract 1 (written for this practice question)
When a fire closed a large UK baby-formula plant, wholesale prices for the affected brands rose by
about 30% within three weeks. Retailers imposed purchase limits of two tins per customer.
Online resellers listed tins at four times the pre-shortage price. Rival manufacturers announced
plans to expand capacity, but new production lines take roughly 18 months to commission and must
be approved by the regulator before they can supply the UK market.`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: demand and supply with supply shifting sharply LEFT; a steep (inelastic) supply curve and inelastic demand producing a large rise in price and a small fall in quantity. Strong answers add a second diagram or extension showing the long-run supply curve becoming more elastic as capacity is commissioned, returning price towards P1.\n• The three functions doing their work: SIGNALLING — the 30% price rise transmits the information that the good is now scarce, instantly and without any central planner needing to know why. INCENTIVE — it raises expected returns and pulls rival manufacturers into expanding capacity, which is exactly what the Extract reports. RATIONING — it clears the market by allocating the reduced supply to those willing to pay most.\n• Speed is the mechanism\'s great virtue: no administrative body could have identified the shortage and reallocated supply within three weeks.\nEvaluation:\n• Rationing by ability to pay is the core objection here: baby formula is a necessity with no substitute for the infants who need it, so allocating by willingness to pay allocates by income, not by need. Retailer purchase limits — a NON-price rationing device — emerged precisely because the price mechanism\'s distribution was judged unacceptable, which is evidence of its failure on equity.\n• The incentive function works, but too slowly: an 18-month commissioning lag plus regulatory approval means PES is near zero over the relevant horizon, so the price signal cannot summon extra supply when it is needed. Effectiveness therefore depends on the TIME PERIOD chosen.\n• Speculation and resale at four times the price transfer surplus to intermediaries without adding any supply, though the defence is that they move stock from low-value to high-value uses and that the prospect of profit encourages holding stocks against shortage.\n• Information failure: parents may not know that other brands are close substitutes, so demand does not switch as smoothly as the model assumes.\n• Intervention options and their costs: a maximum price would create excess demand and queues, and would blunt the incentive to expand capacity; state stockpiles are costly but avoid both problems.\n• Judgement: the mechanism responds FAST and CORRECTLY in direction, but its effectiveness should be judged on allocation as well as speed — a supported conclusion distinguishes efficiency (where it does well) from equity (where it does badly), and notes that the case for intervention is strongest exactly where the good is a necessity with inelastic supply.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — accurate diagram driving the argument, both efficiency and equity weighed, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, analysis one-sided. L1 (1–5) — no or incorrect diagram, descriptive. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram and the information below, the extent to which the burden of an indirect tax falls on consumers rather than producers.

Extract 1 (written for this practice question)
Two goods are subject to a specific duty of 50p per unit.
  Good X — cigarettes: estimated PED -0.4, estimated PES +1.8
  Good Y — restaurant meals: estimated PED -1.5, estimated PES +0.6
Before the duty, both goods sold for £5.00 per unit.`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: supply shifted vertically up by 50p, showing P1, the new consumer price P2 and the producer net price P3, with consumer incidence (P2 - P1) and producer incidence (P1 - P3) shaded separately. The strongest answers draw BOTH cases — a steep demand curve with a flat supply curve for X, and a flat demand curve with a steep supply curve for Y — so the contrast is visible.\n• The rule: the burden falls mainly on the side of the market that is LESS elastic, because that side has fewer alternatives and so absorbs the change rather than adjusting quantity. Formally, the consumer share of the burden is PES/(PES + |PED|).\n• Applied to the data: for cigarettes, 1.8/(1.8 + 0.4) = 0.82, so about 41p of the 50p falls on consumers. For restaurant meals, 0.6/(0.6 + 1.5) = 0.29, so only about 14p falls on consumers and 36p is absorbed by producers. That quantitative contrast is the heart of a top-band answer.\n• Consequences: for X the tax raises substantial revenue and changes consumption little; for Y it raises less revenue, cuts output more, and squeezes producers\' margins and therefore employment.\nEvaluation:\n• Elasticity estimates are uncertain and change with the time period: demand for cigarettes is far more elastic over ten years than over one, as smokers quit or switch to vaping, so the long-run consumer share is lower than 82%.\n• Market structure matters: a firm with market power may absorb the tax to protect share, and in oligopoly the pass-through depends on whether rivals move together. Perfect competition with constant costs gives full pass-through regardless of PED.\n• Cross-border and illicit substitution puts an effective ceiling on how much of a duty can be passed on, regardless of the measured domestic PED.\n• Legal versus economic incidence: who writes the cheque to HMRC is irrelevant; the split is determined entirely by elasticities, which is a point worth making explicitly.\n• Distributional evaluation: the high consumer incidence on cigarettes is precisely why tobacco duty is regressive — the burden falls on a group with below-average incomes.\n• Judgement: the extent to which consumers bear the burden is not a general fact but a function of relative elasticities, and the 82%/29% contrast in the data demonstrates that the same 50p duty can be predominantly a consumer tax or predominantly a producer tax.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — correct diagram, incidence quantified from the data, judgement supported. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, incidence described qualitatively. L1 (1–5) — no or incorrect diagram. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram and the information below, the extent to which government intervention improves resource allocation in the private rented housing market.

Extract 1 (written for this practice question)
Median private rents in England rose 7.7% in the year to March 2025. Around 1.33 million households
were on local authority housing waiting lists in 2024, while net additional dwellings completed fell
from 234 000 in 2019-20 to 199 000 in 2024-25. Campaigners have called for rent controls; landlord
bodies argue controls would reduce the supply of rental property and that planning reform is the
only durable answer.`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: the rental market with demand and a steep (inelastic) supply curve; a rent ceiling below equilibrium producing excess demand Qd - Qs, with the deadweight welfare loss triangle from the fall in quantity traded from Q1 to Qs shaded. Strong answers show the long-run supply curve as more elastic than the short-run one, so the shortage worsens over time as landlords exit.\n• The case for intervention: sitting tenants face high switching costs and a landlord holds bargaining power over a necessity, so rents can rise faster than any underlying cost. Rapid rent inflation (7.7% in the year to March 2025) alongside 1.33 million on waiting lists is evidence of a distributional problem the market will not resolve.\n• Forms of intervention to weigh: rent controls or rent stabilisation; housing benefit and Local Housing Allowance (a demand-side subsidy); social housebuilding (direct provision); security-of-tenure regulation; and planning liberalisation (a supply-side measure).\nEvaluation:\n• Rent controls deliver a large gain to incumbent tenants and a loss to everyone seeking a tenancy: they cause queues, discrimination in allocation, reduced maintenance (landlords cut quality when they cannot cut price) and, in the long run, conversion of rental stock to owner-occupation or short lets. The welfare loss grows as supply becomes more elastic over time.\n• Demand-side subsidies (housing benefit) are better targeted at need, but with the near-vertical short-run supply curve shown by the fall in completions from 234 000 to 199 000, they are substantially capitalised into higher rents — so the subsidy accrues to landlords rather than tenants. This is the strongest single analytical point available.\n• Direct provision (social housing) adds to supply rather than redistributing existing supply, and therefore avoids the capitalisation problem, but it is slow, fiscally expensive, and competes for the same constrained land and labour.\n• Government failure risks: planning restrictions are themselves a government-created barrier to supply, so the honest diagnosis may be that the market failure is caused by intervention rather than cured by it. Political short-termism favours visible demand-side help over unpopular planning reform.\n• Judgement: a supported conclusion should distinguish interventions that shift SUPPLY (planning reform, social building) from those that merely redistribute a fixed supply (controls, subsidies), and argue that with PES this low only the former improves allocation, whatever it does for equity.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — accurate diagram, both equity and efficiency weighed, the capitalisation argument reached, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, argument one-sided. L1 (1–5) — descriptive, no or incorrect diagram. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether a free-market economy allocates scarce resources more efficiently than a mixed economy.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The free-market case: the price mechanism performs signalling, incentive and rationing functions simultaneously and decentrally. Hayek\'s argument is informational — the knowledge needed to allocate resources is dispersed among millions of agents and cannot be assembled by any planner, so prices economise on information in a way no central body can replicate. Competition drives productive efficiency (firms that do not minimise cost are driven out) and the profit motive drives dynamic efficiency through innovation.\n• The theoretical benchmark: in perfect competition with no externalities, long-run equilibrium delivers P = MC (allocative efficiency) and production at minimum AC (productive efficiency), and the sum of consumer and producer surplus is maximised.\n• The market-failure case for a mixed economy: public goods are non-rival and non-excludable, so free-riding means the market provides none (national defence, flood defence); externalities mean private and social costs diverge, so the market over-produces pollution and under-produces vaccination and education; information asymmetry produces adverse selection (the used-car and health-insurance markets); monopoly power restricts output below the competitive level; factor immobility leaves resources idle; and inequality of endowments means the market\'s allocation is efficient only relative to a distribution of income that has no ethical standing.\n• UK evidence of the mixed model in practice: regulated natural monopolies in water and energy (Ofwat\'s PR24 determinations effective 1 April 2025 allowed average bills to rise about £157, or 36%, before inflation over 2025-2030); competition enforcement by the CMA, which concluded its veterinary services market investigation on 24 March 2026 with remedies including compulsory price transparency and a cap on prescription fees; state provision of healthcare and schooling; and the return of franchised rail services to public ownership under the Passenger Railway Services (Public Ownership) Act 2024, beginning with South Western Railway on 25 May 2025.\nEvaluation:\n• Government failure is the decisive counter-argument: intervention carries information problems of its own (the regulator knows less than the firm), administrative cost, regulatory capture, political short-termism and unintended consequences. A comparison of an idealised state with a real market, or vice versa, is the commonest weakness in answers to this question and should be avoided.\n• \'Efficiency\' needs unpacking. Allocative, productive, dynamic and X-efficiency can point in different directions: a monopoly may be dynamically efficient while allocatively inefficient, and a state monopoly may deliver universal access at the cost of X-inefficiency.\n• Efficiency is not the only criterion. A free market can be Pareto-efficient and grossly unequal; if society values equity, the relevant question is the trade-off, not efficiency alone.\n• The real choice is never binary. Every real economy is mixed; the meaningful question is WHERE the boundary should sit, and the answer differs by market — competitive retail markets need little intervention, natural monopolies and public goods need a great deal.\n• Judgement: the strongest conclusions argue that markets are superior wherever the conditions for competition roughly hold, that the market-failure categories identify precisely the areas where they are not, and that the case for intervention in any given market therefore depends on whether the identified market failure is larger than the government failure the remedy would create.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) — sustained, balanced, well-evidenced argument with analysis and evaluation fully integrated and a conclusion that follows from it. L4 (16–20) — well-developed and mostly balanced with sound evaluation. L3 (11–15) — some analysis and evaluation, balance uneven. L2 (6–10) — largely descriptive, one-sided. L1 (1–5) — fragmentary. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram, whether maximum prices are an effective way of making essential goods affordable.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: a binding maximum price below equilibrium with excess demand Qd - Qs marked, the deadweight welfare loss triangle from the fall in quantity traded, and the transfer of producer surplus to those consumers who still obtain the good. Strong answers add a second diagram showing how the shortage worsens as supply becomes more elastic over time, or contrast an inelastic-supply case (rent) with an elastic-supply one.\n• The case for: essentials — energy, water, rent, staple food, some medicines — have very inelastic demand, so a price rise transfers large amounts of surplus from consumers to producers without doing much to ration consumption. Where the seller has market power or where consumers are inert, a ceiling limits exploitation. Consumers who still obtain the good gain unambiguously.\n• UK examples to deploy: the Ofgem domestic price cap, £1 723 a year for a typical dual-fuel direct-debit household from 1 October 2026, up 4% on the July-September level of £1 663; the CMA\'s £21 cap on veterinary prescription fees announced in its final report of 24 March 2026; regulated rail fares. Note that each of these is a REGULATED price, reset against costs, rather than a classic fixed ceiling — the distinction is worth making explicitly.\nEvaluation:\n• The shortage is the central objection: at Pmax quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied, so the good must be rationed by queues, waiting lists, quality reduction or discrimination. Those rationed out are often the very people the policy was meant to help, because the rich have better access to queues and informal markets.\n• Dynamic effects: a ceiling below cost destroys the incentive to invest and to enter. The 2021-22 collapse of about 30 UK energy suppliers illustrates what happens when a capped retail price lags a surging wholesale cost, and long-run under-investment is the standard indictment of rent control.\n• Quality and black markets: landlords cut maintenance; secondary markets emerge at above-cap prices, transferring surplus to intermediaries.\n• Targeting: a universal cap gives the largest absolute benefit to the largest consumers, who are typically better off. Targeted income transfers (social tariffs, Warm Home Discount, housing benefit) reach the poor more precisely and preserve the marginal price signal — though with inelastic supply they are partly capitalised into higher prices, which is the symmetric objection.\n• Alternative instruments to weigh: subsidies to producers (which raise quantity rather than reducing it, at fiscal cost); tax reductions such as the cut in VAT on domestic electricity from 5% to 0% for 1 October 2026 to 31 March 2027; direct state provision; and supply-side measures that lower the equilibrium price itself (insulation, planning reform, new reservoirs).\n• Design matters: a cap on a MARGIN or on a fee (the £21 prescription cap) has very different effects from a cap on the price of the good itself, because it constrains rent extraction without suppressing the quantity signal. A cap that is indexed to costs, like Ofgem\'s, avoids creating a permanent shortage but by the same token stops it protecting consumers when costs rise.\n• Judgement: effective at protecting incumbent consumers in the short run where supply is inelastic and the seller has market power; ineffective and counterproductive over longer horizons where supply can respond. A supported conclusion should specify the time period and the elasticity conditions under which it holds, and should usually prefer targeted transfers plus supply-side action for the affordability objective, reserving caps for the market-power objective.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) / L4 (16–20) / L3 (11–15) / L2 (6–10) / L1 (1–5) as above; an accurate, fully integrated diagram is required for Level 4 and above. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether knowledge of elasticity should be the main determinant of a government\'s decision to tax a good.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Why elasticity matters for revenue: the Ramsey result is that, for a given amount of revenue, the deadweight loss is minimised by taxing goods with inelastic demand most heavily, because the quantity distortion is smallest there. This is why the exchequer relies on tobacco, alcohol and fuel duties.\n• Why elasticity matters for incidence: the consumer\'s share of the burden is PES/(PES + |PED|), so elasticity determines who actually pays, irrespective of who remits the tax. A government aiming to protect domestic producers, or to protect consumers, needs this before it can predict the effect of its own policy.\n• Why elasticity matters for behaviour change: if the purpose is to reduce consumption of a demerit good, an inelastic demand curve means a tax raises revenue and changes little. The Soft Drinks Industry Levy is instructive because it sidestepped this — rates of 20.8p per litre at 5g to under 8g of sugar per 100ml and 27.8p at 8g or more (rates in force from 1 April 2026) worked chiefly through producer REFORMULATION rather than consumer response, and the thresholds tighten to 4.5g per 100ml from 1 January 2028, with milk-based drinks losing their exemption.\nEvaluation — other determinants that can outweigh elasticity:\n• The externality itself. A Pigouvian tax should be set equal to the marginal external cost at the social optimum, which is a question of valuing harm, not of elasticity. Elasticity tells you what the tax will DO, not what it SHOULD be.\n• Equity. Goods with inelastic demand are disproportionately necessities, so the Ramsey rule prescribes exactly the most regressive taxes. Tobacco duty is the clearest case: efficient by the revenue criterion, regressive by the distributional one. Any government weighing efficiency against equity will depart from the elasticity prescription.\n• Administrative feasibility and avoidance. A tax is worthless if it cannot be collected: cross-border shopping, illicit trade and online substitution put a practical ceiling on rates, and this constraint binds precisely on the inelastic goods the theory recommends.\n• Political constraints and salience. Fuel duty has been frozen repeatedly despite inelastic demand, because visible price rises at the pump are politically costly — so political economy, not elasticity, has determined the rate.\n• International competitiveness and evasion by producers, including profit-shifting and relocation.\n• Behavioural design. Threshold taxes, minimum unit pricing (Scotland\'s MUP rose from 50p to 65p per unit on 30 September 2024) and salience effects can achieve more behaviour change per pound of tax than the elasticity of aggregate demand would predict, because they act on the cheapest units or on producer incentives rather than on the average consumer.\n• Measurement: elasticity estimates are historical, vary with the time period (long-run PED substantially exceeds short-run PED for addictive goods), and are not constant along a demand curve, so treating them as a precise parameter overstates what is known.\n• Judgement: elasticity is indispensable for PREDICTING the effects of a tax and should determine the design and the expected yield, but the DECISION to tax rests on the objective — revenue, correction of an externality, or redistribution — and on equity and feasibility constraints that elasticity is silent about. The strongest answers separate the positive question (what will the tax do?) from the normative one (should it be levied?) and conclude accordingly.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) / L4 (16–20) / L3 (11–15) / L2 (6–10) / L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether the UK government should return more industries to public ownership.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The context: franchised passenger rail services are being taken into public ownership as contracts expire, under the Passenger Railway Services (Public Ownership) Act 2024, which received Royal Assent on 28 November 2024. South Western Railway transferred on 25 May 2025, c2c on 20 July 2025, Greater Anglia on 12 October 2025 and West Midlands Trains on 1 February 2026, with further operators following. Water, energy networks and Royal Mail are the industries most often proposed for similar treatment.\n• The case FOR public ownership: many of these industries are NATURAL MONOPOLIES — a single network has falling long-run average cost over the whole range of demand, so duplication is wasteful and competition is impossible. A private natural monopoly restricts output and prices above marginal cost, creating a deadweight welfare loss; public ownership allows pricing at marginal cost, with the resulting loss covered from taxation. It also internalises externalities directly (network integration, environmental standards), avoids the cost of paying a private risk premium and dividends, allows planning across the whole network, and removes the regulator\'s information disadvantage.\n• Evidence sometimes cited: Ofwat\'s PR24 final determinations, effective from 1 April 2025, allowed average household bills to rise about £157 (roughly 36%) before inflation over 2025-2030 alongside a poor record on sewage discharges, which critics read as evidence that regulated private ownership has failed to deliver investment.\nEvaluation — the case AGAINST:\n• X-inefficiency: without the threat of bankruptcy or takeover, state monopolies face weak cost discipline, and managers face no residual claimant demanding minimum cost. This was the central charge against the pre-1980s nationalised industries.\n• Dynamic efficiency: the profit motive and access to private capital drive innovation and investment; public bodies compete for capital against health and education in the annual spending round, so investment becomes hostage to fiscal cycles and political short-termism.\n• Principal-agent and political interference: ministers may set prices below cost for electoral reasons, or resist closures and restructuring, so allocative decisions become political rather than economic.\n• Cost: compensation to shareholders is a large one-off fiscal cost with a real opportunity cost, though the rail case avoids most of it because contracts simply expire.\n• The regulation alternative: the question is not ownership versus no control but PUBLIC OWNERSHIP versus REGULATED PRIVATE OWNERSHIP. RPI-X price capping gives firms an incentive to cut costs (they keep the difference within the period), and competition authorities can act where the monopoly is not natural — the CMA\'s veterinary market investigation, concluded 24 March 2026 with price-transparency remedies and a £21 prescription fee cap, shows an alternative route to consumer protection without ownership change.\n• It depends on the industry: the natural-monopoly argument is strong for water pipes and rail track, weak for rail operations (which can be and are competitively tendered) and very weak for competitive markets with many firms.\n• Evidence is genuinely mixed and comparisons are hard: state-owned railways in some European countries perform well, others badly, and confounding factors (geography, subsidy levels, investment history) make attribution to ownership unsafe.\n• Judgement: the strongest conclusions argue that ownership matters less than market structure and the quality of governance — that the case is strongest where a genuine natural monopoly coexists with demonstrable regulatory failure, and weakest where competition is feasible; and that the choice should be made industry by industry rather than as a general principle.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) / L4 (16–20) / L3 (11–15) / L2 (6–10) / L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram, whether subsidies are the best way of increasing consumption of goods the government wishes to encourage.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: either (a) supply shifted vertically down by the subsidy with P falling, Q rising, and the total cost to government shown as (P3 - P2) x Q2, with the split between consumer and producer benefit depending on elasticities; or (b) the merit-good case, with MPB below MSB, the free-market output below the social optimum, and the welfare gain triangle recovered by a subsidy equal to the marginal external benefit. The strongest answers use (b) to justify the policy and (a) to analyse its incidence.\n• The case for subsidies: where consumption generates positive externalities (vaccination, training, public transport, insulation) or where information failure and myopia cause under-consumption of merit goods, private demand sits below the socially optimal level. A subsidy lowers the price faced by consumers and moves output towards where MSB = MSC, raising welfare. It preserves consumer choice — unlike a mandate — and is quick to implement.\n• Additional arguments: subsidies can build scale in an infant industry until economies of scale and network effects make it self-sustaining; and they can be targeted at particular groups (free school meals, tuition support) to address equity simultaneously.\nEvaluation:\n• Incidence: the benefit splits according to relative elasticities. Where supply is inelastic — housing, childcare places, early electric vehicles — a large share of the subsidy is captured by producers as a higher net price rather than passed to consumers as a lower price, so take-up rises far less than the cost implies. This is the single most powerful analytical objection.\n• Opportunity cost and deadweight cost: subsidies must be funded by taxation, which itself distorts; and much of the money pays for consumption that would have occurred anyway (inframarginal purchases), so the cost per ADDITIONAL unit consumed is far higher than the headline rate.\n• Valuation problem: setting the subsidy equal to the marginal external benefit requires measuring that benefit, which is extremely difficult; error in either direction creates a new welfare loss.\n• Distribution: universal subsidies are frequently regressive in incidence because better-off households consume more of the subsidised good; means-testing improves targeting but adds administrative cost and reduces take-up.\n• Government failure: subsidies are politically easy to introduce and very hard to withdraw, they attract lobbying and regulatory capture, and they can entrench inefficient producers who no longer need to minimise cost.\n• Alternatives to weigh seriously:\n  - Direct state provision, which guarantees the quantity (universal vaccination, state schooling) but risks X-inefficiency and removes choice.\n  - Regulation or mandates, which achieve the target quantity at no exchequer cost — the ZEV mandate on manufacturers is the obvious comparison with EV purchase grants — but raise prices elsewhere and are blunt.\n  - Information provision and nudges (default enrolment, salience), which are extremely cheap per unit of behaviour change where the failure is behavioural rather than financial.\n  - Taxing the substitute instead, which raises revenue rather than spending it.\n• Judgement: a supported conclusion turns on WHY consumption is too low. If the cause is price and supply is elastic, a subsidy is efficient and well-targeted; if the cause is information, inertia or habit, a nudge or an information campaign will do more per pound; if supply is inelastic, the subsidy will be capitalised and a supply-side or regulatory instrument is preferable. The best answers also note that these are complements, not substitutes — most successful policies combine a subsidy with regulation and information.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) / L4 (16–20) / L3 (11–15) / L2 (6–10) / L1 (1–5); an accurate, integrated diagram is required for Level 4 and above. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether rational decision-making is a realistic assumption on which to base economic analysis.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• What the assumption says: consumers maximise utility and firms maximise profit, given stable, consistent preferences, full information and the ability to process it. It underpins the downward-sloping demand curve, the marginal utility theory of demand, the MC = MR rule and essentially every diagram on the specification.\n• Why economists retain it: it is TRACTABLE and it PREDICTS well in aggregate. Demand curves do slope downwards; firms do respond to cost changes; markets do clear approximately. A model is judged on the accuracy of its predictions, not the literal realism of its assumptions (Friedman\'s methodological defence). Deviations that are random cancel out across millions of agents.\n• The behavioural critique, with the specification\'s categories:\n  - Bounded rationality: limited time, information and cognitive capacity mean agents SATISFICE rather than maximise (Simon). Tariff comparison in energy or mobile markets is genuinely too complex to optimise.\n  - Rules of thumb and heuristics: anchoring, availability, representativeness.\n  - Bias and inertia: the status quo bias and default effects explain why large numbers of UK households never switch energy supplier despite available savings, and why pension auto-enrolment transformed participation without changing the financial incentives at all.\n  - Loss aversion and the endowment effect: losses loom larger than equivalent gains, which violates the symmetry the standard model assumes.\n  - Hyperbolic discounting and present bias: systematic under-saving and over-consumption of demerit goods.\n  - Social norms, altruism and fairness: behaviour in ultimatum-game experiments is inconsistent with narrow self-interest.\nEvaluation:\n• The biases are SYSTEMATIC, not random, which is what makes them fatal to the aggregation defence. If everyone is biased in the same direction, the errors do not cancel.\n• But irrationality is often apparent rather than real once costs are counted properly. Declining a £180 energy saving is rational if the search and switching effort is worth more than £180 to that household — so what looks like a failure of rationality may be an omitted search cost in the model rather than a flaw in the agent.\n• Firms face competitive selection: a firm that does not approximate profit maximisation is driven out, so the assumption can hold at the level of surviving firms even if no manager consciously computes MC = MR. This defence is much weaker for consumers, who face no such selection pressure.\n• The assumption fails hardest exactly where policy matters most: pensions, health, addiction, insurance and complex financial products — decisions that are infrequent, complex, and with delayed consequences, so learning cannot occur.\n• Policy consequences are substantial: if consumers are boundedly rational, information provision (which assumes people will process it) works poorly, while choice architecture and defaults work very well and cost almost nothing — which is why nudge units exist. It also justifies paternalistic intervention in merit and demerit goods that a pure rational-agent model cannot justify at all, since a rational agent\'s choices are by definition welfare-maximising for them.\n• Behavioural economics has NOT replaced the standard model: it supplements it. There is no single alternative theory with the generality of utility maximisation, and behavioural findings are often context-specific and have faced replication problems.\n• Judgement: the strongest answers conclude that rationality is a useful approximation whose validity varies systematically by CONTEXT — reliable for frequent, low-stakes, repeated decisions with clear feedback, unreliable for infrequent, complex, delayed-consequence decisions — and that economic analysis should therefore retain it as a baseline while using behavioural evidence to identify where the baseline will mislead.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) / L4 (16–20) / L3 (11–15) / L2 (6–10) / L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},
    ]
  },
  '1.2': {
    name: 'Market Failure & Government Intervention',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'market failure\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `1 mark: a situation in which the free market fails to allocate scarce resources efficiently.\n1 mark: development — the market equilibrium quantity differs from the socially optimum quantity where marginal social benefit equals marginal social cost, so there is a loss of welfare (a deadweight loss). Accept a valid example of a cause (externalities, public goods, information failure, monopoly power, factor immobility) as the second mark only if it is linked to the misallocation.\nAccept complete market failure (the market provides none of the good) and partial market failure (the market provides the wrong quantity).`},

      { q: 'Identify two characteristics of a pure public good.', marks: 2, command: 'Identify',
        markScheme: `1 mark each, maximum 2:\n• Non-rivalry — one person\'s consumption does not reduce the amount available to anyone else, so the marginal cost of an additional user is zero.\n• Non-excludability — once provided it is impossible, or prohibitively costly, to prevent anyone from consuming it, which is what gives rise to the free-rider problem.\nAccept non-rejectability as a third valid characteristic. Do not credit \'provided by the government\' — that is a consequence, not a defining characteristic, and many publicly provided goods (health care, schooling) are private goods in the economic sense.`},

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by a \'negative externality in production\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `1 mark: a cost of production imposed on a third party who is neither the producer nor the consumer, and which is not reflected in the market price.\n1 mark: development — it means marginal social cost exceeds marginal private cost, so the free market over-produces relative to the social optimum and generates a welfare loss. A valid example (factory effluent discharged into a river, emissions from a cement works, antibiotic resistance from intensive livestock farming) can earn the development mark if the third-party cost is identified.`},

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'moral hazard\', using one example.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `1 mark: a situation in which one party changes its behaviour to take on more risk because it does not bear the full consequences of that risk, the other party being unable to observe or control the change.\n1 mark: a developed example — a fully insured driver taking less care to lock the car; a bank taking on risk in the expectation of a public bail-out because it is judged too big to fail; a household with comprehensive travel insurance behaving less carefully with luggage.\nNote the distinction from adverse selection, which arises BEFORE the contract is agreed; moral hazard arises afterwards. Do not credit a pure adverse-selection example.`},

      { q: `Refer to the stimulus material below.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
A chemical plant produces 40 000 tonnes of output a year.
  Marginal private cost: £320 per tonne
  Marginal external cost (river pollution and respiratory illness): £45 per tonne

Calculate the total external cost imposed by the plant each year. You are advised to show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: total external cost = marginal external cost x output = £45 x 40 000 (1 mark for correct method).\nCorrect answer: £1 800 000, or £1.8 million (1 mark).\nAn answer of £14 600 000 (total social cost, £365 x 40 000) shows a misreading of the question and scores a maximum of 1 mark for method if the working is shown. Accept the answer with or without units, but do not credit £45 alone.`},

      { q: 'Explain the difference between a specific tax and an ad valorem tax.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `1 mark: a specific (unit) tax is a fixed amount of money per unit sold regardless of price, so it shifts the supply curve upward in a PARALLEL shift. Example: alcohol duty per litre of pure alcohol, or the Soft Drinks Industry Levy charged in pence per litre.\n1 mark: an ad valorem tax is charged as a percentage of the price, so the absolute amount rises with price and the supply curve PIVOTS upward, the gap widening as price rises. Example: VAT at the standard rate of 20%.\nOne mark only if the candidate defines both but does not distinguish the effect on the supply curve or give the essential per-unit versus percentage contrast.`},

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'regulatory capture\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `1 mark: a situation in which a regulator comes to act in the interests of the industry it regulates rather than in the interests of consumers or the wider public.\n1 mark: development — it arises because the firms hold far better information than the regulator, because staff move between regulator and industry, and because the industry\'s incentive to lobby is concentrated while consumers\' incentive to resist is diffuse. It is one of the standard causes of GOVERNMENT failure, since the intervention meant to correct a market failure ends up entrenching it.`},

      { q: `Refer to the stimulus material below.

Stimulus
Scotland\'s minimum unit price for alcohol rose from 50p to 65p per unit of alcohol on 30 September 2024.
A 70cl bottle of spirits has an alcohol by volume of 40%, which is 28 units of alcohol.

Calculate the minimum legal price at which that bottle may be sold in Scotland. You are advised to show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: minimum price = units x minimum unit price = 28 x £0.65 (1 mark for correct method, including a correct derivation of 28 units from 0.7 litres x 40 if the candidate works it out).\nCorrect answer: £18.20 (1 mark).\nAn answer of £14.00 uses the superseded 50p rate and scores a maximum of 1 mark for method. Accept 1820p.`},

      { q: 'Explain why a pure public good is unlikely to be provided by the free market.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Up to 3 marks for a developed chain of reasoning:\n1 mark — non-excludability means a producer cannot prevent non-payers from consuming the good once it exists, so there is no way to charge a price and no way to withhold supply from a customer who refuses to pay.\n1 mark — this creates the free-rider problem: each individual\'s rational strategy is to let others pay and consume for nothing, so stated willingness to pay understates true valuation and revealed demand collapses.\n1 mark — with no revenue stream a profit-maximising firm cannot cover its costs, so supply is zero even though the total social benefit exceeds the total cost. This is complete market failure, and it is why goods such as national defence, street lighting and flood defence are funded from general taxation.\nCredit the additional point that non-rivalry means the efficient price is zero (marginal cost of an extra user is zero), so charging any positive price would itself be allocatively inefficient.`},

      { q: 'Explain why common pool resources such as ocean fish stocks tend to be over-exploited.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Up to 3 marks:\n1 mark — common pool resources are RIVAL (a fish caught by one boat cannot be caught by another) but NON-EXCLUDABLE (no one owns the open ocean, so no one can be kept out). This combination is the source of the problem.\n1 mark — each individual fishing fleet captures the full private benefit of an extra catch but bears only a small fraction of the cost of depleting the stock, which is spread across all users and across future generations. Marginal private cost therefore lies below marginal social cost and the fleet rationally over-fishes.\n1 mark — because every operator reasons identically, the resource is depleted below the level at which it can regenerate: the tragedy of the commons. The absence of property rights means no one has an incentive to conserve, since a fish left in the water is simply caught by a rival.\nCredit the policy consequence — quotas, licences and tradeable catch shares work by creating the excludability the market lacks.`},

      { q: 'Explain why asymmetric information in the health insurance market can lead to adverse selection.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Up to 3 marks:\n1 mark — the buyer knows more about their own health, family history and lifestyle than the insurer does, so information is asymmetric and the insurer cannot price each individual\'s true risk.\n1 mark — the insurer therefore charges a premium based on the average risk of the pool. That premium is a bad deal for low-risk individuals, who drop out, and a good deal for high-risk individuals, who buy eagerly.\n1 mark — as low-risk customers exit, the average risk of the remaining pool rises, so the insurer must raise premiums again, driving out the next-healthiest group. This adverse-selection spiral can shrink or destroy the market entirely — a missing market, which is a form of market failure.\nCredit the remedies as development: compulsory universal insurance, risk pooling through taxation as in the NHS, employer group schemes, or medical underwriting.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how a negative externality in production leads to a misallocation of resources.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): cost/benefit on the vertical axis and quantity on the horizontal; MPC and MSC both upward sloping with MSC ABOVE MPC, the vertical gap being the marginal external cost; MPB = MSB downward sloping; free-market equilibrium Q1 where MPB = MPC; social optimum Q* where MSB = MSC, with Q* < Q1; the welfare loss triangle between MSC and MSB over the range Q* to Q1 shaded and labelled.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): profit-maximising firms consider only their private costs, so they produce at Q1. Over the range Q* to Q1 each unit costs society more than it benefits society, because MSC exceeds MSB, so society would gain by producing less. The shaded triangle measures that welfare loss.\nDevelopment: resources are over-allocated to this good and the good is under-priced; correcting it requires internalising the external cost, for example with a tax equal to the marginal external cost at Q*.\nNo diagram, or MSC drawn below MPC: maximum 2 marks.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how a positive externality in consumption leads to under-consumption of a good.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): MPC = MSC upward sloping; MPB downward sloping with MSB ABOVE and to the right of MPB, the vertical gap being the marginal external benefit; free-market equilibrium Q1 where MPB = MPC; social optimum Q* where MSB = MSC, with Q* > Q1; the welfare GAIN triangle between MSB and MSC over Q1 to Q* shaded and labelled as potential welfare gain.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): consumers weigh only their own private benefit, so they consume Q1. Over the range Q1 to Q* each additional unit generates more benefit to society than it costs, so under-consumption means a forgone welfare gain. Vaccination is the standard case: the individual values their own protection but not the reduction in transmission risk to everyone else.\nDevelopment: resources are under-allocated to the good and the market price is below the socially optimal level; a subsidy equal to the marginal external benefit at Q*, or state provision, would move output to Q*.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how a Pigouvian tax can correct a negative production externality.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): MPC, MSC above it, MSB downward sloping, free-market output Q1 and social optimum Q*; the tax shown as a vertical shift of MPC (or of the supply curve) upward by the marginal external cost, so that MPC + tax coincides with MSC and the new market equilibrium is at Q*. Label the tax per unit and the price rise.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): the tax makes the firm pay for the external cost it imposes, so the external cost is INTERNALISED and private cost now equals social cost. The firm\'s own profit-maximising decision then delivers the socially optimal output, and the welfare loss triangle is eliminated.\nDevelopment: the tax should equal the marginal external cost AT THE SOCIAL OPTIMUM, not at the free-market output. The revenue raised (tax per unit x Q*) can be hypothecated to clean-up or compensation, though the efficiency of the tax does not depend on how the revenue is used.\nCredit the evaluative note that valuing the external cost accurately is extremely difficult, so the tax is rarely set at exactly the right level.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how a subsidy can correct the under-consumption of a merit good.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): either the externality version (MPB below MSB, subsidy shifting supply down so the new equilibrium is at Q*) or the market version (D and S with supply shifted vertically down by the subsidy per unit, price falling to P2, quantity rising to Q2, producer net price P3, government cost (P3 - P2) x Q2). Axes and all curves labelled; the increase in quantity towards the social optimum must be visible.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): the subsidy lowers the price consumers face, so they extend demand along the demand curve. Because the good is a merit good, consumers under-estimate its private benefit (information failure and short-termism) and there is also an external benefit, so the extra consumption raises welfare.\nDevelopment worth the fourth mark: the subsidy should equal the marginal external benefit at the social optimum; and the split of the benefit between consumers and producers depends on relative elasticities, so with inelastic supply much of it is captured by producers as a higher net price.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, why a demerit good is over-consumed in a free market.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): MPC = MSC upward sloping; MPB downward sloping with MSB BELOW and to the left of MPB, the vertical gap being the marginal external cost of consumption; free-market Q1 where MPB = MPC; social optimum Q* where MSB = MSC, with Q* < Q1; welfare loss triangle between MPB and MSB over Q* to Q1 shaded.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): consumers act on their perceived private benefit, which for a demerit good exceeds the true social benefit for two reasons — consumption imposes costs on third parties (passive smoking, alcohol-related violence, NHS treatment costs) and consumers themselves over-value the good because of information failure, addiction, present bias and bounded rationality.\nDevelopment: the two causes justify different remedies — external costs justify a corrective tax, while information failure and irrationality justify education, advertising bans, and nudges. Strong answers note that the second cause is paternalistic and more contested than the first.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the effect of a minimum unit price for alcohol set above the market equilibrium price.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): demand and supply for alcohol with free-market equilibrium P1Q1; a horizontal minimum price line above P1; quantity demanded Qd on the demand curve and quantity supplied Qs on the supply curve, with Qs > Qd and the gap labelled excess supply / surplus; actual quantity traded is Qd, the shorter side of the market.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): a binding price floor raises the price consumers must pay, so quantity demanded contracts along the demand curve from Q1 to Qd. Because consumption of alcohol generates negative externalities, the reduction in quantity moves output towards the social optimum and raises welfare, even though it creates a surplus in the market.\nDevelopment: unlike a tax, the extra revenue accrues to RETAILERS rather than to the government, which is the principal criticism of the policy. Scotland\'s minimum unit price rose from 50p to 65p per unit on 30 September 2024. It bites hardest on the cheap, high-strength products favoured by the heaviest drinkers, which is precisely why it is argued to be better targeted than a uniform duty.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how a cap on tradeable pollution permits reduces emissions.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): the market for permits — price of a permit on the vertical axis, quantity of permits on the horizontal; a VERTICAL supply curve at the quantity fixed by the regulator (the cap); a downward-sloping demand curve reflecting firms\' marginal abatement costs; equilibrium permit price P1. Show the supply line shifted LEFT as the cap is tightened, raising the permit price to P2.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): the regulator fixes the total quantity of emissions, so the environmental outcome is certain. Firms that can abate cheaply do so and sell their surplus permits; firms facing high abatement costs buy permits instead. Trading therefore equalises marginal abatement cost across firms, which achieves the target at the lowest total cost to the economy.\nDevelopment: the permit price is the carbon price firms face, and tightening the cap raises it, strengthening the incentive to invest in low-carbon technology. Under the UK Emissions Trading Scheme, free allocation for aviation ended on 1 January 2026 and the scheme is being extended to the maritime sector from 2026; the UK and EU announced on 19 May 2025 their intention to link their two schemes.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which the Soft Drinks Industry Levy has reduced sugar consumption.

Stimulus (written for this practice question, using published rates)
The Soft Drinks Industry Levy is charged on the producer or importer, not at the till:
  Less than 5g of sugar per 100ml: zero rate
  5g to less than 8g per 100ml: 20.8p per litre (rate in force from 1 April 2026)
  8g per 100ml or more: 27.8p per litre (rate in force from 1 April 2026)
At the Autumn 2025 Budget the government announced that from 1 January 2028 the lower threshold
falls from 5g to 4.5g per 100ml and the exemption for milk-based drinks is removed.
The levy was designed with a threshold structure rather than as a tax on every gram of sugar.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The design point is the analytical key: because the levy is charged on a THRESHOLD and levied on producers, the cheapest way for a manufacturer to avoid it is to reformulate below 5g per 100ml rather than to raise the price. Most large producers did exactly that, so sugar per litre fell without consumers needing to change what they bought — a behaviour change achieved through producers, not through PED.\n• This matters because demand for sugary drinks is relatively price inelastic and consumption is habitual, so a conventional consumption tax would have raised revenue and changed quantity little. The levy sidestepped the elasticity problem.\n• The 2028 tightening to 4.5g in the stimulus is evidence that the mechanism is understood to work through thresholds: moving the threshold triggers a further round of reformulation.\nEvaluation:\n• Substitution: consumers may switch to untaxed sugar — confectionery, cakes, juices, and (until 2028) milk-based drinks, which the stimulus notes were exempt. Total sugar intake may therefore have fallen by far less than sugar from soft drinks.\n• Reformulation uses artificial sweeteners, whose long-term health effects are debated, so a fall in sugar is not automatically a health gain.\n• Attribution: sugar consumption was already falling because of public-health campaigns, voluntary reformulation commitments and changing tastes, so the counterfactual is unclear.\n• Distribution: to the extent that prices did rise on unreformulated premium brands, the burden is regressive.\n• Judgement: strong evidence that the levy cut the sugar CONTENT of soft drinks, much weaker evidence that it cut total sugar CONSUMPTION — and the credit belongs to the threshold design rather than to indirect taxation as such.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — the threshold design and the rates in the stimulus used explicitly to drive a developed argument, with evaluation weighed. L1 (1–4) — general comments on sugar taxes with no use of the stimulus.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which tradeable pollution permits are an effective way of reducing carbon emissions.

Stimulus (written for this practice question, using published UK developments)
The UK Emissions Trading Scheme caps total emissions from energy-intensive industry, power
generation and aviation, and issues tradeable allowances up to that cap.
  Free allocation of allowances to aviation ended on 1 January 2026.
  The scheme is being extended to the maritime sector from 2026.
  On 19 May 2025 the UK and the EU announced their intention to link the UK ETS with the EU ETS.
Some allowances are still allocated free of charge to industries judged at risk of relocating
production to countries with weaker carbon pricing.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Certainty of outcome: a cap fixes the QUANTITY of emissions, so the environmental target is met by construction. A carbon tax fixes the price and leaves the quantity uncertain — the central theoretical advantage of permits where the damage function is steep.\n• Static efficiency: trading equalises marginal abatement cost across all covered firms, so the target is achieved at the minimum total cost. Low-cost abaters over-comply and sell; high-cost abaters buy.\n• Dynamic efficiency: a positive and rising permit price gives a permanent incentive to invest in low-carbon capital, because every tonne abated is an allowance that can be sold.\n• Extension of coverage (maritime from 2026, aviation losing free allocation on 1 January 2026) widens the share of emissions facing a price, reducing the scope for substitution into uncovered sectors.\nEvaluation:\n• Setting the cap is the weak point: too generous and the permit price collapses, destroying the abatement incentive; this has happened in both the EU and UK schemes at various points. The regulator needs information about abatement costs that it does not have.\n• Free allocation, which the stimulus notes continues for trade-exposed industry, blunts the incentive and transfers rent to incumbents, though it addresses a genuine problem.\n• Carbon leakage: production and emissions relocate to jurisdictions without a carbon price, so global emissions may be unchanged while domestic output falls. Linking the UK and EU schemes, announced 19 May 2025, reduces leakage between those two jurisdictions and ends the price divergence, but does nothing about the rest of the world; border carbon adjustment is the complementary instrument.\n• Coverage: the scheme excludes most transport, agriculture and domestic heating, which are large emission sources, so it cannot be the whole policy.\n• Price volatility raises the cost of capital for abatement investment, which a tax would avoid; hybrid designs with price floors and ceilings try to capture both.\n• Judgement: effective and cost-efficient WITHIN its coverage provided the cap is tight and free allocation is limited; the binding constraints are political willingness to tighten the cap and the sectors left outside.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — stimulus developments used explicitly, mechanism analysed, evaluation weighed. L1 (1–4) — describes how permits work without applying the stimulus.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which information provision is an effective way of reducing obesity.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
Since April 2022, businesses in England with 250 or more employees must display calorie
information on menus. A survey of 1 200 diners found:
  Noticed the calorie labels: 43%
  Said the labels changed what they ordered: 17%
  Estimated average reduction in calories ordered by those who changed: 91 kcal
  Adults in England classified as overweight or obese (Health Survey for England, 2022): about 64%`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The theory: obesity is partly an information failure. If consumers systematically under-estimate the calorie content of restaurant meals, their perceived private benefit exceeds the true private benefit, and correcting the information moves consumption towards the social optimum without restricting choice. It is cheap, politically uncontroversial and preserves consumer sovereignty.\n• The stimulus supports a small effect: 17% of 43% who noticed implies a change in behaviour by roughly 7% of diners, at about 91 kcal each — an average reduction across all diners of roughly 6 kcal per meal, which is trivial against a 64% overweight-or-obese prevalence.\nEvaluation:\n• The premise may be wrong. If consumers already know the information and still choose the meal, the failure is not informational but behavioural — present bias, habit, the pull of immediate reward against delayed cost — and no amount of labelling will fix it.\n• Attention is the binding constraint: only 43% noticed the labels at all. Information that is not attended to has no effect, which is precisely the bounded-rationality point.\n• Distribution of effect: evidence generally shows labelling is attended to most by those who are already health-conscious and least by the groups where obesity prevalence is highest, so it can widen rather than narrow health inequalities.\n• It is very cheap per person reached, so even a small effect may pass a cost-benefit test — effectiveness and value for money are different questions.\n• Alternatives to weigh: the Soft Drinks Industry Levy shows that acting on PRODUCERS through reformulation incentives changes the product itself, so no consumer decision is required; advertising restrictions, promotion restrictions on high-fat, salt and sugar products, and portion-size regulation all act without relying on attention.\n• Judgement: a necessary and cheap component, but the stimulus supports the conclusion that information provision alone cannot materially reduce obesity, because the failure is behavioural rather than informational.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — figures used quantitatively, behavioural critique developed, evaluation weighed. L1 (1–4) — asserts that information helps or does not help without using the data.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which extending property rights can solve the problem of negative externalities.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
A river is used by an upstream factory for waste discharge and by a downstream fishery.
  Cost to the factory of installing filtration: £600 000 a year
  Loss of profit to the fishery from the pollution: £900 000 a year
  Number of separate businesses affected downstream: 1
A second case concerns global greenhouse gas emissions, where the affected parties number in the
billions and include people not yet born.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The Coase theorem: where property rights are clearly assigned and transaction costs are low, the parties will bargain to the efficient outcome regardless of WHO holds the right. In the river case the efficient outcome is filtration, because £600 000 of abatement avoids £900 000 of damage.\n• Work the two assignments through, which is what distinguishes a top answer: if the fishery owns the river, the factory must either install filtration (£600 000) or compensate £900 000, so it filters. If the FACTORY owns the right to discharge, the fishery will offer between £600 000 and £900 000 to persuade it to filter, and the factory accepts. The outcome is efficient either way; only the DISTRIBUTION of the gain changes.\n• The general lesson: many externalities exist because no one owns the resource. Creating ownership creates an incentive to conserve — fishing quotas, tradeable emissions allowances and land ownership all work this way.\nEvaluation:\n• Transaction costs are the decisive limitation, and the stimulus makes the contrast explicit: with one affected business, bargaining is feasible; with billions of affected parties, including future generations who cannot bargain at all, the negotiation is impossible and the Coase solution collapses.\n• Free-riding among many affected parties means each waits for others to pay, so the collective offer never materialises even when the aggregate willingness to pay exceeds the abatement cost.\n• Measurement: the £900 000 figure has to be known and agreed. In practice damage is uncertain, contested and often non-monetary (health, biodiversity), so litigation costs can exceed the externality.\n• Enforcement requires a functioning legal system and the ability to identify the polluter — impossible with diffuse sources such as road traffic or agricultural runoff.\n• Distribution is not neutral even if efficiency is: who gets the right determines who is £900 000 better off, which is a political question the theorem is silent on.\n• Judgement: property rights are a powerful solution for LOCAL externalities with few, identifiable parties, and essentially useless for global, diffuse, long-horizon externalities — where taxation, regulation or permit markets (which are themselves engineered property rights, created by a government precisely because the market could not create them) are required.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — both stimulus cases used and the transaction-cost contrast developed. L1 (1–4) — states the Coase theorem without applying it.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which political short-termism causes government failure.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
  Length of a UK parliamentary term (maximum): 5 years
  Typical payback period on a new reservoir or nuclear plant: 15-40 years
  Ofwat\'s PR24 final determinations, effective 1 April 2025, allowed average household water bills
    to rise by about £157 (around 36%) before inflation over 2025-2030 to fund investment.
  Fuel duty has been held at the same cash rate since 2011, with an additional temporary cut since 2022.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The mechanism: a government facing re-election within five years discounts costs and benefits falling beyond that horizon far more heavily than society does. Policies with immediate visible costs and diffuse long-run benefits — infrastructure, planning reform, carbon pricing — are systematically under-supplied, while policies with immediate visible benefits and deferred costs are over-supplied.\n• The stimulus gives both sides of this. Infrastructure with a 15-40 year payback sits far outside the electoral cycle, and the political pain of a 36% real bill rise falls on the incumbent while the benefit accrues to a successor. Fuel duty frozen in cash terms since 2011 is the textbook case of a deferred-cost policy: the cost is a growing hole in the public finances and weaker carbon pricing, neither of which is visible at the pump.\n• Consequence: resources are misallocated by the intervention itself, which is government failure — the outcome is worse than it need be even though a market failure genuinely existed.\nEvaluation:\n• Short-termism is one cause among several, and probably not the largest. Also creditable: information failure (the regulator knows less than the firm), administrative and enforcement costs, regulatory capture, unintended consequences and distorted incentives, and the sheer difficulty of valuing externalities.\n• Institutional design can mitigate it. Independent bodies with long mandates — Ofwat\'s five-year price reviews, the Climate Change Committee\'s statutory carbon budgets, the independence of the Bank of England — exist precisely to remove decisions from the electoral cycle, and the stimulus\'s PR24 determination is evidence that a 36% rise CAN be imposed when an independent regulator, not a minister, decides it.\n• The counter-argument: responsiveness to voters is not a failure, it is accountability. A government that ignores voters\' current preferences in favour of a technocratic long-run optimum has a legitimacy problem, and the preferences of the living are not obviously less valid than those of the unborn.\n• Markets are short-termist too — quarterly reporting, high private discount rates — so the comparison is between imperfect alternatives, not between a flawed state and a far-sighted market.\n• Judgement: short-termism is a real and systematic source of government failure, best evidenced where a policy\'s costs are immediate and visible and its benefits deferred; but it is mitigable by institutional design, which means it is a feature of particular arrangements rather than an inevitable property of government.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — both stimulus cases used, mechanism analysed, other causes weighed. L1 (1–4) — lists causes of government failure without using the data.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which nudges are a more effective policy tool than taxation.

Stimulus (written for this practice question, with published UK policies for comparison)
  Pension auto-enrolment (phased from 2012) changed the DEFAULT from opting in to opting out;
    workplace pension participation among eligible employees rose from around 55% to about 88%,
    with no change in the financial incentive to save.
  The single-use carrier bag charge in England (5p from 2015, raised to 10p and extended to all
    retailers in May 2021) cut bag use in the main supermarkets by over 95%.
  Administrative cost of a default change: close to zero. Administrative cost of a new duty: HMRC
    collection, enforcement against evasion, and border controls.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Why nudges work where taxes struggle: they act on the actual cause of the behaviour. Auto-enrolment changed nothing about the returns to saving and yet moved participation from about 55% to about 88%, which is direct evidence that the barrier was inertia and status quo bias, not price. A tax subsidy of equivalent power would have cost the exchequer a great deal.\n• Cost-effectiveness: near-zero administrative cost, no deadweight loss from distorting relative prices, no evasion or cross-border leakage, and no regressive burden.\n• Libertarian paternalism: choice is preserved — anyone can opt out — so the welfare cost to those who genuinely prefer the alternative is close to zero.\n• The bag charge is the interesting hybrid: 5p is far too small to matter as a price, so its effect came mainly from SALIENCE and from making the environmental cost visible at the till, not from the income effect.\nEvaluation:\n• Nudges have no revenue. A tax simultaneously changes behaviour AND raises money that can fund treatment, compensation or redistribution; a nudge raises nothing.\n• Scale and durability: nudge effects are often modest and can decay as people habituate. The bag charge and auto-enrolment are the celebrated successes; many trials produce very small effects, and there is a publication bias problem in the literature.\n• Where demand is genuinely price-driven, or where the externality is large and quantifiable, a tax is the theoretically correct instrument because it internalises the external cost at the margin; a nudge does not price the externality at all.\n• Ethical objections: nudges work by exploiting the same biases they identify, without the person\'s knowledge, which raises questions about manipulation and about who chooses the default.\n• Enforcement: a nudge cannot stop determined behaviour. For addictive goods and for firms\' production decisions, only price or regulation bites.\n• Judgement: nudges dominate where the failure is behavioural (inertia, inattention, default effects) and where no revenue is needed; taxes dominate where there is a measurable external cost to internalise and where revenue matters. They are complements rather than rivals, and the strongest answers say which failure they are addressing before choosing.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — both stimulus policies used and the behavioural-versus-price distinction developed. L1 (1–4) — describes nudges without engaging with the evidence.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which road pricing can reduce urban traffic congestion.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
A congestion charge applies to a city centre on weekdays.
  Charge: £15 per day
  Vehicles entering the zone before the charge: 195 000 a day
  Vehicles entering the zone after the charge: 156 000 a day
  Average traffic speed in the zone: 9.4 mph before ; 11.1 mph after
  Net revenue after operating costs: £142 million a year, hypothecated to public transport`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The externality: each additional vehicle imposes delay on every other road user, so marginal social cost exceeds marginal private cost and roads are over-used at a zero price. Road space at peak time is a common pool resource — rival but, without a charge, non-excludable.\n• Evidence from the stimulus: traffic fell by (195 000 - 156 000)/195 000 = 20%, and average speed rose 18% from 9.4 to 11.1 mph. A 20% fall in response to a £15 charge indicates fairly inelastic demand, but the congestion relationship is highly non-linear, so a modest quantity reduction produces a disproportionate speed gain — the analytical point worth the top band.\n• Revenue: £142 million a year hypothecated to public transport both funds a substitute and makes the charge politically saleable; it also means the policy raises revenue where a regulation such as a ban would not.\nEvaluation:\n• Equity: a flat £15 charge is regressive — identical in cash terms for a delivery driver and a company director, so it rations road space by income. Exemptions and discounts complicate this but also erode the effect.\n• Displacement: traffic may divert to the boundary roads just outside the zone, shifting congestion rather than reducing it, and journeys may be retimed to just outside charging hours.\n• Setting the charge: the optimal charge equals the marginal external cost, which varies by time, location and vehicle. A single flat daily charge is therefore a crude approximation; distance- and time-based charging would be more efficient but is costlier and raises privacy objections.\n• Substitute availability determines PED. Where public transport is poor, demand is very inelastic, the charge raises revenue and cuts congestion little, and it simply becomes a tax on necessary journeys — which is why the hypothecation in the stimulus matters.\n• Administrative and enforcement costs are substantial (the £142m is stated as NET, which is the right basis).\n• Judgement: effective at reducing congestion in the charged zone and theoretically well-targeted at the externality, but its equity properties are poor and its success depends entirely on whether an adequate substitute exists; the strongest conclusions link the charge to the public-transport investment it funds.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — percentage changes computed from the stimulus, non-linearity or elasticity point reached, evaluation weighed. L1 (1–4) — describes congestion charging without using the figures.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether minimum unit pricing is more effective than taxation in reducing the harm caused by alcohol.', marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• How MUP differs from duty: a minimum unit price sets a floor per unit of alcohol, so it bites ONLY on the cheapest, strongest products and leaves mid-market and premium prices untouched. Scotland\'s MUP rose from 50p to 65p per unit on 30 September 2024, making a 70cl bottle of 40% spirits cost at least £18.20.\n• Why that targeting matters: the heaviest drinkers, and drinkers in the most deprived groups, buy disproportionately from the cheapest end of the market, so a floor price is concentrated on exactly the consumption that generates the greatest external and internal harm. A uniform duty raises the price of a pub pint as much as of white cider.\n• A duty cannot achieve this: retailers can and do absorb duty on loss-leading alcohol to drive footfall, so duty increases do not necessarily reach the cheapest products at all. MUP makes below-cost selling illegal, which duty cannot.\n• Diagram: a price floor above equilibrium contracting quantity demanded towards the social optimum.\nEvaluation:\n• The decisive weakness of MUP: the extra revenue goes to RETAILERS and producers as higher margins, not to the exchequer. Duty raises revenue that can fund treatment services and NHS costs. On a pure welfare calculation this is a large transfer from consumers to firms.\n• Substitution and avoidance: cross-border purchasing (Scotland to England, or online), and switching to illicit or home-produced alcohol.\n• Regressivity: both instruments are regressive in incidence, but MUP is more sharply so because it targets the cheapest products, which are bought by the poorest drinkers. Defenders answer that the harm is also concentrated there, so the benefits are equally targeted.\n• Dependent drinkers have very inelastic demand and may cut spending on food and heating rather than on alcohol, so a price floor can worsen the welfare of the most vulnerable households it aims to help.\n• Neither instrument addresses availability, marketing or licensing, which are complementary levers.\n• Judgement: a supported conclusion is that MUP is better TARGETED at harmful consumption while duty is better at raising the revenue to treat harm, so they are complements; a defensible alternative is a duty system restructured by alcoholic strength, which captures the targeting of MUP while keeping the revenue.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) / L2 (5–8) / L1 (1–4). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether the government should subsidise merit goods rather than provide them directly.', marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The case for subsidy: it preserves consumer choice and competition between providers, so firms retain the incentive to minimise cost and to innovate. It is administratively lighter than running the service, and the rate can be tuned to the estimated marginal external benefit. It works through the price mechanism rather than replacing it.\n• The case for direct provision: it guarantees the QUANTITY and the universality of supply, which a subsidy cannot — a subsidy leaves the final quantity to the market and therefore to the elasticities. Where the external benefit is very large and universal coverage is the objective (vaccination, compulsory schooling, emergency care) provision delivers it with certainty. It also avoids the incidence problem entirely.\n• Incidence is the strongest analytical argument against subsidy: where supply is inelastic — childcare places, university places, social housing — a large share of the subsidy is captured by providers as higher fees or higher prices rather than reaching consumers. A subsidy into an inelastic market raises prices more than quantity.\nEvaluation:\n• Direct provision risks X-inefficiency: without competitive pressure or the threat of exit, a state provider faces weak cost discipline, and rationing is by queue rather than by price.\n• Information failure is the reason merit goods are under-consumed, and neither instrument addresses it directly — a subsidy lowers the price for people who already under-value the good, so take-up among exactly the target group may be weak. This argues for combining either instrument with information provision or with compulsion.\n• Targeting: a universal subsidy is frequently regressive in incidence because better-off households consume more; means-testing improves targeting but creates administrative cost and reduces take-up through stigma and complexity.\n• Fiscal cost and opportunity cost apply to both; provision usually costs more but buys certainty.\n• Government failure: subsidies attract lobbying and are politically very hard to withdraw; provision creates producer interests that resist reform.\n• Judgement: the answer depends on the elasticity of supply and on whether universality is the objective. Where supply is elastic and choice is valuable, subsidise; where supply is inelastic, or where the external benefit requires near-universal coverage, provide. The strongest answers reach this conditional conclusion rather than picking a side.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) / L2 (5–8) / L1 (1–4). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether regulation is more effective than market-based instruments in reducing pollution.', marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Regulation (command and control): emission limits, technology standards, bans, licensing. Advantages — certainty of outcome, simplicity, immediate effect, and it works where no market can be created or where the harm is so severe that any level is unacceptable (leaded petrol, asbestos, CFCs under the Montreal Protocol). It is also easy for the public to understand and to enforce through inspection and fines.\n• Market-based instruments: Pigouvian taxes and tradeable permits. Advantages — they equalise marginal abatement cost across polluters, so the target is met at the lowest total cost; they give a CONTINUOUS incentive to abate beyond any threshold, whereas a standard gives none once the limit is met; and they raise revenue. The UK ETS is the leading example, extended to maritime from 2026 with aviation free allocation ending 1 January 2026.\n• Information requirements differ sharply: regulation requires the regulator to know each firm\'s abatement technology and costs; a permit market only requires it to know the total quantity target and lets firms reveal their own costs through trading. This is the strongest efficiency argument for market instruments.\nEvaluation:\n• Regulation\'s blunt uniformity is its main cost: the same standard applied to a modern plant and an old one imposes wildly different marginal costs, so the target is met at more than the minimum cost.\n• Market instruments fail where the number of sources is very large and diffuse (household heating, agricultural runoff) or where monitoring emissions is impossible, so regulation is the only feasible instrument there.\n• Equity and hotspots: a permit market permits concentration of emissions wherever abatement is expensive, which can create local pollution hotspots that a uniform standard would prevent. This matters for local pollutants and not for globally mixed ones such as CO2.\n• Political economy: caps are lobbied loose and free allocation dilutes them; regulations are lobbied for long transition periods. Both are vulnerable to regulatory capture.\n• Judgement: the defensible conclusion is that the choice depends on the pollutant. For a uniformly mixed pollutant from a small number of measurable sources, market instruments are clearly superior on cost grounds; for a locally concentrated or unmeasurable pollutant, or where the harm justifies zero tolerance, regulation is superior. In practice every serious pollution policy uses both.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) / L2 (5–8) / L1 (1–4). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether behavioural insights improve the design of policies to correct market failure.', marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• What behavioural economics adds: the traditional model attributes under-consumption of merit goods and over-consumption of demerit goods to externalities and information failure, and prescribes taxes, subsidies and information. Behavioural analysis identifies a distinct cause — bounded rationality, present bias, loss aversion, status quo bias, anchoring and social norms — and therefore a distinct set of instruments.\n• Evidence of improvement: pension auto-enrolment raised workplace pension participation among eligible employees from roughly 55% to about 88% by changing the DEFAULT, with no change to the financial incentive at all — a result no price-based instrument achieved at anything like the cost. The 5p carrier-bag charge in England from 2015 (raised to 10p and extended to all retailers in May 2021) cut usage by over 95% with a charge far too small to matter as a price, which shows salience rather than the income effect doing the work.\n• Better instrument selection: if a policy fails because people do not attend to it, adding money to it is wasteful. Behavioural diagnosis therefore improves the match between failure and remedy.\nEvaluation:\n• Effect sizes are frequently small, context-specific and prone to decay as people habituate. The celebrated successes are the exception, and the literature has a replication and publication-bias problem, so extrapolating from a successful trial is risky.\n• Nudges raise no revenue and do not price an externality, so they cannot replace corrective taxation where there is a genuine external cost to internalise.\n• They can be defeated by counter-nudging from firms, which have far greater resources and use the same techniques to push in the opposite direction (default subscriptions, drip pricing, scarcity cues).\n• Ethical objections: influencing choices by exploiting biases, without the person\'s awareness, raises questions of manipulation and paternalism — and someone must choose the default, which is an exercise of power.\n• Government failure applies here too: a behavioural insights unit can be captured, and the appeal of a cheap nudge can crowd out a more effective but politically costly instrument such as regulation or tax.\n• Judgement: behavioural insights improve policy DESIGN substantially — they widen the toolkit and cut the cost of achieving behaviour change — but they supplement rather than replace the standard instruments, and their greatest value is diagnostic: identifying whether a given failure is informational, financial or behavioural before choosing the remedy.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) / L2 (5–8) / L1 (1–4). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether the remedies imposed by the Competition and Markets Authority in the veterinary services market will correct the information failure in that market.', marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The market failure: veterinary services are a classic credence good. The person who diagnoses the problem also sells the treatment, so the consumer cannot verify either the necessity or the quality of what is recommended — an acute asymmetric-information problem compounded by the emotional urgency of a sick pet, which makes demand highly inelastic at the point of sale. Consolidation of independent practices into large corporate groups that retained their original local branding meant consumers could not tell that apparently rival practices shared an owner.\n• The CMA concluded its market investigation into veterinary services for household pets with a final report on 24 March 2026, imposing 14 remedies. These include compulsory standard price lists for defined services and for the most commonly sold parasiticides, a cap of £21 on prescription fees, a price comparison facility, compulsory disclosure of group ownership on practice branding, clearer presentation of treatment options, and a levy on veterinary businesses to fund the consumer-facing measures. The CMA was to make the Order giving legal effect to the remedies by 23 September 2026, with most remedies taking effect in the following three to twelve months and smaller practices given three months longer.\n• Why they should help: mandatory price lists and ownership disclosure attack the information asymmetry at source, restoring the ability to compare; the prescription fee cap directly limits one identified point of rent extraction; the requirement to set out treatment options counteracts the conflict of interest in the diagnosis.\nEvaluation:\n• Information remedies rely on consumers attending to and acting on the information. The behavioural literature and the emotional context of pet illness both suggest that a distressed owner in a consulting room will not shop around, however good the price list is — this is the strongest doubt available.\n• Price transparency in a concentrated market can facilitate TACIT COLLUSION: if every practice publishes its prices, rivals can monitor each other and prices may converge upward rather than compete downward. This is a well-documented perverse effect of transparency remedies in concentrated markets.\n• A price cap addresses a symptom, not the structure: the £21 prescription cap limits one fee, and firms may recover the margin through consultation fees or medicine prices unless those are also constrained.\n• The remedies are behavioural, not structural: no divestment was ordered, so the concentration that created the problem remains. Enforcement depends on the Order and on continuing monitoring, and the levy funding it is itself a cost that will be passed to consumers.\n• Timing: with most remedies taking effect three to twelve months after the September 2026 Order, no evidence of their effect is yet available, so any confident claim about success is unsupportable.\n• Judgement: the remedies are well matched to the DIAGNOSED failure and should improve comparability for planned, non-urgent treatment; they are unlikely to change behaviour in urgent consultations, where the information asymmetry is most exploitable, and they leave the market structure untouched. A supported conclusion should distinguish those two segments.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) / L2 (5–8) / L1 (1–4). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram and the information below, the extent to which an indirect tax can fully internalise a negative externality.

Extract 1 (written for this practice question)
An independent study of a heavy goods vehicle fleet estimates the external costs per 1 000 vehicle-km:
  Air pollution (health): £38     Noise: £9     Road damage: £26     Accidents: £17
  Congestion imposed on other road users: £61
  Total estimated external cost: £151 per 1 000 vehicle-km
The estimates for congestion vary between studies by a factor of three, and the health estimates
depend on the assumed monetary value of a life-year.`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: MPC and MSC with the vertical gap equal to the marginal external cost, MSB downward sloping, free-market output Q1 and social optimum Q*, the welfare loss triangle shaded; then MPC shifted up by the tax so that MPC + tax coincides with MSC and output falls to Q*. The tax per unit should be shown as equal to the marginal external cost AT Q*, not at Q1 — a distinction that separates Level 3 from Level 2.\n• The theoretical case: a tax equal to the marginal external cost converts the external cost into a private cost, so the profit-maximising firm chooses the socially optimal output. Welfare rises by the shaded triangle, the polluter pays, and revenue is generated. Unlike a quantity regulation, the tax leaves the firm free to choose how to abate, so abatement happens wherever it is cheapest.\n• Applied to the extract: a duty of roughly 15.1p per vehicle-km would, on these estimates, internalise the full £151 per 1 000 vehicle-km.\nEvaluation:\n• Measurement is the central problem, and the extract supplies the evidence: congestion estimates vary by a factor of three, and the health figure depends on an assumed value of a life-year, which is a contested ethical judgement rather than a measurement. A tax set on a number that could plausibly be a third or triple its stated value cannot be described as fully internalising anything.\n• Heterogeneity: the marginal external cost varies enormously by time and place — congestion of £61 per 1 000 vehicle-km is an average across a network on which the true peak-urban figure may be ten times the rural off-peak figure. A uniform per-km duty therefore over-taxes rural off-peak journeys and under-taxes urban peak ones, so it cannot achieve the optimum even if the average is right. Dynamic road pricing is the theoretically correct instrument.\n• The optimum is not zero externality: the tax should equate MSB and MSC, leaving some pollution, because abating the last unit costs more than the damage it causes. Answers that treat full internalisation as elimination misunderstand the model.\n• Elasticity: freight demand is derived and inelastic in the short run, so a tax at the optimal rate may raise revenue and change quantity very little until operators can re-optimise routes and fleets, which takes years.\n• Incidence and equity: with inelastic demand the tax is passed on into the price of goods, which is regressive, and it falls on regions dependent on road freight.\n• Government failure and political constraint: fuel duty has been held at the same cash rate since 2011 with an additional temporary cut since 2022, which is direct evidence that the theoretically optimal rate is not politically sustainable.\n• Alternatives: tradeable permits fix the quantity and so avoid the valuation problem for the quantity target; regulation of emission standards addresses the pollution component directly; and none of these addresses congestion, which pricing by time and place handles best.\n• Judgement: an indirect tax can internalise an AVERAGE estimate of an external cost and move output substantially towards the optimum, but full internalisation requires knowing the marginal external cost precisely and varying the tax with it. The extract shows neither condition holds, so the honest conclusion is partial internalisation — which is still a large welfare improvement over no tax at all.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — accurate diagram driving the argument, extract data used quantitatively, measurement and heterogeneity problems both reached, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, argument one-sided or data under-used. L1 (1–5) — no or incorrect diagram, descriptive. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram and the information below, the extent to which subsidising a merit good raises economic welfare.

Extract 1 (written for this practice question)
A government subsidises childcare places for working parents.
  Subsidy: £6.50 per child per hour
  Places available before the subsidy: 480 000    Places available after: 516 000
  Average price paid by parents per hour: £8.20 before ; £4.90 after
  Average price received by providers per hour: £8.20 before ; £11.40 after
  Annual cost to the exchequer: £3.1 billion
Providers reported that recruitment of qualified staff was the main constraint on opening new places.`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: demand and supply with a steep (inelastic) supply curve; supply shifted vertically down by £6.50; the price paid by parents falling to £4.90, the price received by providers rising to £11.40, quantity rising from 480 000 to 516 000; government cost shown as £6.50 x 516 000 places. Alternatively, or additionally, the MPB/MSB merit-good diagram showing the welfare gain triangle recovered as output moves towards Q*.\n• The welfare case: childcare generates external benefits — higher female labour force participation, higher tax revenue, better early-years outcomes for children — so MSB exceeds MPB and the free market under-provides. Parents also under-value the long-run developmental benefit, the classic merit-good information failure. Moving from 480 000 to 516 000 places therefore captures part of the welfare gain triangle.\n• Incidence, quantified from the extract, is the analytical heart of a top answer: of the £6.50 subsidy, parents gain £8.20 - £4.90 = £3.30 and providers gain £11.40 - £8.20 = £3.20. So providers capture roughly 49% of the subsidy as higher unit revenue. Quantity rose only 7.5% ((516 000 - 480 000)/480 000) on a £3.1 billion outlay — about £86 000 per additional place per year — which is the cost per ADDITIONAL place, vastly above the headline subsidy rate.\nEvaluation:\n• The extract explains why: supply is inelastic because qualified staff cannot be recruited quickly. With PES low, a demand-side subsidy is substantially capitalised into provider prices rather than translating into places. The policy implication is that a supply-side intervention — training bursaries, relaxing ratio requirements, raising staff pay — would deliver more places per pound.\n• Inframarginal cost: most of the £3.1 billion pays for the 480 000 places that existed anyway. That is a transfer to existing users rather than a welfare gain, though it is not a deadweight LOSS and it does have distributional value.\n• Distribution: the subsidy is conditional on working, so it excludes the children of non-working parents, who may have the greatest developmental need. It is therefore poorly targeted on the merit-good rationale even if well targeted on the labour-supply rationale — the two objectives pull in different directions.\n• Opportunity cost: £3.1 billion has alternative uses with their own welfare returns; and the subsidy must be financed by taxation that carries its own deadweight loss.\n• Government failure: a subsidy of this size, once introduced, is politically almost impossible to withdraw, and it creates a provider interest group with strong incentives to lobby for its extension.\n• Valuation: the external benefit has not been measured, so there is no way to know whether £6.50 is above or below the marginal external benefit at the optimum.\n• Judgement: the subsidy raises welfare — output moves towards the social optimum and there are real labour-supply gains — but far less efficiently than the headline suggests, because inelastic supply hands roughly half of it to providers. A supported conclusion is that the subsidy should be paired with supply-side measures that make PES higher, without which the same money buys very few additional places.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — accurate diagram, incidence quantified from the extract, inelastic-supply critique developed, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, data described rather than used. L1 (1–5) — no or incorrect diagram. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram and the information below, the extent to which government intervention has been successful in the market for carbon emissions.

Extract 1 (written for this practice question, using published UK developments)
The UK Emissions Trading Scheme caps emissions from power, industry and aviation and issues
tradeable allowances. Free allocation to aviation ended on 1 January 2026 and the scheme extends
to shipping from 2026. On 19 May 2025 the UK and the EU announced their intention to link the two
schemes. A share of allowances is still given free to industries judged at risk of relocating.
Alongside the cap, the UK uses regulation (the zero-emission vehicle mandate on car makers),
subsidy (heat pump and insulation grants) and taxation (fuel duty, frozen in cash terms since 2011).`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: the permit market — vertical supply at the cap, downward-sloping demand derived from marginal abatement costs, equilibrium permit price; the cap tightened (supply line shifted left) raising the price. An MPC/MSC diagram showing the external cost of emissions and the welfare loss at the free-market output is an acceptable alternative or addition. Curves and axes must be labelled and the diagram must be USED, not decorative.\n• Successes to argue: the cap fixes the quantity, so the environmental outcome is certain within the covered sectors — a decisive advantage over a tax when the damage function is steep. Trading equalises marginal abatement cost across firms, so the target is met at minimum total cost. A positive permit price gives a continuing dynamic incentive to invest in abatement. Coverage has widened (shipping from 2026; aviation free allocation ending 1 January 2026), and the announced UK-EU link of 19 May 2025 removes the price divergence between the two schemes and reduces leakage between them.\n• The policy mix is coherent in principle: the cap prices covered emissions, the ZEV mandate reaches transport (which the cap does not), and subsidies address the split-incentive and up-front-cost barriers in housing that a price alone cannot overcome.\nEvaluation:\n• Cap-setting is the weak link. The regulator must know aggregate abatement costs to set a cap that produces a meaningful price; both the EU and UK schemes have at times issued too many allowances, collapsing the price and destroying the abatement incentive. This is an information problem and therefore a government-failure risk, not a market failure.\n• Free allocation, which the extract confirms continues, dilutes the incentive and transfers rent to incumbents — though the carbon-leakage rationale for it is genuine, and the alternative (a border carbon adjustment) is administratively heavy and contested internationally.\n• Carbon leakage means domestic emission reductions may be offset by increases abroad, so a national scheme can succeed on its own metric while achieving nothing globally. Linking with the EU helps only for that pair of jurisdictions.\n• Coverage gaps are large: domestic heating, agriculture and most surface transport are outside the cap, so most UK emissions do not face the permit price at all. That is why regulation and subsidy are needed, and it means the cap cannot be judged as if it were the whole policy.\n• Inconsistency between instruments is a real failure: fuel duty frozen in cash terms since 2011 means the real carbon price on road fuel has FALLEN for fifteen years while the ETS price was meant to rise. Different effective carbon prices in different sectors mean abatement is not happening where it is cheapest — precisely the inefficiency the permit market was designed to eliminate.\n• Equity and political economy: carbon pricing is regressive in incidence, and the political cost of visible price rises is why the fuel-duty freeze has persisted — evidence that the binding constraint is political, not economic.\n• Judgement: intervention has been genuinely successful WITHIN the capped sectors, where emissions have fallen substantially and at low cost; it has been much less successful across the economy as a whole, because coverage is partial and effective carbon prices are wildly inconsistent between sectors. A supported conclusion should assess the scheme against what it was designed to do and separately against the national target.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — accurate permit-market diagram driving the argument, extract developments used, coverage and consistency points reached, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, argument one-sided. L1 (1–5) — no or incorrect diagram, descriptive. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram and the information below, the extent to which asymmetric information is the most significant cause of market failure in health care.

Extract 1 (written for this practice question)
A private health insurance market shows the following pattern:
  Premium set on average risk: £1 450 a year
  Proportion of low-risk individuals who purchase at that premium: 31%
  Proportion of high-risk individuals who purchase at that premium: 89%
  Revised premium after one year, based on actual claims: £2 180
  Proportion of the population with no cover at the revised premium: 44%
Vaccination against an infectious disease is estimated to generate benefits to non-recipients
worth roughly 1.6 times the benefit to the recipient.`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: EITHER the positive-externality diagram for vaccination (MPB below MSB, free-market Q1 below social optimum Q*, welfare gain triangle shaded) OR a demand and supply diagram of the insurance market showing the adverse-selection spiral as demand from low-risk buyers contracting and price rising. The externality diagram is the cleaner choice because the extract quantifies the external benefit at 1.6 times the private benefit.\n• The asymmetric-information case, using the extract: buyers know their own risk and insurers do not, so a premium based on average risk (£1 450) is unattractive to low-risk individuals (only 31% buy) and attractive to high-risk ones (89% buy). The pool worsens, the premium rises to £2 180, and 44% end up with no cover at all. That is a self-reinforcing adverse-selection spiral producing a MISSING MARKET — the most serious form of market failure, because the market does not merely misprice, it disappears for a large group.\n• Moral hazard compounds it after purchase: insured patients demand more treatment than they would pay for directly, and providers paid per procedure have an incentive to supply it — a credence-good problem in which the seller diagnoses and prescribes.\nEvaluation — competing causes to weigh:\n• Externalities may be larger. The extract values the external benefit of vaccination at 1.6 times the private benefit, which means the free market would provide well under half the socially optimal quantity of a good that protects the whole population. Infectious disease control is a textbook positive externality and is arguably a bigger welfare loss than the insurance failure.\n• Merit-good and behavioural failure: people under-value preventive care because the benefit is deferred and uncertain (present bias), so consumption is too low even with perfect information and no externality. This is distinct from asymmetric information and is not cured by disclosure.\n• Monopoly power: pharmaceutical patents create deliberate, temporary monopolies with prices far above marginal cost; hospital markets are highly concentrated geographically.\n• Equity: the 44% without cover is a distributional failure as much as an efficiency one, and the case for the NHS rests at least as much on the equity argument as on any efficiency argument.\n• The causes interact rather than compete: asymmetric information is what makes the insurance market fail, but the reason society insists on universal coverage is the combination of externalities, merit-good reasoning and equity. Ranking them in the abstract is less useful than identifying which failure each policy addresses.\n• Policy test: compulsory universal insurance or tax-funded provision solves the adverse-selection problem completely, by removing the ability to self-select. That it also happens to address the externality and equity problems is why the single-payer model is so widely adopted — evidence that asymmetric information alone would not justify the full structure of the NHS.\n• Judgement: asymmetric information is the most significant cause of failure in the market for health INSURANCE, and the extract demonstrates it cleanly; it is not the most significant cause across health care as a whole, where externalities in infectious disease and equity considerations do at least as much work. A supported conclusion distinguishes the two markets.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — accurate diagram used, extract data deployed quantitatively on both sides, competing causes weighed, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, one cause developed. L1 (1–5) — no or incorrect diagram, list of causes. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether government intervention to correct market failure always improves economic welfare.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The case that it does: where a market failure is identified, well-designed intervention moves output towards the point where MSB = MSC and recovers the deadweight loss. Taxes internalise external costs; subsidies internalise external benefits; permits fix the quantity of emissions at the optimum; state provision supplies public goods the market cannot supply at all; regulation sets standards where prices cannot reach; competition policy restores output suppressed by market power. Each has a clear theoretical welfare gain attached.\n• UK evidence of intervention working: the Soft Drinks Industry Levy (20.8p and 27.8p per litre at the two thresholds from 1 April 2026) drove reformulation of soft drinks; the carrier-bag charge (5p from 2015, 10p and extended to all retailers in May 2021) cut bag use by over 95%; the UK ETS put a price on emissions from power and industry; the CMA\'s veterinary market investigation, concluded 24 March 2026, imposed price transparency and a £21 prescription fee cap on a market with a severe credence-good problem.\n• The case that it does not — government failure, which is the core of this question:\n  - INFORMATION: the regulator cannot measure the marginal external cost, so the tax or subsidy is set wrong. Valuing a life-year or a tonne of congestion involves contested ethical judgements, not measurements.\n  - UNINTENDED CONSEQUENCES: price ceilings create shortages and black markets; rent controls cut maintenance and long-run supply; subsidies into inelastic markets are capitalised into prices rather than raising quantity; biofuel mandates raised food prices.\n  - ADMINISTRATIVE AND ENFORCEMENT COST: the resources spent administering a policy are a real opportunity cost that must be netted off the welfare gain.\n  - REGULATORY CAPTURE: the regulated industry has concentrated incentives and superior information; consumers have diffuse ones. Over time the regulator may serve the industry.\n  - POLITICAL SHORT-TERMISM: fuel duty frozen in cash terms since 2011 despite an explicit carbon-pricing policy shows the electoral cycle overriding the economics.\n  - DISTORTED INCENTIVES AND MORAL HAZARD: guarantees and bail-outs encourage the risk-taking they insure against.\nEvaluation:\n• The honest comparison is imperfect market against imperfect government, not real market against ideal government. Nirvana fallacy in either direction is the commonest weakness in answers here.\n• It depends on the TYPE of failure. For public goods, intervention is close to unambiguously welfare-improving, because the market supplies nothing. For externalities with measurable damage and few sources, pricing works well. For merit goods, the case rests on a paternalistic judgement that is contestable in principle. For information failure, remedies rely on consumers acting on information, which behavioural evidence suggests they often do not.\n• It depends on the INSTRUMENT and its design. The SDIL worked because it was structured as a producer-facing threshold; a consumer-facing tax on inelastic demand would have raised revenue and changed little. Design does more work than the decision to intervene.\n• It depends on the TIME HORIZON: rent control helps incumbent tenants immediately and reduces supply over decades; a subsidy that builds an industry to viable scale may be justified dynamically even if statically wasteful.\n• Distribution versus efficiency: an intervention can lower total welfare measured as surplus while improving it on a criterion that weights the poor more heavily. \'Welfare\' is not a single unambiguous quantity.\n• Judgement: intervention does NOT always improve welfare, and the word \'always\' is what the question turns on — one clear counterexample settles the literal claim. The substantive conclusion worth reaching is that intervention improves welfare when the failure is clearly identified, the instrument is matched to its cause, the external cost or benefit can be approximated, and the administrative and political costs are smaller than the deadweight loss being recovered; and that it systematically fails when these conditions do not hold. The strongest answers set out that test explicitly and apply it to at least two contrasting cases.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) — sustained, balanced, well-evidenced, analysis and evaluation integrated, conclusion follows from the argument. L4 (16–20) — well developed and mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) — some analysis and evaluation, uneven balance. L2 (6–10) — largely descriptive. L1 (1–5) — fragmentary. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram, whether taxation is the most effective way of reducing the consumption of demerit goods.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: the demerit-good diagram (MPB above MSB, free-market Q1 above social optimum Q*, welfare loss triangle) with the tax shifting supply up so output falls to Q*; or the market diagram showing the incidence split with inelastic demand. Strong answers use the incidence diagram to make the elasticity argument visible, showing that with steep demand the tax raises price a lot and cuts quantity little.\n• The case for taxation: it internalises the external cost so that private decisions produce the social optimum; it preserves choice rather than banning; it raises revenue that can fund treatment and information; it acts at the margin so it discourages the heaviest use most; and it is administratively established for tobacco, alcohol and fuel.\nEvaluation:\n• ELASTICITY is the decisive analytical point. Demerit goods are typically addictive, so short-run demand is very inelastic: the tax raises revenue and changes quantity little. Long-run demand is far more elastic, so the tax works over years rather than months, and the judgement depends on the time horizon chosen.\n• REGRESSIVITY: consumption of demerit goods is concentrated among lower-income households, so a flat duty takes a much larger share of their income. Defenders answer that the health benefit is also concentrated there.\n• SETTING THE RATE: it requires valuing the external cost, which is contested; and for the internal costs (harm to the consumer themselves) taxation is paternalistic, which some reject in principle.\n• EVASION AND SUBSTITUTION: cross-border shopping, illicit trade in tobacco, home production, and switching to an untaxed substitute that may be no better. These put a practical ceiling on the rate regardless of theory.\n• Alternatives to weigh seriously:\n  - MINIMUM UNIT PRICING targets the cheapest, strongest products bought by the heaviest drinkers, which a uniform duty does not; Scotland raised its MUP from 50p to 65p per unit on 30 September 2024. Its weakness is that the margin goes to retailers, not the exchequer.\n  - PRODUCER-SIDE THRESHOLD TAXES: the Soft Drinks Industry Levy (20.8p per litre at 5g to under 8g per 100ml and 27.8p at 8g or more, in force from 1 April 2026, with the threshold falling to 4.5g from 1 January 2028) worked mainly through REFORMULATION rather than consumer response, which is the clearest evidence in UK policy that a tax can bypass the elasticity problem if it is designed to act on producers.\n  - REGULATION AND BANS: age restrictions, the smoking ban in enclosed public places, advertising and promotion restrictions. Certain in effect, no revenue, and risk of black markets at the extreme.\n  - INFORMATION AND NUDGES: cheap, choice-preserving, but weak where the failure is addiction rather than ignorance — and evidence on calorie labelling suggests attention is the binding constraint.\n  - DIRECT PROVISION OF SUBSTITUTES AND TREATMENT: smoking cessation services, addiction treatment, which act on the demand curve itself rather than on price.\n• The strongest structural insight: the right instrument depends on WHY consumption is too high. If the cause is an external cost, tax it. If the cause is addiction and inelastic demand, price alone is weak and treatment or regulation does more. If the cause is producer product design, act on producers. If the cause is inattention, nudge.\n• Judgement: taxation is the most VERSATILE instrument and usually a necessary component, but it is rarely the most effective on its own, precisely because the goods concerned have inelastic demand. A supported conclusion is that a combination — duty to price the externality, minimum unit pricing or threshold design to target the harmful margin, regulation to remove the worst exposure, and treatment to shift demand — dominates any single instrument; and the answer should say which good it is talking about, since tobacco, alcohol and sugar behave very differently.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) / L4 (16–20) / L3 (11–15) / L2 (6–10) / L1 (1–5); an accurate, integrated diagram is required for Level 4 and above. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether the government should intervene in the market for higher education.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The market-failure case for intervention:\n  - POSITIVE EXTERNALITIES: a more educated workforce raises productivity, innovation, tax revenue and civic participation, and lowers crime and welfare dependency. MSB exceeds MPB, so the free market under-provides. Diagram: MPB below MSB with the welfare gain triangle.\n  - IMPERFECT INFORMATION: an 18-year-old choosing a course is choosing a good whose returns are uncertain, deferred by decades and hard to verify — a credence good with a very long feedback loop. Graduate earnings data by course exists but is historical and does not predict the applicant\'s own return.\n  - CAPITAL MARKET FAILURE: no commercial lender will advance £50 000 against human capital, which cannot be used as collateral. Without intervention only those with wealthy families could attend, so the market allocates by parental income rather than by ability — an efficiency loss as well as an equity one.\n  - EQUITY AND SOCIAL MOBILITY: access correlated with family income perpetuates inequality across generations.\n• Forms intervention takes in the UK: tuition fee caps; income-contingent student loans written off after a set period (which is the capital-market remedy, and the write-off is an implicit subsidy); direct teaching grants for high-cost subjects such as medicine and engineering; research funding; and regulation of quality and access through the Office for Students.\nEvaluation — the case against, and the limits:\n• PRIVATE RETURNS ARE LARGE: graduates earn substantially more over a lifetime, so most of the benefit is private and appropriable. If the private return is large, the individual will invest anyway, and a full subsidy transfers money to people who will become high earners — which is regressive, since it is funded by taxpayers who did not attend.\n• The externality is hard to size and varies enormously by subject: the external benefit from training doctors and engineers is plausibly large; from courses with weak labour-market returns it may be near zero. A uniform subsidy across all courses is therefore misallocated even if the average is justified.\n• SIGNALLING VERSUS HUMAN CAPITAL: if a degree mainly SIGNALS pre-existing ability rather than creating productivity, then subsidising expansion raises private returns to individuals without raising national output — an expensive credential arms race. This is the single most damaging argument against subsidy and the best answers engage it directly.\n• GOVERNMENT FAILURE: fee caps below cost create rationing and cross-subsidy from international students; targets for participation can drive expansion into low-return provision; the Office for Students faces the standard information asymmetry against the institutions it regulates; and the loan system\'s write-off means the true subsidy is opaque and its cost falls on a future government, which is political short-termism.\n• INCIDENCE: a fee cap in a market where supply is constrained by capacity does not necessarily lower the resource cost, it shifts who pays; and grants can be capitalised into institutional spending rather than expanding places.\n• The counterfactual: some intervention is unavoidable, because the capital-market failure is genuine and severe. The realistic question is not whether to intervene but HOW — the income-contingent loan is itself an elegant intervention, insuring the student against a bad outcome while preserving a price signal.\n• Judgement: the capital-market failure and the equity argument justify intervention unambiguously, and an income-contingent loan system addresses both at modest net cost. The externality argument justifies additional subsidy, but only where the external benefit is demonstrably large — which argues for differentiated support by subject rather than a uniform one. The strongest conclusions separate the three rationales (capital market, externality, equity) and note that they call for different instruments, so \'should the government intervene?\' is the wrong question and \'which failure is being addressed?\' is the right one.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) / L4 (16–20) / L3 (11–15) / L2 (6–10) / L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether market-based policies are preferable to regulation in tackling climate change.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The externality: greenhouse gas emissions are the largest negative externality in economics — global, long-lived, and imposed on people who cannot bargain, including the unborn. MSC exceeds MPC by a large and uncertain amount, so the unregulated market over-produces emissions massively.\n• The market-based case:\n  - COST EFFICIENCY: a uniform carbon price (tax or permit) equalises marginal abatement cost across every source, so any given reduction is achieved at the lowest possible total cost. A regulator setting technology standards would need to know every firm\'s abatement cost curve; a price lets firms reveal it themselves. This informational advantage is the strongest single argument.\n  - CONTINUOUS INCENTIVE: a standard gives no reason to abate beyond the limit; a price rewards every tonne avoided, so it drives dynamic efficiency and R&D indefinitely.\n  - REVENUE: taxes and auctioned permits raise money that can cut distortionary taxes or compensate losers.\n  - CERTAINTY OF QUANTITY: a cap fixes emissions, which matters when the target is a carbon budget. The UK ETS ended free allocation to aviation on 1 January 2026, extends to shipping from 2026, and the UK and EU announced on 19 May 2025 their intention to link the two schemes.\n• The case for regulation:\n  - WHERE PRICES CANNOT REACH: millions of diffuse sources (home heating, agriculture, vehicle use) cannot be metered individually, so a standard on the PRODUCT is the only feasible instrument — hence the zero-emission vehicle mandate on manufacturers, building regulations, and efficiency standards for appliances.\n  - SPLIT INCENTIVES AND BEHAVIOURAL BARRIERS: a landlord choosing the boiler does not pay the fuel bill, so the tenant\'s carbon price never reaches the decision-maker. Regulation of minimum energy efficiency standards fixes this; a price does not.\n  - CERTAINTY AND SPEED: bans deliver a guaranteed outcome by a known date, which matters when the cost of failure is catastrophic and irreversible. The Montreal Protocol\'s ban on CFCs is the outstanding success in environmental policy and was achieved by regulation, not pricing.\n  - POLITICAL FEASIBILITY: visible price rises are politically fatal, which is why fuel duty has been frozen in cash terms since 2011 with an additional temporary cut since 2022. Regulation hides its cost inside the product price and so survives politically — an uncomfortable but decisive practical argument.\nEvaluation:\n• CARBON LEAKAGE undermines market instruments applied nationally: production and emissions relocate to jurisdictions without a price, so domestic success can coexist with global failure. Free allocation to trade-exposed industry mitigates it but dilutes the incentive; border carbon adjustment is the logical complement and is administratively heavy and diplomatically contested. Regulation on products sold domestically is less exposed to leakage, because it applies to imports too.\n• SETTING THE CAP OR THE TAX requires knowing the social cost of carbon, which spans an order of magnitude across credible estimates and depends on the discount rate applied to harms centuries away — an ethical choice, not a measurement. Both instruments share a version of this problem, but the cap at least fixes the quantity.\n• DISTRIBUTION: carbon pricing is regressive in incidence because energy is a larger share of poor households\' spending. Revenue recycling can offset this entirely, which is a strong argument for a tax over a regulation, since regulation raises no revenue with which to compensate.\n• GOVERNMENT FAILURE in both: caps have been set too loose and permit prices have collapsed; regulations have been captured into long transition periods and loopholes.\n• INCONSISTENCY is the real-world failure: effective carbon prices differ enormously between sectors in the UK — a tonne of carbon from electricity faces the ETS price while a tonne from road fuel faces a duty frozen since 2011 and a tonne from home heating faces almost nothing. That means abatement is NOT happening where it is cheapest, which is exactly the efficiency the market approach promises.\n• Judgement: market-based instruments are theoretically superior wherever emissions are measurable and sources are concentrated, and they should carry the main burden in power and heavy industry. They cannot do the whole job, because large parts of the problem involve diffuse sources, split incentives and political constraints on visible pricing. The defensible conclusion is that the two are complements — a price as the backbone, regulation where pricing cannot reach, and subsidy where up-front cost is the barrier — and that the real policy failure is not the choice of instrument but the inconsistency of the effective carbon price across sectors.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) / L4 (16–20) / L3 (11–15) / L2 (6–10) / L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether government failure is a more serious problem than the market failure it is intended to correct.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Define both precisely: market failure is a misallocation of resources by the price mechanism, measured by the deadweight welfare loss relative to the social optimum. Government failure is intervention that leaves the allocation WORSE than it would have been without it — not merely intervention that is imperfect.\n• The case that government failure is more serious:\n  - INFORMATION: the regulator needs to know the marginal external cost, each firm\'s abatement cost, and the counterfactual. It knows none of these. Hayek\'s argument is that this information is dispersed and cannot be centralised, so intervention is systematically mis-calibrated.\n  - UNINTENDED CONSEQUENCES, with evidence: price ceilings create shortages, queues and black markets; rent controls reduce maintenance and long-run supply; demand-side subsidies into inelastic markets are capitalised into prices, so a childcare or housing subsidy raises provider prices rather than quantity; biofuel mandates raised food prices.\n  - REGULATORY CAPTURE: the industry has concentrated incentives and superior information, consumers diffuse ones, so over time the regulator serves the regulated.\n  - POLITICAL SHORT-TERMISM: fuel duty frozen in cash terms since 2011 with an additional temporary cut since 2022, alongside a stated carbon-pricing policy, shows the electoral cycle overriding the economics; infrastructure with 15-40 year paybacks sits outside a five-year parliament.\n  - ADMINISTRATIVE COST AND IRREVERSIBILITY: interventions create constituencies that make them near-impossible to withdraw, so errors persist.\n• The case that market failure is more serious:\n  - MAGNITUDE: the largest market failures are enormous. Climate change is a global externality imposed on the unborn; the unregulated market would supply no national defence, no flood defence, no lighthouse; a health insurance market left to adverse selection leaves a large fraction of the population uninsured.\n  - IRREVERSIBILITY: environmental and health damage can be permanent, while a bad policy can be repealed. Asymmetry of reversibility is a strong argument for accepting the risk of government failure.\n  - THE COUNTERFACTUAL: judging intervention against its own imperfections without asking what would have happened otherwise is the nirvana fallacy. The smoking ban, the carrier-bag charge (over 95% reduction), the Soft Drinks Industry Levy\'s reformulation effect and the Montreal Protocol are all cases where intervention plainly improved on the market outcome.\n  - Many apparent government failures are failures of DESIGN, not of government: the SDIL worked because it was a producer-facing threshold, while a consumer-facing tax on inelastic demand would not have. That argues for better design rather than for non-intervention.\nEvaluation:\n• The question is not answerable in general — it depends on the market. For PUBLIC GOODS the market failure is total (nothing is supplied) so almost any intervention improves on it. For externalities with measurable damage and few sources, intervention reliably helps. For merit goods and information failures, remedies depend on consumers responding, and the behavioural evidence is discouraging. For markets that are nearly competitive, intervention is most likely to do net harm.\n• It depends on the INSTRUMENT: price controls have a poor record, corrective taxes a good one, and the same objective pursued with different tools produces opposite verdicts.\n• It depends on INSTITUTIONS: independent bodies with statutory mandates — the Climate Change Committee, the Bank of England\'s Monetary Policy Committee, the CMA, Ofwat\'s five-year price reviews — exist specifically to insulate decisions from short-termism and capture. Where they work, the main sources of government failure are attenuated, which means government failure is a feature of particular institutional arrangements rather than an inevitable property of government.\n• Symmetry of the critique: firms are short-termist too (quarterly earnings, high private discount rates) and markets suffer their own information problems, so the comparison is imperfect against imperfect.\n• Judgement: the strongest answers refuse the general form of the question and set out a TEST — intervene where the identified market failure is large, the external cost or benefit can be approximated, the instrument is matched to the cause of the failure, and the institution making the decision is insulated from capture and the electoral cycle. Applied to climate, public goods and health insurance, market failure is clearly the greater problem; applied to price controls in competitive markets and to subsidies into inelastic supply, government failure is. A conclusion that identifies the conditions rather than picking a side reaches Level 5.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) / L4 (16–20) / L3 (11–15) / L2 (6–10) / L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram, whether direct state provision is the best way of supplying public and merit goods.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: the merit-good case (MPB below MSB, free-market Q1 below the social optimum Q*, welfare gain triangle over Q1 to Q*), with state provision shown as moving output to Q* directly. Strong answers add the subsidy diagram — supply shifted down by the subsidy with the incidence split between consumers and producers — so that the two instruments can be compared on the same axes.\n• PUBLIC GOODS first, because the argument is different and much stronger. Non-excludability means no firm can charge, so the free-rider problem makes market supply ZERO despite total social benefit exceeding total cost. Non-rivalry means the efficient price for an extra user is zero, so any positive price would itself be allocatively inefficient. Taxation-funded state provision solves both: it compels contribution, overcoming free-riding, and allows a zero marginal price. For pure public goods — national defence, flood defence, street lighting — there is essentially no alternative, and this part of the question is close to settled.\n• MERIT GOODS are where the argument is genuinely contested. Under-consumption arises from positive externalities, information failure, present bias and unequal ability to pay. Provision guarantees the quantity and universality — every child in school, everyone treated in an emergency — which a subsidy cannot, because it leaves the final quantity to the elasticities.\n• Additional arguments for provision: it eliminates the incidence problem entirely (no share of the money is captured by producers as higher prices); it achieves equity of access directly, which matters where society values universal coverage as an end in itself; it captures economies of scale in a single national system; and it avoids the transaction and selection costs of an insurance market, which asymmetric information makes severe.\nEvaluation:\n• X-INEFFICIENCY is the central objection: without the threat of bankruptcy, takeover or exit, a state provider faces weak cost discipline, and managers face no residual claimant demanding minimum cost. Rationing is then by queue rather than by price, which is not obviously fairer and is certainly not costless.\n• WEAK DYNAMIC EFFICIENCY: innovation is driven by competition and the prospect of profit; a monopoly provider competing for funds in a spending round rather than for customers has little incentive to innovate, and its investment is hostage to fiscal cycles and political short-termism.\n• INFORMATION AND PREFERENCES: the state must decide what and how much to provide without the price signals that reveal consumer valuations, so the composition of provision may be wrong even if the quantity is right.\n• GOVERNMENT FAILURE: political interference in operational decisions, producer-interest capture by professional bodies and unions, and irreversibility once a service and its workforce exist.\n• ALTERNATIVES to weigh seriously:\n  - SUBSIDY to private providers, preserving competition and choice. Its weakness is incidence: where supply is inelastic, much of it is captured as higher provider prices rather than more output, so it buys few additional units.\n  - REGULATION AND MANDATES: compulsory schooling, compulsory insurance, minimum standards. These achieve the quantity target with no exchequer cost but no help with affordability.\n  - CONTRACTING OUT AND QUASI-MARKETS: the state funds and specifies, private or independent bodies deliver in competition. In principle this combines public funding with competitive cost discipline; in practice it depends entirely on the quality of contract specification and monitoring, and it fails badly where quality is hard to measure — which is exactly where merit goods sit.\n  - VOUCHERS: public funding attached to the consumer, preserving choice and competition while guaranteeing access. Weakness: they can encourage cream-skimming and sorting by ability or income.\n  - INFORMATION AND NUDGES where the failure is behavioural rather than financial.\n• Judgement: for PURE PUBLIC GOODS state provision is not merely best but effectively the only option, because the market supplies none. For MERIT GOODS the answer depends on three things — whether universality is the objective (if so, provision or mandate), how elastic private supply is (if inelastic, subsidy is largely capitalised and provision is better), and how measurable quality is (if hard to measure, contracting fails and direct provision is safer). The strongest answers make that distinction explicitly rather than treating public and merit goods as one category, and note that the UK in practice uses a mix — provision for defence and emergency care, subsidy for childcare and training, mandates for schooling and insurance.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) / L4 (16–20) / L3 (11–15) / L2 (6–10) / L1 (1–5); an accurate, integrated diagram is required for Level 4 and above. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},
    ]
  },
  '1.3': {
    name: 'Firm Objectives, Costs & Revenue',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'normal profit\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `1 mark: the minimum level of profit required to keep a firm in its current line of business in the long run.\n1 mark: development — it is the opportunity cost of the entrepreneur\'s resources, so it is counted as a COST in economics; it occurs where total revenue equals total cost including implicit costs, and a firm earning exactly normal profit has no incentive either to leave the industry or to attract new entrants.\nDo not credit \'zero profit\' without the explanation that accounting profit is positive while economic profit is zero.`},

      { q: 'A firm has total costs of £500 000 and produces 10 000 units. Calculate its average total cost. You are advised to show your working.', marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: ATC = total cost / output = £500 000 / 10 000 (1 mark for the correct formula or method).\nCorrect answer: £50 per unit (1 mark).\nAccept 50 without units. An answer of 0.02 (output divided by cost) scores zero. A correct answer with no working still scores 2 marks.`},

      { q: 'A firm produces 100 units at a total cost of £5 000, and 101 units at a total cost of £5 060. Calculate the marginal cost of the 101st unit. You are advised to show your working.', marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: MC = change in total cost / change in output = (£5 060 - £5 000) / 1 (1 mark).\nCorrect answer: £60 (1 mark).\nCommon error: dividing £5 060 by 101 to get £50.10 — that is average cost, not marginal cost, and scores zero. Note for teaching: MC (£60) exceeds AC at 100 units (£50), which is why average cost is rising at this output.`},

      { q: 'A firm\'s total revenue rises from £8 000 to £8 400 when output rises from 40 to 42 units. Calculate the marginal revenue. You are advised to show your working.', marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: MR = change in total revenue / change in quantity = (£8 400 - £8 000) / (42 - 40) = £400 / 2 (1 mark).\nCorrect answer: £200 per unit (1 mark).\nAn answer of £400 fails to divide by the change in quantity and scores 1 mark for method only. Note for teaching: average revenue is 8 000/40 = £200 before and 8 400/42 = £200 after, so AR is constant and MR equals AR — the firm is a price taker.`},

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'sunk cost\', using one example.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `1 mark: a cost that has already been incurred and cannot be recovered if the firm ceases production or exits the market.\n1 mark: a developed example — spending on brand advertising, on industry-specific research and development, on a bespoke oil rig or a rail-specific signalling system, or on a non-refundable licence. Credit the implication that because it cannot be recovered it should not influence any FUTURE decision, since only avoidable costs are relevant at the margin.\nDo not credit fixed costs generally: a delivery van is a fixed cost but is largely recoverable by resale, so it is not sunk.`},

      { q: 'Identify two internal economies of scale.', marks: 2, command: 'Identify',
        markScheme: `1 mark each, maximum 2. Accept any two of:\n• Technical — larger, more specialised or indivisible capital equipment spread over more output; the container principle, where capacity rises faster than surface area and so faster than cost.\n• Purchasing (bulk buying) — discounts on large input orders.\n• Managerial — employing specialists whose salary is spread over a much larger output.\n• Financial — larger firms borrow at lower interest rates because they are lower risk and have more collateral, and can access bond and equity markets.\n• Marketing — the fixed cost of an advertising campaign spread over more units.\n• Risk-bearing — diversification across products and markets.\nDo not credit EXTERNAL economies (industry-wide effects such as a local skilled labour pool), which are excluded by the word \'internal\'.`},

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'satisficing\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `1 mark: aiming for an outcome that is satisfactory or good enough rather than optimal — in a firm, earning enough profit to keep shareholders content rather than the maximum possible.\n1 mark: development — Simon\'s concept, arising from bounded rationality: managers lack the information and the computational capacity to identify the profit-maximising output, and they must balance the competing demands of shareholders, employees, customers and regulators, so they accept a satisfactory compromise. Credit the link to the principal-agent problem, where managers satisfice on profit while pursuing salary, status or a quieter life.`},

      { q: 'A firm has fixed costs of £200 000 and variable costs of £15 per unit. Calculate its average total cost when it produces 10 000 units. You are advised to show your working.', marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: total variable cost = £15 x 10 000 = £150 000; total cost = £200 000 + £150 000 = £350 000; ATC = £350 000 / 10 000 (1 mark for a correct method, including correctly computing total cost).\nCorrect answer: £35 per unit (1 mark).\nAlternative valid method: ATC = AFC + AVC = (£200 000/10 000) + £15 = £20 + £15 = £35.\nAn answer of £20 (average fixed cost only) or £15 (average variable cost only) scores zero.`},

      { q: 'Explain why the marginal cost curve cuts the average total cost curve at its minimum point.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Up to 3 marks for a developed chain of reasoning:\n1 mark — the relationship is arithmetic, not economic: whenever the marginal value lies BELOW the average, it pulls the average down; whenever it lies ABOVE the average, it pushes the average up.\n1 mark — it follows that the average can only be at its lowest point at the moment the marginal is exactly equal to it, because that is the only point at which the average is neither being pulled down nor pushed up.\n1 mark — application and development: to the left of the intersection MC is below ATC so ATC is falling; to the right MC is above ATC so ATC is rising; the same logic applies to the AVC curve, which MC also cuts at its minimum, though at a lower output than ATC because ATC includes falling average fixed cost.\nCredit the standard analogy of a batting or test-score average being pulled by each new score, provided the economic terms are used correctly.`},

      { q: 'Explain why a firm may continue to produce in the short run even when price is below average total cost.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Up to 3 marks:\n1 mark — in the short run fixed costs must be paid whether or not the firm produces, so they are unavoidable and therefore irrelevant to the decision. Only variable costs can be avoided by shutting down.\n1 mark — provided price is at or above AVERAGE VARIABLE COST, each unit sold covers its own variable cost and makes a positive contribution towards the fixed costs. The firm therefore loses LESS by producing than by shutting down and losing the whole of its fixed costs.\n1 mark — the shut-down condition is therefore P < AVC in the short run, and the firm\'s short-run supply curve is the portion of the MC curve above AVC. In the LONG run all costs are variable, so the firm must cover average total cost (P is at least AC) or it will exit.\nCredit the additional point that a firm may also stay open to retain skilled staff, preserve customer relationships or avoid sunk re-entry costs.`},

      { q: 'Explain why marginal revenue lies below average revenue for a firm facing a downward-sloping demand curve.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Up to 3 marks:\n1 mark — average revenue equals price, and the demand curve IS the average revenue curve. A firm with a downward-sloping demand curve must cut its price to sell an extra unit.\n1 mark — if the firm cannot price discriminate, it must cut the price on EVERY unit it sells, not just on the extra one. Marginal revenue is therefore the price of the extra unit MINUS the revenue lost on all the units that were previously sold at the higher price.\n1 mark — because that loss is positive, MR is always less than AR, and the MR curve falls twice as steeply as a straight-line AR curve. Development: MR reaches zero at the output where total revenue is maximised and where PED is exactly -1, and becomes negative beyond it, so a profit-maximising firm never produces on the inelastic part of its demand curve.\nContrast worth crediting: for a price taker AR is horizontal, the firm can sell any quantity at the going price, and MR = AR = price.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the shapes of a firm\'s short-run cost curves.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): cost per unit on the vertical axis and output on the horizontal; AFC falling continuously towards but never reaching the horizontal axis; AVC and ATC both U-shaped with ATC above AVC and the vertical gap between them narrowing as output rises (because that gap is AFC); MC U-shaped and cutting both AVC and ATC at their respective minimum points, with the AVC minimum at a lower output than the ATC minimum. All four curves labelled.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): AFC falls because a fixed total is divided by a rising output. MC falls at first because of increasing marginal returns as the variable factor is combined more effectively with the fixed factor, then rises because of the law of diminishing marginal returns once the fixed factor becomes a constraint. AVC and ATC are U-shaped because MC pulls them down while it is below them and pushes them up once it is above them.\nDevelopment worth the fourth mark: the gap between ATC and AVC narrows but never closes, because AFC approaches zero asymptotically; and the whole diagram is a SHORT-RUN construction, valid only while at least one factor is fixed.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, what is meant by the minimum efficient scale of a firm.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): long-run average cost on the vertical axis and output on the horizontal; a U-shaped or L-shaped LRAC curve; the falling section labelled economies of scale and the rising section (if drawn) labelled diseconomies of scale; the output at which LRAC first reaches its minimum clearly labelled MINIMUM EFFICIENT SCALE.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): the minimum efficient scale is the lowest level of output at which a firm can achieve the lowest attainable long-run average cost, and therefore the smallest size at which it is cost-competitive with the largest firms in the industry. Below it a firm has a unit-cost disadvantage that cannot be overcome by better management, only by growing.\nDevelopment: the MES determines market structure. Where MES is very large relative to total market demand, only a few firms can operate efficiently, so the industry tends towards oligopoly or natural monopoly (rail track, water networks, aircraft manufacture). Where MES is small relative to demand, many firms can coexist efficiently (hairdressing, restaurants), so the structure tends towards monopolistic competition.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the law of diminishing marginal returns.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): either (a) output per period on the vertical axis and quantity of the variable factor (labour) on the horizontal, with marginal product rising to a peak and then falling, average product rising to a peak and then falling, and MP cutting AP at AP\'s maximum; or (b) the MC curve, showing MC falling then rising, with the turning point corresponding to the peak of marginal product. Curves and axes labelled; the point at which diminishing marginal returns set in must be identified.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): the law states that as successive units of a VARIABLE factor are added to a FIXED factor, beyond some point the additional output from each extra unit of the variable factor will fall. It is a SHORT-RUN law, since it depends on at least one factor being fixed.\nWhy it happens: at low output, extra workers allow specialisation and better use of the fixed capital, so marginal product rises. Beyond a point the fixed capital becomes a constraint — workers queue for machines, workspace is crowded, coordination costs rise — so each extra worker adds less than the last.\nDevelopment worth the fourth mark: because MC is the wage divided by marginal product, falling marginal product means rising marginal cost. Diminishing returns is therefore the direct cause of the upward-sloping section of MC, and hence of the U-shape of AVC and ATC. It must not be confused with diseconomies of scale, which are a LONG-RUN phenomenon in which ALL factors vary.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how a firm identifies its profit-maximising output and shows the supernormal profit it earns.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): cost and revenue on the vertical axis, output on the horizontal; a downward-sloping AR (= demand) curve with MR below it and twice as steep; U-shaped MC and ATC with MC cutting ATC at its minimum; profit-maximising output Q* identified where MC = MR; price P* read UP from Q* to the AR curve, not to the MR curve; average cost at Q* read off ATC; supernormal profit shaded as the rectangle (P* - ATC) x Q*.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): profit is maximised where MC = MR because, to the left of Q*, MR exceeds MC so each extra unit adds more to revenue than to cost and profit rises; to the right, MC exceeds MR so each extra unit reduces profit. Q* is therefore the output at which the gap between total revenue and total cost is greatest.\nDevelopment: the second-order condition is that MC must be RISING through the intersection. Reading price off the MR curve rather than the AR curve is the single commonest error and costs the diagram mark.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the difference between a firm\'s profit-maximising output and its revenue-maximising output.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): AR downward sloping with MR below it, MC and ATC drawn; profit-maximising output Qp where MC = MR with price Pp read off AR; revenue-maximising output Qr where MR = 0 with price Pr read off AR; Qr to the RIGHT of Qp and Pr BELOW Pp. Both outputs and both prices labelled.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): profit is maximised where MC = MR; revenue is maximised where MR = 0, that is, at the top of the total revenue curve, which is also the output at which PED equals -1. Since MC is positive, MR = MC occurs where MR is still positive, so the revenue-maximising output must lie beyond the profit-maximising one, at a lower price.\nDevelopment worth the fourth mark: a firm pursuing revenue maximisation therefore sells more at a lower price and earns less profit than it could. Reasons a firm might do so include managerial bonuses tied to sales or turnover (the principal-agent problem), building market share, deterring entry, or exploiting economies of scale — and note that revenue maximisation is distinct from SALES VOLUME maximisation, which occurs further right still, at the output where AR = ATC and only normal profit is earned.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the short-run shut-down point for a firm.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): MC, ATC and AVC drawn with MC cutting both at their minima; the SHUT-DOWN POINT marked at the minimum of AVC, where MC cuts AVC; a price line at that level labelled; ideally a second price line between minimum AVC and minimum ATC labelled \'produce, but at a loss\'.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): if price is at or above minimum AVC, each unit covers its own variable cost and contributes something towards fixed costs, so the firm loses less by producing than by closing and losing all of its fixed costs. If price falls below minimum AVC, every unit produced adds to the loss, so the firm minimises its loss by shutting down and losing only its fixed costs.\nDevelopment worth the fourth mark: the shut-down decision is a SHORT-RUN one, because it takes fixed costs as unavoidable. In the long run all costs are variable, so the firm must cover ATC or exit permanently; the long-run exit point is the minimum of LRAC. The portion of the MC curve above minimum AVC is the firm\'s short-run supply curve.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the relationship between total cost, total variable cost and total fixed cost.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): total cost in pounds on the vertical axis and output on the horizontal; TFC drawn as a HORIZONTAL line, unchanged as output rises; TVC starting at the ORIGIN and rising, first at a decreasing rate and then at an increasing rate (an inverted-S shape); TC starting at the level of TFC on the vertical axis and running PARALLEL to TVC, the constant vertical gap between them being TFC. All three curves labelled.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): TC = TFC + TVC by definition. Fixed costs do not vary with output in the short run — rent, insurance, business rates, depreciation — so the line is horizontal and TC starts above the origin. Variable costs are zero at zero output, which is why TVC begins at the origin.\nDevelopment worth the fourth mark: the changing slope of TVC is the law of diminishing marginal returns in a different form. The slope of TVC at any output IS marginal cost, so TVC rising at a decreasing rate corresponds to falling MC (increasing marginal returns), and TVC rising at an increasing rate corresponds to rising MC (diminishing marginal returns).`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which growth lowers a firm\'s average costs.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
Long-run average cost estimates for a manufacturer at four plant sizes
  Output 50 000 units:    LRAC £41.20
  Output 150 000 units:   LRAC £28.60
  Output 400 000 units:   LRAC £24.90
  Output 900 000 units:   LRAC £27.30
Management reported that the 900 000-unit plant required four additional layers of supervision
and that staff turnover at that site was double the company average.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The data traces a classic U-shaped LRAC. Unit cost falls 39.6% from £41.20 to £24.90 as output rises from 50 000 to 400 000 — substantial economies of scale — and then RISES to £27.30 at 900 000, which is diseconomies of scale. The minimum efficient scale on this evidence is around 400 000 units.\n• Sources of the fall: technical economies as larger, indivisible capital is spread over more output; purchasing economies on bulk input orders; managerial economies as specialists\' salaries are spread more thinly; marketing and financial economies.\n• Sources of the rise, which the stimulus supplies directly: four extra layers of supervision is a communication and coordination diseconomy — information is distorted as it passes through the hierarchy and decisions slow. Turnover at double the company average is a motivational diseconomy: workers in a very large plant feel remote from the firm\'s objectives, so effort and retention fall.\nEvaluation:\n• Growth lowers average cost only up to MES, so the proposition is true over a range and false beyond it. The direction of the effect depends entirely on where the firm currently sits on the curve.\n• The gains are heavily front-loaded: unit cost falls £12.60 over the first tripling of output and only £3.70 over the next near-tripling. Diminishing returns to scale-seeking means the strategic case for growth weakens sharply as the firm gets larger.\n• Diseconomies are largely a MANAGEMENT problem rather than a technological one, so they can be mitigated by decentralisation, divisional structures and better incentives; the stimulus figures describe one firm\'s organisational failure, not an inevitable law.\n• Growth can raise costs for other reasons the LRAC curve does not show: X-inefficiency as competitive pressure falls, and rising input prices as a large buyer bids up the price of scarce specialised inputs (an external DISeconomy).\n• The relevant objective may not be minimum cost: growth can raise market power and therefore price, so profit may rise even where average cost does.\n• Judgement: growth lowers average cost decisively up to the minimum efficient scale and raises it beyond, so the honest answer is conditional — and on these data the firm would cut unit cost by 8.8% by operating at 400 000 rather than 900 000.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — the data used quantitatively to identify MES and both phases of the curve, with evaluation weighed. L1 (1–4) — lists economies of scale with no use of the figures.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which large public limited companies pursue profit maximisation.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
Board minutes and remuneration data for a listed company
  Chief executive bonus: 60% linked to revenue growth, 25% to share price, 15% to profit
  Proportion of shares held by institutional investors: 71%
  Average shareholding period of those institutions: 8 months
  Board decision: to open 40 new stores next year at a forecast first-year loss of £18m,
    described in the minutes as \'securing long-term market position\'`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The principal-agent problem is on display in the stimulus: the owners (shareholders) want profit, but only 15% of the chief executive\'s bonus is tied to it, while 60% rewards REVENUE growth. Managers therefore face a direct financial incentive to pursue revenue or sales maximisation, producing beyond the profit-maximising output at a lower price.\n• Divorce of ownership from control is what makes this possible: with 71% of shares held by institutions holding for an average of eight months, no single owner has either the information or the incentive to monitor management closely — a free-rider problem among shareholders.\n• The 40-store expansion at a forecast £18m loss is evidence of a GROWTH objective: growth raises managerial salary, status and security (larger firms pay their executives more), and may be defended as an investment in future profit, which makes it difficult to distinguish empire-building from long-run profit maximisation.\n• Other objectives worth crediting: satisficing under bounded rationality; corporate social responsibility; survival; and stakeholder objectives.\nEvaluation:\n• Profit maximisation may still be the underlying constraint. 25% of the bonus is tied to the SHARE PRICE, and a share price is the discounted present value of expected future profits, so the incentive package is less divorced from profit than the 15% figure suggests. A £18m loss now that raises expected future profit raises the share price today.\n• Market discipline: a persistently underperforming management invites takeover, and the threat of acquisition forces behaviour close to profit maximisation even where incentives are imperfect.\n• The eight-month average holding period cuts the other way too — short-termist institutional pressure can force managers towards SHORT-RUN profit maximisation at the expense of long-run value, which is a different distortion, not an absence of profit motive.\n• Measurement problem: long-run profit maximisation is observationally very similar to growth-seeking, because both involve sacrificing current profit. Without knowing the true expected return on the 40 stores, the minutes cannot settle the question.\n• Judgement: the stimulus shows incentives pointing at revenue and growth rather than profit, so strict short-run profit maximisation is clearly not what this board is doing; but the takeover threat and the share-price component mean long-run profit maximisation remains a defensible description of the constraint the firm operates under.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — stimulus data used explicitly, principal-agent analysis developed, evaluation weighed. L1 (1–4) — lists alternative objectives without using the evidence.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which diseconomies of scale limit the size of firms.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
A logistics group\'s cost and performance data after a merger
  Long-run average cost before merger: £6.40 per parcel
  Long-run average cost 18 months after merger: £7.15 per parcel
  Layers of management: 5 before ; 9 after
  Staff engagement score: 68% before ; 44% after
  Number of separate IT systems still running in parallel: 4`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Unit cost rose 11.7% ((7.15 - 6.40)/6.40) after the merger, which is prima facie evidence of diseconomies of scale — the firm has grown beyond its minimum efficient scale.\n• The stimulus identifies the classic three sources: CONTROL (nine management layers instead of five, so monitoring is harder and decisions slower), COMMUNICATION (four parallel IT systems means information cannot flow, so coordination costs rise sharply), and MOTIVATION (engagement falling from 68% to 44% as workers become remote from the firm\'s objectives, raising turnover and reducing effort).\n• Consequence: a firm whose LRAC is rising faces a cost disadvantage against smaller rivals, which in a competitive market is a limit on how large it can profitably become. This is one explanation for why industries do not consolidate into a single global firm.\nEvaluation:\n• The evidence may be temporary rather than structural. Four IT systems running in parallel is a POST-MERGER INTEGRATION cost, not a diseconomy of scale: it reflects a transition that will end, and 18 months is short. Attributing the whole 11.7% to scale is unsafe.\n• Diseconomies are largely organisational and therefore manageable: divisionalisation, delegation of authority, profit centres, performance-related pay and better systems can flatten the rising section of LRAC. Many very large firms operate successfully at enormous scale, which they could not do if diseconomies were an inescapable law.\n• Other constraints on firm size may bind first: the size of the market, regulatory limits including competition authorities blocking mergers, managerial capacity to raise finance, and the founder\'s or family\'s willingness to dilute control.\n• Countervailing advantages of size may outweigh higher unit costs: market power allows a higher price, so profit can rise even as average cost does; scale also improves bargaining power with suppliers and buys dynamic efficiency through R&D capacity.\n• In network industries, average cost can fall over the whole relevant range because of network effects, so there is no rising section at all and scale is limited only by the size of the market.\n• Judgement: diseconomies are a genuine limit, but on this evidence they are being confounded with integration costs, and they are better read as a limit on how fast a firm can grow and how well it is organised than as a hard ceiling on size.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — percentage change computed, all three sources identified from the stimulus, integration-cost critique reached. L1 (1–4) — lists diseconomies without using the data.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which the law of diminishing returns determines a firm\'s cost curves.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
Output from a bakery with a fixed number of ovens
  Workers: 1  ; total output 20 loaves per hour
  Workers: 2  ; total output 50 loaves per hour
  Workers: 3  ; total output 90 loaves per hour
  Workers: 4  ; total output 116 loaves per hour
  Workers: 5  ; total output 130 loaves per hour
  Workers: 6  ; total output 136 loaves per hour
Wage: £14 per worker per hour. Fixed costs: £70 per hour.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Compute marginal product from the data: 20, 30, 40, 26, 14, 6. Marginal product RISES to a peak at the third worker and then falls — diminishing marginal returns set in from the fourth worker onwards.\n• Convert to marginal cost: MC = wage / marginal product, so MC per loaf = £14/20 = 70p, £14/30 = 46.7p, £14/40 = 35p, £14/26 = 53.8p, £14/14 = £1.00, £14/6 = £2.33. Marginal cost falls while marginal product rises and rises once it falls — the two are mirror images, and that inverse relationship is exactly what gives MC its U-shape.\n• Because MC pulls the averages, the U-shape of MC produces the U-shapes of AVC and ATC. In that sense diminishing returns is the direct determinant of short-run cost curve shape.\nEvaluation:\n• It determines only the SHORT-RUN curves. The law requires at least one fixed factor, so it says nothing about the long-run average cost curve, whose shape is governed instead by economies and diseconomies of scale — a completely different mechanism in which all factors vary.\n• It assumes all workers are homogeneous and that the wage is constant. If the firm hires less capable workers as it expands, or must pay more to attract them, MC rises for reasons unrelated to diminishing returns.\n• Input prices matter independently. A rise in the wage from £14 shifts the whole MC curve up without changing marginal product at all, so costs are determined by factor prices as well as by productivity.\n• Technology and capital can be varied in practice faster than the model implies — the bakery could hire a second oven — so the strict short run may be a brief and rather artificial period.\n• Fixed costs shape ATC but not MC: the £70 per hour drives the falling AFC that pulls ATC down at low output, and that is arithmetic, not diminishing returns.\n• Judgement: diminishing returns determines the SHAPE of the short-run marginal and average variable cost curves and nothing else — the POSITION of those curves is set by factor prices, and the long-run curve is set by returns to scale. The data illustrate the shape point precisely.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — marginal product computed from the data and linked numerically to marginal cost, short-run limitation recognised. L1 (1–4) — states the law without computing from the stimulus.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which sunk costs influence whether a firm leaves a market.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
Two firms are considering exit from a declining market.
  Firm A — a parcel courier: assets are vans and leased depots.
    Resale value of assets as a share of original cost: 72%
  Firm B — a deep-water drilling contractor: assets are a purpose-built rig and a
    decommissioning liability.
    Resale value of assets as a share of original cost: 9%
    Cost of decommissioning if the rig is abandoned: £240m
Both firms currently earn revenue that covers variable costs but not total costs.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The correct economic principle first: sunk costs SHOULD NOT influence the decision at all, because they are unrecoverable whichever choice is made. Only avoidable future costs and revenues are relevant at the margin. A firm that stays in a market \'because of everything we have already invested\' is committing the sunk cost fallacy.\n• What the stimulus actually shows is not sunk costs influencing the decision but RECOVERABLE VALUE differing. Firm A recovers 72% of its asset cost on exit, so most of its investment was never sunk and exit is cheap. Firm B recovers 9% and faces a £240m decommissioning liability, so exit has a large NEGATIVE payoff — and that liability is a FUTURE AVOIDABLE-BY-DELAY cost, which legitimately belongs in the calculation.\n• Consequence for market structure: high sunk costs raise EXIT barriers, and because rational firms anticipate this, they also raise ENTRY barriers. This is the core of contestable market theory — a market is contestable only if entry and exit are costless, and sunk costs are what destroys contestability.\nEvaluation:\n• Sunk costs do influence decisions in practice, through behavioural rather than rational channels: loss aversion and commitment bias make managers reluctant to crystallise a loss, and admitting a failed investment is personally costly to the executive who authorised it — a principal-agent problem.\n• The decommissioning liability is genuinely relevant, so Firm B\'s reluctance to exit is rational, not a fallacy. Distinguishing a true sunk cost from a future avoidable liability is the key analytical move, and many answers will conflate them.\n• Both firms cover variable costs, so in the SHORT run both should continue producing regardless of asset values; the exit question is a long-run one.\n• Other factors matter at least as much: expected future demand and prices, the cost of re-entry if conditions improve (option value of staying), contractual obligations, and the availability of a buyer.\n• Judgement: sunk costs should not and, for a rational firm, do not determine exit — but the recoverability of assets and the existence of exit liabilities do, and these are systematically correlated with sunk costs, which is why industries with high sunk costs show low exit rates and weak contestability.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — the sunk cost principle stated correctly AND the two firms contrasted using the recovery percentages, with the distinction between sunk and avoidable costs reached. L1 (1–4) — defines sunk cost without applying it.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which a rise in the National Living Wage raises a labour-intensive firm\'s average costs.

Stimulus (written for this practice question, using published UK rates)
The National Living Wage for workers aged 21 and over rose from £12.21 in April 2025 to
£12.71 in April 2026.
A residential care home:
  Staff: 90 ; average hours 34 a week ; all paid at the statutory floor
  Other operating costs (rent, food, utilities, insurance): £1.42m a year
  Residents: 76 ; average annual fee £41 500`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Quantify from the data. Total paid hours = 90 x 34 x 52 = 159 120 a year. Wage bill at £12.21 = £1 942 855; at £12.71 = £2 022 415. The increase is £79 560 a year, about 4.1%.\n• Total cost before: £1 942 855 + £1 420 000 = £3 362 855, giving an average cost per resident of £44 248. After: £3 442 415, giving £45 295. Average cost per resident rises by about £1 047, or 2.4%.\n• The key analytical point: the 4.1% wage rise produces only a 2.4% rise in average cost, because labour is about 58% of total cost, not 100%. The effect on average cost is the wage rise multiplied by labour\'s cost share — the more labour-intensive the firm, the closer the two figures.\n• Squeeze on margins: average fee income is £41 500 against average cost of £45 295, so this home is already loss-making on these figures and the rise deepens it; profitability, not just cost, is the operative concern.\nEvaluation:\n• Real versus nominal: if input prices generally, and the fees the home can charge, rise by a similar percentage, the REAL cost increase is small. A 4.1% nominal uprating in a year of similar inflation changes little.\n• Productivity offsets: efficiency-wage effects mean higher pay can cut turnover and recruitment costs and raise effort. In care, high staff turnover is itself extremely expensive, so part of the £79 560 may be recouped.\n• Substitution and adjustment: the firm can cut hours, reduce agency use, or substitute capital (monitoring technology, rostering software) — though care is hard to automate, which is precisely why it is labour-intensive.\n• Pass-through: the firm\'s ability to raise fees depends on PED for its service and on who pays. Where fees are set by a local authority, the firm cannot pass costs on at all, which turns a cost rise straight into a loss — and this is the decisive point for this sector.\n• Differentials: the rise compresses pay differentials, so the firm may face pressure to raise supervisors\' pay too, making the true cost increase larger than the £79 560 calculated.\n• Employer National Insurance and pension contributions are charged on top of the hourly rate, so the true employment cost rises by more than the wage bill alone.\n• Judgement: the arithmetic shows a 4.1% wage rise producing a 2.4% average cost rise, so the effect is real but proportionate to labour\'s cost share; the severity depends far more on whether the firm can pass the cost on than on the size of the uprating.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — the wage bill and average cost computed from the data, the cost-share point reached, evaluation weighed. L1 (1–4) — asserts that costs rise without using the figures.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which technology has reduced the minimum efficient scale in retailing.

Stimulus (written for this practice question, with published UK market shares)
Kantar grocery market shares in Great Britain, 2026
  Tesco 27.3% ; Sainsbury\'s 15.1% ; Asda 13.7% ; Aldi 10.2% ; Morrisons 9.2%
The four largest chains held around 65% of the market, down from around 73% in 2017.
Illustrative costs for a new online-only grocery retailer
  Fixed cost of a national store estate: none
  Fixed cost of a single automated fulfilment centre serving 2 million households: £185m
  Third-party delivery and payment platforms available on a per-order fee basis`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The case that technology has cut MES: an online entrant avoids the enormous sunk cost of a national store estate, and can rent logistics, payments and cloud computing on a PER-ORDER basis. Costs that were once large and fixed become variable, which flattens the LRAC curve at low output and allows a small firm to be cost-competitive — a genuine reduction in minimum efficient scale, and hence lower entry barriers and greater contestability.\n• Evidence in the stimulus: the big four\'s combined share fell from about 73% in 2017 to about 65% in 2026, consistent with smaller and newer formats competing effectively.\nEvaluation:\n• The evidence does not actually support the technology story. The share loss went largely to ALDI (10.2%), a limited-assortment discounter whose advantage is a narrow range, high volume per line and low-cost stores — an OLD-fashioned economy of scale in purchasing and simplicity, not a technological reduction in MES.\n• The £185m fulfilment centre serving 2 million households is itself a very large indivisible fixed cost. Automation can RAISE minimum efficient scale, because a highly automated facility must run near capacity to spread its cost — the opposite of the proposition.\n• Tesco at 27.3% still has decisive scale advantages in buyer power over suppliers, in data from loyalty schemes, and in marketing costs spread over a huge turnover. A 27.3% share is not what a market with a low MES looks like.\n• Network effects and data create NEW scale economies in digital retail: the more customers, the better the recommendation and demand-forecasting algorithms, which is a source of increasing returns that did not exist before.\n• Counterfactual: the decline in big-four share may reflect price competition after 2008 and changed shopping habits rather than any change in cost structure.\n• Judgement: technology has lowered the minimum efficient scale for ENTRY into narrow niches, by turning fixed costs into variable ones, while simultaneously raising it in automated fulfilment and creating new data-driven scale economies. The stimulus shows falling concentration but attributes it poorly, and a supported conclusion should say which segment of retailing is being discussed.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — the market share data used and the attribution problem recognised, with the two-directional effect of technology developed. L1 (1–4) — asserts that the internet lowered barriers without engaging with the data.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether firms should pursue revenue maximisation rather than profit maximisation.', marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The two rules: profit is maximised where MC = MR; revenue is maximised where MR = 0, which is also where PED = -1. Because MC is positive, the revenue-maximising output is always LARGER and the price always LOWER than the profit-maximising one. A firm switching from profit to revenue maximisation therefore sells more, charges less and earns less profit.\n• The case for revenue maximisation:\n  - Market share and scale: a larger output moves the firm down its LRAC curve, so today\'s lower margin can become tomorrow\'s cost advantage. This is the standard justification in network industries and platforms, where scale is self-reinforcing.\n  - Entry deterrence: a high output and low price make the market unattractive to entrants, protecting long-run profit.\n  - Managerial incentives: where bonuses are tied to turnover, managers pursue revenue because they are paid to — a principal-agent problem, not a strategy chosen by owners.\n  - Retailer and supplier bargaining power rises with volume.\nEvaluation:\n• Revenue is not profit, and a firm cannot pay dividends, service debt or fund investment out of turnover. Persistent revenue maximisation destroys shareholder value, and the market discipline of takeover eventually corrects it — which is why revenue maximisation is best understood as a temporary strategy rather than an objective.\n• Most apparent revenue maximisation is LONG-RUN PROFIT maximisation in disguise: sacrificing current profit to build share is rational if the discounted future profit exceeds what is given up. The two objectives are then observationally similar, and the real question is whether the firm has a credible route back to profitability.\n• It requires knowing the demand curve accurately enough to locate MR = 0; in practice firms use rules of thumb such as cost-plus pricing.\n• Consumers gain in the short run from lower prices and higher output, which is why competition authorities are relaxed about it — but predatory or below-cost pricing to drive out rivals is prohibited, and the boundary between aggressive share-building and predation is exactly where the CMA intervenes.\n• Sales volume maximisation (producing until AR = ATC, earning only normal profit) is a further step again and is more vulnerable still.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion is that revenue maximisation is rational only where it is instrumental to future profit — in markets with strong scale economies, network effects or contestable entry — and irrational as a permanent objective, since a firm that never converts share into profit has no reason to exist.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) / L2 (5–8) / L1 (1–4). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether growth is a desirable objective for a firm.', marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Arguments that growth is desirable:\n  - Economies of scale: moving down the LRAC curve towards the minimum efficient scale lowers unit costs, raising margins or allowing lower prices. Technical, purchasing, financial, managerial, marketing and risk-bearing economies all apply.\n  - Market power: a larger share means a less elastic demand curve, so the firm can raise price above marginal cost and earn supernormal profit.\n  - Risk reduction through diversification across products and markets, and greater resilience to a shock in any one.\n  - Dynamic efficiency: scale funds research and development, which a small firm cannot afford, so growth can be the route to innovation.\n  - Survival: in a market with a large MES, a firm that does not grow is at a permanent unit-cost disadvantage and will be driven out.\n  - Managerial motives: salary, status and job security rise with firm size — a reason growth is pursued that is NOT a reason it is desirable for owners.\nEvaluation:\n• Diseconomies of scale beyond the MES: control, communication and motivation problems raise unit costs. Growth is therefore desirable only up to a point, and the whole question turns on where the firm currently sits on its LRAC curve.\n• Growth by merger frequently destroys value: integration costs, culture clashes, parallel IT systems and overpayment for the target mean many mergers fail to deliver the forecast synergies.\n• X-inefficiency: as a firm becomes dominant, competitive pressure falls and costs drift above the minimum attainable.\n• Regulatory constraint: growth by acquisition is subject to merger control, and a dominant position attracts conduct scrutiny. The CMA cleared the Vodafone-Three merger on 5 December 2024 only against a legally binding commitment to an £11 billion network investment programme and time-limited price caps, which shows that growth can be permitted but only at a price.\n• Overtrading and financial risk: rapid growth absorbs working capital and raises gearing, so a growing firm can be profitable and still fail.\n• Small can be optimal: in markets with a low MES, where the product is personalised, or where the firm competes on flexibility and service, staying small IS the competitive strategy.\n• Whose objective? Growth serves managers more reliably than it serves shareholders, so the principal-agent problem means observed growth is not evidence that growth is desirable.\n• Judgement: growth is desirable where the firm is below its minimum efficient scale, where scale economies or network effects are strong, and where it can be financed without excessive risk; it is undesirable beyond MES, where it is pursued for managerial reasons, and where it is bought at a premium through acquisition.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) / L2 (5–8) / L1 (1–4). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether the separation of ownership from control leads to inefficiency.', marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The mechanism: in a public limited company the owners (shareholders) are the principals and the managers are their agents. Their objectives differ — owners want the present value of profit maximised, managers want salary, status, security and a quieter life — and because managers hold far better information about the firm\'s operations, owners cannot verify whether decisions are made in their interest. This is the principal-agent problem, and asymmetric information is what makes it unsolvable by contract alone.\n• Resulting inefficiencies:\n  - Allocative: managers pursuing revenue or growth produce beyond the profit-maximising output, so resources are allocated to the firm that would earn more elsewhere; or they entrench market power to secure a quiet life.\n  - Productive and X-inefficiency: without a residual claimant demanding minimum cost, costs drift above the technically attainable level — corporate jets, excessive headcount, prestige projects.\n  - Empire-building: acquisitions that raise turnover and managerial pay while destroying shareholder value.\n  - Excessive risk aversion: managers have undiversified human capital tied to the firm, so they may reject positive-expected-value projects a diversified shareholder would want taken.\n  - Short-termism in the opposite direction: managers paid on annual results may cut R&D and maintenance to hit a target.\nEvaluation:\n• Mitigations are substantial and largely effective:\n  - Incentive alignment through share options, long-term incentive plans and bonuses tied to share price, which is the discounted value of future profits.\n  - The MARKET FOR CORPORATE CONTROL: persistent underperformance depresses the share price and invites takeover, after which the incumbent managers lose their jobs. This is the most powerful discipline available and does not require shareholders to monitor anything.\n  - Institutional shareholders with large stakes have both the incentive and the resources to monitor, and governance codes require independent non-executive directors and remuneration committees.\n  - Competitive product markets: a firm that lets costs drift in a competitive market is driven out, so separation of ownership and control matters far less where competition is strong.\n• The separation has genuine EFFICIENCY BENEFITS that the question invites you to weigh: it allows specialisation, with capital supplied by many small savers who need no management expertise and decisions taken by professional managers who need no capital. It allows firms to reach a scale unattainable under owner-management, and it allows shareholders to diversify risk. Without it, firm size would be limited by the personal wealth of entrepreneurs.\n• Incentive schemes create their own distortions: options reward volatility and can encourage excessive risk, and share-price targets can be met by buybacks rather than by investment.\n• Judgement: separation creates a real and measurable inefficiency, but it is a cost incurred to buy much larger gains in scale, specialisation and risk diversification. The defensible conclusion is that it is a net efficiency GAIN whose costs are contained by takeover threat and product-market competition, and that it becomes seriously inefficient only where those disciplines are weak — in firms with dispersed ownership, entrenched boards, and market power.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) / L2 (5–8) / L1 (1–4). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether economies of scale always benefit consumers.', marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The case that they do: economies of scale lower long-run average cost. In a competitive market, firms cannot hold prices above cost, so the saving is competed away into lower prices, and consumers capture the whole benefit. Lower costs also allow greater output, wider product ranges and investment in quality and innovation. Where MES is large relative to the market, scale also makes provision possible at all — a national rail or water network could not be duplicated.\n• Dynamic benefits: financial economies lower the cost of capital, funding research and development that a small firm could not undertake, so consumers gain new and better products over time.\nEvaluation:\n• Pass-through depends entirely on MARKET STRUCTURE. A monopolist that lowers its costs faces the same demand curve and the same MR; profit maximisation at MC = MR means lower cost leads to some price reduction, but the firm retains most of the gain as supernormal profit. The more inelastic demand is, the smaller the share passed on. Consumers benefit fully only where competition forces it.\n• Scale economies are themselves a BARRIER TO ENTRY. If MES is large relative to demand, entrants must enter at scale or face a unit-cost disadvantage, so the very economies that lower cost also entrench the incumbent and reduce the competitive pressure that would have passed them on. This is the central tension in the question.\n• Beyond MES, diseconomies raise costs, and a firm that grows past that point through merger passes higher costs, not lower ones, to consumers.\n• X-inefficiency: a large firm insulated from competition may not realise the theoretically available economies at all, so measured cost stays above minimum even at large scale.\n• Quality, choice and standardisation: economies of scale are often achieved by narrowing the range and standardising the product, so consumers gain on price and lose on variety — the limited-assortment discount grocer is the clearest case.\n• Distributional and wider effects: scale can be achieved by squeezing suppliers or by relocating production, so gains to consumers come partly at the expense of workers and suppliers rather than from genuine efficiency.\n• Natural monopoly is the sharpest version: economies of scale over the whole range of demand mean a single firm is the efficient structure, but an unregulated single firm will price above cost. Consumers benefit only if the monopoly is regulated — Ofwat and Ofgem exist for exactly this reason.\n• Judgement: economies of scale create the POSSIBILITY of consumer benefit but do not deliver it. Whether consumers actually gain depends on the intensity of competition, the elasticity of demand and the quality of regulation where competition is impossible. The strongest conclusions note that the same economies simultaneously lower cost and raise barriers to entry, so the net effect on consumers is genuinely ambiguous.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) / L2 (5–8) / L1 (1–4). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether predatory pricing is a rational strategy for a firm.', marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• What it is: deliberately pricing below average variable cost (the usual legal test) with the intention of driving rivals out of the market, then raising price once they have gone. It is an abuse of a dominant position under Chapter II of the Competition Act 1998 and Article 102-equivalent provisions, and is illegal in the UK.\n• The rational case: the firm sacrifices profit now as an INVESTMENT. If the present value of the supernormal profit earned after rivals exit exceeds the losses incurred during the price war, the strategy pays. It works best where the predator has deeper financial reserves than the target, where it can cross-subsidise from other markets or products, and where the rival is credit-constrained.\n• Reputation effects extend the payoff: a firm known to price predatorily deters future entry in every market it operates in, so the return is not confined to the market where the war was fought. This makes the strategy rational even where a single episode would not pay for itself.\nEvaluation:\n• The recoupment problem is decisive and is what makes predation rarely rational in practice. To recoup, the market must be hard to re-enter after the predator raises its price. If entry barriers are low, the high post-predation price simply attracts new entrants, so the losses were incurred for nothing. Predation is therefore only rational where sunk costs make the market non-contestable — which links directly to contestable market theory.\n• The predator loses more than the prey in absolute terms, because it has the larger output and so loses on more units at every price below cost. This asymmetry is why the strategy is expensive.\n• The rival may not exit: it can mothball capacity and re-enter, or be bought by a better-capitalised owner, so the assets stay in the market even if the firm does not.\n• Legal risk: penalties of up to 10% of global group turnover, director disqualification for up to 15 years, and follow-on damages actions. The expected legal cost can exceed any recoupment.\n• It is extremely hard to distinguish from legitimate aggressive competition, which is why the law uses a cost-based test (below AVC presumed predatory) — and that ambiguity cuts both ways, since it gives a firm some cover but also creates legal uncertainty.\n• Game theory: if the rival knows the predator will eventually have to raise price to recoup, the threat is not credible in a finite game, so a rational rival holds on. Credibility requires commitment — excess capacity, or a reputation built across many markets.\n• Alternatives that achieve similar ends more cheaply and legally: LIMIT PRICING (setting price just below the entrant\'s average cost so entry is unprofitable, while still earning supernormal profit), excess capacity as a deterrent threat, and product proliferation.\n• Judgement: rational only under narrow conditions — deep pockets, a financially weak rival, high sunk costs preventing re-entry, and a low probability of enforcement. Because those conditions rarely coincide, limit pricing is usually the superior strategy: it deters entry without ever incurring losses and without breaking the law.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) / L2 (5–8) / L1 (1–4). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram and the information below, the extent to which economies of scale explain the dominance of the largest UK supermarkets.

Extract 1 (written for this practice question, using published Kantar market shares)
Grocery market shares in Great Britain, 2026: Tesco 27.3%, Sainsbury\'s 15.1%, Asda 13.7%,
Aldi 10.2%, Morrisons 9.2%. The four largest chains together held around 65% of the market,
down from around 73% in 2017. Aldi overtook Morrisons to become the fourth-largest grocer.
Industry estimates suggest a national chain buys branded goods at 8-12% below the price paid by a
regional chain, spreads a national advertising campaign over roughly twenty times the turnover,
and borrows at around 1.5 percentage points below the rate available to a small independent.`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: LRAC with output on the horizontal axis; the falling section labelled economies of scale; the minimum efficient scale marked; the position of a large chain at or beyond MES and a small independent well to the left of it, with the vertical distance between them labelled as the unit-cost disadvantage. Strong answers add total market demand as a vertical line to show how many firms of MES size the market can support.\n• The scale explanation, using the extract: purchasing economies of 8-12% on branded goods are decisive in a business where cost of goods sold is the overwhelming majority of total cost; marketing economies spread a fixed campaign cost over twenty times the turnover; financial economies of 1.5 percentage points lower the cost of capital for store investment. Technical and distribution economies in warehousing and logistics reinforce this.\n• Consequence: a firm below MES faces a permanent unit-cost disadvantage it cannot manage away, so it must either grow, occupy a niche, or exit. That is a barrier to entry and it explains persistence of the leaders\' position — Tesco at 27.3% has been the largest grocer for decades.\nEvaluation:\n• The extract\'s own evidence cuts against the scale explanation. The big four\'s share FELL from about 73% to about 65% while Aldi rose to 10.2% and overtook Morrisons. If scale alone determined outcomes, the largest firms would have gained share, not lost it.\n• Aldi\'s advantage is a different mechanism: a limited assortment of around 2 000 lines against 25 000 in a large supermarket produces very high volume per line, faster stock turn, simpler stores and lower labour cost per pound of sales. That is efficiency from SIMPLICITY, not from absolute size, and it shows a small-format operator can sit at the bottom of a DIFFERENT cost curve.\n• Other explanations for dominance that the scale story omits: planning restrictions on large-format stores create a site-based barrier to entry that protects incumbents; brand loyalty and habit; loyalty-card DATA, which is a modern source of advantage with increasing returns unrelated to physical scale; and location, since a grocery market is local and a convenient site is a genuine barrier.\n• Diseconomies and X-inefficiency: very large chains carry heavy central overheads and slow decision-making, which is part of why the discounters could undercut them.\n• Do consumers benefit? Purchasing economies of 8-12% may reflect BUYER POWER over suppliers rather than genuine efficiency — a transfer from farmers and manufacturers rather than a real resource saving, which is why the groceries supply code and an adjudicator exist. A cost advantage from bargaining power is not an economy of scale in the technical sense.\n• Judgement: economies of scale explain why a minimum size is necessary to compete and why the market is concentrated rather than fragmented; they do not explain WHICH firms dominate or why the ranking changes. The share data in the extract is decisive evidence that scale is a necessary but not sufficient condition, and that format, location, data and price positioning determine outcomes among firms that have all reached MES.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — accurate LRAC diagram driving the argument, extract data used on both sides, the necessary-but-not-sufficient judgement reached. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, economies listed with limited evaluation. L1 (1–5) — no or incorrect diagram, descriptive. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram and the information below, the extent to which a firm\'s profitability depends on its costs rather than its revenue.

Extract 1 (written for this practice question)
Two firms in the same industry, most recent financial year
  Firm P: output 240 000 units ; average total cost £18.40 ; average revenue £21.10
  Firm Q: output 1 100 000 units ; average total cost £16.90 ; average revenue £17.20
Firm P sells a differentiated premium product; Firm Q competes on price in a crowded segment.
Both firms face the same input prices and the same wage rates.`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Quantify first. Firm P: profit per unit £21.10 - £18.40 = £2.70, total profit £648 000, margin 12.8%. Firm Q: profit per unit £17.20 - £16.90 = £0.30, total profit £330 000, margin 1.7%. Firm Q has the LOWER average cost and by far the lower profit — which is the heart of the question.\n• Diagram required: cost and revenue curves for a firm with a downward-sloping AR, MR below it, MC and ATC, with profit shown as (AR - ATC) x Q. Drawing both firms — one with a steep (inelastic) AR reflecting differentiation and a wide profit rectangle, one with a flat (elastic) AR and a thin one — makes the argument visible and is what separates the top band.\n• The analytical point: profit = (AR - ATC) x Q, so it depends on BOTH blades of the scissors. Firm Q has won on cost and lost on revenue, because its product is undifferentiated, its demand curve is highly elastic, and price competition has driven AR down to within 2% of ATC. Firm P\'s differentiation gives it a less elastic demand curve and therefore pricing power.\n• Cost matters, and the mechanisms are worth setting out: economies of scale, productivity, factor prices and X-efficiency all shift ATC, and a lower ATC widens the margin at any given price. Firm Q\'s £16.90 against Firm P\'s £18.40 is a genuine 8.2% cost advantage, presumably from its 4.6-times larger output.\nEvaluation:\n• Revenue is doing more work here, and the reason is MARKET STRUCTURE rather than cost management. The elasticity of the demand curve a firm faces determines how much of any cost advantage it can retain. In a competitive segment, cost savings are competed away into price; in a differentiated one, they are retained as profit. This is why the answer to the question depends on the market the firm is in, not on a general rule.\n• But cost is the more CONTROLLABLE variable in the short run. A firm can cut costs by its own decisions; raising AR requires persuading customers, which depends on rivals, tastes and brand equity built over years.\n• The comparison may be unsafe. One year\'s data cannot distinguish a structural advantage from a temporary one, and Firm P\'s premium position may require large marketing spend already inside its £18.40 — in which case its cost and revenue positions are not independent at all. Differentiation is BOUGHT with cost.\n• Scale and cost interact with volume: Firm Q makes £330 000 on a thin margin precisely because volume is large, so the low margin is not the whole story — a 1.7% margin on high turnover can still be a sound business, and return on capital, not margin, is the right measure.\n• Long-run contestability: Firm P\'s 12.8% margin will attract entry unless it is protected by a genuine barrier (brand, patent, distribution), in which case its supernormal profit is competed away and the cost position becomes decisive again.\n• Risk: Firm Q operating at a 1.7% margin has almost no buffer against an input price shock, whereas Firm P could absorb a substantial cost rise. Cost position determines resilience even where it does not determine current profit.\n• Judgement: profitability depends on the GAP between AR and ATC, so neither blade dominates in general; on this evidence revenue position is decisive, because the cost advantage of the larger firm has been entirely competed away by the elasticity of the demand it faces. A supported conclusion should argue that cost determines the floor and competitive position determines the ceiling, and that in the long run only a barrier to entry allows a firm to keep either advantage.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — margins computed from the extract, diagram used to show the elasticity contrast, judgement supported. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, data described rather than analysed. L1 (1–5) — no or incorrect diagram, assertion. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram and the information below, the extent to which a rise in fixed costs affects a profit-maximising firm\'s output.

Extract 1 (written for this practice question)
A manufacturer faces a new annual regulatory compliance charge.
  Before: fixed costs £900 000 ; variable cost £22 per unit ; output 150 000 units ; price £31
  New compliance charge: £600 000 a year, payable regardless of output
The firm also expects an increase in the cost of a key imported component from £6 to £7 per unit
in the following year.`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: MC, ATC (or AVC) and AR/MR curves; the rise in fixed costs shifting ATC UP while MC is UNCHANGED, so the MC = MR intersection and therefore output and price are unchanged; the profit rectangle shrinking. A second diagram, or the same one extended, should show the variable cost rise shifting MC UP, moving the MC = MR intersection LEFT and raising price. The contrast between the two is the whole question.\n• The core principle: a profit-maximising firm chooses output where MC = MR. A fixed cost, by definition, does not vary with output, so it does not enter marginal cost at all. Output and price are therefore UNCHANGED by the £600 000 charge; only the level of profit falls.\n• Quantify from the extract. Before: total revenue 150 000 x £31 = £4 650 000; total cost £900 000 + (150 000 x £22) = £4 200 000; profit £450 000. After the charge: total cost £4 800 000, so the firm makes a LOSS of £150 000 at the same output. ATC rises from £28.00 to £32.00 per unit while price stays at £31.\n• The variable cost rise is different: £1 per unit raises MC by £1, shifts the MC curve up, and the new MC = MR point is at a lower output and higher price. This is the contrast that demonstrates the principle.\nEvaluation:\n• In the SHORT RUN the firm should continue producing despite the loss, because price (£31) comfortably exceeds average variable cost (£22), so each unit contributes £9 towards fixed costs. Shutting down would mean losing the whole £1 500 000 of fixed costs instead of £150 000.\n• In the LONG RUN all costs are variable and the firm must cover ATC of £32 or exit. So a rise in fixed costs DOES affect output eventually — through the EXIT decision rather than through the marginal decision. Industry supply falls as firms leave, raising price for survivors. This long-run/short-run distinction is what separates Level 3 from Level 2.\n• If the compliance charge applies to all firms, the market-level effect is different from the firm-level one: entry is deterred, some firms exit, market supply shifts left and price rises. So the ceteris paribus firm-level answer of \'no change\' can be wrong at the industry level.\n• If the charge is a barrier to entry, it raises concentration and may increase surviving firms\' market power — a perverse consequence of regulation.\n• Behaviour may depart from the model: a firm may raise price to \'cover\' the new overhead using cost-plus pricing, which is not profit-maximising but is what many firms actually do; and managers under a loss may cut output to reduce apparent exposure.\n• The distinction can blur over time: many costs described as fixed are avoidable over a long enough horizon (leases end, capacity can be mothballed), so a permanently higher fixed cost eventually becomes a variable one at the plant level.\n• Judgement: in the short run a rise in fixed costs has NO effect on the profit-maximising output or price and reduces profit pound for pound; over a longer horizon it affects output through exit and entry deterrence at the industry level. The extract\'s component price rise shows the contrasting case where marginal cost changes and output does move.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — correct diagram showing ATC shifting with MC unchanged, figures computed, short-run/long-run distinction reached. L2 (6–10) — diagram present but the fixed/variable distinction only asserted. L1 (1–5) — states that higher costs cut output, no or incorrect diagram. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram and the information below, the extent to which the shape of the long-run average cost curve determines the number of firms in a market.

Extract 1 (written for this practice question)
Estimated minimum efficient scale as a share of total UK market demand
  Water distribution network:      MES equals roughly 100% of a regional market
  Passenger aircraft manufacture:  MES equals roughly 30% of world demand
  Grocery retailing:               MES equals roughly 3-5% of the national market
  Hairdressing:                    MES equals less than 0.01% of the national market
Number of significant firms in each: water (one per region), large passenger aircraft (two),
grocery (around eight national chains), hairdressing (tens of thousands of businesses).`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: LRAC with minimum efficient scale marked, and a vertical line at total market demand. Where MES is close to market demand, only one firm can operate at minimum cost — natural monopoly. Where MES is a small fraction of demand, many firms can. Ideally draw two versions, one with MES to the right of most of the demand line (natural monopoly, LRAC still falling at the market quantity) and one with MES far to the left (fragmented market).\n• The mechanism, applied to the extract: a firm below MES has a unit-cost disadvantage it cannot manage away, so it is undercut and driven out. The number of firms the market can sustain is therefore roughly market demand divided by MES — and the extract\'s four cases fit that prediction closely: 100% gives one firm, 30% gives two or three, 3-5% gives around eight, and a negligible MES gives tens of thousands.\n• Natural monopoly is the limiting case: LRAC falls across the entire range of demand, so a single firm is the efficient structure and duplication of the network would raise total cost. Water distribution is the textbook example, which is why it is regulated by Ofwat rather than opened to competition.\nEvaluation:\n• The relationship is a tendency, not a determinant, and the extract\'s own numbers show why. Grocery retailing with MES at 3-5% could in principle support twenty to thirty national chains; it supports around eight. Something other than cost is limiting entry — planning restrictions on large-format sites, brand loyalty, loyalty-card data, and the sunk cost of building a national distribution network.\n• Other barriers to entry are frequently more binding than MES: patents and intellectual property, legal licences and franchises, control of an essential input or distribution channel, and network effects. A market can be highly concentrated with a small MES if any of these is present.\n• The geographic definition of the market matters enormously. Hairdressing has a tiny MES but each business serves a LOCAL market, so the relevant structure is monopolistic competition within a neighbourhood, not a national market of tens of thousands of rivals. Defining the market wrongly makes the MES calculation meaningless.\n• Product differentiation allows firms below MES to survive: a firm with a distinctive product faces a less elastic demand curve and can charge a premium that covers a higher unit cost. This is precisely how small craft producers coexist with mass-market ones.\n• MES is not fixed. Technology changes it in both directions — cloud services and third-party logistics have lowered it for entry into narrow niches, while automation and data-driven scale economies have raised it elsewhere — so a structure predicted from today\'s MES may not persist.\n• Contestability matters more than the number of firms for the OUTCOME: two aircraft manufacturers may behave competitively if entry is credible, while eight grocers may not if sunk costs are high. The extract counts firms, which is not the same as measuring competition.\n• Regulation and competition policy intervene directly in the number of firms: a merger that would take the market to the MES-implied number can still be blocked, and the CMA cleared the Vodafone-Three merger on 5 December 2024 only with a binding £11 billion investment commitment and time-limited price caps.\n• Judgement: the shape of the LRAC curve sets an upper bound on the number of firms that can operate at minimum cost, and the extract shows the prediction working well across a wide range. It is a powerful first approximation and a poor complete explanation, because differentiation, non-cost barriers, market definition and regulation all cause actual numbers to depart from it — usually in the direction of FEWER firms than cost alone would allow.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — accurate LRAC-against-demand diagram, extract cases used quantitatively, limitations developed, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, cases described rather than analysed. L1 (1–5) — no or incorrect diagram. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether profit maximisation is the most appropriate objective for a firm.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• State the rule and its logic: profit is maximised where MC = MR, because to the left of that output each extra unit adds more to revenue than to cost, and to the right it adds more to cost than to revenue. Diagram: AR, MR, MC and ATC with the profit rectangle at Q*.\n• The case FOR profit maximisation:\n  - It is what the OWNERS want. Shareholders supply the capital and bear the residual risk, so maximising the present value of profit is maximising the value of what they own; any other objective is managers spending other people\'s money.\n  - Profit funds investment and dynamic efficiency. Retained earnings are the cheapest source of finance, and supernormal profit is what pays for research and development.\n  - Signalling and allocation: profit signals where resources are most valued, attracting entry into high-profit industries and driving exit from loss-making ones. This is the mechanism by which the price system allocates capital, so a firm that ignores profit is jamming the signal.\n  - Survival: in a competitive market a firm that does not approximate profit maximisation is undercut and driven out, so the objective is imposed by selection whether or not it is chosen.\n  - The market for corporate control enforces it: underperformance depresses the share price and invites takeover.\n• Alternative objectives and their rationales:\n  - REVENUE MAXIMISATION (MR = 0): higher output, lower price, used to build share, exploit scale economies or deter entry — and frequently the result of bonuses tied to turnover rather than a strategy chosen by owners.\n  - SALES VOLUME MAXIMISATION (AR = ATC, normal profit only).\n  - GROWTH: moving down the LRAC curve, diversifying risk, and raising managerial salary and status.\n  - SATISFICING under bounded rationality: managers lack the information to locate MC = MR and must balance shareholders, employees, customers and regulators, so they accept a satisfactory compromise (Simon).\n  - SURVIVAL, which dominates in a recession or a cash crisis.\n  - CORPORATE SOCIAL RESPONSIBILITY and stakeholder objectives: environmental standards, fair treatment of suppliers, employee welfare.\nEvaluation:\n• The PRINCIPAL-AGENT problem means the question of what a firm SHOULD do and what it DOES are different. Divorce of ownership from control, plus asymmetric information, allows managers to pursue their own objectives. Incentive alignment through share options, institutional monitoring and the takeover threat narrow but do not close the gap.\n• SHORT RUN against LONG RUN is the most important distinction available. Much apparent departure from profit maximisation — low introductory prices, heavy R&D, generous employment practices, CSR spending — is long-run profit maximisation in disguise, because it builds brand, loyalty, reputation and recruitment advantage. If the time horizon is long enough, the alternatives collapse into the same objective, and answers that treat CSR as inherently anti-profit miss this.\n• INFORMATION: a firm cannot observe its MR and MC curves. In practice it uses cost-plus pricing, price experimentation and rules of thumb, so exact maximisation is unattainable — which is an argument for satisficing as a DESCRIPTION rather than against maximisation as an objective.\n• CONSUMER AND SOCIAL CONSEQUENCES: a profit-maximising firm with market power restricts output below the allocatively efficient level and prices above marginal cost, creating deadweight loss. It also has no incentive to account for externalities. So what is appropriate for the firm is not automatically appropriate for society — which is the justification for competition policy and for environmental regulation, not an argument that the firm should voluntarily do otherwise.\n• MARKET STRUCTURE conditions the answer: in perfect competition profit maximisation is forced and leads to allocative and productive efficiency; in monopoly it produces welfare loss; in oligopoly interdependence means the profit-maximising strategy depends on rivals\' responses, so the simple rule may not even be well defined.\n• OWNERSHIP FORM matters: a public limited company answers to shareholders; a mutual, a cooperative, a charity or a state-owned enterprise has different owners with different objectives, and for them profit maximisation is not the appropriate objective at all.\n• Judgement: the strongest conclusions argue that LONG-RUN profit maximisation is the appropriate objective for a privately owned firm in a competitive market, because it is what owners want, what survival requires and what allocates capital efficiently; that most apparent alternatives are either agents pursuing their own interests or long-run profit maximisation misdescribed; and that the objective becomes inappropriate where the firm has market power or generates externalities — in which case the remedy is external constraint through competition policy and regulation rather than asking the firm to adopt a different objective.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) — sustained, balanced, well-evidenced argument with analysis and evaluation integrated and a conclusion that follows from it. L4 (16–20) — well developed and mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) — some analysis and evaluation, uneven balance. L2 (6–10) — largely descriptive. L1 (1–5) — fragmentary. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram, whether large firms are always more efficient than small firms.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: the LRAC curve with economies of scale, minimum efficient scale and diseconomies of scale labelled; a small firm positioned to the left of MES with a unit-cost disadvantage, a large firm at MES, and a very large firm beyond MES with rising unit cost. Strong answers add a second LRAC curve for a firm using a different technology or format, to show that a small firm can sit on a lower curve entirely.\n• Unpack \'efficiency\' first — this is what separates the top band, because the four concepts give different answers:\n  - PRODUCTIVE efficiency: producing at minimum average cost. Large firms have the advantage up to MES.\n  - ALLOCATIVE efficiency: P = MC. Large firms with market power price ABOVE MC, so they are allocatively INefficient — a point that reverses the presumption.\n  - DYNAMIC efficiency: innovation over time. Ambiguous, and both sides are arguable.\n  - X-EFFICIENCY: operating at the lowest attainable cost given the technology. Large, insulated firms are prone to X-inefficiency.\n• The case that large firms are more efficient:\n  - Internal economies of scale: technical (indivisible capital, the container principle), purchasing, managerial, financial, marketing and risk-bearing. Illustrative magnitudes: a national grocery chain buys branded goods well below a regional chain\'s price and borrows more cheaply.\n  - Dynamic efficiency: R&D requires scale and deep pockets. Pharmaceutical development, aircraft design and semiconductor fabrication are simply not available to small firms, and Schumpeter\'s argument is that the prospect of supernormal profit is what funds innovation.\n  - Where MES is large relative to demand, a large firm is the ONLY efficient structure — natural monopoly in water distribution and rail infrastructure, where duplication would raise total cost.\nEvaluation — the case that they are not:\n  - DISECONOMIES OF SCALE beyond MES: control (too many management layers), communication (information distorted and slowed, incompatible systems) and motivation (workers remote from the firm\'s objectives, so engagement and retention fall). Unit costs rise.\n  - X-INEFFICIENCY: without competitive pressure or the threat of exit, costs drift above the minimum attainable. A monopoly can be productively efficient in theory and wasteful in practice.\n  - ALLOCATIVE INEFFICIENCY is the decisive reversal: a profit-maximising large firm with market power produces where MC = MR, restricting output below the allocatively efficient level and creating deadweight loss. On the criterion society cares most about, size is a disadvantage.\n  - SMALL FIRMS have real advantages: flexibility and speed of decision, closeness to customers, personal service, ability to serve niches profitably, lower overheads, and stronger owner incentives because the owner IS the manager, which eliminates the principal-agent problem.\n  - DIFFERENT COST CURVES, not different positions on one curve: Aldi\'s limited-assortment format achieves low unit costs through simplicity rather than absolute size, and a craft producer selling at a premium faces a less elastic demand curve so it need not match a mass producer\'s costs at all.\n  - MARKET SIZE AND DEFINITION: hairdressing, restaurants and personal services have a tiny MES and serve LOCAL markets, so there is no efficiency case for scale.\n  - Apparent scale advantages may be BUYER POWER rather than efficiency — a transfer from suppliers rather than a real resource saving, which is a distributional effect and not an efficiency gain at all.\n  - DYNAMIC EFFICIENCY cuts both ways: disruptive innovation frequently comes from small entrants with no incumbent revenue to protect, while large firms face the innovator\'s dilemma and may suppress innovations that cannibalise existing products.\n• Judgement: the strongest conclusions reject the word \'always\' immediately, then argue that the answer depends on THREE things — which efficiency concept is being used, where the firm sits relative to its minimum efficient scale, and how competitive the market is. Large firms are generally more PRODUCTIVELY efficient up to MES and generally LESS allocatively efficient once they have market power; small firms can be equally efficient on a different cost curve or in a different market segment; and the best outcome for society is usually a market structure with enough scale to capture the economies and enough competition to force them to be passed on — which is exactly what competition policy tries to engineer.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) / L4 (16–20) / L3 (11–15) / L2 (6–10) / L1 (1–5); an accurate, integrated diagram is required for Level 4 and above. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether the principal-agent problem is the most significant problem facing large companies.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Define it precisely: the principal (shareholder) delegates decisions to an agent (manager) whose objectives differ and whose actions the principal cannot observe. ASYMMETRIC INFORMATION is what makes it a problem — if owners could see everything managers did, a contract would solve it.\n• The costs it imposes:\n  - Objectives substituted: revenue maximisation, growth and empire-building raise managerial pay, status and security while reducing shareholder value. Remuneration tied to turnover rather than profit makes this rational for the manager.\n  - X-inefficiency and cost drift: no residual claimant insists on minimum cost.\n  - Distorted risk-taking in both directions: undiversified managerial human capital causes excessive caution, while share options reward volatility and can cause excessive risk.\n  - Short-termism: managers paid on annual results cut R&D and maintenance to hit a target, damaging long-run value.\n  - Free-riding among dispersed shareholders means nobody bears the cost of monitoring — a collective action problem that compounds the information asymmetry.\n• Evidence and mechanism: a company in which only a small share of executive reward is tied to profit, and where institutional holders turn over their stakes within months, has weak monitoring and weak alignment by construction.\nEvaluation — mitigations that reduce its significance:\n• The MARKET FOR CORPORATE CONTROL is the most powerful discipline: persistent underperformance depresses the share price and invites a takeover in which incumbent managers lose their jobs. It works without any shareholder having to monitor anything.\n• Incentive alignment: share options and long-term incentive plans tie reward to the share price, which is the discounted present value of expected future profits — so a manager sacrificing current profit for genuine long-run value is rewarded, which is the correct outcome.\n• Governance: independent non-executive directors, remuneration and audit committees, the UK Corporate Governance Code, and large institutional holders who DO have the incentive to engage.\n• PRODUCT MARKET COMPETITION: a firm that lets costs drift in a competitive market is driven out, so the problem is severe only where the firm has market power. This means the principal-agent problem and the market-power problem are not independent.\nEvaluation — competing problems that may be more significant:\n• MARKET POWER AND REGULATORY RISK: for a dominant firm, the binding constraint is competition authorities. Fines can reach 10% of global group turnover, and the CMA cleared the Vodafone-Three merger on 5 December 2024 only against a binding £11 billion investment commitment and time-limited price caps; the CMA designated Apple and Google as having strategic market status in mobile platforms on 22 October 2025, opening the way to binding conduct requirements. For firms of this size, regulation shapes strategy more than internal governance does.\n• DISECONOMIES OF SCALE and organisational complexity: control, communication and motivation problems raise unit costs directly, and are arguably the same phenomenon as the principal-agent problem replicated at every layer of a hierarchy — which is a strong argument that the two cannot be separated.\n• DYNAMIC COMPETITION AND DISRUPTION: the greatest threat to a large incumbent is usually a technological shift that makes its assets obsolete. The innovator\'s dilemma — that a profitable incumbent rationally declines to cannibalise its own product — destroys more value than managerial slack ever does.\n• MACROECONOMIC AND EXTERNAL SHOCKS: demand shocks, input price shocks, exchange rate movements, supply chain disruption and geopolitical risk are outside the firm\'s control and can be existential.\n• STAKEHOLDER AND REPUTATIONAL RISK: consumer boycotts, employee retention, environmental liabilities and the cost of capital consequences of poor ESG performance.\n• The framing question: \'most significant\' for WHOM and over what horizon? For shareholders in a firm with dispersed ownership and weak competition, the principal-agent problem is first order. For a firm in a competitive, fast-changing market, competitive and technological threats dominate, because the takeover mechanism and competition already discipline management.\n• Judgement: the principal-agent problem is the most significant INTERNAL problem and the one that is intrinsic to the corporate form, because separation of ownership from control is the defining feature of a public limited company. It is not the most significant problem overall, because it is substantially mitigated by the takeover threat, incentive design and product market competition, while disruption, regulation and macroeconomic shocks are not mitigable in the same way. The strongest answers also note that separation of ownership and control is a deliberate trade — it buys scale, specialisation and risk diversification — so the agency cost is the price of a much larger benefit rather than a defect to be eliminated.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) / L4 (16–20) / L3 (11–15) / L2 (6–10) / L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether rises in the National Living Wage are damaging for UK firms.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The facts to anchor the answer: the National Living Wage for workers aged 21 and over was £11.44 from April 2024, £12.21 from April 2025 and £12.71 from April 2026 — a cash increase of about 4.1% in the most recent uprating. The Low Pay Commission recommends the rate each year against a target expressed as a share of median hourly earnings. Employer National Insurance and pension auto-enrolment mean total employment cost exceeds the headline rate.\n• The case that rises are damaging:\n  - COST: in the competitive labour market model, a wage floor above equilibrium moves firms up and left along the labour demand (MRP) curve, so they demand less labour. Diagram: Ls > Ld at the floor, with the excess supply labelled.\n  - The effect on AVERAGE COST is the wage rise multiplied by labour\'s share of total cost, so it is severe precisely in labour-intensive sectors — hospitality, retail, social care, cleaning, agriculture — where labour can exceed half of total cost.\n  - MARGIN COMPRESSION: these are typically low-margin sectors, so a 4% wage bill rise can wipe out a large share of profit, especially where the firm cannot pass costs on. Where fees are set by a public body, as in much of social care, pass-through is impossible and the rise converts directly into losses.\n  - DIFFERENTIAL COMPRESSION: raising the floor forces increases for supervisors and skilled staff to preserve differentials, so the true cost exceeds the direct wage bill.\n  - SMALL FIRMS are hit hardest: they lack the scale to automate, weaker bargaining power with suppliers, and less access to capital.\n  - ADJUSTMENT: firms respond by cutting hours, freezing recruitment, reducing overtime, cutting training, substituting capital (self-service kiosks, automated checkouts) or reducing non-wage benefits — so the burden falls partly on workers in forms the headline rate conceals.\nEvaluation — the case that they are not damaging, or are less so than predicted:\n• MONOPSONY: where employers have wage-setting power — a dominant local employer, or workers with high search costs — the firm restricts hiring below the competitive level to hold the wage down. A minimum wage set between the monopsony wage and the competitive wage raises BOTH pay and employment. This is the standard explanation for why UK employment effects have been much smaller than early predictions, and a diagram showing MCL above the labour supply curve is what separates the top band.\n• EFFICIENCY WAGES: higher pay reduces quit rates, absenteeism and recruitment costs and raises effort and morale. In sectors with very high turnover, such as care and hospitality, the saving on recruitment and induction can offset a large share of the direct cost.\n• DEMAND-SIDE OFFSET: low-paid workers have a high marginal propensity to consume, so the money is spent largely in the same sectors that paid it — a partial circular flow offset that a single-firm analysis misses.\n• REAL VERSUS NOMINAL is the decisive evaluative point: a 4.1% cash rise is only a real cost increase if it exceeds general inflation and productivity growth. An uprating that tracks prices and productivity imposes no new real burden at all, and the Low Pay Commission\'s target is framed relative to median earnings for exactly this reason.\n• LEVEL PLAYING FIELD: the floor applies to all competitors, so no firm is disadvantaged relative to domestic rivals, and pass-through into prices is easier when every competitor faces the same increase. The exposure is to IMPORT competition, not to domestic rivals.\n• PRODUCTIVITY: a binding floor can force out the least productive firms and push survivors to invest in capital and training, raising aggregate productivity. This is uncomfortable — it means some firm failures are the mechanism working, not evidence of damage.\n• FISCAL AND SOCIAL OFFSET: higher wages reduce in-work benefit spending and raise tax receipts, so the public finances gain what firms lose, which matters for the social judgement even if not for the individual firm.\n• Judgement: the strongest conclusions argue that the answer depends on (i) the REAL size of the uprating relative to productivity growth, (ii) labour\'s share of costs in the sector, (iii) the degree of monopsony power, and (iv) whether the firm can pass costs on — and that it is therefore concentrated damage in specific sectors rather than general damage to UK firms. A conclusion that identifies social care, where fees are administratively set and pass-through is blocked, as the genuinely damaged case, while noting that most of the economy absorbs the rise through prices, hours and productivity, reaches Level 5.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) / L4 (16–20) / L3 (11–15) / L2 (6–10) / L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram, whether a firm making a loss should always shut down.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: MC, ATC and AVC with MC cutting both at their minima; a price line between minimum AVC and minimum ATC, showing a loss rectangle (ATC - P) x Q but with P above AVC so the firm produces; the SHUT-DOWN POINT marked at the minimum of AVC. Strong answers also show a price below minimum AVC and identify that as the case where shutting down minimises the loss.\n• The correct short-run rule: continue producing if P is at or above AVC; shut down if P is below AVC. The reasoning is that fixed costs are unavoidable in the short run, so they are irrelevant to the decision. Every unit sold at a price above its variable cost makes a positive CONTRIBUTION towards fixed costs, so the firm loses less by producing than by closing and losing the whole of its fixed costs.\n• Worked logic worth stating: if a firm has fixed costs of £1.5m, sells at £31 with AVC of £22 and output of 150 000, it makes a loss of £150 000 by producing and would lose £1 500 000 by shutting down — so producing at a loss is clearly correct.\n• The long-run rule is different: all costs become variable, so the firm must cover ATC. If price is persistently below LRAC, the firm should exit, and in a competitive industry exit shifts market supply left and raises price for those who remain until only normal profit is earned.\nEvaluation — reasons to continue even below AVC:\n• EXPECTED RECOVERY: the rule compares today\'s price with today\'s AVC, but the decision is about the future. If the loss is cyclical and demand is expected to recover, closing and reopening costs more than trading through — mothballing has its own cost.\n• RE-ENTRY AND SUNK COSTS: if re-entering requires re-incurring large sunk costs (licences, brand, specialist plant), the OPTION VALUE of staying open can exceed the losses. Contestable market theory is the mirror image of this point — high sunk costs deter exit as well as entry.\n• SKILLED LABOUR: firms hoard labour through a downturn because recruiting and retraining is expensive and the skills may not be available when demand returns.\n• CONTRACTS AND REPUTATION: supply contracts with penalty clauses, obligations to customers, and the reputational cost of failing to deliver can all exceed short-run losses.\n• COMPLEMENTARITY AND LOSS LEADERS: a loss-making product may drive profitable sales elsewhere — a retailer\'s loss-leading staples, a razor sold below cost to sell blades. The decision must be taken across the whole portfolio, not product by product.\n• STRATEGIC LOSSES: an entrant building share, or an incumbent defending a market, may accept planned losses as an investment. Below-cost pricing intended to eliminate a rival is PREDATORY PRICING and is illegal as an abuse of dominance, so the boundary matters.\n• EXIT LIABILITIES: redundancy costs, lease break penalties, decommissioning obligations. Where exit costs are large, continuing to trade at a loss can be the cheaper option — and this is a genuine future cost, not a sunk-cost fallacy.\nEvaluation — reasons to shut down sooner than the rule implies:\n• CASH FLOW, not profit, kills firms. A business covering AVC on paper can still run out of cash if customers pay late or credit is withdrawn, and insolvency forces closure regardless of the economics.\n• OPPORTUNITY COST OF CAPITAL: assets tied up in a loss-making operation could earn a return elsewhere. The correct comparison is against the best alternative use, not against zero.\n• The SUNK COST FALLACY runs the other way: managers stay too long because of what has already been invested, and loss aversion plus the personal cost to the executive who authorised the investment make this a predictable principal-agent failure.\n• INFORMATION: firms do not observe AVC cleanly; overhead allocation is a matter of accounting convention, so the rule is harder to apply than it looks.\n• Judgement: the word \'always\' makes the proposition false — the short-run rule says a loss-making firm should continue whenever price covers average variable cost, and the £150 000 against £1 500 000 comparison shows why. The substantive conclusion is that the decision depends on the TIME HORIZON (short run: compare with AVC; long run: compare with ATC), on the EXPECTED PATH of prices, and on the costs of exit and re-entry — and that the most common real-world error is not shutting down too early but staying too long because of sunk costs.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) / L4 (16–20) / L3 (11–15) / L2 (6–10) / L1 (1–5); an accurate, integrated diagram showing the shut-down point is required for Level 4 and above. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether business objectives other than profit maximisation are in the interests of consumers.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Set up the comparison with a diagram: AR, MR, MC and ATC, with the profit-maximising output Qp (MC = MR) and the revenue-maximising output Qr (MR = 0) to its right at a lower price, and the sales-volume-maximising output where AR = ATC further right still. Consumers face a lower price and larger quantity under either alternative, which is the starting point.\n• The case that alternatives BENEFIT consumers:\n  - REVENUE AND SALES MAXIMISATION deliver lower prices and higher output, which raises consumer surplus directly and moves output closer to the allocatively efficient level where P = MC. Under profit maximisation with market power, output is restricted and price exceeds MC, creating deadweight loss; any objective that pushes output right reduces it.\n  - GROWTH objectives can deliver economies of scale, and where competition forces pass-through, lower unit costs mean lower prices.\n  - CORPORATE SOCIAL RESPONSIBILITY addresses externalities the firm would otherwise ignore: lower emissions, better supply chain standards, fairer treatment of workers. A profit-maximising firm internalises none of these voluntarily, so CSR partially corrects a market failure without regulation.\n  - SATISFICING may mean the firm settles for a smaller margin and passes more to consumers, and stakeholder-oriented firms — mutuals, cooperatives, employee-owned businesses — have no shareholders demanding maximum extraction, so surpluses can be returned as lower prices or better service.\n  - SURVIVAL objectives in a downturn mean aggressive discounting, which benefits consumers in the short run.\nEvaluation — the case that they do NOT benefit consumers:\n• THE GAINS MAY BE TEMPORARY OR PREDATORY: prices below the profit-maximising level are frequently an investment in market share. Once rivals exit, price rises and consumers are worse off than before — the recoupment phase. The line between aggressive competition and illegal predatory pricing is exactly where the CMA intervenes, and below-AVC pricing by a dominant firm is an abuse of dominance.\n• MANAGERIAL OBJECTIVES ARE NOT CONSUMER OBJECTIVES: revenue maximisation usually reflects bonuses tied to turnover, not benevolence. The principal-agent problem means resources are wasted on empire-building and prestige projects, raising costs that consumers ultimately pay, and X-inefficiency in a firm not disciplined by profit means costs drift above the attainable minimum.\n• DYNAMIC EFFICIENCY: supernormal profit funds research and development. A firm that maximises sales at the expense of profit has less to invest, so consumers may gain lower prices today and lose better products tomorrow — the Schumpeterian argument, and the most serious objection.\n• CSR CAN BE COSMETIC OR COSTLY: greenwashing delivers no real benefit; genuine CSR raises costs, which are passed on in higher prices, so consumers pay for the externality correction. Whether that is in their interests depends on whether they value the externality reduction more than the price rise — and they were never asked.\n• SURVIVAL OF INEFFICIENT FIRMS: satisficing allows firms to persist that a profit-maximising market would have eliminated, so resources stay in low-value uses and consumers face higher costs economy-wide.\n• PROFIT MAXIMISATION ITSELF SERVES CONSUMERS in competitive markets: with many firms and free entry, profit maximisation drives price down to minimum ATC and P = MC in long-run equilibrium, delivering both allocative and productive efficiency. The problem is never profit maximisation as such — it is profit maximisation combined with MARKET POWER.\n• Which is the better remedy? If the harm comes from market power, the appropriate response is competition policy and regulation — the CMA designating Apple and Google as having strategic market status in mobile platforms on 22 October 2025, or clearing the Vodafone-Three merger on 5 December 2024 only with a binding £11 billion investment programme and time-limited price caps — rather than hoping firms adopt consumer-friendly objectives voluntarily. External constraint is enforceable; a voluntary objective is not.\n• SHORT RUN AGAINST LONG RUN: many apparent alternatives are long-run profit maximisation in disguise. A firm building brand and loyalty through low prices, good treatment of customers and CSR is maximising the present value of profit over a longer horizon — in which case the question dissolves, and the consumer gain is real but is a by-product of profit-seeking rather than an alternative to it.\n• Judgement: the strongest conclusions distinguish the SHORT-RUN price effect (alternatives clearly benefit consumers) from the LONG-RUN effect (ambiguous, because dynamic efficiency and the risk of recoupment cut the other way); note that whether consumers benefit depends far more on MARKET STRUCTURE than on the stated objective, since competition forces consumer-friendly behaviour from profit maximisers while market power permits exploitation whatever objective is professed; and conclude that regulation and competition policy are a more reliable route to consumer interest than reliance on firms choosing non-profit objectives.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) / L4 (16–20) / L3 (11–15) / L2 (6–10) / L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},
    ]
  },
  '1.4': {
    name: 'Market Structures',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'price taker\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `1 mark: a firm that has no influence over the market price and must accept the price set by the industry as a whole.\n1 mark: development — it arises because the firm is very small relative to the market and sells a homogeneous product, so it faces a perfectly elastic (horizontal) demand curve at the ruling price. Raising its price loses all its customers to identical rivals; cutting it is pointless because it can already sell its entire output at the market price. For a price taker AR = MR = price.`},

      { q: 'Identify two barriers to entry that can protect a monopoly.', marks: 2, command: 'Identify',
        markScheme: `1 mark each, maximum 2. Accept any two of:\n• Economies of scale and a high minimum efficient scale — an entrant must enter at large scale or face a unit-cost disadvantage.\n• High sunk costs — unrecoverable set-up spending on specialised plant, brand or research deters entry because exit would be costly.\n• Legal barriers — patents, copyright, statutory licences and franchises.\n• Control of an essential input or distribution channel (vertical integration).\n• Brand loyalty built by heavy advertising, which raises the cost an entrant must incur to be noticed.\n• Network effects — the product is more valuable the more people use it.\n• Strategic behaviour — limit pricing, excess capacity as a deterrent threat, product proliferation.\nA bare list of two valid barriers earns both marks; no explanation is required at this tariff.`},

      { q: `Refer to the stimulus material below.

Stimulus (published Kantar grocery market shares, Great Britain, 2026)
  Tesco 27.3% ; Sainsbury\'s 15.1% ; Asda 13.7% ; Aldi 10.2% ; Morrisons 9.2%

Calculate the three-firm concentration ratio for this market. You are advised to show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method: add the market shares of the three largest firms: 27.3 + 15.1 + 13.7 (1 mark for identifying the correct three firms and the correct method).\nCorrect answer: 56.1% (1 mark).\nAn answer of 66.3% adds the four largest (including Aldi) and is a four-firm ratio, scoring a maximum of 1 mark for method. Accept 56.1 without the percentage sign. Note that the four-firm ratio for the traditional \'big four\' (Tesco, Sainsbury\'s, Asda, Morrisons) is 65.3%.`},

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'allocative efficiency\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `1 mark: a situation in which resources are allocated so as to maximise the satisfaction of consumers\' wants — no reallocation could make someone better off without making someone else worse off.\n1 mark: development — it occurs where price equals marginal cost (P = MC), so the value consumers place on the last unit exactly equals the resource cost of producing it; equivalently, where the sum of consumer and producer surplus is maximised. Credit the contrast that a monopolist profit-maximises where MC = MR and prices above MC, so output is restricted below the allocatively efficient level and a deadweight loss results.`},

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'dynamic efficiency\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `1 mark: efficiency gains achieved OVER TIME through investment, innovation and technological progress, rather than at a single moment.\n1 mark: development — it is shown by an inward or downward shift of the long-run average cost curve, or by the development of new and better products. It requires supernormal profit to fund research and development, which is Schumpeter\'s argument that some monopoly power may be dynamically beneficial even though it is statically inefficient.\nContrast worth crediting: productive and allocative efficiency are STATIC concepts, assessed at a point in time on a given cost curve; dynamic efficiency is about moving the curve.`},

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'contestable market\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `1 mark: a market in which entry and exit are free or very cheap, so incumbent firms face the constant threat of new competition.\n1 mark: development — the key condition is the absence of SUNK COSTS, which makes hit-and-run entry possible: an entrant can come in, take supernormal profit and leave without loss. Because incumbents know this, they hold prices close to average cost and earn only normal profit even if there are very few firms. Contestability therefore means that the THREAT of competition, not the actual number of firms, determines the outcome.`},

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'X-inefficiency\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `1 mark: producing at a higher average cost than is technically necessary — operating ABOVE the lowest attainable cost curve rather than at a suboptimal point on it.\n1 mark: development — Leibenstein\'s concept. It arises where a firm is insulated from competitive pressure, typically a monopoly or a state-owned monopoly, so managers and workers face no threat of exit or takeover and costs drift upward through overstaffing, excessive perks, slack effort and prestige projects.\nCredit the distinction from productive inefficiency in the textbook sense: X-inefficiency means the firm is not even ON its cost curve, so the whole ATC curve sits higher than it need.`},

      { q: 'Explain why supernormal profits are competed away in the long run under perfect competition.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Up to 3 marks for a developed chain of reasoning:\n1 mark — there is freedom of entry and exit and perfect information, so the existence of supernormal profit is known to potential entrants and nothing prevents them from entering.\n1 mark — new firms enter, so industry supply shifts to the right and the market price falls. Each existing firm is a price taker, so its horizontal AR = MR line shifts DOWN, reducing the profit it earns at every output.\n1 mark — entry continues as long as any supernormal profit remains, so it stops only when price has fallen to the minimum of average cost, where AR = ATC and only normal profit is earned. At that point the firm produces where P = MC = MR = minimum ATC, so the industry is both allocatively and productively efficient.\nCredit the symmetric point that losses cause exit, shifting supply left and raising price back to minimum ATC.`},

      { q: 'Explain why firms in an oligopoly rely heavily on non-price competition.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Up to 3 marks:\n1 mark — oligopolistic firms are INTERDEPENDENT: each firm\'s best action depends on how rivals respond, so a price cut is likely to be matched, leaving market shares unchanged and every firm worse off. A price war destroys profit for all participants.\n1 mark — the kinked demand curve model formalises this: rivals match price cuts but not price rises, so demand is elastic above the current price and inelastic below it, and there is a discontinuity in MR that makes price rigid over a range of costs.\n1 mark — non-price competition is therefore safer and more durable: advertising, branding, product differentiation, quality, loyalty schemes, customer service, packaging and after-sales support all build brand loyalty, making the firm\'s demand curve LESS elastic, and they are harder and slower for rivals to imitate than a price cut.\nCredit the additional point that firms in oligopoly typically have the supernormal profits needed to fund large advertising and R&D budgets.`},

      { q: 'Explain why tacit collusion can be sustained in an oligopoly without any formal agreement.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `Up to 3 marks:\n1 mark — firms recognise their mutual interdependence and understand that competing on price makes all of them worse off, so each independently chooses the high-price strategy in the expectation that rivals will do the same. No communication is necessary, so no illegal agreement exists to be proved.\n1 mark — it is sustained by REPETITION: because the firms interact indefinitely, the threat of retaliation makes defection unprofitable. A firm that undercuts gains for one period and then faces a price war for many, so the discounted cost of defection exceeds the gain. This is how the prisoners\' dilemma outcome is escaped in a repeated game.\n1 mark — the conditions that make it easier: few firms, transparent and easily observed prices (so defection is detected quickly), homogeneous products, stable demand, similar cost structures, high barriers to entry, and a recognised price leader whose announcements others follow (barometric or dominant-firm price leadership).\nCredit the point that tacit collusion is extremely difficult for competition authorities to prosecute, because parallel pricing is also what competition can look like.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how a firm in perfect competition earns supernormal profit in the short run.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): TWO panels or a clearly labelled single firm diagram — the industry with D and S setting price P1, and the firm facing a HORIZONTAL AR = MR = D line at P1; U-shaped MC and ATC with MC cutting ATC at its minimum; profit-maximising output Q where MC = MR; supernormal profit shaded as the rectangle (P1 - ATC at Q) x Q, with price ABOVE ATC.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): the firm is a price taker, so it can sell any quantity at P1 and its AR equals MR equals price. It maximises profit where MC = MR. Because the price set by the industry currently exceeds the firm\'s average total cost at that output, the firm earns supernormal profit — profit above the normal profit already included in costs.\nDevelopment worth the fourth mark: this can only persist in the SHORT run. With freedom of entry and perfect information, new firms enter, industry supply shifts right, price falls, and the horizontal AR line drops until it is tangential to the minimum of ATC and only normal profit remains.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, what happens to a firm in perfect competition that is making a short-run loss.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): the firm with a horizontal AR = MR line at P1 lying BELOW the minimum of ATC but above the minimum of AVC; MC, ATC and AVC drawn with MC cutting each at its minimum; loss-minimising output Q where MC = MR; the loss shaded as the rectangle (ATC at Q - P1) x Q.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): the firm still produces where MC = MR, because that minimises the loss. It continues in the short run because price exceeds AVC, so each unit contributes towards the unavoidable fixed costs; shutting down would mean losing the whole of those fixed costs.\nDevelopment worth the fourth mark: in the LONG run all costs are variable, so firms cannot sustain a loss. Firms exit the industry, industry supply shifts LEFT, the market price rises, and the horizontal AR line moves up until it is tangential to the minimum of ATC. Exit stops when the remaining firms earn exactly normal profit at P = minimum ATC.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the welfare loss caused by a profit-maximising monopolist.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): a downward-sloping AR (= market demand) with MR below it and twice as steep; MC (which may be drawn as the competitive supply curve); monopoly output Qm where MC = MR with price Pm read UP to the AR curve; the competitive outcome Qc where AR = MC with price Pc; Qm < Qc and Pm > Pc; the DEADWEIGHT WELFARE LOSS triangle between the AR and MC curves over the range Qm to Qc shaded and labelled.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): the monopolist restricts output to the point where MC = MR rather than where P = MC, so it is allocatively inefficient. Over the range Qm to Qc consumers value each unit (read off AR) more than it costs to produce (read off MC), so those mutually beneficial transactions are lost — that lost surplus is the shaded triangle.\nDevelopment worth the fourth mark: there is also a TRANSFER of consumer surplus to the monopolist as producer surplus, shown by the rectangle above Pc and left of Qm. That transfer is a distributional effect, not a welfare loss, and distinguishing it from the deadweight loss triangle separates a strong answer from a weak one.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the long-run equilibrium of a firm in monopolistic competition.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): a DOWNWARD-SLOPING but relatively elastic AR curve with MR below it; ATC drawn TANGENTIAL to the AR curve at the profit-maximising output Q where MC = MR; price P read off AR at that point, equal to ATC so only normal profit is earned; the output Q clearly to the LEFT of the minimum of ATC, with the minimum point marked to show excess capacity.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): the firm has some price-setting power because its product is differentiated, so its demand curve slopes down. Freedom of entry means any supernormal profit attracts entrants, whose arrival takes customers away and shifts each firm\'s demand curve left until it is just tangential to ATC — the point at which only normal profit remains.\nDevelopment worth the fourth mark: at this equilibrium the firm is ALLOCATIVELY inefficient, because P > MC, and PRODUCTIVELY inefficient, because it does not produce at minimum ATC. The horizontal distance between Q and the minimum of ATC is the EXCESS CAPACITY that characterises monopolistic competition — the price consumers pay for product variety.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how third-degree price discrimination works between two market segments.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): two panels sharing a common MC line — Market A with a STEEP (inelastic) AR and its MR, Market B with a FLAT (elastic) AR and its MR. In each market the firm produces where its own MR = the common MC, and reads price up to that market\'s AR. The result must show a HIGHER price in the inelastic market A and a LOWER price in the elastic market B.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): the firm charges different prices to different groups of consumers for the SAME product, where the price difference does not reflect any difference in cost. Profit is maximised by equating marginal revenue in each segment to the common marginal cost; because MR = P(1 + 1/PED), the segment with the less elastic demand bears the higher price.\nDevelopment worth the fourth mark: three conditions are required — the firm must have price-setting power, it must be able to identify and separate the segments (by age, time of purchase, location or status), and it must prevent RESALE (arbitrage) between them, or the cheap segment would simply resell to the expensive one. Examples: peak and off-peak rail fares, student and standard cinema tickets, advance and walk-up airline pricing.`},

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, what the kinked demand curve model shows about price stability in an oligopoly.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Diagram (up to 2 marks): a demand (AR) curve with a KINK at the current price P and output Q — relatively ELASTIC above the kink and relatively INELASTIC below it; the corresponding MR curve with a VERTICAL DISCONTINUITY directly beneath the kink; an MC curve passing through that discontinuity; ideally a second MC curve also passing through the gap to show that costs can change without altering the profit-maximising output.\nExplanation (up to 2 marks): the kink arises from the firm\'s ASSUMPTION about rivals\' reactions. If it raises price, rivals do not follow, so it loses a large share of sales — demand is elastic above P. If it cuts price, rivals match to protect their share, so it gains little — demand is inelastic below P. Either move reduces revenue, so the firm leaves price at P.\nDevelopment worth the fourth mark: because MR is discontinuous beneath the kink, marginal cost can rise or fall anywhere within that vertical gap and the profit-maximising output where MC = MR is unchanged. The model therefore predicts PRICE RIGIDITY even when costs change, which is why oligopolists compete through advertising, branding and quality instead.\nCredit as evaluation that the model explains why a price is stable but not how it was reached in the first place, and that observed oligopoly prices are often far from rigid.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which monopoly is against the consumer interest.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
A single firm supplies a patented medical device.
  Price charged: £2 400 per unit     Estimated marginal cost: £610 per unit
  Units sold: 74 000 a year
  Estimated units that would be sold at a price equal to marginal cost: 131 000
  Research and development spending as a share of revenue: 21%
  Remaining patent life: 6 years`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The static case against, quantified from the data: price is 3.9 times marginal cost, so P is far above MC and the market is grossly allocatively inefficient. Output is restricted from 131 000 to 74 000 — 44% of potential transactions are lost — and each of those units would have been valued by a patient above its resource cost. That is the deadweight welfare loss.\n• Consumer surplus is also TRANSFERRED to the firm: the margin of £1 790 per unit on 74 000 units is £132.5m of producer surplus, much of it surplus consumers would have kept in a competitive market. This is a distributional harm distinct from the deadweight loss, and distinguishing the two is what reaches the top band.\n• Additional static harms: X-inefficiency, since a protected firm faces no pressure to minimise cost; and no incentive to respond to consumer preferences.\nEvaluation:\n• DYNAMIC EFFICIENCY is the serious counter-argument, and the stimulus supplies it: R&D at 21% of revenue is very high, and it is funded by exactly the supernormal profit that creates the static loss. Schumpeter\'s point is that without the prospect of temporary monopoly, the device would not have been developed at all — in which case the counterfactual is not 131 000 units at £610 but ZERO units.\n• The patent is TEMPORARY: six years of remaining life means the monopoly is time-limited by design, and generic entry at expiry will push price towards marginal cost. Judging the welfare effect over the product\'s whole life, rather than at one moment, changes the verdict substantially.\n• ECONOMIES OF SCALE: a single large producer may have a much lower MC than many small ones, so the competitive counterfactual price may not be £610.\n• PRICE DISCRIMINATION could raise output above 74 000 — selling at lower prices in lower-income markets increases access while preserving the profit that funds R&D.\n• COUNTERVAILING POWER: a single large buyer, such as a national health service, can negotiate the price down, so the theoretical monopoly outcome need not be the actual one.\n• Judgement: on static criteria the monopoly is plainly against the consumer interest, and the 44% output restriction quantifies it. Over the full product life-cycle, with R&D at 21% of revenue and the patent expiring in six years, the conclusion is far less clear — a supported answer should specify the time period over which consumer interest is being assessed.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — the stimulus used quantitatively (price-cost margin, output restriction), the dynamic counter-argument developed, a judgement reached. L1 (1–4) — lists disadvantages of monopoly without using the figures.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which contestability disciplines a dominant firm.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
Two markets, each supplied by a single dominant firm with over 80% share.
  Market 1 — short-haul air routes from a regional airport
    Sunk costs of entry: low (aircraft are leasable and redeployable; slots are tradeable)
    Price-cost margin: 6%
    New entrants in the last five years: 4, of which 2 subsequently withdrew
  Market 2 — regional water distribution
    Sunk costs of entry: very high (the pipe network cannot be resold or redeployed)
    Price-cost margin: 31%
    New entrants in the last five years: 0`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The theory: Baumol\'s contestable markets argument is that the THREAT of entry, not the number of firms, determines conduct. Where entry and exit are costless, an incumbent that raises price above average cost invites hit-and-run entry — an entrant takes the supernormal profit and leaves before prices fall. Anticipating this, the incumbent prices close to average cost and earns only normal profit.\n• The stimulus is a controlled comparison and supports the theory strongly: both firms have over 80% share, yet the margin is 6% where sunk costs are low and 31% where they are very high. The difference cannot be explained by concentration, which is identical, so it must be explained by contestability. That is the analytical heart of the answer.\n• The evidence of actual entry and exit in Market 1 — four entrants, two of which withdrew — is precisely what hit-and-run behaviour looks like, and it is the withdrawals, not the entries, that prove exit is cheap.\nEvaluation:\n• Perfect contestability is an abstraction. Even in Market 1, brand loyalty, incumbent reputation, consumer switching costs and the risk that the incumbent will price aggressively on entry all blunt the threat. The 6% margin is low but not zero.\n• INCUMBENT STRATEGY can destroy contestability deliberately: limit pricing (holding price just below the entrant\'s average cost), maintaining excess capacity as a credible threat to flood the market, loyalty contracts and frequent-flyer schemes all raise the effective cost of entry without any change in physical sunk costs.\n• Market 2 illustrates the limit of the theory: where the network is a NATURAL MONOPOLY, no amount of policy can make the market contestable, because duplication of the pipes would raise total cost. Contestability is unavailable as a discipline, so REGULATION — Ofwat setting price controls at five-yearly reviews — must substitute for it.\n• Contestability disciplines PRICE but may not discipline INVESTMENT or quality. A market kept contestable by cheap exit gives firms little incentive to make long-lived commitments, and a 6% margin may not fund innovation.\n• Sunk costs are not fixed by nature: regulation can reduce them (slot trading, open access, mandated infrastructure sharing) or raise them, so contestability is partly a policy choice.\n• Judgement: the data support the proposition strongly — contestability, not the number of firms, is what disciplines the dominant firm here. But the discipline is only as strong as the absence of sunk costs, it can be undermined strategically, and where it is structurally unavailable, as in Market 2, regulation is the only alternative.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — the controlled comparison used explicitly, the sunk-cost mechanism developed, limitations weighed. L1 (1–4) — defines contestability without using the two markets.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which barriers to entry explain persistent supernormal profits.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
Three industries, average return on capital employed over ten years
  Branded pharmaceuticals:  21.4%   Patents typically 20 years from filing; R&D cost per approved
                                    drug estimated in the billions
  Airport operation:        14.8%   Runway capacity constrained by planning; slots allocated
                                    administratively
  Independent restaurants:   3.1%   Premises leasable; equipment resaleable; no licence barrier
                                    beyond food hygiene registration
Average return on capital across all UK non-financial companies over the same period: 9.6%`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The mechanism: supernormal profit attracts entry, entry expands supply, price falls and the profit is competed away. Persistent supernormal profit is therefore evidence that something is PREVENTING that process — a barrier to entry.\n• The data fit well: pharmaceuticals at 21.4% and airports at 14.8% are both well above the 9.6% economy-wide average and both have identifiable, durable barriers (patents and R&D sunk costs; planning constraints and administratively allocated slots). Restaurants at 3.1%, below the average, have essentially no barriers — premises are leased and equipment resaleable, so entry is easy and exit is cheap, which is close to the contestable case.\n• Types of barrier illustrated: LEGAL (patents), SUNK COST (R&D that cannot be recovered), and STRUCTURAL/REGULATORY (planning and slot allocation). Each raises the cost or risk of entry rather than making it impossible.\nEvaluation:\n• RETURN ON CAPITAL IS NOT ECONOMIC PROFIT. The 9.6% average includes normal profit — the opportunity cost of capital — so only the excess above the risk-adjusted required return is supernormal. Pharmaceuticals is a high-risk business in which most compounds fail, so a large part of the 21.4% is compensation for risk and for the capital sunk in failed projects, not monopoly rent.\n• SURVIVORSHIP BIAS: a ten-year average of surviving firms omits those that failed. Restaurants at 3.1% look unprofitable partly because the sample of survivors excludes the many that closed; the true ex-ante expected return is lower still, which actually strengthens the free-entry story.\n• Other explanations for persistent high returns: superior efficiency or management, genuine product differentiation and brand equity, first-mover advantages, and network effects. A firm that is simply better than its rivals earns a return above average without any barrier at all — and competition policy correctly treats that differently.\n• Barriers are not permanent. Patents expire and generic entry follows; planning regimes change; technology can dissolve a barrier entirely. The pharmaceutical figure is an average across a patent cycle, not a steady state.\n• Some barriers are SOCIALLY DESIRABLE: patents are a deliberate, time-limited monopoly granted precisely to make R&D worth undertaking. A finding of persistent supernormal profit is therefore not automatically a finding of harm.\n• Judgement: barriers to entry are the best single explanation for persistence, and the contrast between 21.4% and 3.1% supports it clearly. But return on capital measures risk and efficiency as well as monopoly rent, so the data identify where entry is difficult rather than proving that consumers are being exploited.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — the three industries compared against the 9.6% benchmark, the risk and normal-profit caveat reached. L1 (1–4) — lists barriers to entry without using the data.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which the prisoners\' dilemma explains the behaviour of firms in an oligopoly.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
Two supermarket chains each choose HIGH PRICE or LOW PRICE. Annual profits in £m:
                          B: HIGH        B: LOW
  A: HIGH              A 320, B 300    A 140, B 410
  A: LOW               A 425, B 120    A 190, B 175
Both firms interact in the same market every week and observe each other\'s prices daily on
comparison websites.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Solve the game. For A: if B plays HIGH, A gets 320 from HIGH and 425 from LOW, so LOW is better. If B plays LOW, A gets 140 from HIGH and 190 from LOW, so LOW is better. LOW is a DOMINANT strategy for A. By symmetry, for B: 300 against 410 if A plays HIGH, and 120 against 175 if A plays LOW, so LOW dominates for B too.\n• The NASH EQUILIBRIUM is therefore (LOW, LOW), giving 190 and 175 — a joint 365. But (HIGH, HIGH) gives 320 and 300, a joint 620. Both firms are worse off at the equilibrium than under the cooperative outcome, which is exactly the prisoners\' dilemma: individually rational choices produce a collectively worse result.\n• What it explains: the constant temptation to undercut, why collusive agreements are unstable, why cartels need enforcement mechanisms, and why competition authorities offer LENIENCY to the first cartel member to confess — leniency deliberately recreates the dilemma inside an existing cartel.\nEvaluation:\n• The stimulus itself supplies the strongest limitation: the firms interact EVERY WEEK, so this is a REPEATED game, not a one-shot one. In a repeated game with no known end date, tit-for-tat retaliation makes defection unprofitable — a firm gains 425 for one week and then faces (LOW, LOW) at 190 indefinitely. Cooperation at (HIGH, HIGH) is sustainable without any agreement, which is precisely tacit collusion.\n• Daily price observation on comparison websites makes defection DETECTABLE almost immediately, which shortens the payoff to cheating and further stabilises the high-price outcome. Transparency, often assumed to help consumers, here facilitates collusion.\n• The model is a severe simplification: only two firms, one decision variable, one period, known payoffs and simultaneous moves. Real oligopolists compete on quality, advertising, range and location as well as price; they have asymmetric costs and shares; and they move sequentially, with price leadership rather than simultaneous choice.\n• Payoffs are not actually known with certainty, so firms are choosing under uncertainty rather than solving a matrix.\n• Alternative models explain other features better: the KINKED DEMAND CURVE explains why prices are rigid once set; price leadership explains how a focal price is reached; contestability explains why some concentrated markets behave competitively anyway.\n• Judgement: the prisoners\' dilemma explains the TENSION at the heart of oligopoly — why collusion is attractive and why it is unstable — and the matrix demonstrates it cleanly. It does not predict observed behaviour well, because repetition and transparency, both present in the stimulus, push firms towards the cooperative outcome the one-shot game rules out.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — the game solved correctly with dominant strategies and the Nash equilibrium identified, and the repeated-game limitation developed from the stimulus. L1 (1–4) — describes the prisoners\' dilemma without solving the matrix.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which the CMA\'s new digital markets powers will increase competition in mobile platforms.

Stimulus (written for this practice question, using published CMA decisions)
Under the Digital Markets, Competition and Consumers Act 2024 the Competition and Markets
Authority can designate a firm as having \'strategic market status\' (SMS) and then impose binding
conduct requirements on it.
  10 October 2025: Google designated as having SMS in general search and search advertising.
  22 October 2025: Apple and Google each designated as having SMS in their respective mobile
    platforms. The CMA found the two platforms to be an effective duopoly, with around
    90-100% of UK mobile devices running on one of them.
Designation is not a finding of wrongdoing; it is the gateway to imposing tailored conduct
requirements, which the CMA said it would consult on and phase in.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The problem being addressed: a duopoly covering 90-100% of UK devices, protected by very strong NETWORK EFFECTS (users go where the apps are, developers go where the users are) and high consumer SWITCHING COSTS (purchased apps, data, ecosystem lock-in). These are self-reinforcing barriers to entry that ordinary market forces will not erode, so the market is neither competitive nor contestable.\n• Why the new regime may work where traditional enforcement did not: a Competition Act investigation is retrospective, slow and requires proving abuse case by case, by which time the market has moved on. SMS designation is EX ANTE and allows tailored, forward-looking conduct requirements — on app store terms, steering restrictions, interoperability, default settings and data access — without proving an infringement first.\n• Mechanism of benefit: lower commission and freedom to steer users to alternative payment methods would cut developers\' costs and therefore app prices; interoperability and easier switching would reduce lock-in; mandated access to device functionality would let rival browsers and wallets compete.\nEvaluation:\n• Designation alone changes nothing, and the stimulus says so explicitly — it is a gateway, not a remedy. Everything depends on the conduct requirements that follow, on their timing, and on enforcement. Any claim that competition HAS increased is unsupportable on this evidence.\n• The underlying economics may be resistant to conduct remedies. Network effects and the two-sided nature of the platforms mean the duopoly is not simply the product of bad behaviour; a third platform faces a chicken-and-egg problem that no conduct requirement solves. Structural remedies are not on the table.\n• Regulatory risk of unintended consequences: capping commission may push platforms to charge developers in other ways; weakening default settings and app review may reduce security and privacy, which are genuine consumer benefits; and compliance costs can fall hardest on small developers.\n• Regulatory capture and information asymmetry: the CMA must specify conduct requirements for extremely complex technical systems that only the designated firms fully understand.\n• International interaction: the EU\'s Digital Markets Act and US litigation are pursuing similar objectives, so UK-specific effects are hard to isolate — and the firms may make global changes for other reasons and attribute them here, or conversely offer the UK a worse deal because it is a small market.\n• Duration and appeals: designations last a fixed period and decisions can be challenged, so remedies may be delayed for years.\n• Judgement: the regime is well targeted at a genuine and durable market failure, and ex ante conduct rules are better suited to fast-moving digital markets than retrospective enforcement. But designation is only the first step, the barriers are structural rather than behavioural, and on the evidence in the stimulus no effect on competition can yet be demonstrated — a supported conclusion should say that the potential is substantial and the realisation unproven.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — the stimulus dates and the duopoly figure used, the designation-versus-remedy distinction reached, evaluation weighed. L1 (1–4) — general comments on regulating big technology firms.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which price discrimination benefits consumers.

Stimulus (written for this practice question)
A train operator\'s fares on one route
  Advance off-peak single, booked 8 weeks ahead:  £14.50   Tickets sold: 186 000 a year
  Anytime single, bought on the day:              £68.00   Tickets sold:  94 000 a year
  Estimated marginal cost of carrying one extra passenger on an existing service: £3.10
  Estimated single price that would maximise profit if only one fare could be charged: £44.00
  Estimated tickets sold at a single price of £44.00: 172 000 a year`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The mechanism: third-degree price discrimination charges different prices to segments with different price elasticities of demand — the leisure traveller who can book eight weeks ahead has elastic demand and pays £14.50, while the business traveller who must travel today has inelastic demand and pays £68.00. Profit is maximised by equating MR in each segment to the common MC of £3.10.\n• The benefit to consumers, quantified from the stimulus: total tickets sold are 186 000 + 94 000 = 280 000 under discrimination against 172 000 at a single price — 63% more journeys. 108 000 additional passengers travel, and the great majority of them pay £14.50, far below the £44.00 single price they would otherwise have faced. Low-income and price-sensitive travellers are the clear gainers, and that output expansion is the strongest argument available.\n• Output expansion also improves ALLOCATIVE efficiency at the margin: the £14.50 fare is well above the £3.10 marginal cost, so each extra journey is worth more than it costs, and those transactions would not have happened at a single price.\n• Cross-subsidy: revenue from the inelastic segment can keep marginal services running that a single price could not sustain, so some consumers get a service that would otherwise not exist.\nEvaluation:\n• The inelastic segment is clearly worse off: 94 000 travellers pay £68.00 instead of £44.00, a £24 loss each, roughly £2.3m of consumer surplus transferred to the operator. Price discrimination converts consumer surplus into producer surplus — that is its purpose.\n• Total welfare may rise or fall. Output expansion raises it; the surplus transfer is distributional, not a welfare loss. The net effect on CONSUMERS as a group depends on whether the gain to the 108 000 extra travellers and the 186 000 paying £14.50 exceeds the loss to the 94 000 paying £68.00 — on these numbers it plausibly does, but the question cannot be settled by assertion.\n• Equity is ambiguous: business travellers are typically better off, so the incidence is mildly progressive; but the passenger who must travel at short notice for a family emergency pays £68.00 regardless of income, so segmentation by elasticity is not segmentation by ability to pay.\n• Administrative and search costs: complex fare structures impose real costs on consumers, and exploit bounded rationality — many passengers pay more than they need to because the cheapest option is hard to find. That is a behavioural harm the standard model ignores.\n• The profit funds investment, which may benefit consumers dynamically; or it may simply be extracted, depending on regulation and ownership.\n• Judgement: price discrimination benefits consumers IN AGGREGATE where it expands output substantially, as the 63% increase here shows, and harms the inelastic segment. A supported conclusion should turn on the size of the output expansion relative to the surplus transfer, and note that the answer would reverse if discrimination were used simply to raise price with no increase in quantity.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — output comparison computed, both the gain and the transfer identified, judgement supported. L1 (1–4) — describes price discrimination without using the figures.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which concentration ratios measure the degree of competition in a market.

Stimulus (published Kantar grocery market shares, Great Britain, 2026)
  Tesco 27.3% ; Sainsbury\'s 15.1% ; Asda 13.7% ; Aldi 10.2% ; Morrisons 9.2%
  The four largest chains held around 65% of the market, down from around 73% in 2017.
  Aldi overtook Morrisons to become the fourth-largest grocer.
Over the same period the grocery sector saw sustained real price competition, frequent price
matching schemes and the entry and rapid expansion of limited-assortment discounters.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• What the measure does: the n-firm concentration ratio is the combined market share of the n largest firms. Here the three-firm ratio is 27.3 + 15.1 + 13.7 = 56.1%, and the traditional big-four ratio is 65.3%. It is simple, cheap to compute, comparable across time and widely used as a first screen by competition authorities.\n• What it captures correctly: a high ratio means a small number of firms account for most output, which raises the scope for interdependence, tacit collusion and the exercise of market power. A falling ratio — from about 73% in 2017 to about 65% in 2026 — does indicate structural change, and here it correctly identifies the rise of the discounters.\nEvaluation — the limitations, which are the substance of the answer:\n• It ignores the DISTRIBUTION of share within the group. A market with four firms at 16.25% each and one with a firm at 40% and three at 8% have the same four-firm ratio but very different competitive dynamics. The Herfindahl-Hirschman Index, which squares each share, captures this and is what the CMA actually uses.\n• It says nothing about CONTESTABILITY, which is the decisive point. A concentrated market with low sunk costs can behave competitively, because the threat of hit-and-run entry disciplines incumbents; a fragmented market with high switching costs may not. The number of firms is a structural fact; competition is a behavioural outcome.\n• MARKET DEFINITION determines the answer entirely. A national grocery ratio of 65.3% conceals the fact that consumers shop LOCALLY: a town with one supermarket faces a local monopoly however competitive the national figure looks. Widen or narrow the market definition and the ratio changes without anything in the world changing.\n• It ignores IMPORTS and potential entrants, so it can overstate domestic firms\' power in tradeable sectors.\n• It ignores CONDUCT AND OUTCOMES, which is what the stimulus exposes: sustained real price competition, price matching and discounter expansion are direct evidence of vigorous rivalry that a 65.3% ratio would suggest is absent. Margin data, price levels relative to cost, and rates of entry and exit measure competition far better than a structural share count.\n• It ignores COUNTERVAILING POWER: concentrated retailers facing concentrated manufacturers may produce competitive outcomes that neither share alone predicts.\n• It is a snapshot: the CHANGE from 73% to 65% is more informative than the level, because it reveals that positions are not entrenched — Aldi displacing Morrisons is evidence that share is contestable.\n• Judgement: concentration ratios are a useful first screen that flags where market power MIGHT exist, and they are genuinely informative about structural change over time. They are a poor measure of competition itself, because competition depends on contestability, conduct and the correct definition of the market — all of which the grocery data in the stimulus illustrate.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — the ratio computed from the data and the structure-versus-conduct distinction developed using the stimulus evidence. L1 (1–4) — defines the concentration ratio without evaluating it.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether perfect competition is a useful benchmark for competition policy.', marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Why it is useful: perfect competition delivers, in long-run equilibrium, both ALLOCATIVE efficiency (P = MC, so the value of the last unit equals its resource cost) and PRODUCTIVE efficiency (production at minimum ATC, with only normal profit earned). It therefore supplies a precise, theoretically grounded standard against which any real market can be judged, and it identifies the specific harms of market power — restricted output, price above marginal cost, and the deadweight loss triangle.\n• It also gives competition authorities their working concepts: the counterfactual price, the price-cost margin, the measurement of consumer surplus lost, and the idea that entry erodes supernormal profit, which is why barriers to entry are the central concern of merger control.\nEvaluation:\n• The assumptions are never met: infinitely many small firms, a homogeneous product, perfect information, free entry and exit, and no externalities. No real market satisfies them, and a benchmark that is unattainable can generate the NIRVANA FALLACY — condemning a market for failing a test nothing could pass.\n• It is statically defined and therefore ignores DYNAMIC efficiency, which may matter more. Perfectly competitive firms earn only normal profit, so they cannot fund research and development. Schumpeter argued that innovation comes from firms with temporary monopoly power and the profits to invest — and patents are a deliberate policy of granting monopoly for precisely this reason. A policy that pursued perfect competition literally would abolish patents.\n• It ignores ECONOMIES OF SCALE. Where the minimum efficient scale is large relative to demand, atomistic competition is impossible and would be wasteful; a natural monopoly is the efficient structure, and the correct policy is regulation, not fragmentation.\n• It ignores PRODUCT DIFFERENTIATION, which consumers value. Monopolistic competition is productively and allocatively inefficient by this benchmark, yet the excess capacity is the price of variety, which is a genuine consumer benefit.\n• Better practical benchmarks exist. CONTESTABILITY is the one competition authorities actually use: a market can deliver near-competitive outcomes with very few firms if entry and exit are cheap, so policy targets sunk costs and barriers rather than firm numbers. Modern practice also uses the Herfindahl-Hirschman Index, price-cost margins, and counterfactual analysis of what would happen absent a merger.\n• Practice reflects this. The CMA cleared the Vodafone-Three merger on 5 December 2024 despite reducing the number of mobile network operators from four to three, accepting a binding £11 billion network investment commitment and time-limited price caps — a decision that would be incomprehensible if perfect competition were the operative standard, and sensible if dynamic efficiency and investment are weighed alongside static competition.\n• Judgement: perfect competition is indispensable as an analytical BENCHMARK, because it defines what market power costs and gives the deadweight loss its meaning; it is a poor POLICY TARGET, because it is unattainable, static, and hostile to scale economies and innovation. The defensible conclusion is that policy should use it to diagnose harm and use contestability and consumer-welfare tests to choose remedies.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) / L2 (5–8) / L1 (1–4). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether the Competition and Markets Authority was right to clear the merger of Vodafone UK and Three UK.', marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The facts: on 5 December 2024 the CMA cleared the merger of Vodafone UK and Three UK, reducing the number of UK mobile network operators from four to three. Clearance was conditional on behavioural remedies — a legally binding commitment to an £11 billion network investment programme over eight years, reaching 99% of the population, and time-limited protections for at least three years including price caps on certain tariffs and safeguards for mobile virtual network operators, pending the roll-out of the 5G standalone network. Breach can attract penalties of up to 5% of group worldwide turnover.\n• The case AGAINST clearance: reducing four competitors to three raises concentration sharply in an already concentrated market. Standard oligopoly theory predicts that fewer firms makes interdependence more acute, tacit collusion easier to sustain, and unilateral price rises more profitable because fewer rivals are available to absorb diverted demand. MVNOs, which buy wholesale capacity, lose one of four potential suppliers, weakening their bargaining position and reducing competition in the retail market they serve. The static consumer harm is a higher price and a deadweight loss.\n• The case FOR clearance: the merged network can be built once rather than twice, so the merger captures large economies of scale and avoids duplicating fixed infrastructure. Neither party alone had the scale to fund a full 5G standalone roll-out, so the counterfactual — what would have happened without the merger — was arguably two sub-scale networks under-investing, not four vigorous competitors. That is a DYNAMIC EFFICIENCY argument, and the merger-specific £11 billion commitment converts a promise into an enforceable obligation.\nEvaluation:\n• BEHAVIOURAL REMEDIES ARE THE WEAK POINT. Competition authorities generally prefer STRUCTURAL remedies (divestment) because they are self-enforcing and permanent, whereas behavioural remedies require continuing monitoring, are vulnerable to circumvention, and expire. The price caps last at least three years; the market power created is permanent. This asymmetry is the single strongest criticism.\n• The CMA effectively takes on a REGULATORY role it is not designed for, committing itself to supervising investment delivery for eight years — a task with the standard information asymmetry problem, since only the firm knows the true cost and pace of the roll-out.\n• The COUNTERFACTUAL is unverifiable. It is impossible to know whether the two networks would genuinely have under-invested, and merging parties always have an incentive to argue that they would have. Accepting it shifts the burden in a way that invites similar claims in future deals.\n• Against that: the mobile market is not a textbook oligopoly. MVNOs, infrastructure sharing agreements, and the possibility of regulatory intervention by Ofcom all constrain the merged firm, and three well-funded networks may compete harder than four under-funded ones. Several European markets with three operators have seen strong investment.\n• The remedy is at least enforceable, with penalties up to 5% of worldwide turnover — a credible deterrent.\n• Judgement: a defensible conclusion either way, and the grade depends on the reasoning rather than the side. The strongest answers weigh a CERTAIN increase in concentration against an UNCERTAIN investment benefit, note that the remedies are time-limited while the structural change is permanent, and conclude that the decision was reasonable only if the under-investment counterfactual was genuine — in which case the merger traded static competition for dynamic efficiency, which is a coherent choice rather than an error.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) / L2 (5–8) / L1 (1–4). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether a natural monopoly should be regulated rather than broken up.', marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Define the condition: a natural monopoly exists where long-run average cost falls over the ENTIRE range of market demand, so that one firm can supply the whole market at lower average cost than two or more. Diagram: LRAC still falling where it crosses the demand curve, with MC below AC throughout. Water distribution, gas and electricity transmission, and rail track are the standard examples.\n• Why BREAKING IT UP is the wrong remedy: splitting the market between two firms means each produces at a higher point on the LRAC curve, so unit costs RISE. Duplicating a pipe or cable network wastes resources without creating meaningful competition, since consumers cannot practically choose between two sets of pipes. Fragmentation would raise total cost and almost certainly raise prices.\n• Why REGULATION is preferred: an unregulated natural monopolist prices above marginal cost, restricts output and earns supernormal profit, creating a deadweight loss. Regulation can replicate the competitive outcome without sacrificing the scale economies:\n  - RPI-X or CPIH-X price capping forces real price reductions and, crucially, lets the firm keep any cost saving beyond X within the control period, creating a genuine incentive for productive efficiency.\n  - Rate-of-return regulation, by contrast, invites gold-plating (the Averch-Johnson effect), since a guaranteed return on capital rewards over-investment.\n  - Quality, service and investment standards address the incentive to cut costs by degrading service.\n  - Ofwat\'s PR24 final determinations, effective 1 April 2025, allowed average household bills to rise by about £157 (roughly 36%) before inflation over 2025-2030, with wide variation between companies, in order to fund investment — an illustration of the regulator trading current price against future capacity.\nEvaluation:\n• Regulation is itself imperfect. INFORMATION ASYMMETRY is fundamental: the regulator must set X without knowing the firm\'s true costs, and the firm has every incentive to overstate them. REGULATORY CAPTURE is a persistent risk given the industry\'s concentrated interest and the movement of staff between regulator and regulated. Price caps can also create under-investment in assets whose deterioration is not immediately visible, which is one account of the sewage discharge record.\n• Structural separation is a THIRD option the question invites, and often the best one: split the natural monopoly NETWORK from the potentially competitive activities that use it. Energy generation and retail compete while transmission is regulated; rail operations are tendered while track is not. This captures competition where it is possible and regulates only what must be regulated — but it creates complex interface and coordination problems, which the fragmentation of the rail industry illustrated.\n• PUBLIC OWNERSHIP is a fourth option: it removes the profit incentive to exploit and allows marginal-cost pricing funded from taxation, but risks X-inefficiency and politically driven investment. Franchised passenger rail services are returning to public ownership as contracts expire under the Passenger Railway Services (Public Ownership) Act 2024, beginning with South Western Railway on 25 May 2025.\n• Technology can dissolve natural monopoly: mobile networks ended the fixed-line local loop monopoly, and distributed generation and storage weaken the case in electricity, so a structure that is natural today may not be in twenty years.\n• Judgement: breaking up a genuine natural monopoly is almost always wrong, because it sacrifices the economies of scale that define it. The real choice is between regulated private ownership, structural separation of network from services, and public ownership — and the best answer turns on how severe the information asymmetry and capture risks are relative to the X-inefficiency risk of state operation. A conclusion favouring separation of the network, with regulation of the network and competition in everything else, is the most defensible.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) / L2 (5–8) / L1 (1–4). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether monopolistic competition produces a good outcome for consumers.', marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The structure: many firms, each small relative to the market; DIFFERENTIATED products, giving each firm a downward-sloping but elastic demand curve; low barriers to entry and exit; reasonably good information. Restaurants, hairdressers, plumbers, coffee shops and most local services fit.\n• The long-run equilibrium: entry erodes supernormal profit until ATC is tangential to AR at the profit-maximising output, so only NORMAL profit is earned. Diagram: downward-sloping AR with MR below, ATC tangential at Q where MC = MR, and Q to the left of minimum ATC.\n• The case that the outcome is BAD for consumers:\n  - ALLOCATIVELY INEFFICIENT: P > MC, so consumers value the last unit more than it costs to produce and some mutually beneficial transactions do not happen — a deadweight loss, though a small one because demand is elastic.\n  - PRODUCTIVELY INEFFICIENT: the firm does not produce at minimum ATC. The horizontal gap between its output and the minimum point is EXCESS CAPACITY — the market sustains more firms than are needed, each operating below efficient scale.\n  - Resources are absorbed in ADVERTISING and packaging that may only shuffle customers between near-identical products rather than adding value.\n  - Weak DYNAMIC EFFICIENCY: with only normal profit in the long run, there is little to fund research and development, so innovation tends to be incremental.\nEvaluation — the case that it is GOOD for consumers:\n• CHOICE AND VARIETY is the decisive counter-argument. The excess capacity is not waste but the PRICE OF VARIETY: a market with a single standardised product would be productively efficient and would offer consumers nothing to choose between. Consumers demonstrably value differentiation, so a welfare comparison that counts only cost misses the benefit.\n• Only NORMAL PROFIT is earned in the long run, so consumers are not being exploited — there is no transfer of surplus to producers, which is the central harm in monopoly.\n• Low barriers mean the market is close to CONTESTABLE, so any firm that raises price or lets quality slip loses customers quickly. Competitive discipline is real even though each firm has some price-setting power.\n• The inefficiencies are SMALL in magnitude: because demand is highly elastic, the gap between P and MC is narrow and the deadweight loss triangle is thin, quite unlike the monopoly case.\n• Comparison matters: perfect competition is the benchmark against which monopolistic competition looks bad, but perfect competition requires a homogeneous product and does not exist. Against the realistic alternative of oligopoly or monopoly, monopolistic competition is clearly better — lower prices, no supernormal profit, easy entry.\n• Advertising is not purely wasteful: it conveys genuine information about availability, price and quality in markets where consumers would otherwise face high search costs.\n• Caveats: differentiation may be spurious, exploiting bounded rationality and brand loyalty rather than reflecting real differences; and search costs mean consumers may not find the best deal.\n• Judgement: monopolistic competition fails both static efficiency tests but delivers variety, normal profit and easy entry. A supported conclusion is that the outcome is good for consumers once variety is valued properly and the comparison is made against realistic alternatives rather than against an unattainable perfectly competitive benchmark — while acknowledging that the verdict depends on how much consumers genuinely value differentiation in the particular market being discussed.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) / L2 (5–8) / L1 (1–4). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether fines are an effective deterrent against cartel behaviour.', marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The harm: a cartel agrees prices, shares markets or rigs bids, producing the monopoly outcome without the monopoly structure — output restricted, price above marginal cost, consumer surplus transferred to producers and a deadweight loss created. Bid rigging is especially damaging in public procurement, because the taxpayer pays.\n• The enforcement framework: price fixing and bid rigging are prohibited under Chapter I of the Competition Act 1998. Penalties can reach 10% of worldwide group turnover, individuals can be disqualified from acting as directors for up to 15 years, the cartel offence carries criminal liability, and victims can bring follow-on damages claims.\n• Evidence: on 23 March 2023 the CMA fined ten construction firms a total of about £59.3 million for rigging bids on 19 demolition and asbestos removal contracts worth over £150 million, with the conduct running from January 2013 to June 2018. In February 2025 the CMA fined four banks over £100 million in total following settlements in its UK government bonds investigation.\n• The deterrence logic: a rational firm colludes if the expected gain exceeds the expected penalty. Raising the fine raises the expected cost, so at a high enough level collusion is unprofitable. Fines also fund enforcement and signal the seriousness of the prohibition.\nEvaluation:\n• THE PROBABILITY OF DETECTION is the decisive variable, and it is low. Expected penalty = fine x probability of detection. If a cartel has perhaps a one-in-five chance of being caught, a fine must be five times the illicit gain merely to break even as a deterrent. The demolition cartel ran for five and a half years before it was penalised, which tells you detection is slow as well as uncertain.\n• PROPORTIONALITY: £59.3 million against contracts worth over £150 million is a substantial penalty, but the relevant comparison is with the OVERCHARGE on those contracts and with the profits earned over five years, not with contract value.\n• WHO PAYS: a corporate fine falls on current shareholders, who may not be the people who took the decision, while the executives responsible may have left with their bonuses. This is why DIRECTOR DISQUALIFICATION and criminal liability matter — personal sanctions align the deterrent with the decision-maker, and are arguably more effective per pound than corporate fines.\n• PASS-THROUGH: in a concentrated market a fine can be partly recovered through higher prices, so consumers pay twice.\n• LENIENCY IS PROBABLY MORE EFFECTIVE THAN THE FINE ITSELF. Immunity for the first cartel member to come forward recreates the prisoners\' dilemma inside the cartel: each member knows a rival may confess first, so the cooperative arrangement becomes unstable from within. Most cartel detections originate this way, which means the fine\'s main function is to make leniency attractive rather than to deter directly.\n• INSOLVENCY CONSTRAINT: a fine large enough to deter may bankrupt the firm, reducing competition further — so the maximum credible fine is capped by the harm its imposition would do.\n• ALTERNATIVES AND COMPLEMENTS: follow-on damages actions by victims, structural remedies, procurement design that makes bid rigging harder (sealed bids, randomised tender formats, screening for suspicious bid patterns), and market investigations of the kind the CMA concluded into veterinary services on 24 March 2026.\n• Judgement: fines are a necessary component but a weak stand-alone deterrent, because their expected value is heavily discounted by a low probability of detection and because they punish shareholders rather than decision-makers. A supported conclusion is that the deterrent works through the COMBINATION of leniency (which raises detection probability), personal sanctions (which target the decision-maker) and damages actions — with the corporate fine as the anchor that makes the other three bite.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) / L2 (5–8) / L1 (1–4). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram and the information below, the extent to which monopoly power reduces economic welfare.

Extract 1 (written for this practice question)
A firm supplies 74% of a national market for a branded consumer product.
  Price: £39.00 per unit         Marginal cost: £14.20 per unit
  Units sold: 2.4 million a year
  Estimated units that would be sold if price equalled marginal cost: 3.9 million
  Research and development spending: £61m a year (8.4% of revenue)
  Advertising spending: £94m a year
  The nearest competitor holds 9% of the market, and no new entrant has achieved more than
  2% share in fifteen years.`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: downward-sloping AR (= market demand) with MR below and twice as steep; MC drawn; monopoly output Qm where MC = MR with price Pm read UP to AR; the competitive output Qc where AR = MC with price Pc; the DEADWEIGHT LOSS triangle between AR and MC over Qm to Qc shaded and labelled; the rectangle of consumer surplus transferred to the firm shown separately. Distinguishing the transfer from the deadweight loss is what separates Level 3 from Level 2.\n• Quantify from the extract. The price-cost margin is (39.00 - 14.20)/39.00 = 63.6%, so price is 2.75 times marginal cost — a very large departure from allocative efficiency. Output is restricted from 3.9 million to 2.4 million, so 1.5 million units, 38% of the potential market, are not traded. Approximating the deadweight loss as a triangle of half the base times the height gives 0.5 x 1.5 million x £24.80 = about £18.6 million a year of welfare simply destroyed. The surplus TRANSFER on the units actually sold is 2.4 million x £24.80 = about £59.5 million a year moved from consumers to the firm.\n• Other static harms: X-inefficiency, since a firm with 74% share and no effective entrant faces weak cost discipline; and £94m of advertising that may function largely as a barrier to entry, raising the cost a rival must incur to be noticed rather than informing consumers.\n• The persistence evidence matters: no entrant above 2% in fifteen years indicates durable barriers to entry, so the supernormal profit is not being competed away and the harm is structural rather than transitional.\nEvaluation:\n• DYNAMIC EFFICIENCY is the strongest counter-argument. £61m of R&D, at 8.4% of revenue, is high and is funded by the supernormal profit that creates the static loss. Schumpeter\'s argument is that the prospect of temporary market power is what makes innovation worth financing, and that the correct comparison is not monopoly against perfect competition but innovation against no innovation. Over a product\'s life-cycle the dynamic gain can exceed the annual static loss.\n• ECONOMIES OF SCALE: a firm with 74% share may have a far lower MC than several smaller rivals would. If breaking it up raised unit costs, the competitive counterfactual price would not be £14.20, and the estimated 3.9 million is then too optimistic — which would shrink the calculated deadweight loss.\n• THE TRIANGLE IS PROBABLY SMALL RELATIVE TO THE TRANSFER, as the figures show (£18.6m against £59.5m). Economists have long noted that measured deadweight losses from monopoly are modest, and that the politically salient harm is distributional. Whether a transfer from consumers to shareholders counts as a welfare LOSS depends on the distributional weights applied — a point worth making explicitly.\n• PRICE DISCRIMINATION could raise output above 2.4 million, reducing the deadweight loss while increasing the transfer, so the two harms can move in opposite directions.\n• COUNTERVAILING POWER and regulation: large buyers, retailer bargaining power, or intervention by a competition authority can constrain the outcome. The CMA can open a market investigation, and under the Digital Markets, Competition and Consumers Act 2024 it has direct enforcement powers and can fine up to 10% of global turnover for the most serious infringements.\n• CONTESTABILITY: 74% share with no entrant above 2% for fifteen years suggests the market is NOT contestable, which strengthens the case for harm — had entry been easy, share alone would not imply market power.\n• Judgement: on these figures monopoly power clearly reduces static welfare, and the calculation quantifies it at roughly £18.6 million a year of pure loss plus a £59.5 million annual transfer. The honest conclusion weighs that against R&D funded by the same profit, notes that the deadweight triangle is modest relative to the transfer, and turns on whether the barriers sustaining the 74% share are natural (scale, innovation) or strategic (advertising, exclusion) — the fifteen-year absence of entrants points towards the latter, which strengthens the case for intervention.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — accurate diagram distinguishing deadweight loss from transfer, extract data used quantitatively, dynamic counter-argument weighed, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, data described rather than computed. L1 (1–5) — no or incorrect diagram, list of monopoly disadvantages. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram and the information below, the extent to which perfect competition achieves efficiency.

Extract 1 (written for this practice question)
A market for a standardised agricultural commodity approximates perfect competition.
  Number of producers: about 18 000        Largest producer\'s share: under 0.4%
  Product: graded to a common standard, so buyers are indifferent between suppliers
  Long-run average return: close to the cost of capital, with no sustained supernormal profit
  Research and development spending across the sector: 0.3% of revenue
  Production generates nitrate runoff into watercourses, the cost of which falls on water
  companies and downstream users rather than on producers.`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: the standard two-panel construction — the industry with D and S determining price P1, and the representative firm facing a horizontal AR = MR = D line at P1, with U-shaped MC and ATC, in LONG-RUN equilibrium where the horizontal price line is exactly TANGENTIAL to the minimum of ATC and MC cuts ATC at that point. The condition P = MC = MR = minimum ATC must be visible and labelled.\n• The efficiency claims, and why they hold:\n  - ALLOCATIVE efficiency: the firm is a price taker, so AR = MR = P, and it produces where MC = MR. Therefore P = MC. The value consumers place on the last unit exactly equals the marginal resource cost, so no reallocation could raise welfare.\n  - PRODUCTIVE efficiency: freedom of entry competes away supernormal profit until price equals the MINIMUM of average total cost, so output is produced at lowest attainable unit cost with no waste.\n  - The extract supports both: 18 000 producers with no firm above 0.4% and a graded, homogeneous product means no price-setting power; long-run returns close to the cost of capital means normal profit only, which is precisely the tangency condition.\n  - X-EFFICIENCY is also strong: a firm that lets costs drift above the minimum cannot raise price and simply makes a loss, so competitive pressure forces cost minimisation continuously.\nEvaluation — where the efficiency claim fails:\n• DYNAMIC EFFICIENCY is the clearest failure and the extract quantifies it: R&D at 0.3% of revenue is negligible. Normal profit in the long run leaves nothing to fund research; the product is homogeneous so there is nothing to differentiate; and any innovation is immediately imitated because information is perfect and entry is free, so the innovator cannot appropriate the return. Perfect competition is therefore statically efficient and dynamically weak — which is exactly why patents grant temporary monopoly.\n• EXTERNALITIES: the extract reports nitrate runoff imposed on water companies and downstream users. The model\'s efficiency claim is about PRIVATE costs — P = MC means price equals marginal PRIVATE cost, not marginal SOCIAL cost. With MSC above MPC the market over-produces relative to the social optimum, so a perfectly competitive market can be allocatively efficient in the textbook sense and socially inefficient at the same time. This is the single most important qualification.\n• ECONOMIES OF SCALE ARE FOREGONE: 18 000 small producers cannot capture technical, purchasing, marketing or financial economies. If the minimum efficient scale is large, a structure with fewer, larger firms would have a LOWER minimum ATC, so the perfectly competitive industry is productively efficient only relative to its own cost curve, not in absolute terms.\n• NO PRODUCT VARIETY: homogeneity is an assumption, not a benefit. Consumers who value choice get none, and in most markets differentiation is itself a consumer benefit.\n• THE ASSUMPTIONS ARE UNREALISTIC: perfect information, free entry and exit, no transaction costs, and infinitely many firms. Agricultural commodity markets come closer than almost any other, which is why the extract uses one, but even here information about future prices is imperfect and entry requires land.\n• EQUITY AND STABILITY: efficiency says nothing about distribution. Inelastic demand and supply make commodity prices highly volatile, so producers face unstable incomes — which is why agricultural markets are among the most heavily intervened in the world.\n• Judgement: perfect competition achieves ALLOCATIVE and PRODUCTIVE efficiency in long-run equilibrium, exactly as the model predicts and as the extract\'s return and share data suggest. It fails on DYNAMIC efficiency, demonstrably so at 0.3% R&D, and its allocative efficiency claim collapses in the presence of externalities. A supported conclusion should state which efficiency concept is being applied, note that the model is a benchmark rather than a description, and observe that a market can satisfy every condition of the model and still deliver a socially wrong outcome.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — accurate long-run tangency diagram, extract used on both sides, externality and dynamic points reached, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, efficiency conditions asserted rather than derived. L1 (1–5) — no or incorrect diagram, descriptive. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram and the information below, the extent to which price discrimination transfers welfare from consumers to producers.

Extract 1 (written for this practice question)
A concert venue sells the same seats through three channels.
  Early-bird tickets (on sale 6 months ahead): £28   Sold: 4 200
  Standard tickets:                              £52   Sold: 3 100
  Premium on-the-door tickets:                   £85   Sold:   900
  Marginal cost of admitting one extra attendee: £4
  Capacity: 8 200 seats
  Estimated sales at a single profit-maximising price of £49: 5 600 tickets
Resale is prevented by named tickets and identity checks at the door.`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: either the classic two-market third-degree diagram (a steep AR in the inelastic segment and a flat AR in the elastic one, each with its own MR, both equated to a common low MC, yielding a higher price in the inelastic market) OR a single demand curve with successive price bands shaded to show how discrimination converts consumer surplus into producer surplus. Label consumer surplus and producer surplus in both the single-price and the discriminating case; that comparison is the question.\n• Quantify from the extract. Under discrimination: total tickets 4 200 + 3 100 + 900 = 8 200 (capacity is exactly filled). Revenue = 4 200 x £28 + 3 100 x £52 + 900 x £85 = £117 600 + £161 200 + £76 500 = £355 300. Under a single price of £49: 5 600 x £49 = £274 400. Revenue rises by £80 900, or 29.5%.\n• Contribution above marginal cost: discriminating, 8 200 x £4 = £32 800 of variable cost, so contribution = £322 500. Single price, 5 600 x £4 = £22 400, contribution = £252 000. Producer surplus rises by £70 500.\n• The transfer mechanism: the 3 100 standard and 900 premium buyers would have paid £49 under a single price and now pay £52 and £85. The venue is capturing surplus that those consumers would otherwise have kept — which is precisely the purpose of discrimination and the sense in which the proposition is true.\n• The three conditions are satisfied here and worth stating: price-setting power, identifiable separable segments (booking lead time proxies for elasticity), and prevention of resale through named tickets and identity checks — without which arbitrage would collapse the price structure.\nEvaluation — why it is NOT simply a transfer:\n• OUTPUT EXPANSION is the decisive counter. Attendance rises from 5 600 to 8 200 — 2 600 more people, a 46% increase. Those 2 600 pay £28, which they value at or above that price, so they GAIN consumer surplus they would not have had at £49. Price discrimination here creates surplus as well as redistributing it.\n• ALLOCATIVE EFFICIENCY improves: every extra ticket sold at £28 is well above the £4 marginal cost, so each additional admission is a transaction worth more than it costs. A single price of £49 leaves 2 600 seats empty in a venue with a capacity of 8 200 — capacity that is a sunk cost and produces nothing. Filling it is close to a pure welfare gain.\n• The NET effect on consumers as a group is therefore ambiguous and must be argued, not asserted: 2 600 gain access they would not have had, 4 200 pay £21 less than the single price, and 4 000 pay £3 to £36 more. On these figures far more consumers gain than lose, which cuts directly against the proposition.\n• VIABILITY: with a high fixed cost and a marginal cost of only £4, a single price may not cover the fixed cost of staging the event at all. Discrimination can be what makes the event happen, in which case the counterfactual for everyone is no concert — the strongest possible defence and one worth stating explicitly.\n• EQUITY: segmentation is by booking lead time, not by income. A shift worker who cannot commit six months ahead pays £85 regardless of means, so the incidence is not reliably progressive.\n• BEHAVIOURAL COSTS: complex multi-tier pricing exploits bounded rationality and imposes search costs; some consumers pay more than they need to because they do not understand the structure. Dynamic and personalised pricing raise this concern much more sharply.\n• FIRST-DEGREE discrimination is the limiting case: perfect discrimination captures ALL consumer surplus while producing the allocatively efficient output. It is the sharpest illustration that efficiency and distribution can point in opposite directions, and that \'allocatively efficient\' does not mean \'good for consumers\'.\n• Judgement: price discrimination unambiguously transfers surplus from the inelastic segment to the producer — £70 500 of extra producer surplus on these figures — but it does not ONLY transfer. Because it expands output by 46% and fills capacity at a marginal cost of £4, it creates genuine welfare gains and benefits the majority of consumers. A supported conclusion should say that the transfer is real, that the efficiency gain is also real, and that the balance depends on how much output expands — here a great deal, which makes the net consumer effect plausibly positive.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — accurate diagram showing surplus areas, figures computed from the extract, both transfer and output expansion weighed, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, mechanism described without quantification. L1 (1–5) — no or incorrect diagram, definition only. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram and the information below, the extent to which regulation of a natural monopoly improves outcomes for consumers.

Extract 1 (written for this practice question, using published Ofwat and Ofgem decisions)
Water and sewerage companies in England and Wales are regional natural monopolies whose prices
are set by Ofwat at five-yearly price reviews. In the PR24 final determinations, published in
December 2024 and effective from 1 April 2025, average household bills were allowed to rise by
about £157 (roughly 36%) before inflation over 2025-2030 to fund investment, with increases
ranging from about 21% at some companies to about 53% at others.
In energy, Ofgem sets a quarterly cap on default tariffs: £1 663 a year for a typical dual-fuel
direct-debit household from July 2026 and £1 723 from 1 October 2026, a rise of 4%.
Around 35% of households were on fixed tariffs and unaffected by the cap change.`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: the natural monopoly diagram — LRAC falling across the whole range of demand with MC BELOW AC throughout; the unregulated profit-maximising outcome at Qm where MC = MR, with Pm read up to AR; MARGINAL COST pricing at the point where AR = MC, which is allocatively efficient but where P < AC so the firm makes a LOSS requiring subsidy; and AVERAGE COST pricing where AR = AC, which allows the firm to break even with only normal profit and is the usual regulatory compromise. All three price-quantity pairs labelled.\n• The case that regulation improves outcomes: an unregulated natural monopolist restricts output to Qm and prices well above marginal cost, transferring consumer surplus and creating a deadweight loss. Regulation forces price down towards average cost, raising output, cutting price and eliminating supernormal profit, without sacrificing the economies of scale that make single-firm supply efficient. RPI-X or CPIH-X capping adds an incentive for PRODUCTIVE efficiency, because the firm keeps any cost saving beyond X within the control period. Quality and investment standards prevent the firm from meeting the cap by degrading service.\n• Applied to the extract: Ofgem\'s cap protects the roughly 65% of households on default tariffs, who would otherwise be exposed to the full exercise of supplier pricing power over inert customers, and it is reset quarterly so it tracks costs rather than suppressing them. Ofwat\'s five-yearly review sets prices against an explicit investment programme, which is a regulator doing something a market could not — trading current price against future capacity.\nEvaluation:\n• INFORMATION ASYMMETRY is the fundamental weakness. The regulator must set X, or approve an investment programme, without knowing the firm\'s true costs, and the firm has every incentive to overstate them. The 21% to 53% spread in the extract shows how company-specific the judgements are and how much rests on the regulator\'s estimates.\n• A 36% real bill rise is a striking outcome for a regime whose purpose is to protect consumers. Two readings are available and both are creditable: either it is evidence of regulatory FAILURE — capture, or years of approved under-investment now being paid for — or it is evidence of regulation working correctly, forcing investment a profit-maximising unregulated monopolist would have deferred indefinitely. The strongest answers weigh both rather than asserting one.\n• PRICE CAPS AND UNDER-INVESTMENT: a cap creates an incentive to cut costs wherever quality is not directly measured, so assets whose deterioration is slow and invisible are neglected. This is a standard account of the sewage discharge record and is a direct consequence of the regulatory instrument, not of private ownership as such.\n• REGULATORY CAPTURE: the industry has concentrated interests, superior information and staff who move between regulator and regulated; consumers have diffuse interests and no comparable resources.\n• PERVERSE EFFECTS ON COMPETITION: the Ofgem cap has been argued to reduce tariff variety and to weaken switching, because the capped default became good enough to make search unattractive — so a policy designed to protect inert consumers may entrench the inertia. The 35% on fixed tariffs shows a segment still shopping around.\n• RATE-OF-RETURN regulation, the alternative, invites gold-plating (the Averch-Johnson effect) because a guaranteed return on capital rewards over-investment; price capping was adopted precisely to avoid this, and the two failure modes are mirror images.\n• ALTERNATIVES: structural separation of the network from competitive activities (as in energy generation and retail); public ownership, which removes the profit motive but risks X-inefficiency and politically timed investment — franchised passenger rail is returning to public ownership under the Passenger Railway Services (Public Ownership) Act 2024, beginning with South Western Railway on 25 May 2025; and yardstick competition, comparing regional monopolies against each other, which is what makes the 21% to 53% spread analytically useful.\n• Judgement: regulation clearly improves on the unregulated natural monopoly outcome — that comparison is not close, and the diagram shows why. Whether it delivers a GOOD outcome is a different question, and the extract supplies evidence on both sides: a 36% real increase alongside an investment programme, and a cap that protects the inert while possibly discouraging switching. A supported conclusion should compare regulation against the realistic alternatives rather than against an unattainable ideal, and should note that the choice of regulatory INSTRUMENT matters more than the decision to regulate.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — accurate natural monopoly diagram with MC and AC pricing distinguished, extract figures used on both sides, information asymmetry reached, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, argument one-sided. L1 (1–5) — no or incorrect diagram, descriptive. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether competition policy is the most effective way of protecting consumers.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• What competition policy covers in the UK: prohibition of anti-competitive agreements and cartels (Chapter I, Competition Act 1998); prohibition of abuse of a dominant position (Chapter II); merger control; market studies and market investigations; and, since the Digital Markets, Competition and Consumers Act 2024, an ex ante regime for firms designated as having strategic market status, plus direct consumer-protection enforcement powers with fines up to 10% of global turnover.\n• The theoretical case: market power restricts output below the allocatively efficient level, prices above marginal cost, transfers consumer surplus to producers and creates deadweight loss. Competition policy attacks the CAUSE, so it can restore the competitive outcome without the state having to set prices or run the industry — which is why it is preferred to direct regulation wherever competition is feasible.\n• Evidence that it works:\n  - Cartel enforcement: on 23 March 2023 the CMA fined ten construction firms about £59.3 million for rigging bids on 19 demolition and asbestos removal contracts worth over £150 million; in February 2025 it fined four banks over £100 million following settlements in its UK government bonds investigation.\n  - Market investigations: the CMA concluded its veterinary services investigation on 24 March 2026 with 14 remedies, including compulsory price lists, a £21 cap on prescription fees, mandatory disclosure of group ownership and a price comparison facility, with the Order to be made by 23 September 2026 and most remedies effective within three to twelve months.\n  - Merger control with conditions: clearance of Vodafone-Three on 5 December 2024 against a binding £11 billion network investment commitment and time-limited price caps.\n  - Ex ante digital regulation: Google designated with strategic market status in search on 10 October 2025, and Apple and Google in mobile platforms on 22 October 2025, where the two account for around 90-100% of UK mobile devices.\nEvaluation — limitations of competition policy:\n• IT ADDRESSES ONLY MARKET POWER. It does nothing about externalities, public goods, information failure in its own right, inequality, or macroeconomic instability — all of which harm consumers at least as much. A competitive market for a polluting good still over-produces.\n• DETECTION AND SPEED: cartels are secret and detection is uncertain; the demolition cartel ran for five and a half years before penalties. Investigations take years, by which time market conditions have moved on — a particular problem in digital markets, which is exactly why the ex ante SMS regime was created.\n• REMEDY DESIGN: behavioural remedies (price caps, commitments) require monitoring, expire, and can be circumvented; structural remedies are more effective but are used sparingly. The veterinary remedies are behavioural and leave the concentration that caused the problem untouched.\n• INFORMATION ASYMMETRY AND CAPTURE apply to the CMA as to any regulator, and defining the relevant market — the step on which most cases turn — is contested and consequential.\n• TRANSPARENCY REMEDIES CAN BACKFIRE: requiring published prices in a concentrated market can facilitate tacit collusion rather than competition.\n• THE TRADE-OFF WITH DYNAMIC EFFICIENCY: patents are a deliberate grant of monopoly, and breaking up a firm that innovates because of the prospect of supernormal profit can harm consumers over time. Competition policy must weigh static gains against dynamic losses, and the Vodafone-Three decision shows it doing so explicitly.\n• Alternatives and complements to weigh:\n  - SECTOR REGULATION (Ofgem, Ofwat, Ofcom) where competition is impossible because the activity is a natural monopoly. Competition policy has nothing to offer a regional water monopoly; only price control does.\n  - CONSUMER PROTECTION LAW AND INFORMATION REMEDIES: rights of redress, cooling-off periods, bans on drip pricing and fake reviews, mandatory disclosure. These address information asymmetry, which competition alone does not cure — a competitive market full of misled consumers does not protect them.\n  - BEHAVIOURAL REMEDIES: defaults, simplified tariffs and automatic switching, which work where the failure is inertia rather than market power. The Ofgem default tariff cap exists precisely because competition failed to discipline prices for inert customers.\n  - DIRECT PRICE CONTROL AND SUBSIDY where affordability, not competition, is the problem — targeted transfers protect low-income consumers far better than any competition remedy.\n  - PUBLIC OWNERSHIP, as with franchised passenger rail returning to public ownership under the Passenger Railway Services (Public Ownership) Act 2024 from 25 May 2025.\n• Judgement: competition policy is the most effective instrument for the specific harm it targets — market power — because it attacks the cause rather than the symptom and, where it succeeds, requires no continuing state involvement. It is not the most effective way of protecting consumers in general, because most consumer harm arises from information failure, inertia, affordability and externalities, which competition does not address, and because whole sectors are natural monopolies where competition is impossible by construction. The strongest conclusions argue that the right question is diagnostic — identify the failure first, then choose the instrument — and that the CMA\'s own evolution, from cartel enforcement towards market investigations, ex ante digital rules and direct consumer-protection powers, is evidence that competition authorities have reached the same conclusion.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) — sustained, balanced, well-evidenced argument with analysis and evaluation integrated and a conclusion that follows from it. L4 (16–20) — well developed and mostly balanced. L3 (11–15) — some analysis and evaluation, uneven balance. L2 (6–10) — largely descriptive. L1 (1–5) — fragmentary. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram, whether a market with few firms always produces a worse outcome for consumers than a market with many firms.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: the monopoly or oligopoly diagram (AR, MR, MC) showing Qm where MC = MR with Pm above MC, against the competitive outcome Qc where P = MC, with the deadweight loss triangle shaded and the surplus transfer rectangle distinguished. Strong answers add a second diagram — long-run perfect competition with price tangential to minimum ATC, or an LRAC curve showing that a few large firms operate at lower unit cost than many small ones.\n• The case that few firms IS worse:\n  - ALLOCATIVE INEFFICIENCY: with market power, profit maximisation gives MC = MR and therefore P > MC. Output is restricted, price is higher, consumer surplus is transferred and a deadweight loss is created.\n  - COLLUSION: with few firms, interdependence makes tacit or explicit collusion feasible. The prisoners\' dilemma shows why the cooperative high-price outcome is tempting; repetition and price transparency make it sustainable. The CMA fined ten construction firms about £59.3 million on 23 March 2023 for rigging bids on 19 demolition and asbestos removal contracts worth over £150 million.\n  - X-INEFFICIENCY: insulated from competitive pressure, costs drift above the attainable minimum.\n  - BARRIERS TO ENTRY entrench the position, so supernormal profit persists rather than being competed away.\n  - Fewer firms means less consumer CHOICE and weaker incentives to respond to preferences.\n• The case that few firms need NOT be worse:\n  - CONTESTABILITY is the decisive argument. Baumol showed that where entry and exit are cheap, the THREAT of hit-and-run entry disciplines an incumbent to price near average cost regardless of how few firms there are. Conversely, a market with many firms but high switching costs may not be competitive at all. What matters is barriers, not numbers.\n  - ECONOMIES OF SCALE: where the minimum efficient scale is large relative to demand, few firms is the EFFICIENT structure. Fragmenting it raises unit costs and prices. In a natural monopoly, one firm is the only efficient structure, and the remedy is regulation, not more firms.\n  - DYNAMIC EFFICIENCY: supernormal profit funds research and development. Perfectly competitive firms earn only normal profit and cannot innovate; Schumpeter argued that temporary market power is the engine of progress, and patents deliberately create it. A market with many firms may deliver low prices today and no new products tomorrow.\n  - OLIGOPOLY CAN BE FIERCELY COMPETITIVE: the kinked demand model predicts rigid prices, but non-price competition in quality, innovation, service and advertising can be intense, and price wars do occur. UK grocery retailing is concentrated — the four largest chains held around 65% of the Great Britain market in 2026, with Tesco at 27.3% — and yet has seen sustained real price competition and the rapid rise of Aldi to 10.2%, which displaced Morrisons. Concentration fell from around 73% in 2017, which is evidence that positions in a concentrated market can be contested.\n  - COUNTERVAILING POWER: concentrated buyers can offset concentrated sellers.\n  - MANY FIRMS IS NOT COSTLESS: monopolistic competition produces excess capacity, with every firm operating below minimum ATC, and resources absorbed in advertising near-identical products.\n• Evaluation and the decisive distinctions:\n  - STRUCTURE IS NOT CONDUCT. The number of firms is a structural fact; competition is a behavioural outcome. Concentration ratios flag where market power MIGHT exist but measure it badly — they ignore contestability, market definition, imports and the distribution of shares.\n  - TIME PERIOD: static analysis favours many firms; dynamic analysis can favour few. The verdict depends on the horizon chosen.\n  - WHICH EFFICIENCY: allocative and productive efficiency favour many firms; dynamic efficiency and scale economies can favour few. Answers that do not name the criterion cannot reach a supported conclusion.\n  - REGULATION CHANGES THE ANSWER: a regulated natural monopoly can deliver better outcomes than an unregulated fragmented market. The CMA cleared Vodafone-Three on 5 December 2024, reducing four mobile networks to three, accepting a binding £11 billion investment commitment and time-limited price caps — an explicit judgement that fewer, better-funded networks could serve consumers better.\n• Judgement: the word \'always\' makes the proposition false, and a single counterexample — a contestable duopoly, or a regulated natural monopoly against a fragmented high-cost alternative — settles the literal claim. The substantive conclusion is that the number of firms is a weak predictor of consumer outcomes; what matters is the height of barriers to entry, the contestability of the market, whether scale economies make few firms efficient, and the quality of regulation where competition is impossible. Strong answers note that this is precisely why competition authorities investigate conduct and barriers rather than simply counting firms.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) / L4 (16–20) / L3 (11–15) / L2 (6–10) / L1 (1–5); an accurate, integrated diagram is required for Level 4 and above. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether contestability matters more than the number of firms in determining market outcomes.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• The theory: Baumol\'s contestable markets analysis holds that a market is perfectly contestable when entry and exit are costless — in particular when there are NO SUNK COSTS. Under those conditions an incumbent that prices above average cost invites HIT-AND-RUN entry: a rival enters, takes the supernormal profit, and leaves before prices fall. Anticipating this, the incumbent prices at average cost and earns only normal profit. The competitive outcome is produced by the THREAT of entry alone, so a monopoly can behave like a competitive firm.\n• The radical implication: the structure-conduct-performance chain is broken. If contestability determines conduct, then the number of firms is largely irrelevant, and policy should target BARRIERS rather than concentration — deregulating entry, mandating access to essential facilities, making licences and slots tradeable, and prohibiting exclusionary contracts.\n• Supporting arguments and evidence:\n  - Two markets with identical concentration can behave completely differently depending on sunk costs. A regional airport route with leasable aircraft and tradeable slots can sustain a thin price-cost margin under a dominant carrier, while a regional water network with an unrecoverable pipe network sustains a wide one — the difference is sunk costs, not share.\n  - Concentrated markets with low entry barriers do show vigorous competition. UK grocery retailing had the four largest chains at around 65% of the Great Britain market in 2026, with Tesco at 27.3%, yet real price competition was sustained and Aldi rose to 10.2%, overtaking Morrisons, with the four-firm share down from around 73% in 2017.\n  - Conversely, a market with many firms but high consumer switching costs, strong brand loyalty or network effects can be uncompetitive despite fragmentation.\n  - Contestability explains why deregulation and liberalisation — opening routes, unbundling networks — produce competitive outcomes quickly even when the number of firms changes little.\nEvaluation — the limits of the contestability argument:\n• PERFECT CONTESTABILITY IS AS UNREALISTIC AS PERFECT COMPETITION. It assumes an entrant can replicate the incumbent\'s costs instantly, that consumers switch costlessly, and that the incumbent cannot respond before the entrant exits. Real entrants face learning curves, brand disadvantage and reputational barriers, and incumbents do respond.\n• INCUMBENTS DESTROY CONTESTABILITY STRATEGICALLY: limit pricing holds price just below the entrant\'s average cost; excess capacity is a credible threat to flood the market; loyalty contracts, frequent-flyer schemes and long-term exclusive supply agreements raise the effective cost of entry without changing physical sunk costs; predatory pricing, though illegal, builds a reputation that deters entry across every market the firm operates in.\n• SUNK COSTS ARE PERVASIVE AND OFTEN LARGE: R&D, brand-building advertising, specialised plant, regulatory approval. Where they are unavoidable, contestability is simply unavailable as a discipline. A natural monopoly network can never be made contestable, because duplication would raise total cost — so regulation, not entry threat, must do the work.\n• NUMBER OF FIRMS STILL MATTERS for things contestability is silent about: the feasibility of collusion rises sharply as numbers fall, because monitoring and retaliation are easier. The prisoners\' dilemma is escapable in a repeated game precisely when there are few enough players to observe each other, which is why cartel enforcement concentrates on concentrated markets. Consumer CHOICE and variety also depend on the number of suppliers, not on the threat of entry.\n• CONTESTABILITY DISCIPLINES PRICE BUT MAY NOT DISCIPLINE INVESTMENT: firms in a market kept contestable by cheap exit have little incentive to make long-lived commitments, and thin margins may not fund innovation. The Vodafone-Three clearance of 5 December 2024, with its binding £11 billion investment commitment and time-limited price caps, is an explicit judgement that investment capacity mattered more than the number of networks — an argument contestability theory cannot supply on its own.\n• COMPETITION AUTHORITIES USE BOTH: merger control starts from concentration (the Herfindahl-Hirschman Index) and then asks whether entry would be timely, likely and sufficient to counteract the merger. Neither measure alone is treated as decisive, which is the practical verdict on this question.\n• Judgement: contestability matters MORE than the raw number of firms, because it explains cases the structural approach gets wrong in both directions — competitive behaviour in concentrated markets and uncompetitive behaviour in fragmented ones — and because it identifies the variable policy can actually change, namely barriers to entry and sunk costs. But it does not displace the number of firms entirely: collusion, choice and investment all depend on how many firms there are, and where sunk costs are irreducible, contestability is unavailable and regulation must substitute. The strongest conclusions argue that contestability is the better SINGLE predictor of pricing conduct, that firm numbers remain the better predictor of collusion risk and variety, and that competition policy therefore rightly uses both.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) / L4 (16–20) / L3 (11–15) / L2 (6–10) / L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate whether the privatisation of natural monopolies has benefited UK consumers.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Context: the UK privatised telecommunications, gas, water, electricity and rail between 1984 and 1996, in most cases creating regulated private monopolies overseen by sector regulators — Ofcom, Ofgem, Ofwat and their predecessors. The counterfactual is not a competitive market but a STATE monopoly, and that is the comparison the question requires.\n• The case that consumers benefited:\n  - PRODUCTIVE EFFICIENCY AND X-EFFICIENCY: private ownership brings the threat of takeover, the discipline of capital markets and a residual claimant demanding minimum cost. State monopolies faced none of these and were widely judged overstaffed and slack.\n  - RPI-X PRICE CAPPING created a genuine incentive to cut costs, because the firm keeps savings beyond X within the control period — an incentive rate-of-return regulation lacks, since guaranteed returns on capital invite gold-plating (the Averch-Johnson effect).\n  - INVESTMENT OFF THE PUBLIC BALANCE SHEET: nationalised industries competed for capital against health and education in the annual spending round, so investment was hostage to fiscal cycles and political short-termism. Private firms can raise capital against future revenue.\n  - COMPETITION WHERE IT WAS POSSIBLE: privatisation permitted structural separation of the natural monopoly NETWORK from potentially competitive activities. Energy generation and retail supply, and telecommunications services, became genuinely competitive; mobile and broadband markets that did not exist under state ownership developed rapidly. This is the strongest evidence for the policy, because it delivered competition rather than merely changing the owner.\n  - CONSUMER CHOICE, service standards and innovation improved markedly in telecommunications.\n• The case that consumers did not benefit:\n  - PRICES: Ofwat\'s PR24 final determinations, published December 2024 and effective 1 April 2025, allowed average household water bills to rise by about £157 — roughly 36% before inflation — over 2025-2030, with company increases ranging from about 21% to about 53%. Ofgem\'s default tariff cap rose from £1 663 a year for a typical dual-fuel direct-debit household in July 2026 to £1 723 from 1 October 2026. Consumers face high and rising bills for an essential with no substitute.\n  - THE MONOPOLY WAS NOT REMOVED, only transferred: a regional water customer cannot choose a supplier, so the profit motive operates without competitive discipline and the firm\'s incentive is to extract, not to serve.\n  - UNDER-INVESTMENT AND QUALITY: price capping rewards cost-cutting wherever quality is not directly measured, so assets whose deterioration is slow and invisible are neglected. The sewage discharge record is the standard indictment, and it is a consequence of the regulatory instrument as much as of ownership.\n  - FINANCIAL ENGINEERING: high gearing and large dividend distributions in the water sector transferred value to shareholders while raising the cost of capital consumers ultimately fund.\n  - RAIL: fragmentation created complex interfaces and coordination failures, subsidy remained high, and franchised passenger services are now returning to public ownership under the Passenger Railway Services (Public Ownership) Act 2024 — South Western Railway on 25 May 2025, c2c on 20 July 2025, Greater Anglia on 12 October 2025 and West Midlands Trains on 1 February 2026 — which is a direct policy judgement that privatisation did not work in that sector.\n  - REGULATORY CAPTURE AND INFORMATION ASYMMETRY: the regulator must set price controls without knowing the firm\'s true costs, and the industry has concentrated interests and superior information.\n• Evaluation:\n  - IT DEPENDS ENTIRELY ON THE SECTOR, and this is the decisive structural point. Where the natural monopoly element could be separated from competitive activities — telecoms, energy generation and retail — consumers gained substantially, because privatisation delivered actual competition. Where it could not — water distribution, rail track — privatisation delivered a regulated private monopoly, and the gains depend wholly on the quality of regulation.\n  - ATTRIBUTION IS GENUINELY HARD: telecommunications was transformed by mobile technology, fibre and the internet, which would have happened under any ownership; water bills are rising because of environmental standards and ageing Victorian assets, not obviously because of ownership. Comparing outcomes with a counterfactual state industry is speculative.\n  - THE REAL VARIABLE MAY BE REGULATION, NOT OWNERSHIP: a well-regulated private monopoly and a well-governed public one can both perform adequately; both can fail. Evidence across European countries on rail and water ownership is genuinely mixed.\n  - DISTRIBUTION: rising essential-service bills are regressive, falling hardest on low-income households, so a policy that improved average efficiency can still have worsened outcomes for the poorest — social tariffs address this only partially.\n  - TIME PERIOD: early efficiency gains in the 1990s were real; the question is whether they have been sustained, or whether the easy cost savings were taken and the deferred investment is now being paid for in the 36% water bill increase.\n• Judgement: the strongest conclusions refuse a single verdict and argue sector by sector — substantial and durable consumer gains in telecommunications and in energy generation and retail, where competition genuinely replaced monopoly; ambiguous and increasingly questioned outcomes in water, where a private monopoly remains and bills are rising sharply against a poor environmental record; and an effective policy reversal in rail. A conclusion that identifies SEPARABILITY of the natural monopoly network from competitive activities as the variable that determined success, and the quality of regulation as the variable that determined outcomes where separation was impossible, reaches Level 5.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) / L4 (16–20) / L3 (11–15) / L2 (6–10) / L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram, whether monopolies should be broken up.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Diagram required: the monopoly diagram — AR with MR below and twice as steep, MC, profit-maximising output Qm where MC = MR with price Pm read up to AR, the competitive outcome Qc where P = MC, the deadweight loss triangle shaded and the surplus transfer rectangle distinguished from it. A second diagram showing a natural monopoly — LRAC falling across the whole range of demand, so that splitting the firm moves each part to a HIGHER point on the LRAC curve — is what allows the evaluation to be made properly.\n• The case FOR breaking up:\n  - ALLOCATIVE INEFFICIENCY: P > MC means output is restricted below the level at which consumers\' valuation equals resource cost. The deadweight loss is a pure welfare loss, and breaking the firm up moves price towards MC and output towards Qc.\n  - SURPLUS TRANSFER: consumer surplus is converted into producer surplus. Even though this is distributional rather than a welfare loss, it is the harm consumers and politicians care most about.\n  - X-INEFFICIENCY: without competitive pressure, costs drift above the attainable minimum, so the monopolist\'s actual ATC is higher than it need be.\n  - STRUCTURAL REMEDIES ARE SELF-ENFORCING: unlike price caps or conduct commitments, a divestment is permanent and requires no continuing monitoring, no information about the firm\'s costs and no regulator to be captured. This is the strongest procedural argument and explains why competition authorities regard structural remedies as superior in principle.\n  - Breaking up can restore CONTESTABILITY by removing control of an essential facility or distribution channel.\n• The case AGAINST:\n  - NATURAL MONOPOLY: where LRAC falls over the entire range of demand, one firm supplies the whole market at lower average cost than two. Splitting a water network or a rail track raises unit costs and duplicates infrastructure without creating meaningful consumer choice — consumers cannot pick between two sets of pipes. Here the correct remedy is REGULATION (Ofwat, Ofgem) or STRUCTURAL SEPARATION of the network from competitive activities, not fragmentation.\n  - ECONOMIES OF SCALE more generally: even outside pure natural monopoly, a large firm may operate at or near minimum efficient scale, and breaking it up pushes the parts back up the LRAC curve, raising prices.\n  - DYNAMIC EFFICIENCY: supernormal profit funds research and development. Patents are a deliberate, time-limited grant of monopoly precisely because innovation would otherwise be under-supplied. Breaking up a firm whose position rests on successful innovation punishes the behaviour policy wants to encourage, and may leave consumers with lower prices today and no new products tomorrow.\n  - NETWORK EFFECTS: in digital markets the product is more valuable the more people use it, so fragmentation destroys value for consumers directly. Splitting a platform into two smaller platforms can leave every user worse off.\n  - CONTESTABILITY: if entry and exit are cheap, a monopoly already behaves competitively, so break-up addresses a problem that does not exist. Market power should be measured by barriers, not by share.\n  - PRACTICALITY: divestment is slow, legally contested, and hard to design — the parts must be viable, and assets in integrated businesses are often not separable.\n• Better-targeted alternatives to weigh:\n  - EX ANTE CONDUCT REGULATION, which is the modern approach in digital markets: the CMA designated Google as having strategic market status in general search on 10 October 2025 and Apple and Google in their mobile platforms on 22 October 2025, where the two account for around 90-100% of UK mobile devices, opening the way to binding conduct requirements on app store terms, steering and interoperability without any structural change.\n  - MERGER CONTROL, preventing monopoly forming rather than dismantling it afterwards — with conditions where appropriate, as in the clearance of Vodafone-Three on 5 December 2024 against a binding £11 billion investment commitment and time-limited price caps.\n  - MARKET INVESTIGATIONS WITH TARGETED REMEDIES: the CMA concluded its veterinary services investigation on 24 March 2026 with 14 behavioural remedies, including compulsory price lists, a £21 prescription fee cap and mandatory disclosure of group ownership, leaving the ownership structure untouched.\n  - PRICE REGULATION, WINDFALL TAXATION, or opening the market by removing legal barriers.\n• Evaluation and judgement: the answer turns on WHY the monopoly exists. If it rests on a NATURAL cost advantage, break-up is actively harmful and regulation or network separation is correct. If it rests on INNOVATION protected by a time-limited patent, break-up destroys the incentive that created the product and the patent\'s expiry is the built-in remedy. If it rests on STRATEGIC EXCLUSION — acquiring rivals, exclusive contracts, control of an essential facility — break-up is well targeted and its permanence is a decisive advantage over behavioural remedies that expire and need policing. The strongest conclusions set out that diagnostic test, note that structural remedies are superior in principle but appropriate in only a narrow range of cases, and observe that modern competition policy has largely moved towards ex ante conduct rules precisely because break-up is so rarely the right tool.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) / L4 (16–20) / L3 (11–15) / L2 (6–10) / L1 (1–5); accurate, integrated diagram(s) required for Level 4 and above. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},

      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s), whether dynamic efficiency is more important than allocative efficiency.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content — not a checklist:\n• Define both precisely. ALLOCATIVE efficiency is a STATIC condition: resources are allocated so that P = MC, so the value consumers place on the last unit equals the marginal resource cost, and the sum of consumer and producer surplus is maximised at a point in time. DYNAMIC efficiency is about change over time: investment, innovation and technological progress that shift the LRAC curve DOWNWARD or create entirely new products.\n• Diagram: the monopoly diagram showing the deadweight loss from P > MC (the static allocative cost), alongside an LRAC curve shifting downward over time (the dynamic gain). Drawing both makes the trade-off visible and is what allows a Level 5 comparison — the question is whether a permanently lower cost curve outweighs a triangle of lost surplus each year.\n• The case that DYNAMIC efficiency matters more:\n  - MAGNITUDE OVER TIME: measured deadweight losses from monopoly are typically a small percentage of national income, while the cumulative gain from technological progress — antibiotics, semiconductors, mobile telecommunications, vaccines — is of a completely different order. A one-off allocative gain is dwarfed by compounding productivity growth.\n  - GROWTH COMPOUNDS: a permanent downward shift in the cost curve raises living standards indefinitely, whereas restoring P = MC raises welfare once.\n  - SCHUMPETER\'S ARGUMENT: the prospect of temporary monopoly profit is what makes innovation worth financing. \'Creative destruction\' means the relevant competition is not between firms setting prices today but between generations of technology, and a static snapshot of price against marginal cost misses the process entirely.\n  - POLICY REFLECTS THIS: patents deliberately grant a time-limited monopoly, accepting allocative inefficiency as the price of innovation. A society that maximised allocative efficiency would abolish them.\n  - MERGER PRACTICE REFLECTS IT TOO: the CMA cleared Vodafone-Three on 5 December 2024, reducing four mobile networks to three, in exchange for a binding £11 billion network investment commitment and time-limited price caps — an explicit trade of static competition for dynamic investment.\n  - PERFECT COMPETITION IS DYNAMICALLY WEAK: firms earn only normal profit, products are homogeneous, and any innovation is instantly imitated under perfect information, so the innovator cannot appropriate the return. The structure that maximises allocative efficiency is the one least able to innovate.\n• The case that ALLOCATIVE efficiency matters more:\n  - IT IS CERTAIN, MEASURABLE AND IMMEDIATE. The deadweight loss and the surplus transfer from a monopoly are real, ongoing and can be estimated; the dynamic benefit is speculative, hard to attribute and frequently claimed by firms precisely because it cannot be disproved. Accepting a dynamic-efficiency defence invites every dominant firm to make it.\n  - THE TRANSFER IS LARGE: in most monopoly cases the surplus transferred from consumers to producers substantially exceeds the deadweight loss triangle. Framing the harm as a small triangle understates what consumers actually lose, and distributional consequences fall hardest on low-income households in essential markets.\n  - INNOVATION DOES NOT REQUIRE MONOPOLY: much innovation comes from competitive pressure, from small entrants with no incumbent revenue to protect, and from publicly funded research. The INNOVATOR\'S DILEMMA describes incumbents suppressing innovations that would cannibalise existing products — so market power can retard progress as well as fund it.\n  - EMPIRICALLY THE RELATIONSHIP IS AN INVERTED U: some market power aids innovation, but entrenched dominance reduces it. Neither extreme maximises dynamic efficiency, which means the dynamic argument does not licence monopoly without limit.\n  - MONOPOLY PROFIT MAY NOT BE INVESTED: it can be distributed as dividends, used for share buybacks, or spent on advertising and lobbying that function as barriers to entry. Supernormal profit is a necessary condition for R&D, not a sufficient one.\n  - X-INEFFICIENCY compounds the static loss: a protected firm\'s costs drift above the attainable minimum, so consumers lose twice.\n• Evaluation and the decisive distinctions:\n  - IT DEPENDS ON THE MARKET. In pharmaceuticals, semiconductors and software, where R&D costs are enormous and imitation is cheap, dynamic efficiency dominates and time-limited protection is justified. In a mature market for a standardised necessity with little scope for innovation — water distribution, basic groceries — there is no dynamic gain to buy, so allocative efficiency is what matters and price regulation or competition enforcement is the right response.\n  - IT DEPENDS ON THE TIME HORIZON, which is really what the question is asking. Over one year allocative efficiency dominates; over twenty years dynamic efficiency almost certainly does. Answers that do not specify the horizon cannot reach a supported conclusion.\n  - THEY ARE NOT ALWAYS IN CONFLICT: competitive pressure can DRIVE innovation, so many markets deliver both. The trade-off is real only where innovation requires appropriable supernormal profit.\n  - BETTER POLICY DISSOLVES THE TRADE-OFF: patents with limited duration, public funding of basic research, prizes and advance market commitments all buy innovation without granting permanent market power — which suggests the choice is not as stark as the question implies.\n• Judgement: the strongest conclusions argue that dynamic efficiency is more important in the LONG RUN and in innovation-intensive markets, because compounding technological progress dominates any one-off static gain; that allocative efficiency matters more in mature, standardised, essential markets where there is nothing to innovate; and that the practical task of competition policy is not to rank the two in the abstract but to identify which is at stake in the market under examination — which is precisely why the CMA grants patents no scrutiny, clears mergers on investment grounds, and simultaneously regulates water prices.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) / L4 (16–20) / L3 (11–15) / L2 (6–10) / L1 (1–5); an accurate, integrated diagram is required for Level 4 and above. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.`},
    ]
  },

'2.1': {
    name: 'National & International Economy',
    questions: [
      /* ─────────────── 2 marks — points-marked ─────────────── */
      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'real GDP\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) Real GDP is the total value of goods and services produced in an economy over a period of time.\n(1) It is measured at constant prices, i.e. adjusted for inflation using a GDP deflator, so that changes reflect changes in the volume of output rather than in the price level.\n\nAccept: contrast with nominal GDP (current prices) as the second mark. Do not credit a definition that omits the inflation adjustment.` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'the claimant count\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) The claimant count is a measure of unemployment based on the number of people claiming unemployment-related benefits (in the UK, Jobseeker\'s Allowance and the out-of-work elements of Universal Credit).\n(1) It is an administrative measure, so it counts only those eligible for and claiming benefit, rather than everyone who is out of work and seeking it.\n\nAccept the contrast with the ILO/Labour Force Survey measure for the second mark.` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'the output gap\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) The output gap is the difference between actual real GDP and the estimated potential (trend) output of the economy.\n(1) A positive gap means actual output is above trend, with the economy operating beyond its sustainable capacity and inflationary pressure building; a negative gap means actual output is below trend, with spare capacity and typically higher unemployment.\n\nAccept expression as a percentage of potential output.` },

      { q: 'Using information from the stimulus material, identify two causes of cost-push inflation.\n\nStimulus material — written for this practice question\n"Crude oil and refined fuel prices rose sharply again over the summer of 2026 as conflict in the Middle East disrupted supply, and UK CPI inflation rose to 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026, up from 2.9% in July, with the Office for National Statistics attributing much of the increase to transport and motor fuels. Firms in the food processing sector also reported that the rise in the National Living Wage to £12.71 an hour in April 2026 had raised unit labour costs, and that a weaker pound against the dollar had increased the sterling cost of imported ingredients."', marks: 2, command: 'Identify',
        markScheme: `(1) Rising energy/fuel costs — higher crude and refined oil prices raise transport and production costs across the economy.\n(1) Rising wage costs — the increase in the National Living Wage to £12.71 an hour in April 2026 raised unit labour costs for employers.\n\nAlso credit: imported input costs rising because of a weaker pound (imported inflation). Award 1 mark per valid cause identified from the stimulus, maximum 2. Causes not drawn from the stimulus do not earn credit on this question.` },

      { q: `Refer to Fig. 1. Describe the relationship between the unemployment rate and the rate of wage growth shown.

Fig. 1 — written for this practice question
Country X: unemployment rate and annual growth in average weekly earnings, 2019-2025
  2019:  unemployment 6.4%  ;  wage growth 2.1%
  2020:  unemployment 7.8%  ;  wage growth 1.4%
  2021:  unemployment 7.1%  ;  wage growth 1.9%
  2022:  unemployment 5.6%  ;  wage growth 3.6%
  2023:  unemployment 4.8%  ;  wage growth 4.9%
  2024:  unemployment 4.2%  ;  wage growth 6.1%
  2025:  unemployment 4.5%  ;  wage growth 5.4%`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe',
        markScheme: `(1) There is an inverse (negative) relationship — as the unemployment rate falls, wage growth rises, and vice versa.\n(1) Supporting quantification from the data, e.g. unemployment fell from 7.8% in 2020 to 4.2% in 2024 while wage growth rose from 1.4% to 6.1%; the relationship holds in reverse in 2025, when unemployment rose to 4.5% and wage growth fell back to 5.4%.\n\nThis is a describe question — do not require explanation of why the relationship exists. Credit reference to the Phillips-curve shape, but it is not required.` },

      { q: `Using the data below, calculate the level of GDP using the expenditure method. You are advised to show your working.

Table 1 — written for this practice question
Country Y, 2025, £ billion at current prices
  Household consumption (C):        1 620
  Gross investment (I):               390
  Government spending (G):            560
  Exports (X):                        880
  Imports (M):                        950`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method (1 mark): GDP = C + I + G + (X − M) = 1 620 + 390 + 560 + (880 − 950).\nAnswer (1 mark): GDP = £2 500 billion (£2.5 trillion).\n\nAward 2 marks for the correct answer with or without working. Award 1 mark for correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for an answer of £3 450bn that adds imports rather than subtracting them. Accept the answer in £bn or £trillion.` },

      { q: `Using the data below, calculate the rate of CPI inflation in Country Z in the 12 months to June 2026, to two decimal places. You are advised to show your working.

Table 2 — written for this practice question
Consumer Prices Index, Country Z (2015 = 100)
  June 2025:  136.40
  June 2026:  140.60`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method (1 mark): inflation rate = (change in index / original index) × 100 = (140.60 − 136.40) / 136.40 × 100 = 4.20 / 136.40 × 100.\nAnswer (1 mark): 3.08% (to two decimal places).\n\nAward 2 marks for 3.08%. Award 1 mark for correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for an answer of 3.1% or 3% that is correct but not given to two decimal places as instructed.` },

      { q: `Using the data below, calculate Country W\'s current account deficit as a percentage of GDP, to two decimal places. You are advised to show your working.

Table 3 — written for this practice question
Country W, 2025
  Current account balance:  −£64.4 billion
  Nominal GDP:              £2 800 billion`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method (1 mark): (current account balance / nominal GDP) × 100 = 64.4 / 2 800 × 100.\nAnswer (1 mark): 2.30% of GDP (a deficit; accept −2.30%).\n\nAward 2 marks for 2.30%. Award 1 mark for correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for 2.3% not expressed to two decimal places. For context, the actual UK current account deficit was £22.1 billion, or 2.8% of GDP, in Quarter 1 2026 (ONS).` },

      /* ─────────────── 3 marks — points-marked ─────────────── */
      { q: 'Explain why nominal GDP can rise at the same time as real GDP falls.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) Nominal GDP measures output at current prices, so it rises if prices rise, if the volume of output rises, or both.\n(1) Real GDP strips out price changes by valuing output at constant prices, so it measures only the change in the volume of goods and services produced.\n(1) If the price level rises faster than the volume of output falls, the value of output measured at current prices increases while the volume produced has fallen — the economy is in recession in real terms despite a larger nominal figure. This is what makes real GDP the appropriate measure for judging economic performance over time.` },

      { q: 'Explain why hysteresis raises the natural rate of unemployment.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) Hysteresis is the idea that a temporary shock leaves permanent effects — here, that a period of high cyclical unemployment raises unemployment even after demand recovers.\n(1) Mechanism: workers who are unemployed for long periods lose firm-specific and general skills, fall behind technological change and lose the work habits and networks that lead to job offers; employers also use long spells of unemployment as a screening signal, so the long-term unemployed are passed over.\n(1) Consequence: those workers effectively leave the supply of employable labour, or become discouraged and drop out of the labour force altogether. Cyclical unemployment has therefore been converted into structural unemployment, so the natural rate (NAIRU) — the rate consistent with stable inflation — is higher than before the shock, and demand-side policy alone cannot reverse it.` },

      { q: 'Explain why a deficit on the current account of the balance of payments must be matched by a surplus elsewhere in the balance of payments.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) The balance of payments is an accounting identity that records all transactions between residents of a country and the rest of the world, so it must balance overall (subject to a net errors and omissions item).\n(1) A current account deficit means the country is spending more on imports of goods, services and income payments than it earns from exports and income receipts — it is a net borrower from the rest of the world.\n(1) That excess spending has to be financed: it is matched by net inflows on the capital and financial accounts — foreigners buying domestic assets (FDI, portfolio investment, bank deposits and gilts) or the central bank running down its foreign exchange reserves. The identity therefore means a current account deficit is sustainable only for as long as foreigners are willing to hold the country\'s assets.` },

      /* ─────────────── 4 marks — explain with a diagram ─────────────── */
      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how a negative output gap arises.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: AD/AS with price level on the vertical axis and real output on the horizontal axis; AD, SRAS and a vertical LRAS at potential output Yfe (or a business cycle diagram showing actual GDP below the trend line).\n(1) AD shifts left (AD to AD1) following, for example, a fall in consumer confidence or in exports, so equilibrium real output falls to Y1.\n(1) Identification of the gap: the horizontal distance between Y1 and Yfe (or the area between actual and trend GDP) is the negative output gap.\n(1) Explanation of consequences: the economy is operating below productive potential, with spare capacity, idle capital and cyclical unemployment, and with downward pressure on the price level.\n\nAward marks for a correctly labelled and correctly used diagram; a diagram alone with no explanation is a maximum of 2 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how demand-pull inflation occurs.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: AD/AS with price level and real output axes, AD intersecting an upward-sloping SRAS close to, or on, a vertical LRAS.\n(1) AD shifts right (AD to AD1) — causes include a cut in Bank Rate, a fiscal loosening, a rise in consumer confidence or stronger export demand.\n(1) The new equilibrium shows the price level rising from P to P1.\n(1) Explanation: with the economy near capacity, firms cannot easily expand output, so excess demand bids up prices and wages rather than raising real output — the closer the starting point is to LRAS, the greater the share of the shift that appears as inflation rather than growth.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how a sharp rise in world energy prices can cause stagflation.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: AD/AS with AD, SRAS and SRAS1 to the left of SRAS.\n(1) Higher energy costs raise firms\' costs of production at every level of output, shifting SRAS left.\n(1) New equilibrium: the price level rises from P to P1 while real output falls from Y to Y1.\n(1) Explanation: this is stagflation — rising inflation together with falling output and rising unemployment — and it creates a genuine policy dilemma, because tightening policy to control the inflation deepens the fall in output. The rise in UK CPI inflation to 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026, driven largely by transport and motor fuels after conflict-related increases in crude and refined energy prices, is a recent example of the supply-side channel.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the trade-off shown by the short-run Phillips curve.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: inflation rate on the vertical axis, unemployment rate on the horizontal axis, with a downward-sloping short-run Phillips curve (SRPC).\n(1) Correct identification of the inverse relationship: lower unemployment is associated with higher inflation and vice versa.\n(1) Movement along the curve shown between two labelled points, e.g. from (U1, P1) to (U2, P2).\n(1) Explanation of the mechanism: as unemployment falls, the labour market tightens, workers\' bargaining power rises and wage growth accelerates; firms pass higher unit labour costs on as higher prices. Credit the observation that this is a short-run relationship that holds only while inflation expectations are unchanged.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, why monetarists argue the long-run Phillips curve is vertical.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: vertical LRPC at the natural rate of unemployment (NAIRU), with at least two short-run Phillips curves, SRPC1 and SRPC2, the latter further from the origin.\n(1) Starting at the NAIRU, expansionary policy moves the economy up along SRPC1 — unemployment falls below the natural rate and inflation rises.\n(1) Workers then revise their inflation expectations upward, so the SRPC shifts out to SRPC2 and unemployment returns to the NAIRU at a higher rate of inflation.\n(1) Conclusion: there is no long-run trade-off — the only lasting effect of repeated demand expansion is accelerating inflation, so unemployment can be reduced permanently only by supply-side measures that lower the natural rate itself.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the effect of a rise in the marginal propensity to import on the circular flow of income.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: circular flow with households and firms, the inner flows of income and expenditure, injections (I, G, X) entering and withdrawals (S, T, M) leaving.\n(1) A higher marginal propensity to import increases the M withdrawal at each level of income, shown as a larger leakage arrow.\n(1) Withdrawals now exceed injections at the original level of income, so national income contracts until W = J again at a lower equilibrium.\n(1) Explanation: because a larger share of each extra pound of income leaks abroad, the multiplier k = 1/(MPS + MPT + MPM) falls, so any given injection raises national income by less — one reason why small, very open economies have smaller multipliers than large, relatively closed ones.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how sustained net inward migration of working-age people affects an economy\'s productive potential.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: either a PPF shifting outward, or AD/AS with LRAS shifting right from LRAS to LRAS1 (credit a diagram that also shifts AD right).\n(1) Net inward migration of working-age people increases the quantity of labour available, one of the factors of production determining potential output.\n(1) LRAS (or the PPF) therefore shifts right, raising the maximum sustainable level of output the economy can produce.\n(1) Explanation: this raises potential GDP and, if the migrants\' skills match vacancies, can also raise average productivity and reduce wage pressure in shortage occupations. Credit the qualification that GDP per capita rises only if the additional output per migrant exceeds existing output per head, and that AD also rises because migrants consume, so the net effect on the price level depends on which curve shifts further.` },

      /* ─────────────── 8 marks — evaluate using the stimulus ─────────────── */
      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which the Consumer Prices Index is an accurate measure of the change in the cost of living faced by households.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"UK CPI inflation was 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026, up from 2.9% in July, with the increase driven largely by transport and motor fuels. CPIH, which includes owner occupiers' housing costs, rose by 3.3% over the same period. A consumer group pointed out that the CPI basket is based on the spending of the average household, that low-income households spend a far larger share of their budget on food and domestic energy than the average, and that CPI excludes mortgage interest payments even though Bank Rate stood at 3.75% following the Monetary Policy Committee's decision of 16 September 2026."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• CPI is a well-constructed measure: a weighted basket of around 700 representative goods and services, weights revised annually from the Living Costs and Food Survey, prices collected monthly and internationally comparable, which is why it is the target variable in the Bank of England\'s 2% remit.\n• Limitation shown in the stimulus — it is an average. A household spending a large share of income on food and domestic energy experiences a different inflation rate from the headline when those components move differently, so CPI can understate the squeeze on low-income households during an energy shock.\n• Limitation shown in the stimulus — coverage. CPI excludes owner occupiers\' housing costs and mortgage interest; the gap between CPI at 3.1% and CPIH at 3.3% in the 12 months to August 2026 is direct evidence that the choice of measure changes the number, and with Bank Rate at 3.75% mortgage costs are a material part of many households\' outgoings.\n• Other technical limitations: substitution bias (consumers switch to cheaper alternatives faster than weights are updated, so CPI can overstate inflation); quality change, which is hard to separate from price change in goods such as electronics; and a sampling error on the underlying survey.\n• Evaluation: CPI is accurate as a measure of what it sets out to measure — the average change in the price of a fixed representative basket. It is less accurate as a measure of any individual household\'s cost of living, and the appropriate response is to publish complementary measures (CPIH, RPI, income-decile inflation estimates) rather than to abandon it. For policy purposes, consistency over time matters more than perfect coverage.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — developed explanation using specific evidence from the stimulus, with a supported judgement on how far the measure is accurate and for what purpose. L1 (1–4) — limited or generic points about CPI with little use of the stimulus and assertion rather than evaluation.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which the data show an improving labour market.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"Official estimates put the UK unemployment rate for those aged 16 and over at 4.9% in May to July 2026, up 0.2 percentage points on the year but largely unchanged on the quarter. The employment rate for those aged 16 to 64 was 75.1%, down 0.1 percentage points on the year, and the economic inactivity rate fell to 20.9%. The number of payrolled employees fell by 84,000 (0.3%) over the year and by 39,000 over the quarter. The Office for National Statistics has cautioned that Labour Force Survey estimates are currently less reliable than usual and should be considered alongside other sources."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Evidence of improvement: the economic inactivity rate fell to 20.9%, which means people are returning to the labour force rather than withdrawing from it; the unemployment rate was largely unchanged on the quarter rather than climbing; and a rising participation rate expands the potential labour supply and so the economy\'s productive capacity.\n• Evidence against improvement: unemployment at 4.9% was up 0.2 percentage points on the year, the employment rate fell 0.1 percentage points on the year, and payrolled employees fell by 84,000 (0.3%) over the year and 39,000 over the quarter — falling employee numbers alongside rising unemployment is a weakening, not a strengthening, labour market.\n• Interpretation: a fall in inactivity combined with a rise in unemployment can mean that people re-entering the labour force have not yet found work, which is a sign of increased supply rather than of stronger labour demand. The payrolled employee series, drawn from PAYE administrative records, is the most reliable indicator here and it points downwards.\n• What the data omit: hours worked, underemployment, real wage growth, vacancies and the duration of unemployment spells. A labour market can show a stable headline rate while hours and real pay fall, so these indicators cannot settle the question alone.\n• Evaluation: the stimulus itself supplies the decisive caveat — the ONS warns that LFS estimates are currently less reliable than usual, so the survey-based rates (4.9%, 75.1%, 20.9%) carry wide margins of error and should be read alongside PAYE data. On balance the data suggest a gently loosening labour market rather than an improving one, but the strength of that conclusion is limited by the reliability of the source.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — developed use of specific figures from the stimulus on both sides, with a judgement that recognises the data-reliability caveat. L1 (1–4) — restates figures without interpretation, or asserts a conclusion the data do not support.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which the growth shown has improved living standards.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"UK real GDP rose by 0.4% in Quarter 2 2026, following growth of 0.6% in Quarter 1, and was 1.2% higher than a year earlier. Growth was driven mainly by a 1.2% rise in gross fixed capital formation and a 0.3% rise in household consumption, while government consumption fell 0.3%. Over the same period, population growth of around 0.7% a year meant GDP per head grew far more slowly than GDP, CPI inflation was 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026, and a think tank noted that the gains from growth had been concentrated in London and the South East."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Case that living standards improved: real GDP grew 1.2% on the year, and real GDP is already adjusted for inflation, so the volume of goods and services available has risen. Growth driven by investment (gross fixed capital formation up 1.2%) is the composition most likely to raise productivity and therefore future real incomes, rather than a debt-financed consumption boom.\n• Case against: with population growth of roughly 0.7% a year, GDP per head grew by only about half a percentage point — the average person is barely better off. GDP per capita, not GDP, is the relevant measure for living standards.\n• Distribution: the stimulus notes gains concentrated in London and the South East. GDP is an aggregate and says nothing about who receives the income, so national growth is consistent with stagnant or falling real incomes for many households.\n• Other limitations of GDP as a welfare measure: it excludes unpaid household and voluntary work, ignores the value of leisure, treats defensive expenditure and the clean-up of pollution as output, and takes no account of environmental depletion or of the quality of public services.\n• Evaluation: growth is a necessary but not sufficient condition for rising living standards. The decisive test is real GDP per head after tax and transfers, alongside broader indicators. On the evidence given, growth in the first half of 2026 was real but slow, thinly spread per head and unevenly distributed, so the improvement in living standards was modest at best.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — uses specific figures from the stimulus, distinguishes GDP from GDP per head and reaches a supported judgement. L1 (1–4) — generic list of GDP limitations with little reference to the stimulus.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which the current account deficit shown is a sign of economic weakness.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"The UK current account deficit narrowed by £5.0 billion to £22.1 billion, or 2.8% of GDP, in Quarter 1 2026. Excluding volatile trade in precious metals, the underlying deficit was £15.1 billion, or 1.9% of GDP. The UK has run a current account deficit in every year since 1984, financed largely by inflows into gilts, London property and UK equities. Sterling traded at around 1.16 euros in mid-September 2026, and the Bank of England held Bank Rate at 3.75% on 16 September 2026, above the European Central Bank's deposit rate of 2.50%."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Case that it signals weakness: a deficit means imports of goods, services and income payments exceed exports and receipts, which can reflect weak price and non-price competitiveness, low productivity or a persistent goods-trade deficit. Sustained deficits build up foreign ownership of domestic assets and a growing net income outflow, which can become self-reinforcing.\n• Case that it does not: at 2.8% of GDP, and only 1.9% excluding precious metals, the deficit is modest by historical standards and narrowed by £5.0 billion in the quarter. A deficit can reflect strength — an economy growing faster than its trading partners imports more, and capital inflows into gilts, property and equities reflect confidence in UK assets rather than distress.\n• The financing matters more than the deficit. Inflows attracted by a Bank Rate of 3.75%, above the ECB\'s 2.50% deposit rate, are interest-sensitive hot money that can reverse quickly; long-term FDI is far more stable financing for the same deficit. The composition of the financial account is therefore the better indicator of risk.\n• Measurement: the stimulus shows the headline figure distorted by precious-metals trade. The gap between £22.1 billion and £15.1 billion is a warning against treating a single quarter\'s headline as a verdict on the economy.\n• Evaluation: a deficit of this size, in a currency the country issues itself, with deep and liquid asset markets and a floating exchange rate that can adjust, is not in itself evidence of weakness. It would become a concern if it widened persistently, if it were financed increasingly by short-term flows, or if it reflected a deteriorating trend in productivity rather than a cyclical divergence in growth rates.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — uses the specific figures, distinguishes the size of the deficit from how it is financed, and judges. L1 (1–4) — asserts that deficits are bad, or lists causes without using the stimulus.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which the rise in inflation described was caused by supply-side factors.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"At its meeting ending on 16 September 2026, the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee voted 6-3 to hold Bank Rate at 3.75%, with three members voting for an increase to 4%. The Committee noted that protracted conflict in the Middle East had contributed to further increases in crude and refined energy prices, which remained more volatile and higher than before the conflict, and that UK CPI inflation had risen to 3.1% in August and was likely to rise further over coming quarters. The majority judged that domestic activity and tight financial conditions were restraining inflation, while warning that the risk of second-round effects was growing."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Case for supply-side causation: the proximate driver identified by the MPC is higher crude and refined energy prices following conflict in the Middle East. Higher energy costs raise firms\' costs at every level of output, shifting SRAS left, which raises the price level while reducing real output — the textbook cost-push case, consistent with the ONS attributing the August rise largely to transport and motor fuels.\n• Supporting evidence: the majority judged that domestic activity and tight financial conditions were restraining inflation. If domestic demand were the driver, the MPC would not describe it as a restraining force, and Bank Rate at 3.75% is itself restrictive.\n• Case for demand-side contribution: inflation at 3.1% is well above the 2% target, and three of nine members voted to raise Bank Rate to 4%, which implies they saw enough demand pressure, or enough risk of it, to justify tightening. A tight labour market with unemployment at 4.9% can sustain wage growth that feeds through to prices.\n• Second-round effects blur the distinction. An external cost shock becomes a domestic inflation problem if workers bargain for higher nominal wages to protect real pay and firms pass those costs on — the wage-price spiral the MPC explicitly warns about. At that point the cause of continuing inflation is expectations, not the original shock.\n• Evaluation: the initial impulse was clearly supply-side and external, which is why the split vote is defensible — monetary policy cannot lower the price of oil, and tightening into a supply shock deepens the output loss. But whether inflation persists depends on domestic wage and price setting, so the longer it lasts, the less the supply-side explanation accounts for it.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — uses stimulus evidence, distinguishes the initial shock from its persistence and judges. L1 (1–4) — describes cost-push and demand-pull inflation without applying the stimulus.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which GDP per capita is a reliable indicator of economic welfare.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"In the 2025 Human Development Report, the United Kingdom recorded a Human Development Index of 0.946 and ranked 13th of 193 countries, while Iceland topped the index at 0.972, ahead of Norway and Switzerland at 0.970. The HDI combines life expectancy, years of schooling and gross national income per head at purchasing power parity. Several countries with higher GNI per head than the United Kingdom rank below it on the HDI, and the report notes that inequality-adjusted HDI scores are substantially lower than headline scores in the most unequal economies."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Case for reliability: income per head is strongly correlated with almost every other welfare indicator — nutrition, sanitation, schooling and life expectancy all rise with it — because higher output finances both private consumption and the tax revenue that funds public services. It is available frequently, for almost every country, and is internationally comparable when converted at purchasing power parity.\n• Case against, from the stimulus: the HDI deliberately combines income with life expectancy and schooling precisely because income alone is insufficient, and the report notes that some countries with higher GNI per head than the UK rank below it — direct evidence that income and human development can diverge.\n• Distribution: GDP per capita is a mean, so it is raised by gains concentrated at the top. The stimulus notes that inequality-adjusted HDI scores fall substantially in the most unequal economies, which is the same objection applied to a broader index.\n• Coverage: GDP omits unpaid household work, subsistence production and the informal economy (a large share of output in low-income countries), ignores leisure, counts defensive expenditure as output and takes no account of environmental depletion, so two economies with identical GDP per head can offer very different lives.\n• Evaluation: GDP per capita is a reliable indicator of the material resources available per person and an unreliable indicator of welfare itself. It is best used as one component of a dashboard — HDI, inequality-adjusted HDI, the Gini coefficient, measures of health and of environmental quality — rather than as a summary statistic in its own right. Note too that the HDI has limits of its own: three indicators, equally weighted by convention, and published with a lag (the 2025 report uses 2023 data).\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — uses the HDI evidence in the stimulus, evaluates both the measure and its alternatives, and judges. L1 (1–4) — lists limitations of GDP without reference to the stimulus.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which an ageing population threatens long-run economic growth.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"In Country P, the share of the population aged 65 and over is projected to rise from 19% in 2026 to 26% by 2046, while the working-age population is projected to shrink by 4%. State spending on pensions, health and social care currently absorbs 42% of government expenditure. The government's independent fiscal watchdog projects that, on unchanged policy, public sector net debt would rise from 94% of GDP to above 130% of GDP by 2046. Labour productivity growth in Country P has averaged 0.5% a year since 2010, compared with 2.1% a year in the two decades before 2008."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Case that it threatens growth: a working-age population shrinking by 4% reduces the quantity of labour, one of the determinants of potential output, so LRAS shifts left, or grows more slowly than it otherwise would. A rising dependency ratio means fewer workers financing more dependants, and the debt projection rising above 130% of GDP implies higher taxes or lower spending, either of which can dampen incentives and demand.\n• Composition of spending: with 42% of government expenditure already going to pensions, health and social care, demographic pressure crowds out capital spending on infrastructure, education and research — precisely the spending most likely to raise long-run growth.\n• Case that the threat is overstated: growth depends on output per worker as much as on the number of workers. The stimulus shows productivity growth of 0.5% a year against 2.1% before 2008; closing even part of that gap would outweigh a 4% fall in the labour force. Policy can also raise participation — later retirement, higher female participation, net inward migration of working-age people — and automation can substitute capital for scarce labour.\n• Offsetting effects: older workers have accumulated human capital and experience; an economy with more older consumers reallocates demand rather than losing it; and higher life expectancy is itself a welfare gain, so the "threat" is partly the cost of a success.\n• Evaluation: the demographic change is near-certain, but its effect on growth is not, because it is mediated by productivity and participation, both of which are policy-sensitive. The larger risk in the data is fiscal rather than growth-related — the debt path, not the output path, is what forces adjustment. The projection also rests on unchanged policy, an assumption that will not hold over twenty years.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — uses the specific projections, separates the labour-quantity effect from the productivity effect and judges. L1 (1–4) — describes the dependency ratio with little use of the data and no evaluation.` },

      /* ─────────────── 12 marks — evaluate, asterisked ─────────────── */
      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether low unemployment should be the government's most important macroeconomic objective.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"In September 2026 the UK unemployment rate stood at 4.9% for May to July, CPI inflation was 3.1% in the 12 months to August, real GDP grew 0.4% in Quarter 2, the current account deficit was 2.8% of GDP in Quarter 1 and public sector net debt was 93.8% of GDP at the end of August. The Bank of England's remit is to target CPI inflation of 2%, and subject to that to support the government's objectives for growth and employment. The Monetary Policy Committee held Bank Rate at 3.75% on 16 September 2026 by six votes to three."`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Case for prioritising low unemployment: unemployment imposes the heaviest and most concentrated costs — lost output that can never be recovered, lost tax revenue and higher benefit spending, the erosion of skills through hysteresis, and documented effects on physical and mental health, family stability and crime. Those costs fall on identifiable individuals rather than being spread thinly, and hysteresis means a failure to act is not reversible later.\n• Case against: the remit in the stimulus makes price stability the primary objective and employment secondary, and there is a reason for that ordering. Uncontrolled inflation erodes real incomes across the whole population, arbitrarily redistributes from savers to borrowers, distorts the price signals on which resource allocation depends, and, once expectations become unanchored, is costly to reverse — the disinflation of the early 1980s required a deep recession and mass unemployment.\n• The relationship between the two objectives. In the short run the Phillips curve implies a trade-off, so pushing unemployment below the NAIRU raises inflation; in the long run monetarists argue the LRPC is vertical, so the attempt buys no lasting reduction in unemployment at all, only higher inflation. On that view low inflation is a precondition for sustainably low unemployment rather than a rival to it.\n• Context matters. At 4.9%, unemployment in the stimulus is close to most estimates of the UK natural rate, so the scope for demand-side policy to reduce it further is limited; inflation at 3.1% is more than a percentage point above target and, on the MPC\'s own assessment, likely to rise further. On these numbers the binding constraint is inflation, not unemployment — which is why six of nine members held rather than cut.\n• Other objectives have claims too: debt at 93.8% of GDP limits fiscal space; a current account deficit of 2.8% of GDP requires continued financing; and long-run growth depends on productivity, which no demand-side objective addresses.\n• Evaluation: the objectives are not independent and no single one can be permanently paramount. A defensible judgement is that the priority should be state-dependent — unemployment first in a deep recession with a large negative output gap and anchored expectations, price stability first when inflation is above target and unemployment is near the natural rate, as in the September 2026 data.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — sustained analysis with chains of reasoning, specific use of the stimulus data and a judgement that follows from the argument. L2 (5–8) — both sides explained with some application but limited evaluation. L1 (1–4) — descriptive, one-sided, little use of the stimulus. Asterisked question: marks are also available for quality of extended response — a logically structured argument, accurate specialist terminology and a supported conclusion.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether the rise in CPI inflation described should concern policymakers.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"UK CPI inflation rose to 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026, up from 2.9% in July, against a target of 2%. CPI rose 0.5% on the month, compared with 0.3% in August 2025, and the increase was driven largely by transport, particularly motor fuels. The Monetary Policy Committee, holding Bank Rate at 3.75% on 16 September 2026, judged that inflation was likely to rise further over coming quarters and that the risk of second-round effects was growing, while noting that domestic activity and tight financial conditions were restraining inflation. Unemployment was 4.9% and real GDP grew 0.4% in Quarter 2 2026."`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Reasons for concern: at 3.1% inflation is more than a percentage point above the 2% target and rising; the monthly rate of 0.5% against 0.3% a year earlier shows momentum rather than a base effect; and the MPC expects it to rise further. Inflation above target erodes real wages, redistributes arbitrarily from savers and fixed-income households to borrowers, creates menu and shoe-leather costs and distorts relative price signals.\n• The expectations channel is the serious risk. The MPC explicitly warns of growing second-round effects: if workers bargain for higher nominal pay to protect real income and firms pass the cost on, an external energy shock becomes an embedded domestic wage-price spiral. Once expectations de-anchor, restoring them requires a larger rise in Bank Rate and a bigger sacrifice of output — the sacrifice ratio.\n• Reasons for less concern: the driver identified is transport and motor fuels, a volatile external component. Energy price shocks are self-reversing in the 12-month rate — if fuel prices simply stop rising, the contribution drops out of the annual comparison a year later. Monetary policy cannot influence world oil prices, so tightening to offset them imposes output costs for no effect on the cause.\n• Policy is already restrictive. Bank Rate at 3.75% is well above the 2% target and the MPC judges that domestic activity and financial conditions are restraining inflation. Monetary policy works with a lag of around 18 months to two years, so tightening in response to today\'s reading risks hitting the economy when the shock has already passed — a case for holding rather than raising.\n• The cost of over-reaction: real GDP grew only 0.4% in Quarter 2, and raising rates into a supply shock deepens the fall in output and raises unemployment without addressing the source of the inflation. The 6-3 split is precisely a disagreement about the balance of these risks.\n• Evaluation: the level of inflation is less worrying than its persistence and its composition. A defensible judgement is that a supply-driven overshoot from a restrictive starting point warrants vigilance and clear communication to keep expectations anchored, rather than immediate tightening — but that the case for action strengthens sharply if wage growth accelerates or if the overshoot persists into 2027.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — sustained analysis with the expectations channel developed, specific stimulus evidence and a supported judgement. L2 (5–8) — costs of inflation explained with some application. L1 (1–4) — lists costs of inflation, no evaluation. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether the persistent current account deficit described is unsustainable.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"Country Q has run a current account deficit every year for four decades. In 2025 the deficit was 2.8% of GDP, financed by net inflows into government bonds and domestic property. Net foreign ownership of the country's assets has risen from 12% of GDP in 2000 to 41% in 2025, and the net primary income balance has moved from a surplus of 1.4% of GDP to a deficit of 0.6%. The country issues debt in its own floating currency, and its central bank's policy rate of 3.75% is above that of its main trading partner."`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Case for unsustainability: a deficit every year for four decades means the stock of external liabilities compounds, and the data show exactly that — net foreign ownership rising from 12% to 41% of GDP. The consequence is visible in the primary income balance, which has swung from a surplus of 1.4% of GDP to a deficit of 0.6%: interest, profits and dividends now flow out, which widens the current account further. That is a self-reinforcing mechanism, not a stable equilibrium.\n• The financing is the vulnerability. Inflows into bonds and property attracted partly by a policy rate above that of the main trading partner are interest-sensitive portfolio flows, not long-term FDI in productive capacity. If the interest differential closes or sentiment turns, the flows can reverse abruptly, forcing a sharp depreciation, imported inflation and a rise in interest rates to defend the currency — the classic sudden-stop.\n• Case for sustainability: 2.8% of GDP is a modest deficit and the stock has accumulated over forty years without crisis, which is itself evidence that the market has been willing to hold these assets. Crucially, the country issues debt in its own floating currency, so it cannot be forced into default on external debt in the way an emerging economy borrowing in dollars can, and the exchange rate provides an automatic adjustment mechanism: depreciation raises export competitiveness and reduces import demand.\n• Deficits can reflect strength. A country growing faster than its partners imports more; inflows into property and bonds reflect confidence, deep and liquid capital markets and the rule of law. The relevant question is not the deficit\'s existence but what the borrowing finances — imported capital goods that raise future output are very different from imported consumption.\n• Adjustment mechanisms: the Marshall-Lerner condition must hold for depreciation to improve the balance (PEDx + PEDm > 1), and the J-curve means improvement is delayed; supply-side policy to raise productivity is the durable route but works only over years.\n• Evaluation: a defensible judgement is that the deficit is sustainable in the narrow sense that it can continue to be financed, but that the trend is not benign — the deterioration in the primary income balance means the deficit is becoming structurally harder to close. Sustainability is a statement about willingness to lend, and that willingness is conditional, so the honest answer is that it is sustainable until it is not.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — develops the stock-flow mechanism using the data and reaches a supported judgement. L2 (5–8) — explains causes and consequences with some application. L1 (1–4) — asserts that deficits are or are not a problem. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether weak productivity growth is the main cause of slow economic growth.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"Real GDP in Country R grew 1.2% in the year to mid-2026. Output per hour worked has grown by an average of 0.4% a year since 2010, against 2.2% a year between 1990 and 2007. Business investment has averaged 9.4% of GDP, compared with an average of 12.8% across comparable economies. The total number of hours worked rose 0.8% over the year, and the employment rate was broadly flat. Public sector net investment is planned to fall from 2.6% of GDP to 2.0% of GDP by 2030."`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The arithmetic supports the claim. Growth in real GDP is, by definition, growth in hours worked plus growth in output per hour. Hours rose 0.8% and GDP rose 1.2%, so productivity contributed around 0.4 percentage points — consistent with the stated 0.4% a year. Restoring productivity growth to its pre-2008 rate of 2.2% would roughly treble the growth rate without any increase in hours at all, which is why productivity is the dominant long-run determinant.\n• Mechanism: productivity growth comes from capital deepening, technology and human capital. Business investment at 9.4% of GDP against a 12.8% comparator means less capital per worker, so the capital-deepening channel is weak. The planned fall in public sector net investment from 2.6% to 2.0% of GDP removes infrastructure spending that raises private sector productivity, so the diagnosis and one cause point in the same direction. On an AD/AS diagram, this is slow rightward movement of LRAS.\n• Counter-argument — demand matters too. If AD is persistently weak, firms face uncertain future demand and postpone investment, which then depresses productivity; on this reading weak productivity is partly a symptom of weak demand rather than an independent cause. Hysteresis in investment means a long period of deficient demand permanently lowers the capital stock.\n• Other candidate causes: labour supply constraints (a flat employment rate and an ageing population limit hours growth), trade frictions and a small open economy\'s exposure to external shocks, skills mismatch, planning and infrastructure bottlenecks, and low levels of R&D spending. Several of these are themselves determinants of productivity, so the categories overlap.\n• Measurement caveat: productivity is notoriously hard to measure in services and in digital output, and comparisons of investment ratios across countries are sensitive to how intangible investment is capitalised. The 0.4% figure may understate true productivity growth.\n• Evaluation: a defensible judgement is that weak productivity is not so much the main cause of slow growth as the same thing viewed from a different angle — over the long run, growth in output per head simply is productivity growth. The more useful question is what causes weak productivity, and here the investment data in the stimulus point to under-investment, public and private, as the most actionable explanation.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — uses the growth decomposition, links investment to LRAS and judges. L2 (5–8) — explains productivity and other causes with some application. L1 (1–4) — lists causes of slow growth. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether deflation is more damaging to an economy than moderate inflation.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"Country S recorded price falls averaging 0.6% a year between 2020 and 2025. Over the same period, household consumption grew by an average of 0.2% a year, nominal wage growth averaged 0.1%, and the ratio of household debt to income rose from 108% to 121% even though households borrowed less in nominal terms. The central bank's policy rate has been at 0.1% throughout, and the economy's output gap is estimated at -2.8% of potential GDP. A neighbouring economy with inflation averaging 2.4% over the same period grew three times as quickly."`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Deferred consumption: if prices are expected to fall, the real return to holding cash is positive and purchases — especially of durables — are postponed. The stimulus shows consumption growth of just 0.2% a year, consistent with this channel. Weaker consumption reduces AD, which puts further downward pressure on prices, so deflation is self-reinforcing in a way that moderate inflation is not.\n• Debt deflation: falling prices raise the real value of nominal debt. The stimulus shows the household debt-to-income ratio rising from 108% to 121% even though households borrowed less in nominal terms — a rise driven by weak nominal income, not new borrowing. Rising real debt burdens force deleveraging, which cuts spending further, and raise default risk for lenders.\n• Policy impotence: with the policy rate at 0.1% the central bank is effectively at the zero lower bound and cannot cut further, so real interest rates stay positive and restrictive precisely when the economy needs stimulus. With an output gap of -2.8%, conventional monetary policy has run out of room — the liquidity trap. This is the crucial asymmetry: there is no ceiling on the interest rate a central bank can set to fight inflation, but there is a floor on the rate it can set to fight deflation.\n• Labour market rigidity: nominal wages are sticky downward, so with nominal wage growth of 0.1% and falling prices, real wages rise, employment adjusts through job losses rather than pay, and firms\' margins are squeezed. Moderate inflation lets real wages adjust without nominal cuts, which is one of the strongest arguments for a positive target.\n• Case for the other side: moderate inflation is not costless — menu and shoe-leather costs, fiscal drag, arbitrary redistribution from savers to borrowers, and the risk that a moderate rate accelerates once expectations shift. High inflation and hyperinflation are unambiguously worse than mild deflation, so the comparison depends on which rate of inflation is meant. Deflation caused by rising productivity and falling costs (good deflation) is also very different from deflation caused by collapsing demand.\n• Evaluation: a defensible judgement is that demand-driven deflation is more damaging than moderate inflation because it is self-reinforcing and disarms the main policy instrument, whereas moderate inflation is costly but controllable and its costs rise only gradually with the rate. That asymmetry is precisely why central banks target 2% rather than 0%.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — develops the debt-deflation and zero-lower-bound mechanisms with the data and judges. L2 (5–8) — describes costs of both with some application. L1 (1–4) — lists costs. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      /* ─────────────── 15 marks — evaluate with diagram and extract ─────────────── */
      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s) and the information in Extract 1, the extent to which a rise in world energy prices causes a lasting increase in the rate of inflation.

Extract 1 — written for this practice question
"Conflict in the Middle East pushed crude and refined energy prices higher through the summer of 2026, and they remained volatile and above pre-conflict levels. UK CPI inflation rose to 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026, driven largely by transport and motor fuels, and the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee expected it to rise further over coming quarters. Holding Bank Rate at 3.75% on 16 September 2026 by six votes to three, the Committee judged that domestic activity and tight financial conditions were restraining inflation but warned that the risk of second-round effects was growing. Average weekly earnings were growing at an annual rate above the rate of inflation."`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: AD/AS with SRAS shifting left from SRAS to SRAS1, showing the price level rising from P to P1 and real output falling from Y to Y1 — the stagflationary combination. The strongest answers add a second stage: a further leftward shift to SRAS2 if wages rise in response, or a rightward return towards SRAS if energy prices stabilise.\n• The one-off mechanism. Energy is an input to almost every good and to transport, so a rise in its price raises costs at every level of output. The immediate effect is a higher price level. But inflation is the rate of change of the price level, so if energy prices simply stabilise at their new higher level, the contribution drops out of the 12-month comparison after a year — a level effect, not a permanent inflation effect.\n• Why it can become lasting: second-round effects, which the MPC explicitly flags. Extract 1 notes earnings growing faster than prices; if workers bargain to protect real pay and firms pass higher unit labour costs on, the SRAS shift repeats and inflation expectations adjust upward. On a Phillips curve diagram the SRPC shifts out, so any given unemployment rate is now associated with higher inflation. Persistence then depends on expectations, not on oil.\n• The credibility of the inflation target is the decisive variable. If the 2% target is credible and expectations stay anchored, wage bargainers treat the shock as temporary and the SRPC does not shift — which is why the MPC holds Bank Rate at a restrictive 3.75% and communicates so heavily rather than accommodating the shock.\n• Counter-arguments: energy is a smaller share of GDP than it was in the 1970s and economies are far less energy-intensive per unit of output; union density is much lower, weakening the wage-bargaining channel; and the demand-destruction effect of higher energy bills reduces real incomes and therefore AD, which is disinflationary. The MPC\'s own judgement that domestic activity is restraining inflation supports this.\n• Evaluation: a defensible judgement is that an energy shock raises the price level permanently but the inflation rate only temporarily, provided expectations remain anchored — so the lasting damage depends on the policy response and on labour market institutions, not on the size of the shock. The 6-3 split shows this is a genuine judgement about risk rather than a settled matter.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — accurate and fully used diagram, sustained chains of reasoning, explicit use of Extract 1 and a supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present and broadly correct, analysis developed but evaluation limited. L1 (1–5) — descriptive, diagram missing or incorrect. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed; a correctly labelled diagram used in the analysis is required for Level 3.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s) and the information in Extract 1, the extent to which net inward migration raises an economy's productive potential.

Extract 1 — written for this practice question
"Country T recorded net inward migration averaging 0.6% of its population a year between 2021 and 2026. Around two thirds of arrivals were of working age, and the largest concentrations were in health and social care, construction, hospitality and higher education. Vacancy rates in health and social care fell from 9.4% to 5.8% over the period. Real GDP grew by an average of 1.4% a year, while real GDP per head grew by 0.5% a year. Average house prices rose 22% over the period and the government's infrastructure watchdog reported that housing completions had run below household formation in every year since 2010."`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: AD/AS with LRAS shifting right from LRAS to LRAS1 (and normally AD shifting right too, since migrants consume as well as produce). Alternatively a PPF shifting outward. The relative size of the two shifts determines whether the price level rises or falls — this is the analytical heart of the question.\n• Supply-side case: two thirds of arrivals of working age directly increases the labour supply, a determinant of potential output. Extract 1 shows vacancy rates in health and social care falling from 9.4% to 5.8%, which is evidence of shortages being relieved: filling vacancies raises actual output towards potential and reduces the wage pressure that would otherwise feed into costs and prices.\n• Fiscal and dynamic effects: working-age migrants are typically net fiscal contributors in the short run, paying tax without having consumed domestic education and before drawing a pension; migrants raise the dependency ratio in their favour; and there is evidence of higher rates of entrepreneurship and of complementarity with native workers in higher-skilled roles.\n• The per-capita qualification is decisive and is in the data. Real GDP grew 1.4% a year but GDP per head only 0.5%, so most of the growth is arithmetic — more people producing more output, not each person producing more. Potential output rises, but potential output per head rises only if arrivals are at least as productive as the existing average, which is not guaranteed when arrivals concentrate in relatively low-productivity sectors such as hospitality.\n• Capital dilution and congestion: with housing completions below household formation every year since 2010 and house prices up 22%, capital per worker is diluted. If the capital stock, housing and infrastructure do not expand with the workforce, the LRAS shift is smaller than the labour-force increase implies and the AD shift pushes up prices, particularly in housing — a distributional cost concentrated on those who do not own property.\n• Evaluation: a defensible judgement is that net inward migration raises aggregate productive potential fairly reliably, but raises potential per head only when arrivals\' skills match vacancies and when housing and capital investment keep pace. The evidence in Extract 1 shows the first condition partly met and the second clearly not, so the growth gain is real but the welfare gain per person is small. The policy conclusion is about complementary investment, not about the migration number.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — correct diagram fully used, distinguishes aggregate from per-capita effects using the data, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, analysis developed, evaluation limited. L1 (1–5) — descriptive, diagram missing or incorrect. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s) and the information in Extract 1, the extent to which a depreciation of the exchange rate improves the current account.

Extract 1 — written for this practice question
"Sterling traded at around 1.16 euros in mid-September 2026, with the Bank of England holding Bank Rate at 3.75% on 16 September against the European Central Bank's deposit rate of 2.50%. The UK current account deficit was £22.1 billion, or 2.8% of GDP, in Quarter 1 2026, narrowing by £5.0 billion on the quarter. Around 60% of the value of UK manufactured exports is made up of imported components. UK CPI inflation stood at 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026, and a survey of exporters reported that most set prices in the currency of the destination market."`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: a foreign exchange market diagram with the supply and demand for sterling, showing demand shifting left (or supply right) and the rate falling from e to e1. Credit a second diagram — AD/AS showing AD shifting right as net exports rise, and/or SRAS shifting left as imported input costs rise — or a J-curve sketch with the trade balance against time.\n• The basic mechanism: depreciation makes exports cheaper in foreign currency and imports dearer in domestic currency (SPICED in reverse). If demand responds, export volumes rise and import volumes fall, so the trade balance and hence the current account improve, and AD rises.\n• The Marshall-Lerner condition: the balance improves only if PEDx + PEDm > 1. Extract 1 says most exporters price in the destination market\'s currency, which means the foreign-currency price does not fall at all in the short run — exporters take a wider sterling margin instead of selling more volume — so the volume response is muted and the condition may not hold immediately.\n• The J-curve: in the short run, contracts are fixed and demand is price-inelastic, so the value of imports rises immediately while export volumes have not yet responded, and the balance worsens before it improves. Any judgement therefore has to specify a time horizon.\n• The import-content problem, which the extract quantifies: with around 60% of the value of manufactured exports made up of imported components, depreciation raises exporters\' own costs, offsetting much of the competitiveness gain. This is a strong reason why depreciation does less for a highly integrated supply chain than the textbook case implies.\n• The inflation constraint: with CPI inflation already at 3.1%, depreciation raises imported costs and pushes inflation higher, which may force the MPC to keep Bank Rate at 3.75% or raise it. Higher rates attract capital inflows and appreciate the currency, partly unwinding the depreciation — and dampen domestic demand.\n• Evaluation: a defensible judgement is that depreciation can improve the current account, but only over a period of years, only if the Marshall-Lerner condition holds, and only if it is not offset by imported inflation and the policy response to it. It treats the symptom rather than the cause: the durable route to a smaller deficit is improved non-price competitiveness — productivity, quality, design and reliability — because that raises export demand at any exchange rate.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — correct forex diagram used in the analysis, Marshall-Lerner and J-curve applied to the extract\'s evidence, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present and mechanism explained, limited evaluation. L1 (1–5) — asserts that depreciation helps exports; no diagram or an incorrect one. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s) and the information in Extract 1, the extent to which the economy described is operating with a negative output gap.

Extract 1 — written for this practice question
"Real GDP in Country U grew 0.4% in Quarter 2 2026 and 1.2% on the year. The unemployment rate was 4.9%, up 0.2 percentage points on the year, and most estimates put the country's natural rate of unemployment between 4.0% and 5.0%. Capacity utilisation in manufacturing stood at 79%, against a long-run average of 82%. CPI inflation was 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026, above the 2% target, and annual growth in average weekly earnings was 4.3%. The central bank's own estimate of the output gap carried a stated margin of error of plus or minus 1.5 percentage points of GDP."`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: AD/AS with a vertical LRAS at potential output Yfe and equilibrium real output Y1 to the left of it, with the negative gap marked as the horizontal distance Yfe − Y1. Credit alternatively a business cycle diagram showing actual GDP below trend, or a Keynesian AS with the economy on the elastic section.\n• Evidence for a negative gap: capacity utilisation at 79% against a long-run average of 82% indicates idle capital; growth of 1.2% a year is below most estimates of trend; unemployment has risen 0.2 percentage points on the year, which is consistent with demand running below capacity.\n• Evidence against: unemployment at 4.9% sits inside the 4.0-5.0% range of estimates of the natural rate, so on the labour-market measure the gap may be zero. More tellingly, inflation at 3.1% is above target and earnings growth of 4.3% exceeds it — persistent spare capacity should be exerting downward, not upward, pressure on wages and prices. That combination is hard to reconcile with a large negative gap.\n• Reconciling the two: the resolution is that the inflation may be supply-driven, in which case it tells us about costs rather than about the gap; or that potential output itself has fallen, so what looks like deficient demand is actually a smaller LRAS. On an AD/AS diagram those are two very different pictures — AD to the left of an unchanged LRAS, versus LRAS shifted left to meet AD — and they call for opposite policies.\n• Measurement is the core problem. Potential output is unobservable; it is estimated from a filtered trend and is revised heavily after the event. The stimulus gives a margin of error of ±1.5 percentage points of GDP, which is wider than most plausible estimates of the gap itself, so the central estimate cannot bear much policy weight.\n• Policy implications and risk: if the gap is genuinely negative, demand stimulus raises output with little inflation; if potential has fallen, the same stimulus produces inflation with no lasting output gain, which is the error made in the early 1970s. Given inflation above target, the asymmetry of risk argues for caution.\n• Evaluation: a defensible judgement is that the capacity and growth data point to a modest negative gap, but the inflation and earnings data contradict it, and the stated margin of error means the honest conclusion is that the gap cannot be identified with enough confidence to justify acting on it — which is itself an argument against fine-tuning demand on the basis of output gap estimates.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — correct diagram used, both sides of the evidence weighed, measurement uncertainty addressed, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, evidence described, evaluation limited. L1 (1–5) — defines the output gap with little use of the data. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      /* ─────────────── 25 marks — Section B (diagram) and Section C ─────────────── */
      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s), whether economic growth is always beneficial.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: AD/AS distinguishing actual growth (AD shifting right along an upward-sloping SRAS, using spare capacity) from potential growth (LRAS shifting right). Credit a PPF shifting outward, and credit a diagram showing growth driven by AD alone near full capacity producing mainly inflation.\n• Benefits: higher real output raises material living standards and, through the multiplier, employment and incomes; it raises tax revenue at unchanged rates, which finances health, education and infrastructure without higher tax rates; it makes debt sustainability easier because the denominator of the debt-to-GDP ratio grows — relevant where public sector net debt is 93.8% of GDP, as in the UK at the end of August 2026; and it funds the investment and R&D that drive future growth, so the benefits compound.\n• Benefits require the right kind of growth. Growth from a rightward LRAS shift — investment, technology, skills — is non-inflationary and sustainable. Growth from AD alone in an economy near capacity delivers inflation rather than output, which is precisely the trade-off the Bank of England faced when holding Bank Rate at 3.75% with CPI inflation at 3.1% in September 2026.\n• Costs: negative externalities in production and consumption — congestion, pollution, carbon emissions and resource depletion — which GDP counts as output while ignoring the damage. Growth that runs down natural capital raises measured output while reducing the productive base available to future generations.\n• Distribution: GDP is an aggregate, so growth can coexist with stagnant median incomes. Growth concentrated in particular sectors or regions can widen inequality, and the political and social costs of that are real even when the average rises. UK real GDP grew 1.2% in the year to Quarter 2 2026 while real GDP per head grew far more slowly because of population growth.\n• Other costs: structural change destroys jobs and communities in declining sectors (creative destruction); a current account deficit can widen as import demand rises — the UK deficit was 2.8% of GDP in Quarter 1 2026; rapid growth can inflate asset bubbles and, if financed by household borrowing, create financial fragility.\n• Evaluation: the decisive distinctions are between the source of growth (demand-led versus supply-led), its composition (investment versus debt-financed consumption; carbon-intensive versus low-carbon), its distribution and its sustainability. A defensible conclusion is that growth is close to a necessary condition for improving living standards, and that the real question is not whether to grow but how, since the costs listed above are properties of particular growth paths rather than of growth as such. The Kuznets curve and the possibility of decoupling emissions from output can be used to support this, with proper caution about the evidence.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: marks are also available for quality of extended response. L5 requires a correctly used diagram, sustained evaluation integrated throughout rather than reserved for a final paragraph, and a conclusion that follows from the argument rather than being asserted. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s), whether inflation is always the result of excess aggregate demand.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagrams: AD/AS showing demand-pull inflation (AD shifting right along an increasingly steep SRAS, or against a vertical LRAS) and cost-push inflation (SRAS shifting left, raising P and lowering Y). A strong answer uses both and explains how the two are distinguished by what happens to real output — rising in the first, falling in the second.\n• Case for demand-pull as the general cause: monetarists argue inflation is always and everywhere a monetary phenomenon, since a sustained rise in the price level requires a sustained rise in nominal spending, which requires money supply growth in excess of output growth. Cost shocks change relative prices; without accommodating monetary growth they cause a one-off rise in the level, not sustained inflation.\n• Demand-side sources: cuts in the policy rate, fiscal loosening, rising consumer confidence and credit growth, asset price booms and strong external demand. Near full capacity these bid up prices rather than output.\n• Case for cost-push: external input shocks can raise the price level independently of domestic demand. The clearest recent example is the rise in UK CPI inflation to 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026, which the ONS attributed largely to transport and motor fuels after conflict in the Middle East raised crude and refined energy prices, at a time when the Monetary Policy Committee judged that domestic activity and tight financial conditions were restraining inflation. Other cost sources: wage growth in excess of productivity, higher indirect taxes, and depreciation raising imported costs.\n• The distinction blurs in practice. A supply shock becomes an inflation problem through second-round effects: workers bargain to protect real pay, firms pass on unit labour costs and expectations adjust, shifting the short-run Phillips curve out. At that point the cause of continuing inflation is expectations rather than either the original shock or current excess demand — which is why central bank credibility, and the 2% target itself, do so much of the work.\n• Policy consequences, which is why the diagnosis matters: demand-pull inflation responds to tighter monetary and fiscal policy at a modest output cost; cost-push inflation does not, because raising Bank Rate cannot lower the world oil price and tightening into a supply shock deepens the output loss. This is exactly the dilemma behind the MPC\'s 6-3 split on 16 September 2026, when it held Bank Rate at 3.75% while three members voted for 4%.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion distinguishes the trigger from the transmission. Individual inflationary episodes frequently begin on the supply side, so it is false that inflation is always caused by excess demand; but sustained inflation over years requires accommodating monetary conditions and unanchored expectations, so the monetarist claim survives in a longer-run form. The best answers note that the practical difficulty is that policymakers must diagnose in real time, with unreliable output gap estimates.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires correctly used diagrams, sustained integrated evaluation and a supported conclusion. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s), whether there is a trade-off between the objectives of low inflation and low unemployment.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagrams: a downward-sloping short-run Phillips curve showing the trade-off, plus a vertical long-run Phillips curve at the NAIRU with the SRPC shifting out as expectations adjust. Credit AD/AS used alongside, showing AD expansion raising output and employment while also raising the price level.\n• The case for a trade-off: Phillips\' original 1958 finding related wage inflation to unemployment, and the mechanism is straightforward — a tighter labour market raises workers\' bargaining power, wage growth accelerates and firms pass higher unit labour costs on. On an AD/AS diagram, expansionary policy that reduces unemployment shifts AD right along an upward-sloping SRAS, raising both output and prices. The trade-off is therefore real in the short run.\n• The case against: the Friedman-Phelps critique. If the trade-off is exploited, workers revise expectations upward, the SRPC shifts out and unemployment returns to the NAIRU at higher inflation. The long-run Phillips curve is vertical, so there is no permanent trade-off — only a choice of inflation rate. The stagflation of the 1970s, with high inflation and high unemployment together, is the standard evidence against a stable trade-off.\n• Supply shocks break the relationship in the other direction too: an SRAS shift left raises inflation and unemployment simultaneously, so the two objectives move together rather than in opposition — the situation the Bank of England described in September 2026, when it held Bank Rate at 3.75% with CPI inflation at 3.1% and unemployment at 4.9%, judging the inflation to be driven largely by energy and transport costs.\n• The relationship is not stable over time. The curve appeared flat for much of the 2010s, when large falls in unemployment produced little wage inflation, prompting arguments about globalisation, weaker union density, low union coverage and well-anchored expectations. A flat curve means the short-run trade-off is available on very unfavourable terms in one direction and very favourable in the other.\n• Escaping the trade-off: supply-side policy that lowers the NAIRU — education and retraining to cut structural unemployment, improved job-matching to cut frictional unemployment, measures to raise productivity so that wage growth is non-inflationary — shifts the LRPC left and delivers lower unemployment and lower inflation together. This is the only durable resolution, but it works over years, not quarters.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that the trade-off exists in the short run, is unstable and state-dependent, and does not exist in the long run; that the terms of the trade-off depend on the credibility of the inflation target and on the flexibility of the labour market; and that supply shocks can reverse it altogether. The policy implication is that demand management can choose a point on the short-run curve, but only supply-side policy can change which curve the economy is on.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires both SRPC and LRPC used accurately in the analysis and integrated evaluation. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate whether GDP is a satisfactory measure of a country\'s economic performance.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• What GDP does well: it is a consistent, frequently published, internationally comparable measure of the total value of output, computed three ways (income, output, expenditure) that should reconcile. It correlates strongly with employment, tax revenue and most development indicators, and real GDP strips out price changes so that comparisons over time are meaningful. For short-run stabilisation policy, which is about the gap between actual and potential output, it is the right variable.\n• Aggregate versus per head: GDP takes no account of population. UK real GDP rose 1.2% in the year to Quarter 2 2026 while GDP per head rose far more slowly, so the aggregate overstates how much better off the average person became. GDP per capita at purchasing power parity is the minimum correction for cross-country comparison.\n• Distribution: GDP is silent on who receives the income. A country can record strong growth while median real incomes stagnate, which is why the Gini coefficient, income decile data and inequality-adjusted measures are necessary complements.\n• Coverage: GDP omits unpaid household work, care and voluntary activity, and much of the informal economy — a large share of output in low-income countries, so cross-country comparisons systematically understate them. Conversely it counts defensive expenditure (crime prevention, disaster clean-up, commuting) as output even though it offsets a harm rather than creating a benefit.\n• Sustainability and externalities: GDP records the output from depleting a forest or a fishery but not the loss of the asset, and counts pollution-generating production without netting off the damage. An economy can therefore show growth while its productive base shrinks — the case for green GDP or genuine savings measures.\n• Alternatives and their own limits: the HDI combines life expectancy, schooling and GNI per head at PPP — in the 2025 Human Development Report the UK scored 0.946 and ranked 13th of 193, with Iceland first at 0.972 — but it uses only three indicators, weighted equally by convention, and is published with a lag. Measures of subjective well-being are hard to compare across cultures; the Genuine Progress Indicator and Inclusive Wealth Index require contested valuations of non-market goods.\n• Evaluation: the question is "satisfactory for what?". As a measure of the scale of market economic activity and of the short-run cyclical position, GDP is fit for purpose and no alternative matches it for timeliness and comparability. As a measure of welfare, of sustainability or of how the gains are shared, it is not, and was never designed to be. The reasonable conclusion is not to replace GDP but to stop using it alone: a dashboard of real GDP per head, an inequality measure, a health and education measure and an environmental measure answers the questions GDP cannot.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires the "satisfactory for what purpose" distinction, engagement with the limits of the alternatives as well as of GDP, and a conclusion that follows from the argument. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate whether a current account deficit on the balance of payments matters.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• What a deficit is: the current account records trade in goods and services plus primary income (investment income) and secondary income (transfers). A deficit means net outward payments on these items, financed by net inflows on the capital and financial accounts — the balance of payments must balance, so the deficit is by construction matched by net borrowing from, or asset sales to, the rest of the world.\n• The case that it matters: a persistent deficit accumulates external liabilities, and the resulting outflow of interest, profits and dividends worsens the primary income balance, which widens the current account further — a self-reinforcing dynamic. It can reflect underlying weakness in price and non-price competitiveness or in productivity. If financed by short-term portfolio flows, it exposes the economy to a sudden stop: capital flight, sharp depreciation, imported inflation and a forced rise in interest rates.\n• Leakage from the circular flow: net imports are a withdrawal, so a deficit reduces AD and, through the multiplier, output and employment relative to a balanced position, other things equal.\n• The case that it does not matter: size and context are decisive. The UK deficit was £22.1 billion, or 2.8% of GDP, in Quarter 1 2026 — modest, and £15.1 billion or 1.9% of GDP excluding volatile precious metals — and the UK has run deficits every year since 1984 without crisis. A country that issues debt in its own floating currency cannot be forced to default on external liabilities the way a dollar-borrowing emerging economy can, and the exchange rate provides automatic adjustment.\n• A deficit can signal strength: fast-growing economies import more, and inflows into gilts, property and equities reflect confidence in the rule of law and in deep, liquid capital markets. What the borrowing finances matters more than its size — imported capital goods that raise future output are very different from imported consumption.\n• Adjustment and its limits: depreciation improves the balance only if the Marshall-Lerner condition holds, and only after the J-curve delay; with a high import content in exports the competitiveness gain is partly offset; deflationary demand management works but at the cost of output and jobs; supply-side policy to raise productivity is the durable route but operates over years. Protectionism risks retaliation and higher input costs.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that the deficit itself is not the object of policy — the composition of its financing and its trend are. A deficit of 2-3% of GDP financed by long-term FDI in an economy with a floating currency is close to harmless; the same deficit financed by short-term flows attracted by an interest differential, and widening as the primary income balance deteriorates, is a genuine vulnerability. Answers should also note that not every country can run a surplus, so treating deficits as a failure is a fallacy of composition.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires the distinction between the size of the deficit and the nature of its financing, and a conclusion that follows from the argument. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate whether unemployment imposes greater costs on an economy than inflation.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Costs of unemployment: output permanently forgone, since an hour of labour not worked cannot be recovered; lost income tax and National Insurance receipts combined with higher benefit spending, which worsens the public finances at both ends; hysteresis, as long spells erode skills and employability and convert cyclical into structural unemployment, raising the NAIRU; and well-documented effects on physical and mental health, family breakdown, crime and the intergenerational transmission of disadvantage. These costs are concentrated on identifiable individuals and on particular regions and age groups.\n• Costs of inflation: erosion of the real value of money incomes and savings; arbitrary redistribution from savers and those on fixed incomes to borrowers; menu costs and shoe-leather costs; fiscal drag where thresholds are not indexed; distortion of relative price signals, so resources are misallocated; and loss of international competitiveness if domestic inflation exceeds that of trading partners. At high and volatile rates, inflation destroys the usefulness of money as a unit of account and makes long-term contracting impossible.\n• The comparison depends entirely on the rates being compared. Moderate inflation of 3.1%, the UK rate in the 12 months to August 2026, is a far smaller cost than unemployment of, say, 10%. Hyperinflation is worse than almost any level of unemployment. Comparing 3% inflation with 5% unemployment is not the same exercise as comparing 30% inflation with 5% unemployment, and the best answers say so explicitly.\n• The incidence differs in kind. Unemployment concentrates severe costs on a minority; inflation imposes smaller costs on nearly everyone but redistributes sharply, hurting savers, pensioners on non-indexed incomes and low-income households facing higher food and energy weights. An equity judgement about which is worse therefore depends on the weight given to concentrated versus dispersed harm.\n• Reversibility asymmetry: hysteresis makes unemployment partly irreversible, whereas moderate inflation can be reduced by policy — though at a cost in output and jobs (the sacrifice ratio), which means fighting inflation typically creates unemployment. This is the argument that the two are not independent evils.\n• Institutional evidence for the other side: the Bank of England\'s remit makes 2% CPI inflation the primary objective, with support for growth and employment secondary, and the MPC held Bank Rate at 3.75% on 16 September 2026 with unemployment at 4.9% and inflation at 3.1%. The rationale is that price stability is a precondition for sustainably high employment, because unanchored expectations eventually require a deeper recession to correct.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that at the rates typically observed in a developed economy the costs of unemployment are larger, more concentrated and less reversible, but that this does not establish a case for tolerating inflation, because the long-run Phillips curve implies that accepting higher inflation buys no lasting reduction in unemployment. The better framing is that the two are not substitutes: supply-side policy that lowers the NAIRU reduces both.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires explicit attention to the rates being compared and to the incidence of each cost, with integrated evaluation. Indicative content is not a checklist.` }
    ]
  },
  '2.2': {
    name: 'AD/AS & Equilibrium',
    questions: [
      /* ─────────────── 2 marks — points-marked ─────────────── */
      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'aggregate demand\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) Aggregate demand is the total planned expenditure on domestically produced goods and services at each price level in a given time period.\n(1) Its components are AD = C + I + G + (X − M): household consumption, gross investment, government spending and net exports.\n\nAward the second mark for the formula with the components correctly identified, or for an accurate statement of the components in words. Do not credit "total demand in the economy" alone.` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'the multiplier\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) The multiplier is the ratio of the final change in real national income to the initial change in an injection into the circular flow.\n(1) It arises because one agent\'s spending is another\'s income, which is partly re-spent, so an injection raises income by more than the initial amount; k = 1/(1 − MPC) or, allowing for all withdrawals, k = 1/(MPS + MPT + MPM).\n\nAccept either formula for the second mark.` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'crowding out\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) Crowding out occurs when an increase in government spending or borrowing reduces private sector spending, so the net effect on aggregate demand is smaller than the initial injection.\n(1) The financial mechanism: increased government borrowing raises the demand for loanable funds and therefore interest rates, which reduces private investment and interest-sensitive consumption. (Accept resource crowding out: at full capacity the public sector bids scarce labour and materials away from the private sector.)` },

      { q: `Using information from the stimulus material, identify two factors that would shift the aggregate demand curve to the right.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"The central bank of Country A cut its policy rate from 4.25% to 3.75% in March 2026, and mortgage approvals rose over the following six months. The government announced a £14 billion increase in capital spending on rail and grid infrastructure to begin in 2027. A survey of households reported the strongest consumer confidence reading in four years, while the country's currency appreciated by 6% on a trade-weighted basis and exporters reported losing orders in their main overseas markets."`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify',
        markScheme: `(1) The cut in the policy rate from 4.25% to 3.75% — lower borrowing costs raise consumption and investment.\n(1) The £14 billion increase in government capital spending — a direct rise in the G component.\n\nAlso credit: the rise in consumer confidence raising C. Do NOT credit the 6% currency appreciation, which reduces net exports and shifts AD left. Award 1 mark per valid factor, maximum 2.` },

      { q: `Refer to Fig. 2. Describe the relationship between business investment and the rate of change of real GDP shown.

Fig. 2 — written for this practice question
Country B: annual growth in real GDP and annual growth in business investment, 2019-2025
  2019:  GDP growth  1.6%  ;  investment growth   2.4%
  2020:  GDP growth −9.2%  ;  investment growth −14.8%
  2021:  GDP growth  7.4%  ;  investment growth  11.9%
  2022:  GDP growth  4.1%  ;  investment growth   6.2%
  2023:  GDP growth  0.9%  ;  investment growth  −2.1%
  2024:  GDP growth  1.3%  ;  investment growth   1.8%
  2025:  GDP growth  1.1%  ;  investment growth   0.4%`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe',
        markScheme: `(1) There is a positive relationship — investment growth moves in the same direction as GDP growth.\n(1) Supporting quantification showing that investment is more volatile than GDP: in 2020 GDP fell 9.2% while investment fell 14.8%; in 2021 GDP rose 7.4% while investment rose 11.9%. Accept the observation that investment fell 2.1% in 2023 even though GDP still grew 0.9%.\n\nDescribe question — explanation (the accelerator) is not required, though credit reference to it.` },

      { q: 'Using the following data, calculate the value of the multiplier. You are advised to show your working.\n\nIn Country C the marginal propensity to save is 0.15, the marginal rate of tax is 0.25 and the marginal propensity to import is 0.20.', marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method (1 mark): MPW = MPS + MPT + MPM = 0.15 + 0.25 + 0.20 = 0.60; multiplier k = 1/MPW = 1/0.60.\nAnswer (1 mark): k = 1.67 (to two decimal places). Accept 1.6 recurring or 5/3.\n\nAward 1 mark for correct method with an arithmetic slip. An answer of 6.67, from using MPS alone, earns no more than 1 mark for method.` },

      { q: 'Using the following data, calculate the final change in real national income. You are advised to show your working.\n\nIn Country D, government spending rises by £12 billion. The marginal propensity to consume out of national income is 0.6, and there are no other withdrawals.', marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method (1 mark): k = 1/(1 − MPC) = 1/(1 − 0.6) = 1/0.4 = 2.5; final change = initial injection × k = £12bn × 2.5.\nAnswer (1 mark): an increase of £30 billion.\n\nAward 1 mark for correct method with an arithmetic slip. Credit the qualification that this assumes spare capacity, but it is not required for the marks.` },

      { q: `Using the data below, calculate the size of the inflationary gap in Country E. You are advised to show your working.

Table 1 — written for this practice question
Country E, 2026, £ billion at constant prices
  Equilibrium real national income:      2 184
  Full employment level of real income:  2 100`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method (1 mark): inflationary gap = equilibrium real income − full employment real income = 2 184 − 2 100.\nAnswer (1 mark): £84 billion (equivalent to 4% of the full employment level).\n\nAccept the answer expressed either in £bn or as a percentage of full employment income, provided the working shows the subtraction. Award 1 mark for correct method with an arithmetic slip.` },

      /* ─────────────── 3 marks — points-marked ─────────────── */
      { q: 'Explain why the short-run aggregate supply curve slopes upward.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) In the short run some costs are fixed or sticky — wages are set by contract, rents and many input prices are agreed in advance, so they do not adjust immediately to changes in the price level.\n(1) If the general price level rises while these nominal costs are unchanged, the price firms receive rises relative to their costs, so profit margins widen at every level of output.\n(1) Higher expected profit gives firms an incentive to expand output, hiring additional workers and running existing capital more intensively even at rising marginal cost. A higher price level is therefore associated with greater real output, giving an upward-sloping SRAS. Credit the further point that the curve becomes steeper as the economy approaches full capacity, because marginal costs rise faster when spare resources are scarce.` },

      { q: 'Explain why the value of the multiplier is likely to be smaller in a more open economy.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) The multiplier works through successive rounds of spending: income received is partly re-spent, generating income for someone else, and the size of the multiplier depends on how much leaks out of the domestic circular flow at each round.\n(1) A more open economy has a higher marginal propensity to import, so a larger share of each extra pound of income is spent on foreign rather than domestic output. That spending becomes income for foreign producers and leaves the domestic circular flow.\n(1) Since k = 1/(MPS + MPT + MPM), a larger MPM raises the denominator and reduces k. For example, with MPS 0.15 and MPT 0.25, raising MPM from 0.10 to 0.20 cuts the multiplier from 2.0 to 1.67 — so the same fiscal injection raises national income by materially less, and part of the stimulus benefits trading partners instead.` },

      { q: 'Explain why an economy can remain in equilibrium below the full employment level of output.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) Macroeconomic equilibrium occurs where AD equals AS (or where injections equal withdrawals) — there is nothing in that condition that requires the resulting level of output to be the full employment level.\n(1) Keynes argued that wages and prices are sticky downwards: workers resist nominal pay cuts, contracts are fixed and firms fear the effect on morale and productivity, so when AD falls firms reduce output and employment rather than cutting wages.\n(1) Because wages do not fall, SRAS does not shift right to restore full employment, and the resulting unemployment itself reduces incomes and consumption, keeping AD low — a self-sustaining underemployment equilibrium. On a Keynesian AS diagram the economy sits on the elastic section, where increases in AD raise output with little effect on prices, which is the case for active demand management.` },

      /* ─────────────── 4 marks — explain with a diagram ─────────────── */
      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the effect of an increase in government spending on an economy with substantial spare capacity.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: AD/AS with a Keynesian AS curve, the initial equilibrium on the horizontal (perfectly elastic) section well below full employment output Yfe.\n(1) The rise in G shifts AD right from AD to AD1.\n(1) The new equilibrium shows real output rising substantially from Y to Y1.\n(1) The price level is unchanged, or rises only slightly, because ample spare capacity means firms can raise output without bidding up wages and input prices. Credit the further point that the multiplier amplifies the initial injection, so the rise in output exceeds the rise in G.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the effect of an increase in government spending on an economy operating at full capacity.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: AD/AS with the initial equilibrium on the vertical section of a Keynesian AS curve, or on a vertical classical LRAS at Yfe.\n(1) AD shifts right from AD to AD1.\n(1) The price level rises sharply from P to P1.\n(1) Real output rises only marginally, or not at all in the classical case, because there are no unemployed resources to draw on — the extra demand is entirely absorbed by higher prices. Credit the conclusion that the same policy produces very different outcomes depending on the starting position, and that complete crowding out of private spending is possible at full capacity.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the effect on the economy of a sharp fall in world demand for a country\'s exports.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: AD/AS with AD shifting left from AD to AD1 against an upward-sloping SRAS.\n(1) Net exports (X − M) fall, reducing a component of AD, so the AD curve shifts left.\n(1) The new equilibrium shows real output falling from Y to Y1 and the price level falling from P to P1.\n(1) Explanation of the consequences: lower output means firms cut production and shed labour, raising cyclical unemployment; the multiplier amplifies the initial fall because those losing income reduce their own spending, so the final fall in national income exceeds the initial fall in exports. A negative output gap opens up.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how a rise in the price of imported raw materials affects short-run aggregate supply.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: AD/AS with SRAS shifting left from SRAS to SRAS1, AD unchanged.\n(1) Higher imported raw material prices raise firms\' costs of production at every level of output, so at each price level firms are willing to supply less.\n(1) The new equilibrium shows a higher price level (P to P1) and lower real output (Y to Y1).\n(1) Explanation: this is cost-push inflation combined with falling output — stagflation — and it presents a policy dilemma because measures to reduce the inflation reduce output further. Credit the point that the size of the shift depends on the import intensity of production and on whether the exchange rate is also moving.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how investment in education and training affects an economy\'s long-run aggregate supply.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: AD/AS with a vertical LRAS shifting right from LRAS to LRAS1 (credit a Keynesian AS whose vertical section shifts right, or a PPF shifting outward).\n(1) Education and training raise human capital — the skills, knowledge and adaptability of the workforce — which is a determinant of the economy\'s productive potential.\n(1) Higher labour productivity means more output can be produced from the same quantity of factors, so LRAS shifts right and potential output rises from Yfe to Yfe1.\n(1) Explanation of the consequences: with AD unchanged the price level falls from P to P1 while output rises — non-inflationary growth. Credit the qualification that the effect operates over many years, since a cohort must be trained before it enters the workforce.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how equilibrium national income is determined in the Keynesian 45-degree line model.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: aggregate expenditure on the vertical axis, real national income on the horizontal axis, with a 45-degree line from the origin along which planned expenditure equals income.\n(1) The aggregate expenditure line AE = C + I + G + (X − M) drawn with a positive intercept (autonomous expenditure) and a slope less than 45 degrees, equal to the marginal propensity to consume out of national income.\n(1) Equilibrium identified at the intersection of AE and the 45-degree line, with the equilibrium level of income Ye marked on the horizontal axis.\n(1) Explanation of the adjustment: if income exceeds Ye, planned spending is below output, unplanned stocks accumulate and firms cut production; if income is below Ye, stocks run down and firms expand. Credit the identification of a deflationary or inflationary gap where Ye differs from the full employment level Yfe.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how simultaneous rightward shifts of aggregate demand and long-run aggregate supply can produce non-inflationary growth.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: AD/AS with both AD shifting right (AD to AD1) and LRAS shifting right (LRAS to LRAS1) by a similar magnitude.\n(1) Explanation of the AD shift, e.g. rising consumption, investment or government spending raising total planned expenditure.\n(1) Explanation of the LRAS shift, e.g. investment in capital, technology, infrastructure or skills raising productive potential.\n(1) The new equilibrium shows higher real output with the price level approximately unchanged, because the extra demand is met by extra capacity rather than bidding up prices. Credit the conclusion that this is the combination policymakers aim for, and the qualification that the two shifts rarely occur at the same speed, since supply-side changes work over a much longer horizon than demand changes.` },

      /* ─────────────── 8 marks — evaluate using the stimulus ─────────────── */
      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which the fiscal stimulus described is likely to raise real national income by the amount the government claims.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"The government of Country F announced an £18 billion increase in public spending and said it expected national income to rise by £45 billion, based on a multiplier of 2.5. Independent analysts noted that the marginal propensity to save in Country F is 0.14, the marginal rate of tax is 0.28 and the marginal propensity to import is 0.23. Unemployment stands at 4.6%, capacity utilisation in manufacturing is 84% against a long-run average of 82%, and the central bank's policy rate is 3.75% with inflation running at 3.1%."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The claimed multiplier is not supported by the data. MPW = 0.14 + 0.28 + 0.23 = 0.65, so k = 1/0.65 = 1.54, not 2.5. On that value the £18 billion injection raises national income by about £27.7 billion, some £17 billion less than claimed — the government appears to have used 1/(1 − MPC) and ignored tax and import leakages.\n• Capacity is the second problem. Capacity utilisation at 84% is above the long-run average of 82% and unemployment at 4.6% is close to most estimates of the natural rate, so the economy has little spare capacity. On an AD/AS diagram the starting point is on the steep section of AS, so the rightward AD shift raises the price level substantially and real output only modestly.\n• Monetary offset: with inflation at 3.1% and the policy rate at 3.75%, a fiscal loosening that adds to demand makes it more likely the central bank keeps rates high or raises them. Higher rates reduce private investment and interest-sensitive consumption, so part of the fiscal injection is crowded out and the effective multiplier is lower still.\n• Timing: capital spending in particular is slow to disburse — planning, procurement and construction mean the AD injection arrives over years rather than months, by which point the cyclical position may have changed and the stimulus may be pro-cyclical.\n• Evaluation: the direction of the effect is right but the magnitude is overstated on at least three counts — the arithmetic of the multiplier, the lack of spare capacity and the likely monetary offset. A defensible judgement is that national income will rise by well under £45 billion and that a significant part of the nominal increase will be inflation rather than real output. The case for the spending, if there is one, rests on its supply-side effects on LRAS rather than on the demand multiplier.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — uses the data to test the claim, including the multiplier calculation, and judges. L1 (1–4) — describes the multiplier without applying the figures, or accepts the government claim uncritically.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which consumer confidence is the main determinant of household consumption.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"In Country G, a widely followed index of consumer confidence fell from +4 to −18 between January and July 2026, its sharpest fall in five years. Over the same period, household consumption fell by only 0.4%. Real household disposable income fell 1.1%, house prices fell 3.2%, the household saving ratio rose from 8.1% to 9.6%, and the effective interest rate on outstanding mortgage debt rose from 3.6% to 4.4% as fixed-rate deals expired."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Case for confidence mattering: expectations about future income and job security determine how much of current income households feel able to spend, particularly on durables and on debt-financed purchases. The rise in the saving ratio from 8.1% to 9.6% is consistent with precautionary saving in response to the collapse in confidence from +4 to −18.\n• Case against, from the data: consumption fell only 0.4% despite a 22-point fall in the confidence index, which is a very weak response for a supposedly dominant determinant. Confidence indices are surveys of sentiment and are known to be a better predictor of what people say than of what they do.\n• Rival determinants present in the stimulus, each with a clear mechanism. Real disposable income fell 1.1% — the single most important determinant of consumption in the consumption function C = a + bYd. Wealth fell, with house prices down 3.2%, so the wealth effect reduces spending. Interest rates on outstanding mortgage debt rose from 3.6% to 4.4%, which raises the cost of borrowing, increases the return to saving and reduces the disposable income of mortgaged households directly.\n• Interaction rather than competition: falling incomes, falling house prices and rising mortgage costs are plausibly the cause of the fall in confidence, not independent of it. If so, confidence is a transmission channel and a symptom rather than a primary determinant, and treating it as a separate cause risks double counting.\n• Evaluation: on this evidence confidence is not the main determinant — real disposable income is, with wealth and interest rates as significant secondary influences. Confidence matters most at turning points and for the timing of large discretionary purchases, and it can be self-fulfilling where it shifts spending sharply, but a 22-point swing producing a 0.4% fall in consumption is evidence that income and balance-sheet effects dominate.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — uses the specific figures, weighs confidence against the rival determinants and judges. L1 (1–4) — lists determinants of consumption with little use of the stimulus.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which interest rates are the main determinant of business investment.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"The central bank of Country H cut its policy rate from 5.25% to 3.75% over eighteen months to September 2026. Business investment grew by only 0.6% over the same period. A survey of 1,200 firms found that 61% cited 'uncertainty about future demand' as the main constraint on investment plans, 34% cited the availability of skilled labour and 12% cited the cost of finance. Corporate profits as a share of GDP were at a ten-year high, and the average expected rate of return on new projects was reported at 9%."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The theoretical case for interest rates: investment is undertaken where the expected rate of return exceeds the cost of borrowing, so a lower policy rate should make marginal projects viable, reduce the opportunity cost of using retained profits and raise the present value of future returns. A 150 basis point cut should, on this logic, produce a substantial response.\n• The data contradict a dominant role. Investment grew only 0.6% despite the cut, and only 12% of firms cited the cost of finance as their main constraint. With corporate profits at a ten-year high, firms are not finance-constrained, and with expected returns of 9% against a policy rate of 3.75% the margin was comfortable at either rate — so the rate cut changed little at the margin.\n• The dominant constraint identified is expected demand: 61% of firms cite uncertainty about future demand. This is consistent with the accelerator theory, in which investment depends on the rate of change of output rather than on its level or on the interest rate; if firms do not expect demand to grow, additional capacity is not worth having at any plausible interest rate. Keynes\' "animal spirits" captures the same point.\n• Other determinants in the data: 34% cite the availability of skilled labour, a supply-side constraint that cheap finance cannot relieve. Add technological change, corporation tax and capital allowances, regulation and planning delays, and the degree of spare capacity already in place.\n• Evaluation: interest rates are a genuine determinant but clearly not the main one in this economy — they operate on the cost side of a decision that is dominated by expectations of revenue. A defensible judgement is that monetary policy is most powerful when uncertainty is low and firms are finance-constrained, and close to "pushing on a string" when the binding constraint is expected demand, which is exactly the case the survey describes.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — uses the survey and profit data to weigh interest rates against expectations, and judges. L1 (1–4) — asserts that lower rates raise investment with little use of the stimulus.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which the economy described has spare capacity.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"In Country J, capacity utilisation in manufacturing was 78% against a long-run average of 82%. The unemployment rate was 5.4% and the economic inactivity rate 21.6%. Real GDP was 2.1% below the level implied by extending the pre-2008 trend. However, annual growth in average weekly earnings was 5.2%, CPI inflation was 3.4% against a 2% target, and the ratio of vacancies to unemployed people stood at 0.71, close to its ten-year average of 0.73."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Evidence for spare capacity: capacity utilisation at 78% is four percentage points below its long-run average, so capital is idle; unemployment at 5.4% is above most estimates of the natural rate; inactivity at 21.6% represents a further pool of potential labour; and real GDP is 2.1% below the extrapolated pre-2008 trend, a conventional measure of a negative output gap.\n• Evidence against: earnings growth of 5.2% and inflation of 3.4% are the signatures of a tight economy, not a slack one. If there were genuine spare capacity, competition for jobs would restrain wage growth and firms would find it hard to pass costs on. The vacancy-to-unemployment ratio of 0.71 against a ten-year average of 0.73 indicates a labour market close to normal rather than obviously slack.\n• Reconciling the evidence: the most likely explanation is that potential output has itself fallen, so the pre-2008 trend is the wrong benchmark. Weak investment since 2008, hysteresis among the long-term unemployed and skills mismatch would reduce LRAS, meaning that what looks like deficient demand is in fact a smaller productive capacity. Inflation and earnings data are the market\'s verdict on where capacity actually lies.\n• Measurement caveat: capacity utilisation surveys cover manufacturing, which is a small and unrepresentative share of a modern service economy; potential output is unobservable and trend extrapolation is a weak method after a structural break such as 2008.\n• Evaluation: the quantity indicators suggest spare capacity and the price indicators deny it. Where they conflict, the price and wage data are the more reliable guide, because they reflect actual bargaining outcomes rather than an assumed trend. A defensible judgement is that any spare capacity is smaller than the output-gap estimate implies, which matters because demand stimulus applied to an economy without slack produces inflation rather than growth.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — weighs quantity indicators against price indicators using the figures and judges. L1 (1–4) — lists the indicators without resolving the conflict between them.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which the rise in the saving ratio described will harm the economy.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"In Country K the household saving ratio rose from 6.8% to 10.4% between 2024 and 2026. Household consumption, which accounts for 62% of aggregate demand, grew by an average of just 0.3% a year over the period, and retail sales volumes fell in five of the eight quarters. Business investment grew 2.7% a year, and the corporate sector reported that bank lending conditions had eased. Gross fixed capital formation rose from 17.2% to 18.1% of GDP, while the current account deficit narrowed from 3.4% to 2.1% of GDP."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Short-run harm — the paradox of thrift. Saving is a withdrawal from the circular flow, so a rise in the saving ratio from 6.8% to 10.4% reduces consumption, which is 62% of AD. AD shifts left, and the multiplier amplifies the fall, so the decision that is rational for each household is damaging in aggregate. The data support this: consumption growth of 0.3% a year and falling retail volumes in five of eight quarters.\n• Consequences of the demand shortfall: lower output, a negative output gap, rising cyclical unemployment and, if prolonged, hysteresis. Firms facing weak sales may also cut investment, which would compound the problem.\n• Long-run benefit: saving is the source of loanable funds for investment. The data show the mechanism working — gross fixed capital formation rose from 17.2% to 18.1% of GDP, business investment grew 2.7% a year and lending conditions eased. Higher investment raises the capital stock and shifts LRAS right, raising potential output and future living standards.\n• External benefit: the current account deficit narrowed from 3.4% to 2.1% of GDP, consistent with lower domestic consumption reducing import demand and the economy relying less on foreign borrowing.\n• Evaluation: the harm depends on the state of the economy and on whether the saving is actually intermediated into investment. In a deep recession with rates at the zero lower bound, higher saving is straightforwardly contractionary; in an economy with functioning banks, adequate demand and an investment shortfall, it finances capital accumulation, which is what the data here suggest. A defensible judgement is that the rise has weakened short-run demand but improved the composition of the economy, so the net effect over a decade is more likely positive than negative.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — applies the paradox of thrift and the investment channel to the specific data and judges. L1 (1–4) — states that saving reduces AD with little use of the stimulus.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which the slowdown described was caused by supply-side rather than demand-side factors.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"Real GDP growth in Country L fell from 2.4% to 0.5% over two years. Over the same period, the price level rose by 7.9%, the unemployment rate rose from 4.1% to 5.3%, and the prices of imported energy and industrial metals rose 31% and 19% respectively. Consumer confidence fell sharply and real household disposable income fell 2.2%. The central bank raised its policy rate from 2.00% to 4.25%, and the government announced tax rises worth 1.1% of GDP."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Case for supply-side causation: output fell while the price level rose 7.9% — the combination that only a leftward SRAS shift produces. A leftward AD shift would have reduced output and reduced price pressure. The 31% rise in imported energy prices and 19% rise in metals prices supply the mechanism, raising firms\' costs at every level of output.\n• Case for demand-side causation: the fall in real household disposable income of 2.2%, falling consumer confidence, a policy rate raised from 2.00% to 4.25% and tax rises worth 1.1% of GDP are all contractionary influences on AD. Rising unemployment from 4.1% to 5.3% is consistent with AD falling as well as with AS contracting.\n• The two are connected, and this is the strongest analytical point available. The cost shock raised prices, which cut real incomes and therefore consumption; it also provoked the monetary and fiscal tightening. The demand-side contraction is largely a consequence of the supply shock and of the policy response to it, so they are stages of one sequence rather than rival explanations.\n• Diagrammatic test: a pure SRAS leftward shift gives higher P and lower Y; a pure AD leftward shift gives lower P and lower Y; the observed combination of a 7.9% rise in the price level with falling output means the supply shift must dominate, with the AD shift moderating the price rise and deepening the output loss.\n• Evaluation: the initiating cause was clearly supply-side and external, but the depth of the slowdown owes a great deal to the demand contraction that followed, part of it a deliberate policy choice. A defensible judgement is that the supply shock caused the slowdown and the policy response determined its severity — which also explains why disinflation was achieved at the cost of higher unemployment.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — uses the price-and-output combination as a diagnostic, recognises the interaction and judges. L1 (1–4) — lists demand and supply factors without using the data to discriminate between them.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which crowding out is likely to reduce the effect of the government's borrowing.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"The government of Country M plans to borrow an additional 2.4% of GDP to fund investment. Public sector net debt stands at 94% of GDP. The yield on ten-year government bonds rose from 4.1% to 4.6% following the announcement. Unemployment is 4.7%, capacity utilisation is 83% against a long-run average of 82%, and private sector investment intentions surveys report the cost of finance as a binding constraint for 29% of firms, up from 12% two years ago. The central bank's policy rate is 3.75% and inflation is 3.1%."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Financial crowding out is visible in the data. Additional borrowing of 2.4% of GDP raises the demand for loanable funds; the ten-year yield rose from 4.1% to 4.6% on the announcement, and government bond yields anchor corporate borrowing costs. With 29% of firms now reporting the cost of finance as a binding constraint, up from 12%, the channel through which higher rates reduce private investment is demonstrably open.\n• Resource crowding out: capacity utilisation at 83% is above the long-run average and unemployment at 4.7% is near the natural rate, so the public sector will be bidding for labour and materials that are already employed rather than idle. At full capacity the crowding out is real, not just financial — public spending displaces private output roughly one for one.\n• Monetary policy reaction: with inflation at 3.1% against target and the policy rate at 3.75%, fiscal expansion makes tightening more likely, which reinforces the crowding out. Debt at 94% of GDP also raises the risk that investors demand a higher premium, steepening the effect.\n• Case that crowding out will be limited: the borrowing funds investment, not current spending. Public capital — grid, transport, housing — is often complementary to private capital and raises its return, so it can crowd in private investment over time. The yield rise of 50 basis points is modest, and an open economy can attract foreign savings at the going world rate, weakening the closed-economy loanable funds mechanism.\n• Evaluation: the conditions here are close to the worst case for a fiscal expansion — little spare capacity, inflation above target, high existing debt and a demonstrable rise in borrowing costs — so crowding out is likely to be substantial. A defensible judgement is that the demand-side effect will be small but that the case for the borrowing, if it genuinely finances productive capital, rests on the supply-side LRAS effect over the following decade rather than on the multiplier now.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — distinguishes financial from resource crowding out, applies the yield and capacity data and judges. L1 (1–4) — defines crowding out with little application.` },

      /* ─────────────── 12 marks — evaluate, asterisked ─────────────── */
      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether an increase in aggregate demand will mainly raise real output or mainly raise the price level.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"Two economies each announce a fiscal stimulus worth 2% of GDP. In Country N, unemployment is 9.2%, capacity utilisation is 71% against a long-run average of 82%, inflation is 0.4% and the central bank's policy rate is 0.1%. In Country P, unemployment is 4.2%, capacity utilisation is 86%, inflation is 4.1% against a 2% target and the policy rate is 4.75%. Both economies have a marginal propensity to import of around 0.2."`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The analytical framework: the split of a rightward AD shift between output and prices depends on the slope of aggregate supply at the starting point. On a Keynesian AS curve, the horizontal section gives almost all output and no inflation; the intermediate section splits the effect; the vertical section gives all inflation and no output. A correctly drawn diagram with the two economies at different points on the same AS curve is the clearest way to make this argument.\n• Country N is on the elastic section. Unemployment of 9.2% and capacity utilisation of 71%, eleven points below the long-run average, mean abundant idle labour and capital, so firms can raise output without bidding up wages or input prices. Inflation of 0.4% confirms the absence of price pressure, and a policy rate of 0.1% means no monetary offset — the central bank cannot tighten much even if it wished to. Expect most of the stimulus to appear as real output.\n• Country P is on the steep or vertical section. Unemployment of 4.2% is at or below most estimates of the natural rate and capacity utilisation of 86% is above average, so additional demand meets a supply constraint. Inflation of 4.1% is already twice the target, and a policy rate of 4.75% signals a central bank that will tighten further in response, offsetting the fiscal expansion. Expect most of the stimulus to appear as higher prices.\n• Second-order considerations that apply to both: an MPM of 0.2 means part of the demand leaks abroad, reducing the multiplier and diverting some of the effect to trading partners; time lags mean the stimulus may arrive after the cyclical position has changed; and expectations matter, since if firms believe demand will be sustained they add capacity rather than simply raising prices.\n• The hardest issue is that the position of LRAS is not observable. Capacity utilisation covers manufacturing only, and estimates of the natural rate carry wide error margins, so a policymaker cannot be sure which economy they are in — the error made repeatedly in the 1970s was to assume an economy was Country N when it was Country P.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that the answer is entirely state-dependent and that the case for demand stimulus is strong only when the output gap is large, inflation is below target and monetary policy is constrained — the configuration in Country N. In Country P the same policy would raise inflation, invite tighter money and achieve little real output, so composition matters: spending that shifts LRAS is the only expansion that works in an economy at capacity.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — sustained analysis with the AS-slope framework applied to both sets of data, and a supported judgement. L2 (5–8) — explains both cases with some application. L1 (1–4) — describes AD shifts without distinguishing the two economies. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether the Keynesian model of aggregate supply describes the economy better than the classical model.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"Between 2009 and 2015, Country Q had unemployment above 7.5% for six consecutive years, nominal wage growth below 1.5% a year but almost no nominal wage cuts, and inflation below its 2% target throughout. Output remained around 4% below the pre-crisis trend for the whole period, and the central bank's policy rate stayed at 0.5%. Between 2022 and 2026, by contrast, the same economy saw unemployment fall to 4.2%, inflation rise to 4.1%, and firms report acute shortages of skilled labour with output unable to expand despite strong order books."`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The two models: the classical model has a vertical LRAS at full employment, with flexible wages and prices ensuring the economy self-corrects to potential output; the Keynesian model has an AS curve with an elastic section at low output, an intermediate section, and a vertical section at full capacity, so the economy can settle in an underemployment equilibrium.\n• The 2009-2015 evidence supports Keynes. Unemployment above 7.5% for six years with almost no nominal wage cuts is precisely the downward wage stickiness that the classical adjustment mechanism requires and does not get. Under the classical model, excess labour supply should have cut wages, shifted SRAS right and restored full employment within a normal cycle; instead output stayed 4% below trend for six years. Inflation below target throughout is consistent with operating on the elastic section, where AD changes affect output rather than prices.\n• The policy rate stuck at 0.5% strengthens the Keynesian reading: at the effective lower bound the interest rate cannot fall enough to equate saving and investment at full employment, which is the second classical adjustment mechanism failing.\n• The 2022-2026 evidence supports the classical model. Unemployment at 4.2% with acute skill shortages and firms unable to expand output despite strong order books is an economy on a vertical supply constraint, where additional demand produces the inflation observed at 4.1% rather than growth. Here the classical prediction is exactly right.\n• Synthesis: the two periods are not evidence for rival models so much as for different sections of the same Keynesian AS curve. The Keynesian model contains the classical case as its vertical section, which is why it is the more general description; the classical model cannot accommodate 2009-2015 at all.\n• Policy implications, which is why the choice matters: in the first period demand stimulus would have raised output at little inflationary cost, and austerity would have been self-defeating; in the second, only supply-side measures that raise capacity can deliver growth, and demand stimulus is inflationary. Getting the diagnosis wrong is costly in both directions.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that the Keynesian model describes the economy better because it accommodates both episodes, while noting the classical model\'s strength as a long-run account and its warning that demand management cannot raise output permanently. The practical difficulty remains identifying, in real time, which section of the curve the economy is on.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — uses both periods, links them to the sections of the AS curve and judges. L2 (5–8) — describes both models with some application. L1 (1–4) — describes the models with no use of the stimulus. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether the multiplier provides a strong justification for fiscal expansion.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"In a deep recession in Country R, independent estimates put the fiscal multiplier on public investment at between 0.9 and 1.7. In a later period of near-full employment, the same institution estimated the multiplier at between 0.3 and 0.7. Country R has a marginal propensity to import of 0.24, a marginal rate of tax of 0.27 and a marginal propensity to save of 0.13. Public sector net debt is 94% of GDP, and the average maturity of that debt is 14 years. Major infrastructure projects in Country R take an average of 4.5 years from announcement to first spending."`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The case for: the multiplier means an injection raises national income by more than itself, so in a recession fiscal expansion is unusually cost-effective — the estimated range of 0.9 to 1.7 in a deep recession implies that at the upper end each pound of spending generates £1.70 of income. Because higher income raises tax receipts and cuts benefit spending, a multiplier above 1 means the expansion partly pays for itself, and it prevents the hysteresis that would permanently reduce potential output.\n• Why the multiplier is larger in recession: spare capacity means output can respond, so the AS curve is elastic; interest rates are typically low or at the lower bound, so there is little monetary offset and little crowding out; and credit-constrained households spend a high share of any income they receive.\n• The case against: the same institution estimates the multiplier at 0.3 to 0.7 near full employment, which means a pound of public spending raises income by less than a pound — the expansion displaces private activity. The leakages in Country R are large: MPW = 0.13 + 0.27 + 0.24 = 0.64, so the simple multiplier is only 1/0.64 = 1.56 before any crowding out or monetary offset, and a quarter of each extra pound leaks abroad.\n• Timing destroys much of the case in practice. Infrastructure takes an average of 4.5 years from announcement to first spending, so by the time the injection arrives the recession may be over and the multiplier may have fallen to the 0.3-0.7 range — the stimulus becomes pro-cyclical, adding demand when the economy is already at capacity.\n• Debt and sustainability: at 94% of GDP, further borrowing raises the debt ratio and the interest burden, although an average maturity of 14 years means the government is insulated from short-run rate rises, which weakens the immediate sustainability objection.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that the multiplier justifies fiscal expansion conditionally rather than generally — specifically, in a deep recession with monetary policy constrained, using spending that can be disbursed quickly. It is a weak justification for permanent expansion, a weak justification in an open economy with large leakages, and a weak justification for long-lead infrastructure as counter-cyclical policy, though such projects may still be justified by their supply-side effect on LRAS.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — uses the multiplier arithmetic and the state-dependence of the estimates, addresses timing and judges. L2 (5–8) — explains the multiplier with some application. L1 (1–4) — describes the multiplier process. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether the paradox of thrift is a serious concern for policymakers.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"During a sharp downturn, households in Country S raised their saving ratio from 7.2% to 13.8% within four quarters. Consumption, 64% of aggregate demand, fell 5.1%. Bank lending to businesses fell 6.4% over the same period despite the rise in deposits, and the central bank's policy rate had already been cut to 0.1%. Unemployment rose from 4.4% to 7.9%. In a later expansion, the saving ratio rose again from 6.9% to 9.1%, but this time business investment grew 3.2% a year and the policy rate was 3.75%."`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The paradox stated: saving is rational for the individual household seeking a buffer against job loss, but saving is a withdrawal from the circular flow. If all households save more at once, consumption and therefore AD fall, national income falls, and households may end up saving less in absolute terms than they intended — a fallacy of composition.\n• The first episode fits the paradox exactly. The saving ratio nearly doubled from 7.2% to 13.8% in four quarters, consumption (64% of AD) fell 5.1%, AD shifted left, the multiplier amplified the fall and unemployment rose from 4.4% to 7.9%. The rising unemployment then justifies further precautionary saving, which is what makes the process self-reinforcing.\n• Why the classical answer fails here: the usual reply is that saving funds investment through the loanable funds market, so the withdrawal returns as an injection. The data refute that in this episode — bank lending to businesses fell 6.4% despite rising deposits, because banks were rationing credit and firms facing collapsing demand did not want to borrow. With the policy rate already at 0.1%, the interest rate could not fall further to restore the balance. This is the liquidity trap, and it is precisely the condition under which the paradox bites.\n• The second episode shows the opposite. The saving ratio rose from 6.9% to 9.1%, but business investment grew 3.2% a year and the policy rate at 3.75% left room for the central bank to cut if demand weakened. Here the saving was intermediated into investment, raising the capital stock and shifting LRAS right — saving was beneficial, not harmful.\n• Policy implications: where the paradox applies, the appropriate response is fiscal expansion to replace the missing private demand, since monetary policy is exhausted; where it does not, fiscal expansion risks crowding out and inflation. Automatic stabilisers help in both cases without requiring a diagnosis.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that the paradox of thrift is a serious concern in a specific and identifiable set of circumstances — a sharp demand shock, impaired credit intermediation and policy rates at the lower bound — and not a general objection to saving. The two episodes in the stimulus are the same behaviour with opposite consequences, which is the clearest demonstration that the concern is conditional rather than permanent.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — distinguishes the two episodes, develops the liquidity trap condition and judges. L2 (5–8) — explains the paradox with some application. L1 (1–4) — states that saving reduces AD. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether inflation expectations are the most important determinant of actual inflation.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"Surveys of households in Country T show expected inflation one year ahead rising from 2.2% to 4.6% over twelve months, while expectations five to ten years ahead rose only from 2.9% to 3.1%. Actual CPI inflation over the same period rose from 2.4% to 4.3%, driven mainly by a 34% rise in imported energy prices. Annual growth in average weekly earnings rose from 3.1% to 5.4%. The central bank raised its policy rate from 2.0% to 4.25% and repeatedly restated its 2% target. Union membership covers 22% of employees, down from 49% in 1980."`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The mechanism by which expectations become inflation: if workers expect prices to rise they bargain for higher nominal pay to protect real income; if firms expect costs and rivals\' prices to rise they raise their own prices pre-emptively and index contracts. Both raise costs and prices, confirming the original expectation — a self-fulfilling process. On a Phillips curve diagram, higher expected inflation shifts the SRPC outward, so any given unemployment rate is associated with higher inflation.\n• Evidence for the expectations channel: short-run expectations rose from 2.2% to 4.6% and earnings growth rose from 3.1% to 5.4%, which is consistent with wage bargaining responding to expected price rises. Once that happens, the inflation persists independently of its original cause.\n• Evidence against expectations being most important: the stimulus identifies the immediate driver as a 34% rise in imported energy prices — an external cost shock that would have raised the price level whatever households expected. The expectations rose after, and because of, the observed inflation, which suggests they are largely adaptive and therefore a transmission channel rather than a primary cause.\n• The crucial distinction is between short-run and long-run expectations. Five-to-ten year expectations rose only from 2.9% to 3.1%, which means expectations remain broadly anchored — households expect the overshoot to be temporary. Anchored long-run expectations are what prevent a temporary shock becoming permanent inflation, and they reflect the credibility the central bank has built, reinforced by raising the policy rate to 4.25% and restating the 2% target.\n• Institutional change weakens the channel: union coverage of 22% against 49% in 1980 means the wage-bargaining mechanism that transmitted expectations into wages in the 1970s is much weaker, so a given rise in expected inflation produces a smaller rise in actual wages and prices.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that expectations are not usually the trigger but are decisive for persistence — they determine whether a shock produces a one-off rise in the price level or a sustained rise in the inflation rate. That is why central banks treat long-run anchoring as their central task and why the near-stability of five-to-ten year expectations in the data is the most reassuring number in the stimulus.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — distinguishes trigger from persistence, uses the short-run/long-run expectations split and judges. L2 (5–8) — explains the expectations mechanism with some application. L1 (1–4) — asserts that expectations cause inflation. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      /* ─────────────── 15 marks — evaluate with diagram and extract ─────────────── */
      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s) and the information in Extract 1, the extent to which an increase in government spending will raise real output.

Extract 1 — written for this practice question
"The government of Country U announced an increase in spending worth 2% of GDP. Unemployment stands at 4.9% and most estimates put the natural rate between 4.0% and 5.0%. Capacity utilisation is 83% against a long-run average of 82%. CPI inflation is 3.1% against a 2% target, and the central bank held its policy rate at 3.75%, with three of its nine members voting for an increase. Public sector net debt is 93.8% of GDP. The marginal propensity to import is 0.22 and the marginal rate of tax is 0.27."`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: AD/AS with AD shifting right from AD to AD1. The decisive feature is where the starting equilibrium sits — on a steep section of AS or close to a vertical LRAS, given the capacity data — so that the shift raises the price level substantially and real output only modestly. Credit a second diagram showing the contrasting elastic-section case.\n• The mechanism: G is a component of AD, so the increase directly raises planned expenditure, and the multiplier amplifies it through successive rounds of income and spending.\n• Why the output effect will be small here. Unemployment at 4.9% sits inside the estimated 4.0-5.0% natural rate range and capacity utilisation at 83% is above its long-run average, so there are few idle resources. Additional demand therefore bids up wages and input prices rather than calling forth output — the economy is near the vertical section of AS.\n• Leakages: MPW = 0.22 + 0.27 + 0.13 or more, so even before crowding out the multiplier is well below the textbook 1/(1 − MPC) figure; at MPW = 0.49 the multiplier is only about 2.0, and higher saving would reduce it further. A fifth of each extra pound leaks abroad as imports, benefiting trading partners rather than domestic output.\n• Monetary offset: with inflation at 3.1% and three of nine members already voting to raise the policy rate above 3.75%, a fiscal loosening makes tightening more likely. Higher rates reduce interest-sensitive consumption and investment, so part of the fiscal injection is crowded out. With debt at 93.8% of GDP, higher gilt yields would also raise the government\'s own financing costs.\n• Composition is the strongest counter-argument. If the spending is capital spending on infrastructure, skills or research, it shifts LRAS right as well as AD, so real output rises sustainably and the inflationary consequence is smaller. On the diagram, the best answers show both curves shifting. But this effect operates over years and does not change the short-run analysis.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that the increase will raise nominal spending much more than real output, because the economy has little spare capacity, leakages are large and the monetary response is likely to be contractionary. The policy is defensible only on supply-side grounds, and answers should note that estimates of the natural rate and of capacity carry wide error margins, so the judgement is probabilistic rather than certain.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — correct diagram used in the analysis, the capacity position, leakages and monetary offset all applied to the extract, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, mechanism explained, evaluation limited. L1 (1–5) — describes an AD shift; diagram missing or incorrect. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s) and the information in Extract 1, the extent to which shifts in long-run aggregate supply matter more than shifts in aggregate demand for a country's growth.

Extract 1 — written for this practice question
"Over the last fifteen years, Country V's real GDP has grown at an average of 1.1% a year. Output per hour worked has grown 0.4% a year, against 2.2% a year in the fifteen years before 2008. Business investment has averaged 9.4% of GDP against 12.8% in comparable economies, and spending on research and development is 1.7% of GDP against an OECD average of 2.7%. Over the same fifteen years the policy rate has ranged from 0.1% to 5.25%, and the government has run budget deficits averaging 4.6% of GDP, yet the growth rate has barely varied."`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: AD/AS showing the distinction between actual growth (AD shifting right along an upward-sloping SRAS, closing a negative output gap) and potential growth (LRAS shifting right). The essential point is visual: repeated AD shifts against a stationary LRAS move the economy along the price axis, not the output axis, once the output gap is closed.\n• Case that LRAS matters more: over fifteen years the growth rate is determined by the growth of productive capacity, which depends on the quantity and quality of factors of production. The data show the supply side failing on every dimension — productivity growth of 0.4% against a previous 2.2%, investment of 9.4% of GDP against a 12.8% comparator, R&D of 1.7% of GDP against an OECD average of 2.7%. All three point to a slow-moving LRAS, and growth of 1.1% a year is the consequence.\n• The natural experiment in the extract is powerful. The policy rate has ranged from 0.1% to 5.25% and deficits have averaged 4.6% of GDP, yet growth has barely varied. Very large variation in demand-side settings producing almost no variation in the growth rate is direct evidence that demand is not the binding constraint over this horizon.\n• Case for AD mattering: in the short run, demand determines whether the economy uses the capacity it has, and persistent deficient demand causes hysteresis — long-term unemployment erodes skills, and firms facing weak expected demand postpone the very investment that would raise LRAS. On that reading the weak investment of 9.4% of GDP is partly a symptom of chronically weak demand, so the two are not independent and demand management has a supply-side legacy.\n• Demand also matters for the avoidance of harm: a deep recession destroys capacity permanently, so demand stabilisation protects LRAS even if it cannot raise it.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that over a fifteen-year horizon LRAS is decisive — the growth rate is a supply-side variable and demand policy can only determine the position within capacity — but that the dichotomy is too clean, because demand conditions influence investment and therefore feed back into LRAS. The practical implication is that the policy response to the data should be supply-side (investment, R&D, skills, infrastructure), while demand policy should aim to avoid the deep recessions that destroy capacity.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — correct diagram distinguishing actual from potential growth, uses all the supply-side data plus the demand-variation evidence, and reaches a supported conclusion that engages with the feedback from AD to LRAS. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, both sides described, limited evaluation. L1 (1–5) — describes AD and AS shifts. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s) and the information in Extract 1, the extent to which the accelerator explains the volatility of investment.

Extract 1 — written for this practice question
"In Country W, real GDP growth and business investment growth over seven years were: 2019, GDP +1.6% and investment +2.4%; 2020, GDP -9.2% and investment -14.8%; 2021, GDP +7.4% and investment +11.9%; 2022, GDP +4.1% and investment +6.2%; 2023, GDP +0.9% and investment -2.1%; 2024, GDP +1.3% and investment +1.8%; 2025, GDP +1.1% and investment +0.4%. Over the same period a survey found that 61% of firms cited uncertainty about future demand as their main constraint on investment, and the policy rate ranged between 0.1% and 5.25%."`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: AD/AS or a circular flow showing investment as a component of AD, with the multiplier-accelerator interaction generating cyclical movement around the trend; credit a business cycle diagram showing actual GDP oscillating around trend, with the accelerator explaining the amplitude. A labelled diagram showing an initial AD shift, the induced rise in investment and a further AD shift is the strongest treatment.\n• The accelerator stated: desired capital stock is proportional to expected output, so net investment depends on the rate of change of output, not its level. A fall in the growth rate — not a fall in output — is enough to cut net investment to zero.\n• The data support it strongly. Investment is consistently more volatile than GDP in the same direction: GDP fell 9.2% in 2020 while investment fell 14.8%; GDP rose 7.4% in 2021 while investment rose 11.9%. The decisive observation is 2023, when GDP still grew 0.9% but investment fell 2.1% — exactly the accelerator prediction that decelerating growth produces absolute falls in investment.\n• The multiplier-accelerator interaction explains self-sustaining cycles: an initial rise in autonomous spending is multiplied into higher income; faster income growth triggers a more than proportional rise in investment; that investment is itself an injection, multiplied again. When growth decelerates, the process reverses, which is why cycles turn without an external shock.\n• Limitations of the accelerator: it assumes a fixed capital-output ratio and no spare capacity, whereas firms with idle capital can meet rising demand without investing at all — which is why the response is weaker early in a recovery. It ignores expectations, and Extract 1 shows 61% of firms citing uncertainty about future demand: the accelerator uses current output growth as a proxy for expected future demand, and that proxy breaks down when uncertainty is high. It also ignores the cost and availability of finance, and the policy rate ranged from 0.1% to 5.25% over the period.\n• Other sources of investment volatility: lumpiness and irreversibility of capital projects, animal spirits, corporation tax and capital allowance changes, and the availability of bank credit.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that the accelerator explains the amplitude and the timing of investment swings better than any single alternative and fits the 2023 observation that nothing else explains as neatly, but that it is a mechanical rule standing in for an expectations process. It should be read as a good first approximation whose accuracy depends on how closely current output growth tracks expected future demand.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — diagram used, the 2023 data point exploited, limitations engaged and a supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — accelerator explained and applied to some data, limited evaluation. L1 (1–5) — describes the accelerator. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s) and the information in Extract 1, the extent to which a fall in household consumption will cause a recession.

Extract 1 — written for this practice question
"Household consumption accounts for 62% of aggregate demand in Country X, where real household disposable income fell 1.1% and house prices fell 3.2% in the year to mid-2026. The household saving ratio rose from 8.1% to 9.6% and consumption fell 0.4%. Over the same period, government investment spending rose 4.1%, exports rose 2.6% as trading partners grew faster than Country X, and business investment rose 1.9%. The central bank's policy rate is 3.75% and inflation is 3.1%. Country X's marginal propensity to import is 0.22."`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: AD/AS with AD shifting left from AD to AD1, showing real output falling from Y to Y1 and the price level falling from P to P1; mark the resulting negative output gap against a vertical LRAS. Credit a circular flow diagram showing the rise in the S withdrawal.\n• The mechanism: consumption is 62% of AD, the largest single component, so a fall in C shifts AD left. The multiplier amplifies it — those who lose income as firms cut output reduce their own spending, so the final fall in national income exceeds the initial fall in consumption. Falling output raises cyclical unemployment, which reduces incomes further.\n• Why the causes matter: the fall is driven by real disposable income falling 1.1% and by a negative wealth effect from house prices falling 3.2%, with precautionary saving raising the saving ratio from 8.1% to 9.6%. Income-driven falls are more persistent than confidence-driven ones, which can reverse quickly.\n• Why a recession may not follow. The fall in consumption is only 0.4%, which against a 62% weight contributes roughly a quarter of a percentage point to AD. The other components are rising: government investment up 4.1%, exports up 2.6% and business investment up 1.9%. Those offsets are large enough to keep total AD growing, so a technical recession — two consecutive quarters of falling real GDP — need not occur.\n• Dampening mechanisms: automatic stabilisers cut tax receipts and raise benefit spending without any policy decision; an MPM of 0.22 means part of the demand reduction falls on imports, so the domestic multiplier is smaller; and with the policy rate at 3.75% the central bank has room to cut if activity weakens, though inflation at 3.1% constrains how quickly it would.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that a fall in consumption is the most likely single cause of a recession given its weight in AD, but that whether one occurs depends on the size of the fall, on whether other components offset it and on the policy response. On the specific evidence here the fall is small and offset, so a recession is unlikely — though the underlying causes (falling real incomes and falling wealth) are the kind that deepen if they persist, so the risk is greater over two years than over two quarters.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — correct diagram used, weighs the consumption fall against the offsetting components with the data, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, mechanism explained, limited use of offsets. L1 (1–5) — asserts that falling consumption causes recession. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      /* ─────────────── 25 marks — Section B (diagram) and Section C ─────────────── */
      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s), whether demand-side policies are more effective than supply-side policies in raising an economy\'s rate of economic growth.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: AD/AS distinguishing actual growth (AD shifting right along an upward-sloping SRAS, closing a negative output gap) from potential growth (LRAS shifting right). A strong answer shows that repeated AD shifts against a stationary LRAS deliver inflation once the gap is closed, and uses a PPF outward shift for the supply-side case.\n• The case for demand-side policy: it works quickly. Monetary policy can be changed at any meeting — the Bank of England\'s MPC decides eight times a year and held Bank Rate at 3.75% on 16 September 2026 — and fiscal changes can be announced at a Budget, with the UK\'s next set for 28 October 2026. Where a negative output gap exists, demand stimulus converts idle resources into output with little inflationary cost, and it prevents the hysteresis that would otherwise permanently reduce capacity.\n• The limits of demand-side policy: once the output gap is closed it cannot raise output at all, only prices — the vertical LRAS result. It is subject to recognition, implementation and impact lags of up to two years, to unreliable output gap estimates, to crowding out where debt is high (UK public sector net debt was 93.8% of GDP at the end of August 2026) and to leakage abroad through the marginal propensity to import.\n• The case for supply-side policy: it is the only route to a higher long-run growth rate, because growth in output per head is growth in productivity. Interventionist measures — infrastructure, education and training, R&D subsidies, apprenticeships — and market-based measures — deregulation, tax reform, competition policy, labour market flexibility — both aim to shift LRAS right, which raises output and lowers the price level simultaneously, so there is no inflation trade-off.\n• The limits of supply-side policy: very long lags, since a trained cohort takes a decade to reach peak productivity and infrastructure takes years to build; high fiscal cost with uncertain returns; market-based measures can worsen inequality and job security; and supply-side capacity is worth little if demand is too weak to use it.\n• The interaction is the sophisticated point. Weak demand depresses investment, and weak investment depresses LRAS, so demand management has supply-side consequences. Equally, supply-side policies such as infrastructure spending are themselves an injection into AD, so the categories overlap — one reason public capital spending is often defended on both grounds at once.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that the question is wrongly posed as a competition. Demand-side policy determines whether the economy uses the capacity it has and is the right instrument in a recession; supply-side policy determines how much capacity there is and is the only instrument for the long-run growth rate. The strongest answers argue that the effectiveness of each is state-dependent, and that the appropriate mix depends on the size of the output gap, the credibility of the inflation target and the fiscal space available.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires correctly used diagrams distinguishing actual from potential growth, integrated evaluation and a conclusion that follows from the argument. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s), whether the Keynesian view of aggregate supply or the classical view provides the better guide for macroeconomic policy.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagrams: a Keynesian AS curve with its elastic, intermediate and vertical sections, alongside a classical vertical LRAS with an upward-sloping SRAS that shifts as wages adjust. The clearest treatment shows the same AD shift producing opposite outcomes under the two models.\n• The classical position: output is determined by the quantity and quality of factors of production, and flexible wages and prices return the economy to full employment. Any negative output gap is self-correcting — excess labour supply cuts wages, SRAS shifts right and output returns to potential at a lower price level. It follows that demand management is at best unnecessary and at worst inflationary, and that policy should concentrate on supply-side reform and on a credible monetary rule.\n• The Keynesian position: wages are sticky downwards because of contracts, minimum wages, union bargaining and efficiency-wage considerations, so the self-correcting mechanism fails. An economy can settle in an underemployment equilibrium, and on the elastic section of AS a rightward AD shift raises output with little inflation. Active demand management is therefore both possible and necessary.\n• Evidence for the Keynesian view: the persistence of unemployment through the 1930s and after 2008, when output in several advanced economies remained below the pre-crisis trend for the better part of a decade with inflation below target and policy rates at their effective lower bound. Almost no nominal wage cuts occurred despite high unemployment, which is precisely what the classical mechanism requires.\n• Evidence for the classical view: the 1970s, when repeated demand expansion produced accelerating inflation without lasting reductions in unemployment, vindicating the vertical long-run Phillips curve; and episodes such as September 2026 in the UK, when CPI inflation of 3.1% coexisted with unemployment of 4.9%, close to most estimates of the natural rate, so demand stimulus would have added to prices rather than output.\n• Synthesis: the Keynesian AS curve contains the classical case as its vertical section, which makes it the more general model, and most modern macroeconomics is a version of this — new Keynesian models with sticky prices in the short run and classical outcomes in the long run. The practical disagreement is not about the shape of the curve but about how long the short run lasts and about where the economy currently sits on it.\n• The identification problem is the decisive practical issue. Potential output is unobservable, estimates carry margins of error of well over a percentage point of GDP, and they are revised heavily after the event. The asymmetry of the error matters: treating a supply-constrained economy as demand-constrained produces inflation that is costly to reverse, while treating a demand-constrained economy as supply-constrained produces avoidable unemployment and hysteresis.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that the Keynesian view is the better guide in deep downturns and at the effective lower bound, that the classical view is the better guide for long-run growth and for the limits of demand management, and that a policy framework should therefore be state-contingent — which is close to what inflation-targeting central banks with a secondary growth objective actually do.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires both AS representations drawn and used, historical evidence weighed on both sides, and a conclusion that follows from the argument. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s), whether an increase in investment is the most important cause of long-run economic growth.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: AD/AS with LRAS shifting right, or a PPF shifting outward. Credit a diagram showing investment as both an AD component (short-run effect) and an LRAS determinant (long-run effect) — the dual role is the core of the question.\n• The case for investment: investment raises the capital stock, so each worker has more and better capital to work with (capital deepening), which raises output per hour. It is also the vehicle through which new technology is embodied — most innovation reaches production through new machines, software and plant, so investment and technical progress are hard to separate. Investment in infrastructure lowers costs across the whole economy, generating positive externalities that private returns understate.\n• Evidence: economies with persistently low investment ratios tend to have persistently low productivity growth. A country investing 9.4% of GDP against a 12.8% comparator will accumulate capital more slowly, and if its productivity growth has fallen from 2.2% to 0.4% a year, weak capital deepening is a leading candidate explanation.\n• The case against — diminishing returns. The Solow model shows that capital accumulation alone produces transitional growth, not permanent growth: as the capital-labour ratio rises, the marginal product of capital falls, and the economy converges on a steady state where investment merely replaces depreciation. Permanent growth in output per head requires technological progress, which in the basic model is exogenous.\n• Rival causes with real force. Human capital: an educated, adaptable workforce raises output from the same capital and is a precondition for using advanced capital productively. Technology and R&D: endogenous growth theory makes knowledge the source of increasing rather than diminishing returns, and knowledge is non-rival, so its growth effects compound. Institutions: secure property rights, contract enforcement, low corruption, competitive markets and macroeconomic stability determine whether investment is productive at all — the same investment ratio produces very different growth in different institutional settings. Also credit labour supply, trade openness and natural resources.\n• The categories interact. Investment responds to expected demand and to institutional quality; human capital raises the return to physical capital; R&D is itself a form of investment in intangibles that national accounts have historically undercounted. Ranking them as independent causes is therefore partly artificial.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that investment is the most important proximate cause — it is the channel through which most other improvements reach production — but not the ultimate cause, since what determines whether investment happens and whether it is productive is the institutional and technological environment. The policy implication follows: raising the investment ratio by subsidy alone will have limited effect if the underlying constraints are skills, uncertainty about demand or planning and regulatory delay.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires the diagram used to distinguish short-run and long-run effects of investment, engagement with diminishing returns and a supported conclusion. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate whether the multiplier is a reliable guide for government policy.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• What the multiplier claims: an injection into the circular flow raises national income by more than itself, because one agent\'s spending is another\'s income and is partly re-spent. k = 1/(MPS + MPT + MPM). If k exceeds 1, fiscal expansion is unusually cost-effective, partly self-financing through higher tax receipts and lower benefit spending, and capable of preventing the hysteresis that would permanently reduce potential output.\n• Reasons for reliability: the underlying logic is an accounting identity about successive rounds of spending, and the direction of the effect is not seriously disputed. The parameters — saving, tax and import propensities — are measurable, and the model correctly predicts that multipliers are larger in closed economies, in recessions and where households are credit-constrained.\n• Reason for caution 1 — the value is not a constant. Empirical estimates vary widely, typically well above 1 in a deep recession with monetary policy at the lower bound and well below 1 near full employment, when crowding out and monetary offset dominate. A policymaker who applies a recession multiplier to a full-employment economy will badly overstate the effect, and the distinction matters most precisely when the stakes are highest.\n• Reason for caution 2 — capacity. The multiplier assumes output can respond. On a vertical LRAS the real multiplier is zero: nominal spending rises, prices rise and real output does not. Since the output gap is unobservable and estimates carry margins of error wider than the gap itself, the policymaker cannot know which case applies in real time.\n• Reason for caution 3 — offsetting responses. Central bank tightening in response to fiscal expansion reduces the effective multiplier; Ricardian equivalence suggests households may save a tax cut in anticipation of future taxes; and in an open economy a high marginal propensity to import diverts part of the stimulus abroad.\n• Reason for caution 4 — timing. Recognition, decision, implementation and impact lags mean the injection typically arrives well after the diagnosis. Major infrastructure can take years from announcement to first spending, by which time the multiplier itself may have fallen. Stimulus that arrives late is pro-cyclical and can do harm.\n• What the multiplier is reliable for: comparing policies rather than forecasting levels. It correctly predicts that spending directed at credit-constrained households and at domestically produced goods has a larger effect than a tax cut for high earners or a subsidy for imported capital equipment. Automatic stabilisers exploit the mechanism without requiring a real-time estimate of its size, which is a strong argument for relying on them rather than on discretionary fine-tuning.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that the multiplier is a reliable guide to the direction and to the relative merits of different policies, and an unreliable guide to magnitude — so it should inform the design of fiscal policy without being used to justify precise claims about how much national income will rise. Governments that quote a single point estimate are overstating what the model can deliver.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires the distinction between direction and magnitude, engagement with state-dependence and lags, and a conclusion that follows from the argument. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate whether an increase in aggregate demand is always desirable for an economy.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• When it is desirable: with a negative output gap, a rightward AD shift converts idle labour and capital into output at little cost in inflation. It reduces cyclical unemployment and the associated costs — forgone output, lost tax revenue, higher benefit spending, and the health and social costs borne by the unemployed. Crucially it prevents hysteresis: the longer a downturn lasts, the more cyclical unemployment becomes structural, permanently lowering potential output, so acting early protects LRAS as well as current output.\n• The fiscal arithmetic can favour it: if the multiplier exceeds 1 in a deep recession, the expansion partly pays for itself through higher receipts and lower transfers, and growth raises the denominator of the debt-to-GDP ratio.\n• When it is not desirable — capacity. Near or at full employment the AS curve is steep or vertical, so additional demand raises the price level with little or no real output gain. The UK position in September 2026 illustrates the constraint: CPI inflation of 3.1% against a 2% target with unemployment of 4.9%, close to most estimates of the natural rate, is why the MPC held Bank Rate at 3.75% rather than easing.\n• Inflationary consequences: erosion of real incomes, arbitrary redistribution, distorted relative prices and, if expectations de-anchor, a wage-price spiral that costs far more output to reverse than the original expansion gained. The long-run Phillips curve implies that demand expansion buys no lasting reduction in unemployment.\n• External consequences: higher domestic demand raises import volumes, widening the current account deficit — the UK deficit was 2.8% of GDP in Quarter 1 2026 — and higher relative inflation erodes price competitiveness, compounding the effect.\n• Fiscal and financial consequences: debt-financed expansion at a debt ratio of 93.8% of GDP raises the interest burden and can push up gilt yields, crowding out private investment. Demand growth fuelled by household or corporate borrowing can inflate asset bubbles and leave balance sheets fragile.\n• Composition and sustainability: demand growth concentrated in carbon-intensive consumption carries environmental costs GDP does not record; growth in imports rather than domestic output does little for employment; and an expansion that raises consumption at the expense of investment lowers future potential output.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that an increase in AD is desirable when there is a negative output gap, inflation is at or below target and monetary policy is constrained, and undesirable when the economy is at capacity with inflation above target — so the answer is state-dependent rather than general. The best answers add that the composition of the increase matters as much as its size, since investment-led demand growth shifts LRAS as well as AD and is therefore sustainable in a way consumption-led growth is not, and that the practical obstacle is the unreliability of real-time output gap estimates.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires sustained state-dependent analysis, attention to the composition of the demand increase and a supported conclusion. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate whether macroeconomic equilibrium can be relied upon to occur at the full employment level of output.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The claim examined: macroeconomic equilibrium occurs where AD = AS, or equivalently where injections equal withdrawals. Nothing in that condition requires the resulting output to be the full employment level, so the question is whether market forces reliably move the economy there.\n• The classical case that they do: in the goods market, flexible prices clear excess supply; in the labour market, unemployment means excess supply of labour, so wages fall, firms\' costs fall, SRAS shifts right and output returns to potential at a lower price level; in the loanable funds market, an excess of saving over investment reduces the interest rate until they are equal, so withdrawals return as injections. Say\'s Law expresses the underlying idea that supply creates its own demand.\n• The Keynesian case that they do not: wages are sticky downwards because of contracts, minimum wage legislation (the UK National Living Wage rose to £12.71 an hour in April 2026), union bargaining, and efficiency-wage effects on morale and productivity. Firms respond to falling demand by cutting output and employment rather than pay, so SRAS does not shift right and the economy settles in an underemployment equilibrium.\n• The interest rate mechanism can fail too. At the effective lower bound the nominal rate cannot fall enough to equate saving and investment at full employment — the liquidity trap — and impaired credit intermediation means rising deposits need not become lending. Both were visible after 2008, when several advanced economies held policy rates near zero for years with output below the pre-crisis trend.\n• The paradox of thrift shows the mechanism failing in the other direction: a simultaneous attempt to save more reduces consumption, income and ultimately saving itself, so the market adjustment is destabilising rather than equilibrating.\n• Evidence on both sides: prolonged high unemployment in the 1930s and after 2008 supports the Keynesian reading; the 1970s, when demand expansion produced accelerating inflation without lasting employment gains, supports the classical reading that the economy does return to the natural rate. The honest summary is that the economy behaves classically over long horizons and Keynesian over short ones — the disagreement is about how long the short run lasts.\n• Hysteresis complicates the distinction: if a long downturn permanently reduces potential output, then the economy does converge on "full employment", but at a lower level of potential than before. Equilibrium is restored by the target moving, which is not a vindication of the self-correcting mechanism.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that equilibrium cannot be relied upon to occur at full employment within a policy-relevant time horizon, even if it tends there eventually, and that the adjustment mechanisms fail most badly precisely when the shortfall is largest — deep recessions with impaired credit and rates at the lower bound. That is the strongest case for active stabilisation policy, subject to the caveat that demand management cannot raise output above potential for long, and that mistaking a supply constraint for a demand shortfall produces inflation rather than growth.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires the adjustment mechanisms set out and tested against evidence, attention to the time horizon, and a conclusion that follows from the argument. Indicative content is not a checklist.` }
    ]
  },
  '2.3': {
    name: 'Economic Policy',
    questions: [
      /* ─────────────── 2 marks — points-marked ─────────────── */
      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'fiscal policy\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) Fiscal policy is the use of government spending and taxation to influence the economy.\n(1) It can be used to manage aggregate demand — expansionary fiscal policy (higher spending or lower taxes) raises AD, contractionary fiscal policy reduces it — and also to alter the distribution of income and to correct market failure.\n\nCredit reference to the distinction between discretionary changes and automatic stabilisers.` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'automatic stabilisers\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) Automatic stabilisers are features of government spending and taxation that moderate the economic cycle without any deliberate policy decision.\n(1) Mechanism: in a downturn, receipts from income tax, National Insurance, VAT and corporation tax fall as incomes and profits fall, while spending on unemployment-related benefits rises — both support aggregate demand. In a boom the reverse happens, dampening demand and inflationary pressure.\n\nCredit the point that the effect is stronger the more progressive the tax system and the more generous the benefit system.` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'the structural budget deficit\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) The structural (cyclically adjusted) budget deficit is the part of the deficit that would remain even if the economy were operating at its potential output, i.e. with a zero output gap.\n(1) It therefore strips out the cyclical component created by automatic stabilisers, and indicates the extent to which spending permanently exceeds revenue — so it can only be closed by deliberate tax rises or spending cuts, not by recovery.\n\nCredit the point that measuring it requires an estimate of potential output, which is unobservable and carries a wide margin of error.` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'quantitative tightening\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) Quantitative tightening is the reversal of quantitative easing: the central bank reduces the stock of assets — principally government bonds — that it purchased with newly created central bank reserves, either by selling them or by allowing them to mature without reinvestment.\n(1) Effect: it drains reserves from the banking system and increases the supply of gilts held by private investors, which tends to raise longer-term yields and tighten financial conditions alongside the policy rate.\n\nCredit a dated example: on 17 September 2026 the Bank of England set out a multi-year plan to reduce its stock of gilts held for monetary policy purposes to zero by the end of 2034, at an average pace of £46 billion a year including £20 billion of annual sales, having stopped selling long-dated gilts.` },

      { q: `Using information from the stimulus material, identify two supply-side policies.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"The government of Country A set out a package of measures in 2026. It will spend an extra £3.1 billion a year on technical education and apprenticeships, cut the headline rate of corporation tax from 25% to 22%, raise the rate of Universal Credit by 4%, increase the state pension in line with earnings, and fund a £9 billion programme of upgrades to the electricity grid. It also announced a temporary cut in VAT from 20% to 17.5% for twelve months to support high street retailers."`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify',
        markScheme: `(1) Additional spending on technical education and apprenticeships — an interventionist supply-side policy raising human capital and reducing structural unemployment.\n(1) The cut in corporation tax from 25% to 22% — a market-based supply-side policy intended to raise the post-tax return on investment.\n\nAlso credit: the £9 billion grid upgrade programme (infrastructure investment raising productive capacity). Do NOT credit the Universal Credit rise, the state pension increase or the temporary VAT cut, which are demand-side or redistributive measures. Award 1 mark per valid policy, maximum 2.` },

      { q: `Refer to Fig. 1. Describe the relationship between the policy rate and the rate of CPI inflation shown.

Fig. 1 — written for this practice question
Country B: average policy rate and CPI inflation, 2021-2026
  2021:  policy rate 0.10%  ;  CPI inflation 2.6%
  2022:  policy rate 1.50%  ;  CPI inflation 9.1%
  2023:  policy rate 4.75%  ;  CPI inflation 7.3%
  2024:  policy rate 5.00%  ;  CPI inflation 3.2%
  2025:  policy rate 4.25%  ;  CPI inflation 2.4%
  2026:  policy rate 3.75%  ;  CPI inflation 3.1%`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe',
        markScheme: `(1) The policy rate rises after inflation rises and falls after inflation falls — the two move together but with the policy rate lagging, so the relationship is positive but not contemporaneous.\n(1) Supporting quantification: inflation peaked at 9.1% in 2022 while the policy rate was only 1.50%; the rate peaked at 5.00% in 2024 by which time inflation had fallen to 3.2%. Accept the alternative reading that high rates in 2023-25 are followed by falling inflation, consistent with a transmission lag.\n\nDescribe question — do not require explanation of the transmission mechanism, but credit accurate reference to policy lags.` },

      { q: `Using the data below, calculate Country C\'s budget deficit in 2026 as a percentage of GDP, to two decimal places. You are advised to show your working.

Table 1 — written for this practice question
Country C, 2026, £ billion
  Total government receipts:     1 145
  Total government expenditure:  1 270
  Nominal GDP:                   2 860`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method (1 mark): deficit = expenditure − receipts = 1 270 − 1 145 = £125 billion; as a share of GDP = 125 / 2 860 × 100.\nAnswer (1 mark): 4.37% of GDP (to two decimal places).\n\nAward 2 marks for 4.37%. Award 1 mark for correctly calculating the £125 billion deficit but failing to express it as a share of GDP, or for correct method with an arithmetic slip.` },

      { q: `Using the data below, calculate Country D\'s public sector net debt as a percentage of GDP, to two decimal places. You are advised to show your working.

Table 2 — written for this practice question
Country D, end of 2026, £ billion
  Public sector net debt:  2 680
  Nominal GDP:             2 860`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method (1 mark): (public sector net debt / nominal GDP) × 100 = 2 680 / 2 860 × 100.\nAnswer (1 mark): 93.71% of GDP.\n\nAward 1 mark for correct method with an arithmetic slip. For context, UK public sector net debt was 93.8% of GDP at the end of August 2026, 1.3 percentage points lower than a year earlier (ONS). Credit, but do not require, the observation that debt is a stock and the deficit a flow.` },

      /* ─────────────── 3 marks — points-marked ─────────────── */
      { q: 'Explain why an independent central bank is likely to achieve lower inflation than one controlled by the government.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) Governments face electoral incentives: cutting interest rates before an election raises output and employment quickly while the inflationary consequence arrives with a lag, often after polling day. This is the political business cycle, and it creates a time-inconsistency problem — the government has an incentive to promise low inflation and then deviate.\n(1) Because private agents anticipate this, they build higher inflation into wage bargains and price setting, so the economy ends up with higher inflation without any lasting gain in output — the inflationary bias of discretionary policy.\n(1) Delegating the instrument to an independent committee with a clear mandate removes the incentive and makes the low-inflation commitment credible, so inflation expectations stay anchored and a given inflation rate can be achieved at a lower cost in output. The Bank of England has had operational independence since 1997, with a 2% CPI target; its Monetary Policy Committee held Bank Rate at 3.75% on 16 September 2026 by six votes to three even though inflation was above target at 3.1%, which is the kind of decision independence is designed to protect.` },

      { q: 'Explain why the fiscal multiplier is likely to be larger during a recession than during a boom.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) Spare capacity: in a recession there are unemployed workers and idle capital, so the AS curve is elastic and additional spending raises real output rather than prices. In a boom the economy is near the vertical section of AS, so the same injection raises the price level and crowds out private activity in real terms.\n(1) Monetary offset and crowding out: in a recession interest rates are typically low, possibly at the effective lower bound, and the central bank is unlikely to tighten in response to fiscal expansion, so private investment is not displaced. In a boom, with inflation above target, additional borrowing raises yields and invites a policy rate rise, both of which reduce private spending.\n(1) Household behaviour: in a recession a larger share of households are credit-constrained and therefore spend a high proportion of any extra income, raising the marginal propensity to consume; in a boom, incomes are less constrained and a larger share of additional income is saved or spent on imports, so more leaks from the circular flow at each round.` },

      { q: 'Explain why monetary policy affects the economy only after a time lag.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) The transmission mechanism works through several channels that each take time: a change in the policy rate passes into market rates and then into the rates households and firms actually face, but much household borrowing is on fixed-rate deals that reprice only when they expire, and corporate borrowing is often on existing terms.\n(1) Spending decisions themselves are slow. Firms plan capital projects months or years ahead, and households respond to changed mortgage costs gradually as budgets adjust. The exchange rate and asset price channels move quickly, but their effect on trade volumes and on consumption follows only as contracts and spending patterns change.\n(1) The consequence is that a rate change is generally judged to have its peak effect on inflation after around eighteen months to two years. Policy must therefore be set on a forecast of where inflation will be, not where it is — which is why the Bank of England\'s Monetary Policy Committee held Bank Rate at 3.75% on 16 September 2026 while judging that inflation, then 3.1%, was likely to rise further in the coming quarters.` },

      /* ─────────────── 4 marks — explain with a diagram ─────────────── */
      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the effect of a cut in the central bank\'s policy rate on the economy.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: AD/AS with AD shifting right from AD to AD1 against an upward-sloping SRAS.\n(1) Transmission: a lower policy rate reduces borrowing costs and the return to saving, so consumption of credit-sensitive goods and business investment rise; it also tends to depreciate the currency, raising net exports, and raises asset prices, creating a wealth effect.\n(1) The new equilibrium shows real output rising from Y to Y1.\n(1) The price level rises from P to P1, with the size of the split depending on how much spare capacity there is — a large output response with spare capacity, mainly inflation near full employment. Credit the point that the effect arrives with a lag of around eighteen months to two years.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the effect of a programme of fiscal consolidation on an economy in recession.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: AD/AS with the initial equilibrium below full employment output, and AD shifting left from AD to AD1.\n(1) Cuts in government spending reduce the G component directly; tax rises reduce disposable income and therefore consumption.\n(1) The new equilibrium shows real output falling further from Y to Y1 and the price level falling from P to P1, widening the negative output gap.\n(1) Explanation: the negative multiplier amplifies the initial cut, since those losing income reduce their own spending; unemployment rises. Credit the point that falling output reduces tax receipts and raises benefit spending, so the deficit may not fall by the amount intended — self-defeating consolidation.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the relationship between the tax rate and tax revenue suggested by the Laffer curve.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: tax rate (%) on the horizontal axis from 0 to 100, total tax revenue on the vertical axis, with an inverted-U curve starting and ending at zero revenue.\n(1) The revenue-maximising rate t* identified at the peak of the curve.\n(1) Explanation of the left-hand section: raising the rate from a low level raises revenue, because the higher rate more than offsets any reduction in the tax base.\n(1) Explanation of the right-hand section: beyond t*, higher rates reduce revenue because they weaken incentives to work, to invest and to locate in the country, and increase avoidance and evasion, so the tax base shrinks by more than the rate rises. Credit the qualification that the position of t* is unknown and differs by tax and by country.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how a successful supply-side policy affects output and the price level.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: AD/AS with a vertical LRAS shifting right from LRAS to LRAS1 (credit SRAS shifting right as well), AD unchanged.\n(1) The policy raises the quantity or quality of factors of production — for example investment in technical education and apprenticeships raises human capital, or infrastructure investment raises the productivity of private capital.\n(1) The new equilibrium shows real output rising from Y to Y1.\n(1) The price level falls from P to P1, so the policy delivers growth and lower inflation at the same time — the feature that distinguishes supply-side from demand-side policy, which faces a trade-off between the two. Credit the qualification that the effect takes years to appear.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the effect of an increase in the rate of VAT on the price level and real output.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: AD/AS with SRAS shifting left from SRAS to SRAS1, and AD shifting left from AD to AD1.\n(1) The SRAS shift: VAT is an indirect tax added to the selling price, so at every level of output firms must charge more to cover the tax — costs of supply rise and SRAS shifts left, raising the price level.\n(1) The AD shift: the higher price level reduces real disposable income, so consumption falls and AD shifts left.\n(1) Net effect: real output falls unambiguously from Y to Y1 because both shifts reduce it, while the effect on the price level is ambiguous — the SRAS shift raises it and the AD shift lowers it, so the outcome depends on which shift is larger. Credit the point that VAT changes have a one-off effect on the price level rather than a permanent effect on the inflation rate.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how government borrowing can crowd out private sector investment.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: a loanable funds market with the real interest rate on the vertical axis and the quantity of loanable funds on the horizontal axis, showing the supply of savings and the demand for funds shifting right from D to D1 as the government borrows more.\n(1) The equilibrium real interest rate rises from r to r1.\n(1) At the higher interest rate, fewer private investment projects have an expected return above the cost of finance, so private investment contracts — shown as a movement along the original private demand curve, or as the reduction in private borrowing at r1.\n(1) Explanation: the net effect on AD is therefore smaller than the initial government injection. Credit resource crowding out as an alternative or additional explanation — at full capacity the public sector bids scarce labour and materials away from private firms — and credit the qualification that crowding out is weak when there is spare capacity and the policy rate is at its lower bound.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the policy trade-off faced by a government that uses demand management to reduce unemployment.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: a short-run Phillips curve, inflation on the vertical axis and unemployment on the horizontal axis, downward sloping, with a vertical long-run Phillips curve at the natural rate (NAIRU) if the long run is addressed.\n(1) Expansionary demand management moves the economy along the SRPC from (U1, P1) to (U2, P2) — unemployment falls and inflation rises.\n(1) Explanation of the mechanism: higher AD tightens the labour market, raising workers\' bargaining power and wage growth, which firms pass on as higher prices.\n(1) The long-run qualification: as inflation expectations adjust, the SRPC shifts out and unemployment returns to the NAIRU at a higher inflation rate, so the trade-off is temporary — reducing unemployment permanently requires supply-side measures that shift the LRPC left. Award the fourth mark for any accurate development of the trade-off, including the reverse case of disinflation raising unemployment.` },

      /* ─────────────── 8 marks — evaluate using the stimulus ─────────────── */
      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which monetary policy is an effective tool for controlling inflation in the circumstances described.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"At its meeting ending on 16 September 2026, the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee voted 6-3 to hold Bank Rate at 3.75%, with three members voting for an increase to 4%. CPI inflation had risen to 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026, driven largely by transport and motor fuels after conflict in the Middle East pushed crude and refined energy prices higher, and the Committee expected it to rise further. The majority judged that domestic activity and tight financial conditions were restraining inflation, while warning that the risk of second-round effects was growing. Real GDP grew 0.4% in Quarter 2 2026 and unemployment was 4.9%."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The case for effectiveness: Bank Rate at 3.75%, well above the 2% target, is restrictive, and the MPC itself judges that tight financial conditions are restraining inflation. Higher rates reduce credit-financed consumption and investment, raise the return to saving, and tend to appreciate the currency, which lowers import prices directly — the fastest of the transmission channels.\n• The expectations channel may be the most important one here. An independent committee with a clear 2% mandate that visibly refuses to accommodate an overshoot keeps long-run inflation expectations anchored, which prevents the second-round effects the Committee warns about. On this view the decision to hold at a restrictive level, and to communicate why, is itself the policy.\n• The case against effectiveness: the identified driver is external energy prices. No level of Bank Rate can reduce the world price of crude oil, so tightening addresses the symptom at the cost of output. Raising rates into a supply shock reduces AD further at a time when real GDP is growing only 0.4% a quarter, deepening the output loss without removing the cause.\n• Lags are a fundamental limitation. Monetary policy affects inflation with a lag of around eighteen months to two years, so a decision taken in September 2026 acts on 2028 inflation. The Committee must act on a forecast, and the 6-3 split is evidence that reasonable people reading the same forecast disagree about the balance of risk.\n• Evaluation: monetary policy is effective against demand-driven inflation and against the persistence of any inflation through the expectations channel, but largely powerless against the initial impulse when that impulse is an external supply shock. A defensible judgement is that holding at a restrictive rate, rather than tightening further, is the right use of a blunt instrument in these circumstances — and that its effectiveness will be judged by what happens to wage growth, not to the oil price.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — uses stimulus evidence, distinguishes the channels monetary policy can and cannot reach, and judges. L1 (1–4) — describes how interest rates affect AD with little application.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which the fiscal rules described constrain government policy usefully.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"Country E operates two fiscal rules announced at a Budget: a 'stability rule' requiring day-to-day spending to be covered by tax revenue, and an 'investment rule' requiring net financial debt to fall as a share of GDP, both assessed against the independent fiscal watchdog's forecast for a target year. From 2026-27 the rules bind on the third year of the forecast rather than the fifth, and the current budget is treated as balanced if it is in surplus or deficit within a margin of 0.5% of GDP. At the last forecast the government met the stability rule with headroom of £9.9 billion, and public sector net debt stood at 93.8% of GDP."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The case for usefulness: rules address the deficit bias of discretionary fiscal policy, in which governments find it easier to spend and cut taxes than to consolidate. Committing publicly to a target assessed by an independent forecaster raises credibility with gilt investors, which lowers the risk premium and therefore the interest cost of the 93.8% of GDP already borrowed.\n• The design is defensible. The stability rule follows the "golden rule" logic of borrowing only to invest, which protects capital spending from being the first casualty of consolidation — historically the easiest line to cut and the most damaging to long-run growth. The investment rule targets net financial debt rather than gross debt, recognising that borrowing which buys an asset is not equivalent to borrowing which funds consumption.\n• Recent changes improve it: binding on the third rather than the fifth year of the forecast reduces the temptation to promise consolidation that never arrives, and the 0.5% of GDP margin acknowledges that forecasts are not precise enough to justify treating a target as a knife edge.\n• The case against: headroom of £9.9 billion against a forecast five years out is within the normal error of any such forecast, so the rule can be met or missed on a revision that has nothing to do with policy. That creates pressure for repeated small tax and spending adjustments timed to the forecast round rather than to the economy — policy driven by an arithmetic constraint rather than by the cyclical position.\n• Rules can be pro-cyclical. If a downturn reduces receipts, the rule requires tightening precisely when demand is weakest, which deepens the recession and may be self-defeating as the tax base shrinks further. Rules also invite gaming: reclassifying current spending as capital, or shifting liabilities off balance sheet.\n• Evaluation: a defensible judgement is that the rules are useful as a commitment device and in protecting investment, but that their usefulness depends on the escape clauses and on how mechanically the headroom is treated. Rules assessed on a five-year forecast with £9.9 billion of headroom provide the appearance of discipline with limited substance; the credibility comes more from the independent forecaster than from the rule itself.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — uses the specific design features and the headroom figure, weighs credibility against pro-cyclicality and judges. L1 (1–4) — describes what fiscal rules are with little application.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which supply-side policies can reduce unemployment.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"In Country F, unemployment is 5.4%. A breakdown estimates that 1.9 percentage points is cyclical, 2.3 percentage points structural and 1.2 percentage points frictional. The government has announced £3.1 billion a year on technical education and apprenticeships, a reform of job-search requirements, a digital vacancy-matching service, and a cut in employers' social contributions for workers under 25. Real GDP is growing at 0.5% a year, capacity utilisation is 76% against a long-run average of 82%, and previous training programmes in Country F took an average of three years to show measurable effects on employment."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Where supply-side policy works: structural unemployment, 2.3 percentage points of the total and the largest single component, is caused by a mismatch between workers\' skills and the skills employers demand. Technical education and apprenticeships address that mismatch directly, raising human capital and the employability of displaced workers. The cut in employers\' social contributions for under-25s lowers the cost of hiring the group with the weakest labour market attachment.\n• Frictional unemployment, 1.2 percentage points, is a matching problem rather than a skills problem, and a digital vacancy-matching service plus reformed job-search requirements attack it directly by reducing search time and improving information. Together, structural and frictional account for 3.5 of the 5.4 percentage points, so the policies target most of the total.\n• Why the effect will be limited here: 1.9 percentage points is cyclical, caused by deficient demand, and no amount of training creates vacancies that do not exist. With real GDP growing 0.5% a year and capacity utilisation at 76% against a long-run average of 82%, the economy plainly has a demand shortfall, and supply-side measures alone cannot fix it.\n• Time lags: previous training programmes took an average of three years to show measurable effects, so none of this reduces unemployment in the current downturn — and unemployed workers left waiting are exposed to hysteresis, which converts cyclical into structural unemployment and makes the problem larger.\n• Distributional and other costs: market-based measures such as tightening job-search conditions may reduce measured unemployment by pushing people into low-paid, insecure or part-time work, raising underemployment rather than genuinely improving outcomes; £3.1 billion a year is a substantial fiscal cost with uncertain returns.\n• Evaluation: a defensible judgement is that supply-side policy is the right and indeed the only instrument for the structural and frictional components, which are the majority here, but that it cannot address the cyclical component and works too slowly to help in the current downturn. The sensible conclusion is that the package should be combined with demand support, not that it is misconceived.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — matches the policies to the components of unemployment using the breakdown, addresses lags and judges. L1 (1–4) — lists supply-side policies with little use of the data.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which quantitative easing has been a successful policy.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"Country G's central bank purchased government bonds worth 34% of GDP between 2009 and 2021, financed by newly created reserves. Over that period the policy rate stayed between 0.1% and 0.75%, inflation remained below the 2% target in nine of twelve years, and real GDP recovered more quickly than in comparable economies that acted later. House prices rose 61% and equity prices 118% over the same period, while median real wages rose 4%. The central bank is now reducing its holdings, with a plan announced in September 2026 to run the stock down to zero by the end of 2034 at an average pace of £46 billion a year."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The case for success: with the policy rate between 0.1% and 0.75%, conventional monetary policy was at its effective lower bound and could not be eased further. Asset purchases worked through the portfolio balance channel — buying gilts raises their price and lowers their yield, pushing investors into corporate bonds and equities and lowering the cost of finance across the economy — and through the signalling channel, reinforcing the message that rates would stay low. The stimulus notes faster recovery than in comparable economies that acted later, which is evidence of effect.\n• Avoiding deflation: inflation below target in nine of twelve years suggests the risk QE was deployed against was real. Given the asymmetry at the lower bound, a policy that prevented a deflationary spiral would be worth substantial costs even if its measured output effect were modest.\n• The case against — distribution. House prices rose 61% and equity prices 118% while median real wages rose 4%. Asset ownership is concentrated among older and wealthier households, so QE raised wealth inequality and worsened housing affordability for those who own nothing. This is a real cost, not merely a presentational one, and it was a foreseeable consequence of a policy that works by raising asset prices.\n• Other costs: the difficulty of unwinding, illustrated by the stimulus itself — reducing a stock worth 34% of GDP is planned to take until the end of 2034 at £46 billion a year, and sales can put upward pressure on yields and generate losses for the central bank, which are borne by the taxpayer. Prolonged low yields also support unproductive "zombie" firms and can encourage excessive risk-taking.\n• The counterfactual problem: the effect of QE cannot be observed directly, because there is no controlled comparison. Estimates of its impact on output and inflation vary widely, which is itself a reason for caution about strong claims in either direction.\n• Evaluation: a defensible judgement is that QE was successful in the narrow terms it was deployed for — easing financial conditions when the policy rate could not fall further and preventing a deflationary outcome — but that it had significant distributional costs and an awkward exit. The reasonable conclusion is that it is an emergency instrument justified at the lower bound rather than a normal tool, which is consistent with the decision to run the stock down.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — uses the asset price and wage data, engages with the counterfactual and judges. L1 (1–4) — describes how QE works with little use of the stimulus.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which the government should reduce its budget deficit.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"Country H has a budget deficit of 4.4% of GDP and public sector net debt of 93.8% of GDP. Debt interest absorbs 3.9% of GDP a year, more than the country spends on defence. The average maturity of the debt is 14 years and it is all denominated in the domestic currency. Unemployment is 5.6%, capacity utilisation is 77% against a long-run average of 82%, real GDP is growing at 0.5% a year and the central bank's policy rate is 3.75% with inflation at 3.1%. Ten-year bond yields are 4.6%."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The case for reducing it: debt interest at 3.9% of GDP is a large and rising claim on revenue that buys no public services, and with bond yields at 4.6% each year of deficit adds to it. Debt at 93.8% of GDP leaves little fiscal space for the next recession or shock, and a deficit of 4.4% with growth of only 0.5% means the debt ratio is rising, since the deficit exceeds nominal growth times the debt stock. Market confidence, once lost, is expensive to regain.\n• Intergenerational and crowding-out arguments: borrowing to fund current spending transfers the tax burden to future taxpayers without leaving them an asset, and higher yields raise the cost of private borrowing.\n• The case against reducing it now: the cyclical position is weak. Unemployment at 5.6%, capacity utilisation at 77% against an average of 82% and growth of 0.5% indicate a negative output gap, so the fiscal multiplier is likely to be relatively large. Consolidation would reduce AD, deepen the downturn and raise unemployment — and by shrinking the tax base and raising benefit spending, might not reduce the deficit much at all, which is the self-defeating consolidation argument.\n• Sustainability is less pressing than the headline suggests. The debt is all in the domestic currency, so there is no external default risk, and an average maturity of 14 years means rising yields feed through to the government\'s interest bill very slowly — only as existing bonds mature and are refinanced.\n• Composition matters more than the total. Reducing a deficit by cutting capital spending lowers future potential output and can raise the debt ratio in the long run by shrinking the denominator; reducing it by closing the structural current deficit, phased in as the recovery proceeds, avoids both problems.\n• Evaluation: a defensible judgement is that the deficit should be reduced but not now and not by cutting investment — a credible medium-term plan to close the structural component as the output gap closes gets the credibility benefit without the immediate demand cost. The data on capacity and unemployment argue strongly against front-loaded consolidation, while the debt interest figure argues against doing nothing.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — distinguishes timing and composition from the question of whether to consolidate at all, uses the specific figures and judges. L1 (1–4) — lists arguments for and against austerity with little application.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which macroeconomic objectives conflict with one another.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"In September 2026, Country J recorded CPI inflation of 3.1% against a 2% target, unemployment of 4.9%, real GDP growth of 0.4% in the latest quarter, a current account deficit of 2.8% of GDP and public sector net debt of 93.8% of GDP. The central bank held its policy rate at 3.75%, with three of nine members voting for an increase. The government is committed to reducing carbon emissions by 68% from 1990 levels by 2030 and to raising business investment, which stands at 9.4% of GDP against a comparator average of 12.8%."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Inflation versus growth and employment: with inflation at 3.1% against target and three members voting to raise the policy rate above 3.75%, tightening would bear down on inflation but reduce AD further when growth is only 0.4% a quarter — the classic short-run Phillips curve trade-off, and the reason for the split vote.\n• Growth versus the current account: faster growth raises import demand, widening a deficit already at 2.8% of GDP, because the marginal propensity to import is positive. Conversely, deflating demand to improve the external position costs output and jobs.\n• Growth versus the fiscal position: with debt at 93.8% of GDP, fiscal stimulus to support growth raises the debt ratio and the interest burden, while consolidation to protect the public finances reduces AD.\n• Growth versus the environment: a 68% emissions reduction target requires carbon pricing and regulation that raise costs for carbon-intensive firms, reducing output and competitiveness in those sectors in the short run.\n• Objectives that are complementary, which is the other half of the answer. Raising business investment from 9.4% towards 12.8% of GDP shifts LRAS right, which raises growth and lowers the price level simultaneously, improves non-price competitiveness and so the current account, and — if directed at low-carbon capital — reduces emissions while raising output. Low, stable inflation is itself a precondition for the investment that delivers growth, so price stability and growth are complements over the long run even where they conflict quarter to quarter.\n• Evaluation: a defensible judgement is that conflicts are characteristic of demand-side policy, where a single instrument is used to move a single aggregate and everything else adjusts, whereas supply-side improvements can advance several objectives at once. The conflicts are also time-dependent — sharp in the short run, much weaker over a decade — so the apparent incompatibility in the September 2026 data is a statement about the current cyclical position rather than a permanent feature.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — identifies specific conflicts using the data, recognises complementarities and judges. L1 (1–4) — lists objectives with generic assertions of conflict.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which government policy should aim to reduce income inequality.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"In Country K the Gini coefficient for original income is 0.51 and for income after taxes and benefits 0.34. The top 10% of households receive 28% of post-tax income and the bottom 10% receive 3%. The government raised the statutory minimum wage to £12.71 an hour in April 2026 and increased the highest marginal rate of income tax to 47%. Employers in hospitality reported reducing hours in response to the wage increase, and the revenue raised by the higher marginal rate came in 18% below forecast, with a rise in the number of high earners declaring non-resident status."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The case for acting: the fall in the Gini coefficient from 0.51 to 0.34 shows that taxes and benefits already do substantial work, and that the pre-tax distribution would otherwise be very unequal. Equity arguments rest on diminishing marginal utility of income — a pound is worth more to the bottom decile on 3% of post-tax income than to the top decile on 28% — and on equality of opportunity, since children in poor households have measurably worse health and education outcomes, which wastes human capital and lowers long-run growth.\n• Efficiency arguments for redistribution: lower-income households have a higher marginal propensity to consume, so transfers raise AD more per pound than tax cuts for high earners; and inequality is associated with lower social mobility and higher social costs.\n• The case against, evidenced in the stimulus: the higher marginal rate of 47% raised 18% less than forecast, with more high earners declaring non-resident status — the Laffer curve mechanism and the mobility of high-income taxpayers in practice. The minimum wage rise to £12.71 an hour led hospitality employers to cut hours, so the intended gain to low-paid workers is partly offset by reduced employment or hours, particularly for the least experienced workers.\n• Incentive effects more generally: high marginal rates on earnings and high withdrawal rates on benefits can create a poverty trap where additional work yields little additional income, discouraging labour supply and skill acquisition.\n• Evaluation: a defensible judgement is that the case for reducing inequality is strong on both equity and long-run efficiency grounds, but that the choice of instrument determines whether the policy works. Raising the top marginal rate has weak revenue yield where the base is mobile; a minimum wage has employment costs concentrated on the least skilled. Instruments with better incentive properties — in-work benefits, childcare provision, education and training, and taxes on immobile bases such as property — are likely to reduce inequality at lower efficiency cost. The evidence here argues about how, not whether.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — uses the Gini and revenue data, weighs equity against incentive effects and judges. L1 (1–4) — asserts that inequality is unfair, or lists policies without evaluation.` },

      /* ─────────────── 12 marks — evaluate, asterisked ─────────────── */
      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether fiscal policy is more effective than monetary policy in stimulating an economy in recession.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"Country L is in recession. Real GDP has fallen for three consecutive quarters, unemployment has risen from 4.4% to 7.6%, capacity utilisation is 71% against a long-run average of 82%, and inflation is 0.3% against a 2% target. The central bank's policy rate has been cut to 0.1% and bank lending to businesses has fallen 6.4% despite a rise in deposits. Public sector net debt is 93.8% of GDP, the average maturity of that debt is 14 years, and the marginal propensity to import is 0.22. Major infrastructure projects take an average of 4.5 years from announcement to first spending."`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The case for fiscal policy in these specific conditions. With the policy rate at 0.1%, conventional monetary policy has effectively run out of room — the liquidity trap. Cutting further is impossible, and the stimulus shows the transmission mechanism broken: lending to businesses fell 6.4% despite rising deposits, so cheap money is not reaching firms. Fiscal policy bypasses the banking system entirely, injecting demand directly through G or through transfers to households.\n• The fiscal multiplier is at its largest in exactly these circumstances: capacity utilisation at 71% means output can respond, so the AS curve is elastic; the central bank will not tighten in response, so there is no monetary offset; and with unemployment at 7.6% many households are credit-constrained and will spend any transfer received. Acting also prevents hysteresis, which would permanently reduce potential output.\n• Fiscal policy can be targeted at the regions, sectors and households most affected, which a single national interest rate cannot.\n• The case for monetary policy. It is far quicker to change — a committee decision, effective immediately, with no legislation — and it can be reversed as quickly if conditions change, whereas spending commitments are politically difficult to withdraw. It is set by an independent body insulated from electoral incentives, and unconventional tools remain available at the lower bound: quantitative easing, forward guidance and funding schemes for lending, which address the very credit-supply failure the stimulus describes.\n• The case against fiscal policy in this instance. Debt at 93.8% of GDP limits fiscal space and may raise yields. The timing problem is severe: infrastructure takes 4.5 years from announcement to first spending, so the injection may arrive after the recovery and prove pro-cyclical. An MPM of 0.22 means over a fifth of the stimulus leaks abroad. And discretionary fiscal changes are subject to political capture — spending chosen for constituency reasons rather than for multiplier value.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that in these particular conditions — the lower bound, impaired credit transmission, a large output gap and inflation far below target — fiscal policy is the more effective instrument, but that this is a statement about the circumstances rather than about the instruments in general. The best answers add that the two are complements rather than substitutes: fiscal expansion is far more effective when the central bank commits to holding rates low, and that the timing objection argues for transfers and current spending that can be disbursed quickly, plus automatic stabilisers, rather than for long-lead infrastructure as counter-cyclical policy.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — sustained analysis using the lower-bound and credit data, engages with timing and debt, supported judgement. L2 (5–8) — both policies explained with some application. L1 (1–4) — describes the two policies. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether a government should prioritise reducing its national debt.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"Public sector net debt in Country M stands at 93.8% of GDP, a level last seen in the early 1960s, and debt interest absorbs 3.9% of GDP a year. The debt is entirely denominated in the domestic currency, its average maturity is 14 years and ten-year bond yields are 4.6%. Nominal GDP is growing at 3.3% a year. Public sector net investment is 2.6% of GDP and is planned to fall to 2.0% by 2030. Business investment is 9.4% of GDP against a comparator average of 12.8%, productivity growth is 0.4% a year, and the share of the population aged 65 and over is projected to rise from 19% to 26% by 2046."`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The case for prioritising debt reduction: interest at 3.9% of GDP is a large recurring claim on revenue that delivers no services and crowds out other spending. At 93.8% of GDP there is little fiscal space for the next recession, pandemic or security shock, and the demographic projection — the over-65 share rising from 19% to 26% by 2046 — means age-related spending will rise sharply on unchanged policy, so acting later requires a larger adjustment. High debt can also raise the risk premium demanded by investors, and yields of 4.6% show borrowing is no longer nearly free.\n• The debt dynamics argument cuts the other way. What matters is the relationship between the effective interest rate on debt and the growth rate of nominal GDP. With nominal growth at 3.3% and yields at 4.6%, r exceeds g, so the ratio rises unless the primary balance is in surplus — which supports the case for action. But an average maturity of 14 years means the 4.6% yield feeds into the average interest cost only slowly, so the pressure is much weaker than the headline implies.\n• The case against prioritising it: the debt is entirely in the domestic currency, so there is no external default risk and no forced adjustment of the kind faced by a country borrowing in foreign currency. Debt-to-GDP is a ratio, and raising the denominator is as effective as lowering the numerator — with productivity growth at 0.4% a year, policy that raises growth would do more for the ratio than consolidation that suppresses it.\n• Composition is the strongest point available. Public sector net investment falling from 2.6% to 2.0% of GDP is precisely the wrong way to consolidate: capital spending is complementary to private investment, which at 9.4% of GDP is already well below the 12.8% comparator. Cutting it lowers future potential output, so the debt ratio may be higher in a decade, not lower — consolidation that shrinks the denominator.\n• Timing and the cycle: front-loaded consolidation in a weak economy has a large multiplier and can be self-defeating, since falling output reduces receipts and raises benefit spending. A credible medium-term plan to close the structural current deficit, phased with the recovery, captures the credibility benefit at lower cost.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that the debt should be stabilised rather than reduced as an overriding priority, and that the means matter more than the target — protecting public investment and raising productivity growth address both the numerator and the denominator, whereas cutting capital spending to hit a ratio target is self-defeating. The demographic projection is the strongest argument for acting on the structural current balance sooner rather than later.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — uses the r-versus-g relationship and the investment composition point, supported judgement. L2 (5–8) — arguments both ways with some application. L1 (1–4) — asserts debt is good or bad. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether supply-side policies are the most effective way of improving an economy's performance.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"Country N has had real GDP growth averaging 1.1% a year for fifteen years. Productivity growth is 0.4% a year against 2.2% before 2008, business investment is 9.4% of GDP against a comparator average of 12.8%, and research and development spending is 1.7% of GDP against an OECD average of 2.7%. Unemployment is 4.9%, of which an estimated 2.3 percentage points is structural. Inflation is 3.1% against a 2% target, the policy rate is 3.75% and public sector net debt is 93.8% of GDP. Previous training programmes took an average of three years to show measurable effects."`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The case for supply-side policy: over fifteen years the growth rate is determined by the growth of productive capacity, and every capacity indicator in the stimulus is weak — productivity growth of 0.4% against a previous 2.2%, investment of 9.4% of GDP against 12.8%, R&D of 1.7% against 2.7%. Demand-side policy cannot raise any of these. On an AD/AS diagram, supply-side improvement shifts LRAS right, raising output and lowering the price level simultaneously, so it is the only policy that improves growth and inflation together.\n• It addresses the largest component of unemployment: 2.3 percentage points of the 4.9% total is structural, caused by skills mismatch, which training, apprenticeships and improved job-matching target directly. It also lowers the NAIRU, shifting the long-run Phillips curve left, so a lower unemployment rate becomes compatible with stable inflation.\n• It is the only option consistent with the constraints. With inflation at 3.1% against target and the policy rate at 3.75%, there is little room for monetary loosening; with debt at 93.8% of GDP, fiscal expansion is constrained. Supply-side measures relieve the inflation constraint rather than adding to it.\n• The case against: the lags are severe and the stimulus quantifies them — training programmes took three years to show measurable effects, and infrastructure and R&D take longer. Nothing here helps the economy for several years, and interventionist measures cost money that a government at 93.8% debt must borrow.\n• Effectiveness is uncertain and contested. Market-based measures — deregulation, tax cuts, labour market flexibility — have distributional costs, may increase insecurity and inequality, and their growth effects are disputed. Interventionist measures depend on government choosing well, and government failure is a real risk when picking sectors or technologies.\n• Supply-side capacity is worth little without demand. If AD is too weak to use existing capacity, adding more does not raise output, and firms facing uncertain demand will not invest however favourable the tax treatment — which is why the categories interact rather than compete. Demand policy also matters for avoiding the deep recessions that destroy capacity through hysteresis.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that supply-side policy is the most effective route to improving performance over the horizon that matters for this economy, because the diagnosis in the data is unambiguously a capacity problem, but that "most effective" does not mean sufficient — it works slowly, requires demand conditions that justify investment, and its success depends heavily on choosing instruments with good evidence behind them.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — uses the capacity data and the structural unemployment breakdown, engages with lags and the interaction with demand, supported judgement. L2 (5–8) — describes supply-side policies with some application. L1 (1–4) — lists policies. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether inflation targeting is the best framework for monetary policy.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"Country P's central bank has targeted CPI inflation of 2% since 1997, with operational independence and a committee that meets eight times a year. Over that period inflation averaged 2.4%, compared with 6.9% in the two decades before. Inflation was below 1% in three years after the 2008 crisis when the policy rate was at 0.1%, and reached 9.1% in 2022 following an energy shock. House prices rose 61% and equity prices 118% between 2009 and 2021. In September 2026 the committee held the rate at 3.75% by six votes to three, with inflation at 3.1% driven mainly by imported energy and transport costs."`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The case for inflation targeting: it solves the time-inconsistency problem by giving an independent body a single, measurable, publicly announced objective. The record in the stimulus supports it — average inflation of 2.4% against 6.9% in the two preceding decades. Anchored expectations mean that a given inflation outcome is achieved at a lower cost in lost output, and a transparent target with published minutes makes the committee accountable and its decisions predictable.\n• Flexibility within the framework: the target is symmetric, and most regimes allow the committee to look through temporary supply shocks and to return inflation to target over an appropriate horizon rather than immediately — which is exactly what holding at 3.75% with inflation at 3.1% from imported energy represents. That flexibility is a feature, not a departure from the framework.\n• The case against — it ignores asset prices. House prices rose 61% and equity prices 118% between 2009 and 2021 while consumer price inflation was below target, so the framework registered no problem while financial imbalances and wealth inequality built up. The 2008 crisis was preceded by exactly this configuration: stable consumer prices alongside an unsustainable credit and asset boom.\n• It handles supply shocks badly. The framework is designed for demand-driven inflation; when inflation comes from imported energy, as in 2022 at 9.1% and again in 2026, raising rates cannot affect the cause and reduces output. The committee is left balancing a mandate it cannot meet against a recession it would cause, which is what the 6-3 split reflects.\n• It is impotent at the lower bound. With the policy rate at 0.1% and inflation below 1% for three years, the framework could not deliver its own target from below, requiring unconventional tools with side effects the framework does not account for.\n• Alternatives and their problems: nominal GDP targeting automatically accommodates supply shocks and stabilises real activity, but is hard to communicate and relies on data revised heavily after the event. A higher target, say 4%, would create more room above the lower bound but sacrifices some of the credibility already built. Price-level targeting requires making up past misses, which is difficult to explain. Dual mandates including employment risk diluting accountability. Adding financial stability is better handled by macroprudential tools — capital requirements, loan-to-value limits, stress tests — which target the problem directly rather than using a single blunt interest rate for two objectives.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that inflation targeting is the best framework available rather than an ideal one — its weaknesses are real and well documented, but every alternative trades one of them for another, and the credibility built over nearly three decades is itself an asset that a change of regime would spend. The sensible reform is to supplement it with macroprudential policy rather than to replace it.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — uses the record and the asset-price evidence, engages seriously with at least one alternative, supported judgement. L2 (5–8) — advantages and disadvantages with some application. L1 (1–4) — describes inflation targeting. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether the government should intervene to correct the market failures described rather than relying on markets.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"In Country Q, carbon emissions are 32% below their 1990 level against a legally binding target of 68% by 2030. A carbon price of £42 per tonne applies to electricity generation and heavy industry but not to agriculture, aviation or domestic heating. Research and development spending is 1.7% of GDP against an OECD average of 2.7%, and firms report capturing only around half the benefit of their own research. Of the 5.4% unemployment rate, 2.3 percentage points is structural, and firms report that they under-invest in transferable training because trained workers are recruited by competitors."`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The theoretical case for intervention is market failure, and each example in the stimulus is a recognised type. Carbon emissions are a negative production externality: the private cost of emitting is below the social cost, so the free-market quantity exceeds the socially optimal one. R&D and transferable training generate positive externalities — firms report capturing only half the benefit of their research, and trained workers are poached — so the private return falls short of the social return and provision is below the optimum. Both are cases where markets fail systematically, not occasionally.\n• The appropriate instruments follow from the diagnosis. A carbon price internalises the externality and lets firms choose the cheapest abatement, which is more efficient than command-and-control regulation. R&D tax credits and public research funding raise the private return towards the social return. Apprenticeship levies and publicly funded technical education address the training free-rider problem by removing the incentive to poach rather than train.\n• Evidence that the existing intervention is insufficient rather than misconceived: emissions are 32% below 1990 against a 68% target, and the carbon price of £42 a tonne covers only electricity generation and heavy industry, exempting agriculture, aviation and domestic heating. Partial coverage distorts relative prices between covered and uncovered sectors and can shift activity rather than reduce emissions — an argument for broader intervention, not less.\n• The case against intervention — government failure. Setting the carbon price requires valuing the external cost, which is contested and uncertain; set too high it destroys output unnecessarily, too low it achieves nothing. Governments choosing which research or which sectors to support are poorly placed to pick winners, and subsidies attract lobbying and regulatory capture. Interventions also have unintended consequences: a carbon price applying to some countries but not others can cause carbon leakage as production relocates, reducing domestic output without reducing global emissions.\n• Administrative and opportunity costs: monitoring, enforcement and compliance absorb real resources, and every pound spent on subsidy is a pound not spent elsewhere or an extra pound borrowed.\n• Where markets do better: price signals and competition drive the innovation that has made renewable generation cheap, and market-based instruments such as carbon pricing and tradable permits harness that mechanism rather than overriding it.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that intervention is justified where a specific, identifiable market failure exists and the chosen instrument works with prices rather than against them — which is true of all three failures described. The real question is not intervention versus markets but which instrument, at what level and with what coverage; the partial coverage of the carbon price is the clearest evidence in the stimulus that the design, not the principle, is what needs attention.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — identifies the specific failures, matches instruments to them, engages with government failure and judges. L2 (5–8) — describes market failure and intervention with some application. L1 (1–4) — asserts that government should or should not intervene. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      /* ─────────────── 15 marks — evaluate with diagram and extract ─────────────── */
      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s) and the information in Extract 1, the extent to which raising the policy rate is the right response to the inflation described.

Extract 1 — written for this practice question
"CPI inflation in Country R rose to 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026 against a 2% target, up from 2.9% in July, with the increase driven largely by transport and motor fuels after conflict abroad raised crude and refined energy prices. The central bank held its policy rate at 3.75% by six votes to three, the dissenters favouring 4%. The majority judged that domestic activity and tight financial conditions were restraining inflation but warned that the risk of second-round effects was growing. Real GDP grew 0.4% in the latest quarter, unemployment was 4.9%, annual growth in average weekly earnings was 4.3%, and five-to-ten year inflation expectations had risen only from 2.9% to 3.1%."`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: AD/AS showing SRAS shifting left (the energy shock) raising P and lowering Y, with a tighter policy shifting AD left — demonstrating that the policy response lowers the price level at the cost of further output loss. Credit a Phillips curve diagram showing the SRPC shifting out as expectations rise and the policy choice of a point along it.\n• The case for raising the rate: inflation at 3.1% is more than a percentage point above target and rising, earnings growth of 4.3% exceeds it, and unemployment at 4.9% is close to most estimates of the natural rate, so the labour market has little slack. Acting early is cheaper than acting late, because once expectations de-anchor the sacrifice ratio rises — this is the case the three dissenters are making.\n• Credibility matters more than the immediate effect. A central bank that tolerates a sustained overshoot invites wage bargainers to assume the target is soft, which is precisely how a one-off shock becomes persistent inflation.\n• The case against: the driver is external energy prices. Monetary policy cannot influence the world oil price, so tightening treats a symptom, and with GDP growing only 0.4% a quarter it deepens the output loss. Energy effects are also self-reversing in the 12-month rate — if prices merely stabilise at their new level, the contribution drops out a year later without any policy action.\n• Policy is already restrictive and works with a lag. At 3.75% the rate is well above target, the majority judges financial conditions are restraining inflation, and a change now affects inflation in 2028. Tightening into a supply shock risks arriving after the shock has passed, which is the classic source of policy-induced instability.\n• The decisive evidence is the expectations data. Five-to-ten year expectations rose only from 2.9% to 3.1%, which means households and firms still expect the overshoot to be temporary. Anchored long-run expectations are the condition under which "looking through" a supply shock is safe, and they are the strongest argument for the majority\'s position.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that holding at a restrictive level is the better response while long-run expectations remain anchored, with an explicit commitment to act if they move or if wage growth accelerates further. The 6-3 split reflects a genuine disagreement about the balance of risks rather than an error by either side, and the asymmetry matters: the cost of tightening unnecessarily is lost output that recovers, while the cost of de-anchoring expectations is a recession to restore them.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — correct diagram used in the analysis, exploits the expectations data, engages with lags and the split vote, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, both sides explained, limited evaluation. L1 (1–5) — describes how interest rates affect inflation; diagram missing or incorrect. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s) and the information in Extract 1, the extent to which fiscal consolidation will reduce the budget deficit.

Extract 1 — written for this practice question
"Country S has a budget deficit of 4.4% of GDP and public sector net debt of 93.8% of GDP. The government has announced spending cuts and tax rises worth 2% of GDP over two years. Unemployment is 5.6%, capacity utilisation is 77% against a long-run average of 82%, real GDP is growing 0.5% a year and the policy rate is 3.75% with inflation at 3.1%. The independent fiscal watchdog estimates the fiscal multiplier in current conditions at between 0.8 and 1.4. Around 40% of the announced consolidation falls on public sector net investment."`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: AD/AS with AD shifting left from AD to AD1, showing real output falling and a negative output gap opening or widening against a vertical LRAS. The strongest answers add a second, smaller leftward shift of LRAS to represent the 40% of consolidation falling on public investment, which reduces future productive capacity.\n• The direct arithmetic: cuts and tax rises worth 2% of GDP reduce the deficit mechanically by 2% of GDP if nothing else changes, which would take the deficit from 4.4% to 2.4% of GDP.\n• Why the outturn will be worse than the arithmetic. With a multiplier of 0.8 to 1.4, a 2% of GDP withdrawal reduces output by 1.6% to 2.8%. Lower output reduces income tax, National Insurance, VAT and corporation tax receipts and raises benefit spending, so the automatic stabilisers partially reverse the consolidation. At the upper end of the multiplier range the offset is large enough that the deficit falls by well under half the headline amount — self-defeating consolidation.\n• The cyclical conditions make the multiplier large. Capacity utilisation at 77% against an average of 82% and unemployment at 5.6% mean a negative output gap, so the AS curve is elastic and demand withdrawal reduces real output rather than prices. Monetary offset is limited too: with inflation at 3.1% above target, the central bank is unlikely to cut the policy rate from 3.75% to cushion the fiscal tightening.\n• The denominator effect: the deficit and debt are measured as shares of GDP, so consolidation that reduces GDP raises the ratios for any given cash amount. This is why debt ratios sometimes rise during consolidation programmes.\n• Composition is the most serious problem. With 40% of the consolidation falling on public sector net investment, the package cuts the spending with the highest multiplier in the short run and the largest supply-side return in the long run. Public capital is complementary to private capital, so cutting it lowers future potential output — shrinking the denominator permanently and making the debt ratio harder to reduce over a decade.\n• Counter-arguments: credibility effects may lower gilt yields and therefore debt interest, and in extreme cases confidence effects can partly offset the demand contraction — though the evidence for expansionary fiscal contraction is weak outside small open economies with large depreciations. A phased plan tied to the recovery, concentrated on the structural current balance rather than on investment, would achieve more deficit reduction per unit of output lost.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that the consolidation will reduce the deficit, but by materially less than 2% of GDP, and that the composition makes the long-run debt position worse rather than better. The timing and the choice of what to cut matter more than the headline size.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — diagram used including the LRAS effect, applies the multiplier range quantitatively, addresses composition and the denominator, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, demand effect explained, limited evaluation. L1 (1–5) — asserts that cuts reduce the deficit. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s) and the information in Extract 1, the extent to which supply-side policies can reduce the natural rate of unemployment.

Extract 1 — written for this practice question
"Unemployment in Country T is 5.4%: an estimated 1.9 percentage points cyclical, 2.3 percentage points structural and 1.2 percentage points frictional. The government has announced £3.1 billion a year for technical education and apprenticeships, a digital vacancy-matching service, reformed job-search requirements, and a cut in employers' social contributions for workers under 25. Previous training programmes took an average of three years to show measurable effects, and 38% of the long-term unemployed have been out of work for more than two years. Real GDP is growing 0.5% a year and capacity utilisation is 76% against a long-run average of 82%."`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: a vertical long-run Phillips curve at the NAIRU shifting left, with the short-run Phillips curve shifting in as expectations adjust to the lower sustainable unemployment rate; or an AD/AS diagram with LRAS shifting right. Either shows the key claim — that supply-side policy lowers the unemployment rate compatible with stable inflation, which demand policy cannot.\n• Structural unemployment, 2.3 percentage points and the largest component, is a skills-mismatch problem. Technical education and apprenticeships raise human capital and retrain workers displaced from declining sectors, so vacancies and workers can be matched at a lower unemployment rate. The cut in employers\' social contributions for under-25s reduces the cost of hiring the group with the weakest attachment, addressing the way employers screen on experience.\n• Frictional unemployment, 1.2 percentage points, is an information and search problem. A digital vacancy-matching service reduces search time directly, and reformed job-search requirements raise search intensity. These are the fastest-acting measures in the package.\n• Together, structural and frictional are 3.5 of the 5.4 percentage points, and both are components of the natural rate — so the package is correctly targeted at the right 65% of the problem.\n• Limits on effectiveness. The lags are long: training took three years to show measurable effects, and a trained cohort takes longer still to reach full productivity. With 38% of the long-term unemployed out of work for over two years, hysteresis has already set in — skills have decayed, employers screen them out, and some have effectively left the effective labour supply, so retraining this group has a much lower success rate than retraining recent leavers.\n• The demand constraint: 1.9 percentage points is cyclical, and with growth at 0.5% and capacity utilisation at 76% against an average of 82%, there are simply fewer vacancies than there would be at full capacity. Training people for jobs that do not currently exist produces qualified unemployed workers, and prolonged cyclical unemployment converts into structural unemployment, raising the NAIRU while the policy is trying to lower it.\n• Costs and side effects: £3.1 billion a year is a substantial fiscal commitment with uncertain returns and a long payback; tightening job-search conditions can reduce measured unemployment by pushing people into underemployment or inactivity rather than into good matches, which flatters the statistics without improving welfare.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that supply-side policy is the only instrument capable of reducing the natural rate and that this package targets the right components, but that its effect will be gradual, partial and largest for the recently unemployed. The strongest answers note the interaction: supply-side measures work far better alongside demand conditions strong enough to generate vacancies, so the two are complements rather than alternatives.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — correct LRPC or LRAS diagram used, matches instruments to the components of unemployment, engages with hysteresis and the demand constraint, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, policies described and applied, limited evaluation. L1 (1–5) — lists supply-side policies; diagram missing or incorrect. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s) and the information in Extract 1, the extent to which a carbon tax is an effective way of reducing emissions.

Extract 1 — written for this practice question
"Country U applies a carbon price of £42 per tonne to electricity generation and heavy industry, but not to agriculture, aviation or domestic heating. Emissions are 32% below their 1990 level against a legally binding target of 68% by 2030. Estimates put the short-run price elasticity of demand for domestic energy at -0.2 and the long-run elasticity at -0.7. The lowest-income tenth of households spend 11% of their income on energy, against 3% for the highest tenth. Two energy-intensive manufacturers have relocated production to countries with no carbon price, and revenue from the carbon price is £6.4 billion a year."`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: a negative production externality diagram with MPC and MSC, showing the free-market output Q1 where MPC = MPB exceeding the social optimum Q*, the welfare loss triangle, and a tax equal to the marginal external cost shifting supply from MPC to MSC so that output falls to Q*. Label the tax revenue as the rectangle.\n• The case for a carbon tax: it internalises the externality so that the private cost of emitting reflects the social cost, which is the textbook solution to a negative externality. Unlike regulation it leaves firms free to choose how to abate, so emissions are cut wherever it is cheapest to cut them — the least-cost property that makes price instruments more efficient than command-and-control. It also generates a continuous incentive to innovate, since every further tonne avoided saves money, and it raises £6.4 billion a year in revenue that can fund green investment or compensate losers (the double dividend).\n• Elasticity determines effectiveness and the data are decisive. Short-run PED for domestic energy of −0.2 means demand is highly inelastic, so a tax raises prices and revenue but cuts consumption little — the tax falls on households as a transfer rather than changing behaviour. Long-run elasticity of −0.7 is much higher, because over time households insulate, replace boilers and buy more efficient appliances, so the tax works far better over a decade than over a year. The implication is that a carbon tax needs to be credible and pre-announced to change investment decisions.\n• Coverage is the clearest failure in the stimulus. The price applies to electricity generation and heavy industry but not to agriculture, aviation or domestic heating, so it distorts relative prices between covered and uncovered sectors and leaves much of the emissions base untouched. Emissions 32% below 1990 against a 68% target for 2030 suggest the current design is nowhere near sufficient.\n• Carbon leakage: two energy-intensive manufacturers have relocated to countries with no carbon price. This reduces domestic output and employment without reducing global emissions, and may increase them if production moves to a less efficient location. It is the strongest practical objection, and the standard remedies — free allocation of allowances, or a carbon border adjustment mechanism — are administratively complex and can provoke trade disputes.\n• Equity: the lowest-income tenth spend 11% of income on energy against 3% for the highest, so a carbon tax is sharply regressive in its direct incidence. This is politically corrosive and can undermine the durability of the policy, though the £6.4 billion of revenue could fund targeted compensation that preserves the marginal price signal.\n• Setting the rate requires valuing the external cost, which is contested and uncertain. £42 a tonne is below most central estimates of the social cost of carbon, which is consistent with the emissions shortfall.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that a carbon tax is the right instrument in principle — it is efficient, revenue-raising and innovation-promoting — but that this implementation is too narrow, too low and unaccompanied by the border adjustment and compensation needed to address leakage and regressivity. The failure in the data is one of design rather than of the instrument, and the low short-run elasticity means it must be judged over a decade, not a year.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — correct externality diagram used with the tax shown, exploits the elasticity, leakage and distribution data, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, mechanism explained, limited evaluation. L1 (1–5) — describes a carbon tax; diagram missing or incorrect. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      /* ─────────────── 25 marks — Section B (diagram) and Section C ─────────────── */
      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s), whether monetary policy is the most effective means of controlling inflation.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagrams: AD/AS showing tighter policy shifting AD left to reduce the price level; a second AD/AS showing a cost-push SRAS shift, to demonstrate that a leftward AD shift addresses the price level only by sacrificing more output; and, for the long run, a Phillips curve with the SRPC shifting out as expectations adjust.\n• The case for monetary policy: it is the designated instrument in almost every advanced economy, with the Bank of England given operational independence in 1997 and a 2% CPI target. Independence solves the time-inconsistency problem that gives governments an inflationary bias, and the framework has coincided with far lower average inflation than the preceding decades. The instrument is flexible — the MPC decides eight times a year and can move at any meeting, as it did in holding Bank Rate at 3.75% on 16 September 2026 — and reversible, which fiscal changes are not.\n• The transmission mechanism gives it several channels: borrowing and saving costs, asset prices and the wealth effect, the exchange rate (the fastest channel, working directly on import prices) and, most importantly, expectations. Where inflation is demand-driven, raising the rate reduces credit-financed consumption and investment, moving the economy back along AD.\n• The expectations channel may be its greatest strength. A credible independent committee keeps long-run expectations anchored, which prevents temporary shocks becoming persistent inflation and reduces the output cost of any given disinflation — the sacrifice ratio.\n• Limitations — supply shocks. When inflation originates abroad, as with the rise in UK CPI inflation to 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026 driven largely by transport and motor fuels after conflict raised energy prices, no domestic interest rate can affect the cause. Tightening then reduces output without removing the source, which is why the MPC split six to three.\n• Limitations — lags and bluntness. Policy affects inflation after around eighteen months to two years, so decisions rest on forecasts; and a single national rate cannot distinguish between overheating and depressed regions or sectors.\n• Limitations — the lower bound. Against below-target inflation the instrument runs out: at 0.1% the rate cannot fall further, requiring quantitative easing and forward guidance, which have significant distributional side effects through asset prices.\n• Alternatives: fiscal policy can reduce demand directly through tax rises or spending cuts, and can be targeted, but is slow, politically constrained and rarely used counter-cyclically against inflation. Supply-side policy raises capacity, shifting LRAS right, which lowers the price level and raises output at once — the only approach without a trade-off, but effective only over years. Direct wage and price controls suppress the symptom, create shortages and misallocation, and have a poor historical record. Exchange rate targeting imports another country\'s monetary policy and sacrifices domestic control.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that monetary policy is the most effective instrument available for controlling demand-driven inflation and for anchoring expectations, which is most of the task, but that it is close to powerless against the initial impulse of a supply shock and constrained at the lower bound. The best answers argue that the instruments are complements — that inflation control depends on a credible monetary framework supported by a disciplined fiscal stance and supply-side reform to raise capacity — rather than choosing one outright.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires correctly used diagrams, serious engagement with at least one alternative instrument, integrated evaluation and a supported conclusion. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s), whether a government should use expansionary fiscal policy to reduce unemployment.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagrams: AD/AS with AD shifting right, drawn at two different starting points — on the elastic section of a Keynesian AS, where output rises substantially and prices barely move, and near the vertical section, where prices rise and output does not. Credit a Phillips curve showing the movement along the SRPC and the subsequent outward shift as expectations adjust.\n• The case for: cyclical unemployment is caused by deficient demand, and fiscal policy addresses it directly. Government spending is a component of AD, so the injection raises output and employment, amplified by the multiplier. With spare capacity the multiplier can exceed 1, so the expansion is partly self-financing through higher tax receipts and lower benefit spending. Acting quickly also prevents hysteresis — the conversion of cyclical into structural unemployment as skills decay — which protects potential output.\n• Fiscal policy can be targeted at particular regions, sectors and income groups in a way a single national interest rate cannot, and it works even when monetary policy is at its lower bound and the credit channel is impaired.\n• The case against — it cannot address the type of unemployment that dominates. Structural unemployment arises from skills mismatch and frictional unemployment from search, and neither responds to higher AD; only supply-side measures lower the natural rate. If unemployment is already close to the NAIRU, expansion produces inflation, not jobs, which is the long-run Phillips curve result.\n• Crowding out and the fiscal position: with UK public sector net debt at 93.8% of GDP at the end of August 2026, additional borrowing raises yields and the interest burden and may displace private investment. Where the economy is at capacity, resource crowding out is real as well as financial.\n• Monetary offset: with CPI inflation at 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026 against a 2% target and Bank Rate at 3.75%, a fiscal loosening makes tightening more likely, which reverses part of the stimulus.\n• Timing and leakage: recognition, decision and implementation lags mean the injection often arrives after the downturn, making it pro-cyclical; major infrastructure can take years from announcement to first spending. In an open economy a high marginal propensity to import diverts part of the stimulus to trading partners.\n• Composition is decisive. Capital spending on infrastructure, skills and research shifts LRAS right as well as AD, so it raises employment without the inflationary consequence and improves the long-run debt ratio by raising the denominator. Current spending raises demand only.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that expansionary fiscal policy is the right instrument for cyclical unemployment in a recession with a large output gap and constrained monetary policy, and the wrong instrument for structural or frictional unemployment or for an economy near capacity. The strongest answers argue that the decision turns on three diagnostic questions — what type of unemployment, how large the output gap and how much fiscal space — and that the composition of the spending matters as much as its size.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires diagrams used to show the state-dependence of the outcome, attention to the type of unemployment, and a conclusion that follows from the argument. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s), whether supply-side policies are more effective than demand-side policies in achieving the government\'s macroeconomic objectives.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagrams: AD/AS with LRAS shifting right, showing output rising and the price level falling simultaneously — the feature that distinguishes supply-side policy; alongside AD shifting right, showing the trade-off between output and prices that demand-side policy cannot escape. Credit an LRPC shifting left.\n• The objectives to test against: sustainable growth, low and stable inflation, low unemployment, a sustainable balance of payments position, and in most modern framings a sustainable fiscal position and environmental targets.\n• The supply-side case: a rightward LRAS shift improves growth, inflation and employment at once, because higher capacity means demand can be met without bidding up prices. It lowers the natural rate of unemployment by tackling the structural and frictional components that demand policy cannot reach. It improves the current account by raising non-price competitiveness — productivity, quality and design — which is the durable route to a better external position, unlike depreciation. And it improves the fiscal position by raising the growth rate and therefore the tax base.\n• Evidence and instruments: interventionist measures such as investment in technical education and apprenticeships, infrastructure and R&D support; market-based measures such as corporation tax reform, deregulation, competition policy and labour market flexibility. Where an economy shows productivity growth well below its historic rate and business investment well below comparable economies, the diagnosis is unambiguously a capacity problem.\n• The supply-side limits: very long lags — training programmes typically take years to show measurable effects and infrastructure longer; high fiscal cost with uncertain returns, which is constrained when public sector net debt is 93.8% of GDP, as in the UK at the end of August 2026; government failure in picking sectors or technologies; and significant distributional costs from market-based measures, which can raise inequality and job insecurity.\n• The demand-side case: it is the only instrument that works quickly. Monetary policy can be changed at any meeting — the MPC held Bank Rate at 3.75% on 16 September 2026 — and it is the right response to a negative output gap, where stimulus converts idle resources into output at little inflationary cost. It also prevents the deep recessions that destroy capacity through hysteresis, so demand stabilisation protects the supply side.\n• The demand-side limits: it cannot raise output beyond capacity, so once the output gap is closed it delivers only inflation; it faces trade-offs between objectives; it is subject to lags and to unreliable output gap estimates; and fiscal expansion is constrained by debt while monetary policy is constrained at the lower bound.\n• The interaction is the sophisticated point. Firms invest when they expect demand, so persistently weak demand suppresses the investment that would shift LRAS; equally, supply-side spending on infrastructure is itself an injection into AD. The two are not independent instruments acting on separate variables.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that supply-side policy is more effective for the long-run objectives — the growth rate, the natural rate of unemployment, competitiveness and the fiscal trajectory — while demand-side policy is more effective for the short-run objective of stabilising output around capacity, and that the right answer is therefore state-dependent. A government facing a large negative output gap should use demand policy; one facing a capacity constraint with inflation above target, as in the September 2026 UK data, has only supply-side options that do not worsen the inflation problem.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires diagrams contrasting the two approaches, explicit testing against more than one objective, and a conclusion that follows from the argument. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate whether governments should set binding fiscal rules.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The problem rules are meant to solve: discretionary fiscal policy has a deficit bias, because spending and tax cuts are politically rewarding and consolidation is not, and because the costs of borrowing fall largely on future taxpayers who cannot vote on it. The political business cycle gives governments an incentive to loosen before elections regardless of the cyclical position.\n• The case for rules: a publicly announced, measurable commitment assessed by an independent forecaster makes fiscal discipline credible, which lowers the risk premium on government debt and therefore the interest cost — significant where debt is 93.8% of GDP, as UK public sector net debt was at the end of August 2026. Rules constrain successors as well as the current government, and they force the long-run consequences of spending decisions into the debate.\n• Good design mitigates the objections. The UK framework announced at the October 2024 Budget pairs a "stability rule" requiring the current budget to be in balance or surplus with an "investment rule" requiring net financial debt to fall as a share of GDP. Separating current from capital spending follows the golden-rule logic and protects investment from being the first line cut. From 2026-27 the rules bind on the third rather than the fifth year of the forecast, reducing the temptation to promise consolidation that never arrives, and the current budget counts as balanced within a margin of 0.5% of GDP, acknowledging forecast imprecision. The Office for Budget Responsibility, which produces the forecasts the rules are assessed against, publishes twice a year — most recently in March 2026, with the next alongside the Budget on 28 October 2026.\n• The case against — pro-cyclicality. A rule expressed in terms of the deficit forces tightening when receipts fall in a downturn, deepening the recession and shrinking the tax base, which can be self-defeating. Cyclically adjusted rules address this in principle but require an estimate of potential output that is unobservable and revised heavily after the event.\n• Rules distort behaviour rather than constraining it. Headroom against a forecast several years out is within the normal forecast error, so governments make small tax and spending adjustments timed to the forecast round rather than to the economy, and are tempted into accounting manoeuvres — reclassifying current as capital spending, moving liabilities off balance sheet, or scheduling implausible consolidation beyond the forecast horizon.\n• Rules are not genuinely binding. Fiscal rules have repeatedly been abandoned, suspended or rewritten by the governments that set them, most obviously during the 2008 crisis and the pandemic. If a rule is always suspended when it binds hardest, its value is presentational; but if it is never suspended, it produces exactly the pro-cyclicality that makes suspension necessary.\n• Alternatives: an independent fiscal institution producing forecasts and assessing sustainability without a numerical rule; escape clauses with defined triggers and a required return path; or debt-anchor frameworks with wide bands that constrain the trajectory without dictating annual adjustments.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that rules are worth having but should be judged on design rather than in principle — they should target the structural balance rather than the headline deficit, exempt productive investment, include pre-specified escape clauses, and be assessed by an independent institution whose credibility does more work than the rule itself. The strongest answers note the paradox that a rule strict enough to bind is a rule that will be broken, so the realistic aim is to raise the political cost of loosening rather than to make it impossible.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires engagement with rule design rather than a simple for-and-against, and a conclusion that follows from the argument. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate whether there are inevitable conflicts between a government\'s macroeconomic objectives.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The objectives: sustainable economic growth, price stability, low unemployment, a sustainable balance of payments position, and increasingly a sustainable fiscal position, reduced inequality and environmental targets.\n• Inflation versus unemployment: the short-run Phillips curve implies a trade-off, since expansionary policy that reduces unemployment tightens the labour market and raises wage and price inflation. The UK position in September 2026 illustrates the tension — CPI inflation of 3.1% in the 12 months to August against a 2% target with unemployment at 4.9%, and an MPC that held Bank Rate at 3.75% by six votes to three rather than easing to support activity.\n• Growth versus the balance of payments: faster growth raises import demand because the marginal propensity to import is positive, so it widens a current account deficit — the UK deficit was 2.8% of GDP in Quarter 1 2026. Deflating demand to improve the external position costs output and jobs.\n• Growth versus the fiscal position: fiscal stimulus to support growth raises the debt ratio, while consolidation to protect the public finances reduces AD. With public sector net debt at 93.8% of GDP at the end of August 2026, the constraint is binding.\n• Growth versus the environment: reaching emissions targets requires carbon pricing and regulation that raise costs for carbon-intensive producers, reducing output and competitiveness in those sectors in the short run.\n• Growth versus equity: measures to redistribute — higher marginal tax rates, higher minimum wages — can weaken incentives to work and invest and raise employment costs, while growth that is unevenly distributed widens inequality even as the average rises.\n• The case that the conflicts are NOT inevitable. First, they are characteristic of demand-side policy, where one instrument is moved and every other variable adjusts. Supply-side improvement shifts LRAS right, which raises output and lowers the price level simultaneously, lowers the natural rate of unemployment, improves non-price competitiveness and hence the current account, and raises the tax base and so the fiscal position. Where the cause of poor performance is a capacity constraint, several objectives improve together.\n• Second, Tinbergen\'s rule: conflicts arise when there are fewer instruments than targets. Assigning monetary policy to inflation, macroprudential tools to financial stability, carbon pricing to emissions and supply-side policy to capacity reduces the apparent conflict, because each objective has its own instrument rather than competing for one.\n• Third, many conflicts are short-run only. Price stability is a precondition for the investment that drives long-run growth, so the two are complements over a decade even where they clash over a quarter. Green investment raises LRAS as well as cutting emissions.\n• The counter-argument: some conflicts are genuinely structural. A finite planet places a real constraint on some forms of growth; equity and efficiency involve real trade-offs at the margin; and an economy at full capacity cannot raise output whatever the instrument.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that the conflicts are real but conditional rather than inevitable — they depend on the state of the economy, the time horizon and the number of instruments available. The strongest answers argue that conflict is the signature of demand management operating at capacity, and that expanding capacity is the route by which several objectives can be advanced at once, while conceding that the environmental and distributional trade-offs are the most durable.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires more than a list of conflicts — it needs the argument about instruments, time horizons and the supply side, and a conclusion that follows. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate whether an independent central bank is desirable.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• What independence means: operational independence, in which an elected government sets the objective — in the UK, a 2% CPI inflation target, with support for growth and employment secondary — and an appointed committee chooses the instrument setting. The Bank of England has had this since 1997; its Monetary Policy Committee decides eight times a year and held Bank Rate at 3.75% on 16 September 2026 by six votes to three.\n• The case for — time inconsistency. A government that controls interest rates has an incentive to promise low inflation and then cut rates before an election, because the output gain arrives quickly and the inflation arrives with a lag. Private agents anticipate this and build higher inflation into wage bargains, so the economy ends with higher inflation and no lasting output gain — the inflationary bias of discretionary policy. Delegation removes the incentive.\n• Credibility lowers the cost of disinflation. If expectations are anchored, a given inflation outcome can be achieved with a smaller rise in unemployment — a lower sacrifice ratio. This is the practical payoff, and the reason central banks invest so heavily in communication, published minutes and forecasts.\n• The evidence: inflation in the UK and comparable economies has averaged far closer to target since independence than in the 1970s and 1980s. The willingness to hold Bank Rate at a restrictive 3.75% with inflation above target at 3.1% in September 2026, resisting pressure to support a weak economy, is the kind of decision independence exists to protect.\n• The case against — democratic accountability. Monetary policy has large distributional consequences: it affects mortgage holders and savers differently, and asset purchases between 2009 and 2021 raised house and equity prices far faster than median real wages, benefiting asset owners. Decisions with those effects are made by appointed officials who cannot be voted out, which is a genuine legitimacy problem rather than a technicality.\n• Coordination failure: separating monetary from fiscal policy can produce an inconsistent overall stance, with one loosening while the other tightens. At the effective lower bound in particular, fiscal and monetary policy need to act together, and institutional separation can impede that.\n• Narrow mandate, wide effects: an inflation target takes no account of asset prices, financial stability, inequality or the climate transition, so a central bank can hit its target while imbalances build — the configuration before 2008. Central banks have accumulated financial stability and regulatory powers anyway, extending unelected authority further.\n• Independence is conditional in practice. Governments set the target, appoint the members and can change the remit or legislate, so independence is delegated rather than absolute — a point that answers both the accountability objection and the claim that independence is absolute protection.\n• Alternatives and refinements: dual mandates including employment, as in the United States, give democratic weight to a second objective at some cost to clarity; independence with stronger parliamentary scrutiny and diversity of appointment addresses accountability without returning the instrument to ministers; macroprudential tools under separate governance address the narrow-mandate problem.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that independence is desirable because the time-inconsistency problem is real and the credibility gains are well evidenced, but that the accountability objection is serious and is best answered by strengthening scrutiny and broadening the toolkit rather than by returning rate-setting to ministers. The strongest answers note that independence works only while the delegated objective remains politically acceptable — a central bank that is seen to protect asset owners at the expense of everyone else will not retain the political consent on which its independence rests.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires the time-inconsistency argument developed properly, serious engagement with the accountability objection, and a conclusion that follows from the argument. Indicative content is not a checklist.` }
    ]
  },
  '2.4': {
    name: 'International Economics & Development',
    questions: [
      /* ─────────────── 2 marks — points-marked ─────────────── */
      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'comparative advantage\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) A country has a comparative advantage in a good when it can produce that good at a lower opportunity cost than another country.\n(1) Opportunity cost here means the quantity of the alternative good that must be given up to produce one more unit — so a country can hold a comparative advantage even if it is absolutely less efficient at producing everything.\n\nDo not credit a definition based on producing more output or using fewer resources — that is absolute advantage.` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'the terms of trade\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) The terms of trade measure the rate at which a country\'s exports exchange for its imports — the ratio of export prices to import prices.\n(1) It is calculated as (index of export prices / index of import prices) × 100. A rise is an improvement, because a given volume of exports buys a greater volume of imports; a fall is a deterioration.` },

      { q: 'Explain what is meant by the term \'a customs union\'.', marks: 2, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) A customs union is a form of economic integration in which members abolish tariffs and quotas on trade between themselves.\n(1) Members also adopt a common external tariff on imports from non-members, which means they must negotiate trade agreements with third countries as a single bloc rather than individually.\n\nCredit the contrast with a free trade area, where members remove internal barriers but each sets its own external tariffs.` },

      { q: `Using information from the stimulus material, identify two barriers to economic development in Country A.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"Country A earns 71% of its export revenue from two agricultural commodities, whose world prices fell 28% over three years. Only 34% of the rural population has access to reliable electricity and the main port operates at 140% of design capacity. External public debt service absorbs 22% of government revenue, up from 8% a decade ago. The country ranks 142nd of 180 on a widely used index of perceived corruption, and gross domestic saving is 6% of GDP against an average of 22% for middle-income countries."`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify',
        markScheme: `(1) Primary product dependency — 71% of export revenue comes from two agricultural commodities whose prices fell 28%, exposing the country to volatile and declining terms of trade.\n(1) Inadequate infrastructure — only 34% of the rural population has reliable electricity and the main port operates at 140% of design capacity, raising costs and limiting market access.\n\nAlso credit: the external debt burden (debt service absorbing 22% of revenue); weak institutions and corruption (ranked 142nd of 180); a low savings ratio (6% of GDP against 22%), which constrains investment. Award 1 mark per valid barrier identified from the stimulus, maximum 2.` },

      { q: `Refer to Fig. 1. Describe the relationship between gross national income per head and life expectancy shown.

Fig. 1 — written for this practice question
Selected countries: GNI per head at purchasing power parity (US$) and life expectancy at birth (years)
  Country V:  GNI per head    2 100  ;  life expectancy 58.4
  Country W:  GNI per head    6 400  ;  life expectancy 68.9
  Country X:  GNI per head   14 200  ;  life expectancy 75.1
  Country Y:  GNI per head   31 800  ;  life expectancy 80.6
  Country Z:  GNI per head   58 300  ;  life expectancy 82.3`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe',
        markScheme: `(1) There is a positive relationship — higher GNI per head is associated with higher life expectancy.\n(1) The relationship shows diminishing returns: life expectancy rises steeply at low income levels (a rise of 10.5 years between Country V and Country W for an increase of US$4 300) and much more slowly at high ones (a rise of 1.7 years between Country Y and Country Z for an increase of US$26 500).\n\nDescribe question — do not require explanation. Award the second mark for any accurate quantified feature of the relationship, including the observation that it is non-linear.` },

      { q: `Using the data below, calculate Country B\'s terms of trade index for 2026, to two decimal places. You are advised to show your working.

Table 1 — written for this practice question
Country B, 2026 (2015 = 100)
  Index of export prices:  108.40
  Index of import prices:  115.00`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method (1 mark): terms of trade = (index of export prices / index of import prices) × 100 = (108.40 / 115.00) × 100.\nAnswer (1 mark): 94.26.\n\nAward 2 marks for 94.26. Award 1 mark for correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for an inverted calculation giving 106.09. Credit, but do not require, the interpretation that a value below 100 means the terms of trade have deteriorated since the base year.` },

      { q: `Using the data below, calculate the tariff revenue the government of Country C will collect. You are advised to show your working.

Table 2 — written for this practice question
Country C, imported vehicles, 2026
  World price per vehicle:                    £8 000
  Ad valorem tariff rate:                     10%
  Volume of imports after the tariff:         40 000 vehicles`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method (1 mark): tariff per vehicle = 10% × £8 000 = £800; total revenue = £800 × 40 000.\nAnswer (1 mark): £32 000 000, i.e. £32 million.\n\nAward 1 mark for correct method with an arithmetic slip. Credit, but do not require, the observation that revenue is calculated on the post-tariff import volume, which is lower than the free-trade volume — so a higher tariff does not necessarily raise more revenue.` },

      { q: 'Using the data below, calculate the price in euros of a good sold in the United Kingdom. You are advised to show your working.\n\nThe good is priced at £250 in the UK and the exchange rate is £1 = €1.16, the approximate rate in mid-September 2026.', marks: 2, command: 'Calculate',
        markScheme: `Method (1 mark): price in euros = price in sterling × the euro value of £1 = 250 × 1.16.\nAnswer (1 mark): €290.\n\nAward 1 mark for correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for dividing rather than multiplying (giving €215.52). Credit, but do not require, the observation that an appreciation of sterling would raise the euro price and reduce the competitiveness of UK exports in the euro area.` },

      /* ─────────────── 3 marks — points-marked ─────────────── */
      { q: 'Explain why a country that has an absolute advantage in producing every good can still gain from trade.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) Absolute advantage concerns productivity — producing more output from given resources — but the gains from trade depend on comparative advantage, which concerns opportunity cost: what must be given up to produce one more unit of a good.\n(1) Even a country that is absolutely more efficient at everything cannot be relatively better at everything, because producing more of one good always means producing less of another. Its opportunity cost will be lower in some goods and higher in others, so a comparative advantage necessarily exists on both sides.\n(1) If each country specialises where its opportunity cost is lower and then trades at a rate between the two domestic opportunity cost ratios, total world output rises and both countries can consume at a point beyond their own production possibility frontier. The more efficient country gains by concentrating its superior resources where its advantage is greatest.` },

      { q: 'Explain why the Prebisch-Singer hypothesis predicts that the terms of trade of primary commodity exporters will decline over time.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) The demand side: primary commodities such as food and basic raw materials have low income elasticity of demand, so as world incomes rise, demand for them grows more slowly than demand for manufactures and services. Export prices therefore rise more slowly than import prices.\n(1) The supply side: supply of primary commodities tends to expand as new producers enter and yields improve, and because individual producers are price takers there is no mechanism to restrain output — so gains from productivity growth are passed to consumers as lower prices. In manufacturing, market power, branding and product differentiation allow producers to retain those gains as higher prices or profits.\n(1) The consequence is a long-run fall in the ratio of export prices to import prices. A country must export a growing volume to finance the same volume of imported capital goods and technology, which reduces foreign exchange earnings and constrains the investment needed for development. Credit the qualification that the evidence is contested and that commodity prices are extremely volatile around any trend.` },

      { q: 'Explain why remittances from migrant workers can support economic development in their home country.', marks: 3, command: 'Explain',
        markScheme: `(1) Remittances are a transfer recorded in the secondary income section of the current account. They provide foreign exchange that the receiving country can use to import capital goods, technology and intermediate inputs, easing the foreign exchange constraint that limits investment in many low-income economies.\n(1) They go directly to households rather than through governments, so they are less exposed to corruption than aid, and recipient households typically spend a high proportion on food, health care and schooling — raising human capital — and some on small business investment. The additional spending also has a multiplier effect in the local economy.\n(1) Remittances are counter-cyclical: they tend to rise when the home economy is in difficulty, because migrants send more to support relatives, so they provide a stabilising insurance flow that private capital does not. Credit the qualification that transfer fees can be high, that remittances can foster dependency, and that emigration itself can represent a loss of human capital (brain drain).` },

      /* ─────────────── 4 marks — explain with a diagram ─────────────── */
      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the effect of imposing a tariff on an imported good.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: domestic supply and demand for the good, with a horizontal world supply curve at the world price Pw below the domestic equilibrium price, showing imports as the gap between domestic demand and domestic supply at Pw.\n(1) The tariff raises the price paid by domestic consumers from Pw to Pw + t, shown as a new horizontal line above the world price.\n(1) At the higher price, domestic supply extends and domestic demand contracts, so the volume of imports falls — identified on the diagram as the narrower gap between the two domestic curves.\n(1) Welfare effects identified: consumer surplus falls; producer surplus rises; the government gains tariff revenue equal to the tariff multiplied by the remaining import volume; and two deadweight loss triangles remain — the production distortion (higher-cost domestic output replacing cheaper imports) and the consumption distortion (consumers priced out of the market).` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how a quota restricts imports.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: domestic supply and demand with a horizontal world supply curve at the world price Pw, showing free-trade imports as the gap between domestic demand and domestic supply.\n(1) The quota caps imports at a fixed quantity, so the effective supply curve becomes domestic supply plus the quota amount — drawn as the domestic supply curve shifted right by the quota beyond the point where the quota binds.\n(1) The new equilibrium shows the domestic price rising above the world price and domestic production increasing, with total consumption falling.\n(1) Welfare effects: consumer surplus falls, producer surplus rises, deadweight losses arise as with a tariff — but the revenue equivalent of the price rise on the quota volume accrues to whoever holds the import licences (often foreign exporters) rather than to the government, which is the key difference from a tariff.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the effect of an export subsidy on the domestic market.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: domestic supply and demand with the world price Pw, showing the free-trade position.\n(1) The subsidy raises the price domestic producers effectively receive to Pw + s, which they can also charge at home because they would otherwise export, so the domestic price rises to Pw + s.\n(1) At the higher price domestic production expands and domestic consumption contracts, so the volume exported increases — shown as the widened gap between the domestic supply and demand curves.\n(1) Welfare effects: domestic consumer surplus falls; producer surplus rises; the government bears a cost equal to the subsidy per unit multiplied by the quantity exported, which exceeds the gain to producers, leaving a net welfare loss. Credit the point that the subsidy depresses the world price, harming producers in other countries — a frequent source of trade disputes.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how a rise in a country\'s interest rate relative to others affects its exchange rate.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: foreign exchange market with the exchange rate (e.g. euros per pound) on the vertical axis and the quantity of the currency on the horizontal axis, showing initial demand and supply curves for the currency.\n(1) A higher relative interest rate raises the return on deposits and bonds denominated in that currency, attracting short-term capital inflows — so demand for the currency shifts right (and/or supply shifts left as domestic holders keep funds at home).\n(1) The equilibrium exchange rate rises from e to e1 — an appreciation.\n(1) Consequences: exports become more expensive in foreign currency and imports cheaper in domestic currency, so net exports and AD tend to fall, and imported inflation falls. For example, the Bank of England held Bank Rate at 3.75% on 16 September 2026 while the European Central Bank\'s deposit rate stood at 2.50%, a differential that supports sterling; it traded at around 1.16 euros in mid-September 2026.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, the J-curve effect following a depreciation of the exchange rate.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: the trade balance (or current account balance) on the vertical axis and time on the horizontal axis, with a horizontal line at zero, showing the balance dipping below its starting level immediately after the depreciation and then rising above it — the J shape. Mark the point of depreciation.\n(1) The initial deterioration: in the short run, demand for exports and imports is price inelastic because contracts are already agreed and buyers take time to switch suppliers. Import volumes barely fall while each import costs more in domestic currency, so the value of imports rises.\n(1) The subsequent improvement: over time demand becomes more elastic as contracts are renegotiated and buyers respond to the price change, so export volumes rise and import volumes fall.\n(1) The condition: the balance improves only once the Marshall-Lerner condition holds, i.e. the sum of the price elasticities of demand for exports and imports exceeds one. Credit the point that the depth and length of the dip depend on how quickly elasticities rise.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how specialisation according to comparative advantage allows two countries to consume beyond their production possibility frontiers.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: two production possibility frontiers, one for each country, with the same two goods on the axes and different slopes, showing that the opportunity cost ratios differ.\n(1) Identification of comparative advantage: each country has the lower opportunity cost in one good, shown by the relative steepness of its PPF.\n(1) Each country specialises in the good in which its opportunity cost is lower, moving to the relevant intercept (or towards it) on its own PPF, which raises combined world output of both goods.\n(1) The countries then trade at a rate between the two domestic opportunity cost ratios, shown as a consumption possibility line lying outside each country\'s own PPF, so both can consume combinations they could not produce themselves. Credit reference to the assumptions — no transport costs, constant returns to scale, factor mobility within countries — as a limit on the result.` },

      { q: 'Explain, using an appropriate diagram, how inward foreign direct investment affects a developing economy\'s productive potential.', marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `(1) Diagram: either a PPF shifting outward, or AD/AS with LRAS shifting right from LRAS to LRAS1 (credit AD also shifting right, since the investment spending is itself an injection).\n(1) FDI adds to the domestic capital stock — factories, machinery and infrastructure that domestic saving, at perhaps 6% of GDP, could not finance — so the quantity of capital available per worker rises.\n(1) It also transfers technology and management practice, and trains local workers, raising the quality of capital and labour; both raise productivity, so LRAS or the PPF shifts right and potential output increases.\n(1) Explanation of the consequences: higher potential output means growth can be sustained without inflation, and export capacity and tax revenue rise. Credit the qualification that profits may be repatriated rather than reinvested, that the technology may not diffuse beyond the foreign-owned firm (an enclave economy), and that the LRAS gain depends on complementary domestic investment in skills and infrastructure.` },

      /* ─────────────── 8 marks — evaluate using the stimulus ─────────────── */
      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which the tariffs described benefited the economy that imposed them.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"The overall average effective tariff rate applied by the United States rose from 2.5% in January 2025 to an estimated 27% by April 2025, the highest level in over a century. On 20 February 2026 the Supreme Court ruled by six votes to three in Learning Resources Inc. v. Trump that the International Emergency Economic Powers Act did not authorise the president to impose sweeping tariffs; those tariffs had accounted for roughly 70% of the US tariff architecture. By July 2026 the average effective tariff rate had fallen to 6.7%, and the trade-weighted average fell from 15.3% to 8.3% as a result of the ruling."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The case that tariffs benefited the economy: protected domestic producers face a higher import price, so domestic supply extends and producer surplus rises; employment in the protected sector is supported; and the government collects revenue on the remaining imports. Infant industry and strategic-sector arguments can justify temporary protection where an industry would eventually achieve economies of scale or where national security is involved.\n• The case against — consumers. On a tariff diagram, the rise in the domestic price reduces consumer surplus by more than the gain to producers and the government combined, leaving two deadweight loss triangles: a production distortion, as higher-cost domestic output replaces cheaper imports, and a consumption distortion, as consumers are priced out. A move from 2.5% to 27% is an enormous price shock to import-using households and firms.\n• Intermediate goods are the sharpest problem. Most imports are components and raw materials, so a broad tariff raises the costs of domestic manufacturers, shifting SRAS left, raising the price level and reducing output — protecting some producers by taxing others. A trade-weighted average of 15.3% is a substantial cost increase across the economy.\n• Retaliation and uncertainty: trading partners typically retaliate, harming export industries, and the scale and reversibility of the changes — from 2.5% to 27% and back to 6.7% within eighteen months — create uncertainty that deters the very investment protection is supposed to encourage. Firms cannot plan supply chains around a tariff that may be struck down.\n• Evaluation: concentrated, visible gains to protected producers against dispersed, larger losses to consumers and downstream firms is the standard verdict, and the scale here makes the losses large. The reversal itself is evidence: the trade-weighted rate falling from 15.3% to 8.3% following the ruling removed a significant cost from the economy. A defensible judgement is that narrow, temporary, well-targeted protection can be justified, but that broad tariffs on this scale imposed net costs, with the additional harm that policy instability discouraged long-term investment.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — uses the specific tariff rates, distinguishes producer gains from consumer and downstream losses, and judges. L1 (1–4) — lists arguments for and against protectionism with little use of the stimulus.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which the Human Development Index is a better measure of development than gross national income per head.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"In the 2025 Human Development Report, Iceland topped the Human Development Index with a value of 0.972, followed by Norway and Switzerland at 0.970; the United Kingdom scored 0.946 and ranked 13th of 193 countries. The HDI combines life expectancy at birth, mean and expected years of schooling, and gross national income per head at purchasing power parity, each converted to an index and equally weighted. Several countries with higher GNI per head than the United Kingdom rank below it. The report also publishes an inequality-adjusted HDI, which is substantially lower than the headline value in the most unequal economies. The 2025 report uses data for 2023."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Why HDI is better: development is a broader concept than income, involving health, education and the capability to live a life one values. The HDI captures three dimensions rather than one, and the stimulus provides direct evidence that they diverge — several countries with higher GNI per head than the UK rank below it, which could not happen if income alone determined development.\n• Health and education are both outcomes of development and inputs to it: a healthier, better-educated population is more productive, so the HDI measures the human capital that generates future income as well as current welfare. It also has practical force, being comparable across 193 countries and widely used to direct attention and aid.\n• Limits of the HDI. It uses only three indicators, equally weighted by convention rather than by evidence — there is no principled reason why a year of life expectancy should count the same as a year of schooling. Years of schooling measures quantity, not quality of education, so two countries with identical scores may have very different levels of literacy and numeracy. Income is capped and logged, which compresses differences at the top.\n• Timeliness and omission: the 2025 report uses 2023 data, a two-year lag, whereas GNI per head is available quarterly. The HDI also ignores inequality, political freedom, gender equality, environmental sustainability and security — which is why the report publishes an inequality-adjusted HDI that falls substantially in the most unequal economies, effectively conceding the gap in the headline measure.\n• What GNI per head does well: it is timely, precise, available for almost every country and strongly correlated with health and education outcomes, so it remains a reasonable single proxy. At purchasing power parity it also adjusts for differences in the cost of living.\n• Evaluation: a defensible judgement is that the HDI is the better measure of development because development is by definition multidimensional, but that "better" does not mean sufficient — the inequality-adjusted HDI, the Gini coefficient, measures of gender equality, environmental indicators and GNI per head itself are complements rather than rivals. The best answers note that the two measures are not independent, since GNI per head is a component of the HDI.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — uses the specific HDI evidence, evaluates the limits of both measures and judges. L1 (1–4) — describes what the HDI contains with little evaluation.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which foreign aid promotes economic development.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"The United Kingdom announced that official development assistance would fall from 0.5% of gross national income to 0.3% in 2027, to fund an increase in defence spending to 2.6% of GDP. The aid budget in 2027 would be around £9.2 billion, compared with around £15.4 billion if the 0.5% commitment had been kept - a reduction of about 40%. Aid had previously been cut from 0.7% to 0.5% of GNI in 2021. The last time UK aid was at or below 0.3% of GNI was in 1999. Over the same period, more than a third of sub-Saharan African countries were assessed as at high risk of, or already in, debt distress, and the ratio of external public debt service to government revenue in the region doubled from 9% in 2017 to 18% in 2025."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The case that aid promotes development: it fills the savings gap identified by the Harrod-Domar model, funding investment that domestic saving cannot. Aid directed at health and education raises human capital with returns that compound over decades — vaccination, malaria control and primary schooling have some of the highest measured social returns of any spending. Humanitarian aid saves lives in crises where no market mechanism will act.\n• The timing argument from the stimulus is strong. With external debt service absorbing 18% of government revenue in sub-Saharan Africa, up from 9% in 2017, and more than a third of countries at high risk of or in debt distress, governments face a binding fiscal constraint: every pound of aid withdrawn is a pound that must come from health and education budgets already squeezed by debt payments. A 40% cut, from £15.4 billion to £9.2 billion, removes support precisely when the need is greatest.\n• The case against: aid can create dependency, weakening the incentive for governments to build domestic tax capacity and to undertake difficult reforms. It can sustain poor governments in power, be diverted by corruption, and be tied to donor procurement so that much of the money returns to the donor economy. Large inflows can also appreciate the recipient\'s exchange rate, harming its export competitiveness.\n• Effectiveness is conditional on institutions. Aid delivered into a system with weak rule of law and insecure property rights produces little; the same aid in a country with functioning institutions and public financial management can be highly productive. This is why the debate has shifted from how much aid to how it is delivered, and towards conditionality, budget support and results-based financing.\n• Scale and alternatives: aid is small relative to remittances, FDI and trade revenue for most recipients, so treating it as the main lever overstates its role. Debt relief and improved market access can transfer more resources at lower administrative cost, and the debt service data in the stimulus suggest relief may be the binding constraint.\n• Evaluation: a defensible judgement is that aid is neither the engine of development nor a waste — it is effective when targeted at high-return public goods in countries with the institutional capacity to use it, and ineffective or harmful when it substitutes for domestic revenue-raising or props up poor governance. The cut described will do measurable damage to health and education spending in recipient countries, but the honest conclusion is that its effect on long-run development rates will be smaller than its effect on immediate welfare.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — uses the aid and debt-service figures, distinguishes conditions under which aid works, and judges. L1 (1–4) — lists advantages and disadvantages of aid with little use of the stimulus.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which debt relief would promote development in the countries described.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"The ratio of external public debt service to government revenue in sub-Saharan Africa doubled from 9% in 2017 to 18% in 2025. More than a third of countries in the region are assessed as at high risk of, or already in, debt distress, and in 21 countries fiscal deficits are larger than the level required to stabilise the debt. Regional growth is forecast at 4.1% for 2026, the same rate as in 2025, but well below the 7% or more often cited as necessary to absorb new entrants to the labour market and reduce poverty substantially. A growing share of the region's debt is owed to private bondholders and to non-traditional bilateral creditors rather than to multilateral institutions."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The case for relief: with 18% of government revenue absorbed by external debt service, relief would release roughly one pound in every five and a half of revenue for health, education and infrastructure — spending with high social returns that is currently crowded out. The proportion has doubled since 2017, so the constraint is tightening rather than easing.\n• Debt overhang theory: when a country\'s debt is large enough that creditors expect to capture most of the returns from any reform or investment, both the government and private investors have weakened incentives to undertake them. Relief can therefore raise investment and growth by more than the cash value of the write-off. With growth at 4.1% against the 7% needed, and 21 countries running deficits above the debt-stabilising level, current trajectories imply rising ratios and repeated crises.\n• The case against: moral hazard. Relief granted once creates an expectation that it will be granted again, weakening both borrower discipline and lender scrutiny, and potentially raising the cost of future borrowing for all similar countries as lenders price in the risk. Relief also treats the symptom — if the underlying deficits and governance problems are unaddressed, debt simply re-accumulates, as it has since the HIPC and MDRI initiatives of the 2000s.\n• The creditor composition is the decisive practical obstacle, and the stimulus flags it. When debt was owed mainly to multilateral institutions and Paris Club governments, coordinated relief was achievable. With a growing share owed to private bondholders and non-traditional bilateral creditors, each has an incentive to hold out for full repayment while others write down — a collective action problem that has stalled recent restructurings for years, during which the crisis deepens.\n• Relief is not sufficient. Without improved domestic revenue mobilisation, public financial management and investment quality, released resources may not reach the intended uses; and some borrowing is productive, so writing off debt that financed useful infrastructure is not obviously right.\n• Evaluation: a defensible judgement is that relief is necessary for the countries already in distress, because the debt service burden is demonstrably crowding out development spending and growth is too slow to outgrow the debt, but that it is effective only if paired with conditionality on governance and with a workable mechanism to bring private and non-traditional creditors into a common framework. Relief without that mechanism risks transferring value to holdout creditors rather than to the population.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — uses the debt-service and growth data, engages with moral hazard and the creditor composition, and judges. L1 (1–4) — states that debt relief frees money for services.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which membership of a trade bloc benefits a country.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"The United Kingdom's accession to the Comprehensive and Progressive Agreement for Trans-Pacific Partnership took effect for eight members from 15 December 2024, and it gained access to all eleven member markets from September 2025. The government expects membership to add around £2 billion a year to the UK economy in the long term, with more than 99% of current UK goods exports to CPTPP members eligible for zero tariffs. UK trade with CPTPP members accounts for a smaller share of total UK trade than trade with the European Union. A UK-India free trade agreement signed in July 2025 came into force on 15 July 2026."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Benefits: trade creation. Removing tariffs allows members to import from the lowest-cost producer within the bloc rather than producing domestically behind protection, which raises consumer surplus and reallocates resources towards comparative advantage. With over 99% of current UK goods exports to CPTPP members eligible for zero tariffs, exporters gain price competitiveness in those markets.\n• Wider gains: a larger market allows firms to exploit economies of scale, lowering average costs; greater competition raises productivity and reduces prices; and access to a bloc can attract inward FDI from firms seeking a base inside it. Membership also gives a voice in setting rules on standards, services and digital trade.\n• Costs: trade diversion. A preferential agreement can shift imports from an efficient non-member to a less efficient member simply because the latter faces no tariff, which reduces welfare. The net effect on welfare depends on whether trade creation exceeds trade diversion, and that is an empirical question rather than a presumption.\n• Scale is the strongest qualification here. The expected gain is around £2 billion a year "in the long term" — a very small figure relative to a UK economy of well over £2.5 trillion, roughly 0.1% of GDP. The stimulus notes that CPTPP trade is a smaller share of UK trade than EU trade, and the gravity model predicts exactly this: trade volumes fall sharply with distance, so agreements with distant partners deliver less than agreements with near neighbours.\n• Other costs: loss of policy autonomy, since members accept common rules and dispute procedures; adjustment costs for sectors exposed to new competition, concentrated on particular regions and workers; and the administrative burden of rules of origin, which can leave nominal tariff-free access unused by smaller firms.\n• Evaluation: a defensible judgement is that membership of a trade bloc is beneficial but that the magnitude depends almost entirely on the size and proximity of the partners. Agreements such as CPTPP and the UK-India FTA, which came into force on 15 July 2026, are clearly positive at the margin and carry strategic value in diversifying markets, but the quantified gains are small relative to trade with close neighbours — so blocs should be judged on numbers rather than on the principle.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — applies trade creation and diversion, uses the £2 billion and 99% figures to assess scale, and judges. L1 (1–4) — lists benefits of trade blocs with little use of the stimulus.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which foreign direct investment benefits the host developing economy.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"Inward FDI into Country D rose from 1.9% to 5.4% of GDP over five years, concentrated in mineral extraction and in a special economic zone producing electronic components for export. Employment in the zone reached 41,000, with average wages 34% above the national average. Around 78% of the profits generated were repatriated to the investing firms' home countries. Domestic firms supplied only 11% of the inputs used in the zone. The government granted a ten-year corporation tax holiday to firms locating there, and gross domestic saving in Country D is 6% of GDP."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The case for benefit: with gross domestic saving at only 6% of GDP, domestic funds cannot finance investment on the scale needed, so FDI relaxes the savings constraint identified by the Harrod-Domar model — inflows rising from 1.9% to 5.4% of GDP is a substantial addition to the capital stock. It brings foreign exchange without creating debt, unlike borrowing.\n• Employment and wages: 41,000 jobs at wages 34% above the national average raise incomes directly, and the additional spending has a multiplier effect locally. Workers acquire skills and exposure to modern management practice that can diffuse when they move to domestic firms.\n• Export capacity: production of electronic components for export earns foreign exchange to fund imports of capital goods, and moves the export base away from primary commodities towards manufactures with better long-run terms of trade — addressing the Prebisch-Singer problem directly.\n• The case against — leakage. With 78% of profits repatriated, most of the return on the investment leaves the country as an outflow on the primary income account of the balance of payments, worsening the current account over time. The ten-year corporation tax holiday means the government captures very little of the remaining value either, so the fiscal benefit is close to zero for a decade.\n• Weak linkages: domestic firms supply only 11% of inputs, so the zone is close to an enclave — connected to the world economy but barely to the domestic one. Without backward linkages, the technology transfer and supply-chain development that justify FDI largely fail to occur, and the jobs created remain vulnerable to relocation.\n• Sectoral concentration: mineral extraction is capital-intensive, creates few jobs per pound invested, and carries environmental costs and the risk of Dutch disease, where resource exports appreciate the currency and damage other tradable sectors.\n• Evaluation: a defensible judgement is that FDI has brought real benefits in employment, wages and export capacity, but that the terms on which it was attracted — a ten-year tax holiday, minimal local content requirements — mean the host country captures an unusually small share of the gains. The policy conclusion concerns bargaining, not whether to accept FDI: local content requirements, skills transfer conditions and a shorter tax holiday would raise the domestic share, subject to the constraint that competing locations are offering similar terms.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — uses the repatriation, linkage and tax-holiday data to assess who captures the gains, and judges. L1 (1–4) — lists advantages and disadvantages of FDI.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, the extent to which the international trading system can resolve disputes between members effectively.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"The World Trade Organization's Appellate Body has remained non-functional for several years because of continued opposition by one major member to the appointment of new members. In its place, 61 WTO members are now party to the Multi-Party Interim Appeal Arbitration Arrangement, under which two appeals have been resolved. Members brought 22 new dispute cases to the WTO in the two years to 2026. The organisation's Director-General has described the interim arrangement as a practical, confidence-building bridge pending agreement on wider reform, and its parties describe it as temporary. Over the same period, one major economy raised its average effective tariff rate from 2.5% to an estimated 27% before domestic courts struck most of those tariffs down in February 2026."`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The case that it works: the underlying dispute settlement system continues to function at panel stage, and members brought 22 new cases in two years, which shows they still regard it as worth using. The interim appeal arrangement has 61 parties spanning regions and income levels and has resolved two appeals, so an appellate route exists for a large share of world trade. Rules-based settlement protects small economies, which have no retaliatory power of their own and would otherwise be at the mercy of bilateral pressure.\n• The case that it does not: the Appellate Body, the system\'s apex, has been non-functional for years because a single major member blocks appointments — a design flaw, since appointments require consensus and any member can therefore veto. A respondent that loses at panel stage can appeal "into the void", leaving the ruling unadopted and unenforceable.\n• The interim arrangement is a partial and voluntary fix. It binds only those who join, so a member that stays outside is unaffected, and its parties themselves describe it as temporary. Two resolved appeals in several years is a thin record against the volume of world trade.\n• The tariff episode is the most damaging evidence. One major economy raised its average effective tariff from 2.5% to an estimated 27% — a scale plainly inconsistent with bound tariff commitments — and what reversed most of it in February 2026 was a domestic court ruling, not a WTO process. When the constraint that bites is national law rather than the multilateral system, the system\'s effectiveness against its largest members is in question.\n• Structural problems: consensus decision-making across a very large membership has stalled negotiation for years, and enforcement ultimately depends on authorised retaliation, which is only credible for large economies — a developing country cannot meaningfully retaliate against a major trading partner.\n• Evaluation: a defensible judgement is that the system remains effective for routine disputes between members willing to be bound by it, and ineffective precisely where it matters most — against the largest members when they choose to act unilaterally. Its value is therefore real but conditional on consent, which is a weak foundation; the interim arrangement is evidence both of members\' commitment and of the depth of the underlying problem.\n\nLevels: L2 (5–8) — uses the MPIA and dispute-volume evidence alongside the tariff episode, and reaches a judgement distinguishing routine from major-member disputes. L1 (1–4) — describes the WTO with little use of the stimulus.` },

      /* ─────────────── 12 marks — evaluate, asterisked ─────────────── */
      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether protectionism can ever be justified.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"One major economy raised its overall average effective tariff rate from 2.5% in January 2025 to an estimated 27% by April 2025, the highest for over a century, before courts struck down most of the measures in February 2026, bringing the rate to 6.7% by July 2026. Country E, a lower-middle-income economy, protects a domestic solar panel industry with a 22% tariff; output has risen from 0.3 to 2.1 gigawatts of annual capacity over eight years and unit costs have fallen 44%, though they remain 12% above world levels. Country E also restricts imports of a staple grain, citing food security, at an estimated cost to consumers of 1.4% of GDP a year."`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The general case against: on a tariff diagram, protection raises the domestic price, so the loss of consumer surplus exceeds the combined gain to producers and government, leaving deadweight losses from both production and consumption distortions. It shelters inefficient firms from competition, reducing the incentive to innovate; it raises input costs for downstream industries; and it invites retaliation. The move from 2.5% to 27% is an extreme instance, and the reversal to 6.7% removed a large cost.\n• The infant industry argument, which the solar data test. The claim is that a new industry cannot initially compete but will achieve economies of scale and learning-by-doing if given temporary protection. Country E\'s output rose from 0.3 to 2.1 gigawatts and unit costs fell 44%, which is exactly the predicted path — the industry is learning. But costs remain 12% above world levels after eight years, so protection has not yet produced a viable industry, and the political difficulty of removing protection once granted is the standard objection: temporary protection becomes permanent because the beneficiaries are organised and the losers are dispersed.\n• Other arguments that can justify protection: preventing dumping, where a foreign producer sells below cost to drive out competitors — though proving dumping is difficult and anti-dumping duties are frequently misused as ordinary protection; strategic and national security sectors; and protection against imports produced under labour or environmental standards a country has chosen to prohibit domestically.\n• The food security case is genuinely contested. Restricting grain imports costs consumers 1.4% of GDP a year, a large and regressive burden falling hardest on poor households who spend most of their income on food. Against that, dependence on world markets exposes a country to export bans and price spikes by other governments — a risk that materialised in several recent food price crises. The question is whether restriction is the cheapest way to buy that insurance, or whether strategic reserves and diversified sourcing would cost less than 1.4% of GDP.\n• Adjustment and distribution: free trade raises aggregate welfare but concentrates losses on particular workers and regions. The economically efficient response is compensation and retraining rather than protection, but where those are not provided, protection may be the only politically available response — which is an argument about political feasibility rather than about economics.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that protectionism can be justified in narrow, specific and time-limited cases — genuine infant industries with a credible sunset clause, genuine dumping, and defined security needs — but that the justifications are much narrower than their use. The evidence here shows both: the solar case is the strongest available argument for protection and still has not delivered competitiveness after eight years, while the 27% tariff episode is protection with no justification at all.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — tests the infant industry argument against the solar data, weighs the food security case, supported judgement. L2 (5–8) — arguments both ways with some application. L1 (1–4) — lists arguments for and against protectionism. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether primary product dependency is the main barrier to economic development.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"Country F earns 71% of export revenue from two agricultural commodities, whose world prices fell 28% over three years; its terms of trade index stands at 94.26 against a base of 100. Only 34% of the rural population has reliable electricity and the main port runs at 140% of design capacity. External public debt service absorbs 22% of government revenue, up from 8% a decade ago. Country F ranks 142nd of 180 on an index of perceived corruption, gross domestic saving is 6% of GDP against 22% for middle-income countries, and mean years of schooling are 4.9 against a regional average of 7.1."`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The case that primary product dependency is the main barrier: 71% of export revenue from two commodities means foreign exchange earnings swing with world prices, which fell 28% in three years. That volatility makes planning impossible, disrupts budgets and makes borrowing more expensive. The terms of trade index at 94.26 shows the Prebisch-Singer mechanism operating — export prices falling relative to import prices, so a growing volume of exports is needed to buy the same capital goods.\n• The mechanism connects to the other problems. Falling commodity revenue reduces government income, which is part of why debt service now absorbs 22% of revenue against 8% a decade ago, and why saving is only 6% of GDP. On that reading, dependency is the root cause and the other barriers are consequences.\n• Further costs of dependency: primary sectors have limited linkages, low value added and few opportunities for learning-by-doing, so they generate little productivity growth; resource rents invite rent-seeking and corruption; and resource exports can appreciate the currency, damaging other tradable sectors (Dutch disease).\n• The case for rival barriers. Infrastructure: 34% rural electrification and a port at 140% of capacity impose costs on every sector and would limit diversification even if commodity prices were high — a country cannot move into manufacturing without power and freight capacity. Human capital: mean schooling of 4.9 years against a regional average of 7.1 means the workforce cannot staff the industries diversification requires. Institutions: ranked 142nd of 180 on corruption, the country will struggle to attract the FDI or retain the domestic investment that diversification needs, whatever its export mix.\n• The savings constraint: at 6% of GDP against 22%, domestic finance for investment is very limited, and debt service at 22% of revenue removes much of what the government might otherwise invest. In the Harrod-Domar framework this alone caps the growth rate.\n• Causation runs both ways, which is the key analytical point. Countries remain dependent on primary products partly because weak institutions, poor infrastructure and low human capital prevent them from producing anything else. Dependency is therefore as much a symptom of the other barriers as a cause of them, and removing it requires fixing them.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that primary product dependency is the most visible barrier and the one that transmits external shocks, but not the most fundamental — institutions, human capital and infrastructure determine whether diversification is possible at all. The policy implication supports this: schemes to stabilise commodity prices have a poor record, whereas investment in schooling, power and governance addresses several barriers at once, including dependency itself.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — weighs dependency against the rival barriers using the specific data, addresses the direction of causation, supported judgement. L2 (5–8) — describes barriers with some application. L1 (1–4) — lists barriers to development. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether a floating exchange rate is preferable to a fixed exchange rate.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"Country G floats its currency. Sterling, also floating, traded at around 1.16 euros in mid-September 2026, with the Bank of England holding Bank Rate at 3.75% on 16 September against a European Central Bank deposit rate of 2.50%. Country H maintains a fixed peg to the US dollar, holds foreign exchange reserves equal to nine months of imports, and has raised its policy rate three times in eighteen months to defend the peg despite domestic unemployment of 11%. Country G's exports have an estimated price elasticity of demand of -0.6 in the short run and -1.4 in the long run, and around 60% of the value of its manufactured exports consists of imported components."`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The case for floating — automatic adjustment. A current account deficit increases the supply of the currency on the foreign exchange market, depreciating it, which raises export competitiveness and reduces import demand without any policy action. On a forex diagram this is a movement to a new equilibrium rather than a defended price.\n• Monetary policy independence is the decisive advantage, and the stimulus illustrates its absence. Country H has raised its policy rate three times in eighteen months to defend the peg while unemployment is 11% — monetary policy is being used for the exchange rate rather than for the domestic economy, which is exactly the cost of a fixed rate. A floating country can hold Bank Rate at 3.75% because domestic inflation requires it, as the Bank of England did on 16 September 2026, rather than because of a currency target.\n• No reserves are required to defend a float, releasing the resources Country H ties up in nine months of import cover, and a floating rate cannot be the target of a speculative attack in the same way — there is no announced level to break.\n• The case for fixing — certainty. Exporters, importers and investors face no exchange rate risk within the peg, which reduces hedging costs and can encourage trade and FDI. A credible peg to a low-inflation currency imports that country\'s monetary discipline, which is valuable for an economy with a history of high inflation or a weak central bank.\n• The case against floating — volatility and weak adjustment. Short-run export price elasticity of −0.6 means the Marshall-Lerner condition may not hold immediately, so depreciation can worsen the trade balance before improving it (the J-curve); only the long-run elasticity of −1.4 makes adjustment work. With 60% of the value of manufactured exports made up of imported components, depreciation raises exporters\' own costs, offsetting much of the competitiveness gain — so the automatic adjustment mechanism is weaker than the textbook case implies. Floating rates are also driven substantially by speculative capital flows and interest differentials rather than by trade fundamentals, so they can stay misaligned for years.\n• The impossible trinity frames the choice: a country cannot simultaneously have a fixed exchange rate, free capital movement and an independent monetary policy. Country H has chosen the peg and capital mobility and surrendered monetary independence; Country G has chosen independence and mobility and accepted volatility. Intermediate regimes — managed floats, crawling pegs, bands — trade off between them.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that floating is preferable for a large, diversified economy with a credible central bank and deep financial markets, because monetary independence is worth more than exchange rate certainty and the adjustment mechanism, though imperfect, works over time. Fixing is preferable for a small, very open economy heavily dependent on trade with one partner, or one that needs to import monetary credibility — so the answer depends on the country, not on the regime in the abstract. The evidence in the stimulus, particularly 11% unemployment with rates being raised to defend a peg, shows the cost of fixing when the two objectives conflict.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — uses the elasticity and import-content data, applies the impossible trinity, supported judgement. L2 (5–8) — advantages and disadvantages with some application. L1 (1–4) — describes the two regimes. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether globalisation has reduced global poverty and inequality.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"In June 2025 the World Bank raised its international poverty line from $2.15 to $3.00 a person a day at 2021 purchasing power parity. On the new line, the global extreme poverty rate for 2022 is estimated at 10.5%, against 9.0% on the old line, and the number in extreme poverty rises from around 713 million to around 838 million. A large share of those remaining in extreme poverty are in sub-Saharan Africa or in fragile and conflict-affected states, where growth of 4.1% forecast for 2026 falls well short of the 7% or more often cited as necessary for substantial poverty reduction. Within many countries, the share of income going to the top decile has risen over the same decades in which absolute poverty has fallen."`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The case that globalisation has reduced poverty: trade liberalisation, FDI and the fragmentation of production into global value chains allowed low-income countries to specialise according to comparative advantage and to move labour from low-productivity agriculture into manufacturing — the Lewis dual-sector mechanism. The result has been the largest reduction in absolute poverty in recorded history, concentrated in East and South Asia, driven by export-led growth. Technology transfer through FDI and access to larger markets allowed economies of scale that domestic markets could not support.\n• Between-country inequality has narrowed as a result: fast-growing exporters converged on richer economies, so the global distribution has compressed at the level of country averages.\n• The case that the gains are overstated or uneven. The poverty count depends on where the line is drawn, and the stimulus shows the stakes: raising the line from $2.15 to $3.00 at 2021 prices moves 125 million people from "not extremely poor" to "extremely poor" without anything changing in their lives, taking the rate from 9.0% to 10.5%. Progress against an absolute line is real but its measured scale is a matter of convention.\n• The gains have been geographically concentrated. A large share of those remaining in extreme poverty are in sub-Saharan Africa and fragile states, where growth of 4.1% forecast for 2026 is well below the 7% associated with substantial poverty reduction. Globalisation has bypassed economies that lack the infrastructure, institutions and human capital to participate in global value chains, so the benefits are conditional on prior capacity rather than automatic.\n• Within-country inequality has risen in many economies, as the stimulus notes. Trade raises the return to skills and to capital while exposing low-skilled workers in rich countries to competition and workers in poor countries to volatile demand; the gains accrue disproportionately to the top decile. So globalisation can reduce absolute poverty and increase relative inequality at the same time — these are not contradictory findings but answers to different questions.\n• Attribution: over the same decades, domestic policy — China\'s agricultural reforms, India\'s deregulation, expansions of schooling and health provision — also changed, so isolating the effect of globalisation from that of domestic reform is genuinely difficult. Openness may have been a necessary condition rather than the cause.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that globalisation has contributed substantially to reducing absolute poverty and to narrowing differences between countries, while contributing to wider inequality within many of them, and that it has largely failed the poorest and least-connected economies. The policy implication is not to reverse it but that its benefits depend on complementary domestic investment in infrastructure, institutions and human capital, and on redistribution to compensate the losers within countries.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — distinguishes absolute poverty from relative inequality and between-country from within-country, uses the poverty line revision, supported judgement. L2 (5–8) — describes effects of globalisation with some application. L1 (1–4) — asserts globalisation is good or bad. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using evidence from the stimulus material, whether the quality of a country's institutions matters more than the quantity of its investment for economic development.

Stimulus material — written for this practice question
"Country J and Country K had similar income per head twenty-five years ago. Country J has since invested an average of 26% of GDP a year and grown at 1.2% a year; it ranks 142nd of 180 on an index of perceived corruption, contract enforcement takes an average of 1,180 days, and 41% of large infrastructure projects were abandoned before completion. Country K has invested 19% of GDP a year and grown at 5.1% a year; it ranks 48th on the same corruption index, contract enforcement takes 340 days, and it has an independent central bank and a functioning land registry. Both receive similar volumes of official development assistance."`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The case for investment: capital accumulation is the proximate mechanism of growth in both the Harrod-Domar and Solow frameworks — g = s/k in the former, so a higher savings and investment ratio raises the growth rate for a given capital-output ratio. Investment embodies new technology, raises capital per worker and shifts LRAS or the PPF outward. It is also the variable most directly open to policy.\n• The data contradict a simple version of that claim. Country J invests 26% of GDP and grows at 1.2%; Country K invests 19% and grows at 5.1%. More investment is producing far less growth, which means the capital-output ratio in Country J is much worse — capital is being accumulated but not converted into output.\n• The institutional data explain why. Contract enforcement taking 1,180 days against 340 means firms cannot rely on the courts, so they under-invest in relationships with new suppliers and customers and prefer short, low-risk projects. A corruption ranking of 142nd of 180 means resources are diverted and investment decisions are made on the basis of rents rather than returns. And 41% of large infrastructure projects abandoned before completion is the clearest possible evidence: that capital is recorded as investment in the national accounts and produces nothing at all.\n• Institutions determine the return on everything else. A functioning land registry makes land usable as collateral, unlocking credit for small firms; an independent central bank anchors inflation expectations, which lengthens planning horizons; secure property rights mean investors capture the returns from improving assets. Aid, FDI and domestic saving all pass through the same institutional filter — which is why both countries receiving similar aid volumes have such different outcomes.\n• The case that this goes too far: institutional quality is slow to change and partly endogenous — richer countries can afford better courts and administration, so causation may run from development to institutions as well as the reverse. Some economies have grown rapidly with weak formal institutions by substituting informal enforcement or state coordination, so the relationship is not deterministic. And institutions cannot substitute for investment: a country with perfect courts and no capital will not develop either.\n• Human capital is a third factor the comparison does not isolate, and it is plausibly prior to both — an educated population supports better institutions and uses capital more productively.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that investment is necessary but institutions determine its productivity, so that on this evidence institutions matter more at the margin — Country J\'s problem is plainly not a shortage of investment. The policy implication is that raising the investment ratio in a weak institutional setting wastes resources, and that governance reform, though slower and politically harder, raises the return on all subsequent investment.\n\nLevels: L3 (9–12) — uses the investment-growth comparison and the institutional indicators, addresses endogeneity, supported judgement. L2 (5–8) — describes both factors with some application. L1 (1–4) — lists determinants of development. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      /* ─────────────── 15 marks — evaluate with diagram and extract ─────────────── */
      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s) and the information in Extract 1, the extent to which a tariff improves economic welfare in the country that imposes it.

Extract 1 — written for this practice question
"Country L imposes a 10% ad valorem tariff on imported vehicles, which have a world price of £8,000. Domestic production rises from 90,000 to 130,000 vehicles a year and imports fall from 70,000 to 40,000, generating £32 million a year in tariff revenue. Domestic producers' unit costs are estimated to be 14% above world levels. Around 60% of the value of domestically assembled vehicles consists of imported components, which are subject to a separate 6% tariff. Two trading partners have announced retaliatory duties on Country L's agricultural exports, worth an estimated £190 million a year."`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: domestic supply and demand with a horizontal world supply curve at Pw = £8 000, the tariff raising the effective price to £8 800, domestic supply extending from 90 000 to 130 000, demand contracting, and imports falling to 40 000. The four areas must be identified: the loss of consumer surplus; the transfer to producer surplus; the government revenue rectangle (£800 × 40 000 = £32 million); and the two deadweight loss triangles.\n• The gains: producer surplus rises and output increases by 40 000 vehicles, supporting employment in the sector; the government collects £32 million; and if there is an infant industry or strategic case, the higher output may allow economies of scale that lower future costs.\n• The losses. Consumers pay £800 more per vehicle and some are priced out altogether. The production distortion triangle is the resource waste from producing 40 000 extra vehicles domestically at unit costs 14% above world levels — society uses more resources than necessary for the same output. The consumption distortion triangle is the surplus lost by buyers who no longer purchase. Both are pure deadweight losses transferred to nobody.\n• The input tariff compounds the problem. With 60% of the value of domestic assembly made up of imported components facing a separate 6% tariff, domestic producers\' own costs are raised by the tariff regime, which partly offsets the protection and explains why unit costs remain 14% above world levels. Effective rates of protection can differ sharply from nominal ones when inputs are also taxed.\n• Retaliation is decisive for the overall verdict. Retaliatory duties worth £190 million a year on agricultural exports exceed the £32 million of tariff revenue by a factor of nearly six, and fall on a sector that was internationally competitive. The economy is therefore protecting a high-cost sector at the expense of a low-cost one — the opposite of specialising according to comparative advantage.\n• Distribution: the gains are concentrated and visible (vehicle producers and their workers), the losses dispersed and invisible (every vehicle buyer, and agricultural exporters), which explains why such tariffs are politically durable even when they reduce welfare.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that the tariff reduces national economic welfare. Even ignoring retaliation, the deadweight losses exceed the revenue gain when domestic costs are 14% above world levels; including £190 million of retaliation, the loss is unambiguous. Protection could only be justified if the 14% cost gap were falling towards zero under a credible, time-limited infant industry programme, and the extract gives no evidence of that.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — accurate tariff diagram with all four areas identified and used, quantitative comparison of revenue and retaliation, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, effects described, limited evaluation. L1 (1–5) — asserts that tariffs protect jobs; diagram missing or incorrect. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s) and the information in Extract 1, the extent to which a depreciation of the currency would improve Country M's current account.

Extract 1 — written for this practice question
"Country M has a current account deficit of 2.8% of GDP. Its currency has depreciated 9% on a trade-weighted basis over twelve months. The estimated price elasticity of demand for its exports is -0.6 in the first year and -1.4 after three years; for imports the figures are -0.3 and -0.9. Around 60% of the value of its manufactured exports consists of imported components, and a survey found that most exporters set prices in the currency of the destination market. CPI inflation is 3.1% against a 2% target and the central bank's policy rate is 3.75%."`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: a foreign exchange market showing demand for and supply of the currency, with the rate falling from e to e1. Credit a second diagram — the J-curve, with the trade balance against time, or AD/AS showing AD shifting right as net exports rise and SRAS shifting left as imported costs rise.\n• The mechanism: depreciation lowers the foreign-currency price of exports and raises the domestic-currency price of imports, so if volumes respond, export revenue rises and import spending falls.\n• The Marshall-Lerner test, applied to the data. In year one, PEDx + PEDm = 0.6 + 0.3 = 0.9, which is less than one, so the condition fails and the trade balance will worsen. After three years, 1.4 + 0.9 = 2.3, comfortably above one, so the balance improves. The elasticities therefore settle the question: the answer depends entirely on the time horizon, and a one-year judgement would be exactly wrong.\n• The J-curve follows directly from this. In the short run, contracts are fixed and buyers have not switched, so import volumes barely fall while each import costs more — the value of imports rises and the deficit widens before it narrows.\n• Two features of Extract 1 weaken the improvement even in the long run. Most exporters price in the destination currency, which means the foreign-currency price does not fall, so exporters take a wider domestic-currency margin instead of selling more volume — the volume response is muted, and the measured elasticity may overstate what actually happens. And with 60% of the value of manufactured exports consisting of imported components, depreciation raises exporters\' own costs substantially, offsetting much of the competitiveness gain.\n• The inflation constraint. Depreciation raises imported costs directly, and with CPI inflation already at 3.1% against a 2% target, this pushes inflation further above target. The central bank may respond by holding the policy rate at 3.75% or raising it, which attracts capital inflows and appreciates the currency, partly unwinding the depreciation — and dampens domestic demand. A depreciation is therefore partly self-reversing in an economy with an inflation target.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that the depreciation will worsen the current account in the first year and improve it over three, but by less than the long-run elasticities alone suggest, because of destination-currency pricing and high import content. The deeper point is that depreciation addresses price competitiveness only; a deficit rooted in weak productivity and non-price competitiveness — quality, design, reliability, delivery — is not solved by a cheaper currency, and a policy of repeated depreciation imports inflation and reduces real incomes.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — correct diagram used, Marshall-Lerner applied quantitatively to both time horizons, engages with pricing behaviour and import content, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, mechanism and J-curve explained, limited evaluation. L1 (1–5) — asserts depreciation helps exports; diagram missing or incorrect. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s) and the information in Extract 1, the extent to which trade liberalisation raises living standards in a developing economy.

Extract 1 — written for this practice question
"Country N cut its average applied tariff from 24% to 7% over a decade. Manufactured exports rose from 11% to 34% of total exports, and employment in export manufacturing rose by 1.4 million, at wages 29% above the national average. Consumer prices for imported goods fell an estimated 18%. Over the same period, employment in previously protected textile and food-processing industries fell by 900,000, concentrated in three inland regions where unemployment rose from 6% to 17%. Real GDP per head rose 41%, while the share of income going to the top decile rose from 27% to 33%."`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: a PPF-based comparative advantage diagram showing specialisation and a consumption possibility line beyond the PPF; or a tariff diagram in reverse, showing the removal of a tariff lowering the domestic price, raising consumer surplus and eliminating the deadweight loss triangles. Credit AD/AS with LRAS shifting right as resources move to higher-productivity uses.\n• The case that living standards rose: real GDP per head rose 41%, which is the headline measure of material living standards. Specialisation according to comparative advantage moved resources into manufacturing, where exports rose from 11% to 34% of the total, creating 1.4 million jobs at wages 29% above the national average. Consumers gained directly from an 18% fall in the price of imported goods — a real income gain that falls on every household, not just exporters.\n• Dynamic gains: access to world markets allows economies of scale that a small domestic market cannot support; exposure to competition raises productivity; and imported capital goods and intermediate inputs embody technology, shifting LRAS right over time. Export earnings also relax the foreign exchange constraint on importing capital equipment.\n• The case that the gains are overstated. Adjustment costs are large and concentrated: 900,000 jobs lost in textiles and food processing, with unemployment in three inland regions rising from 6% to 17%. Those workers are not the ones taking the new coastal manufacturing jobs, because labour is not perfectly mobile geographically or occupationally — the assumption of costless factor reallocation that underpins the comparative advantage model fails in practice.\n• Distribution: the top decile\'s income share rose from 27% to 33%, so the aggregate gain of 41% in GDP per head is not what the median household experienced. Trade raises the return to capital and to skills, and an average that rises while the distribution widens can leave substantial numbers no better off.\n• Structural risks: specialising according to current comparative advantage can lock a country into low value-added assembly with limited learning-by-doing; dependence on export demand increases exposure to external shocks; and infant industries that might have developed are exposed before they achieve scale.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that trade liberalisation raised living standards substantially in aggregate — 41% real GDP per head with an 18% fall in imported goods prices is a large gain, and the export employment created exceeds the employment lost — but that it did so unevenly, with severe and persistent costs in specific regions and a worsening distribution. The policy implication is not to reverse liberalisation but that its benefits depend on complementary policy: active labour market support, regional investment and redistribution to compensate the losers, without which the aggregate gain conceals real and lasting harm.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — appropriate diagram used, balances aggregate gains against adjustment and distribution using the data, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, gains and costs described, limited evaluation. L1 (1–5) — asserts trade is beneficial; diagram missing or incorrect. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      { q: `Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s) and the information in Extract 1, the extent to which inward foreign direct investment raises long-run growth in a developing economy.

Extract 1 — written for this practice question
"Inward FDI into Country P rose from 1.9% to 5.4% of GDP over five years, against gross domestic saving of just 6% of GDP. Employment in a special economic zone reached 41,000 at wages 34% above the national average, and manufactured exports rose from 9% to 28% of total exports. However, 78% of profits were repatriated, domestic firms supplied only 11% of the zone's inputs, and a ten-year corporation tax holiday applied. Mean years of schooling are 4.9 against a regional average of 7.1, and the country ranks 142nd of 180 on an index of perceived corruption."`, marks: 15, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: AD/AS with LRAS shifting right (and AD shifting right, since the investment is an injection), or a PPF shifting outward. The strongest answers use the diagram to distinguish the one-off level effect on potential output from a change in the growth rate.\n• The case for: with domestic saving at only 6% of GDP, the country cannot finance investment on the scale required — the savings gap in the Harrod-Domar framework. FDI rising from 1.9% to 5.4% of GDP nearly triples the external contribution to capital formation, and unlike borrowing it creates no debt service obligation.\n• Structural transformation: manufactured exports rose from 9% to 28% of total exports, moving the export base away from primary commodities towards goods with better long-run terms of trade — a direct answer to the Prebisch-Singer problem. Export earnings also relax the foreign exchange constraint on importing capital goods.\n• Employment and human capital: 41 000 jobs at wages 34% above the national average raise incomes and, in principle, transfer skills and management practice to the domestic economy when workers move on.\n• The case against — the gains are not being retained or diffused. With 78% of profits repatriated, most of the return flows out as a primary income outflow on the balance of payments, so the domestic capital stock grows by far less than the headline FDI figure suggests. A ten-year corporation tax holiday means the government captures almost none of the remainder, so there is no fiscal channel to fund the schooling and infrastructure that would raise growth elsewhere.\n• Linkages are the decisive weakness. Domestic firms supply only 11% of inputs, so the zone is an enclave: the technology and productivity gains that justify FDI depend on backward linkages to domestic suppliers, and those are almost absent. Without diffusion, the effect is a one-off increase in the level of output in one sector, not a higher economy-wide growth rate — which on the diagram is a single rightward shift of LRAS rather than a repeated one.\n• Absorptive capacity limits what can diffuse. With mean schooling of 4.9 years against a regional average of 7.1, domestic firms may lack the capability to adopt transferred technology even if it were available, and a corruption ranking of 142nd of 180 deters the domestic entrepreneurship that would build supplier networks.\n• Volatility and footloose capital: export-platform FDI attracted by a tax holiday can relocate when the holiday expires or when wages rise elsewhere, so the employment gain is not secure.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that FDI has raised the level of output, exports and wages, but that on these terms it is unlikely to raise the long-run growth rate, because the mechanisms that would do so — profit reinvestment, tax revenue, supplier linkages and technology diffusion — are all blocked. The policy implication concerns the terms on which FDI is accepted and the complementary investment in schooling and institutions needed to absorb it, not whether to accept it at all.\n\nLevels: L3 (11–15) — correct diagram used to distinguish level from growth-rate effects, exploits the repatriation, linkage and schooling data, supported conclusion. L2 (6–10) — diagram present, benefits and costs described, limited evaluation. L1 (1–5) — lists advantages and disadvantages of FDI; diagram missing or incorrect. Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed.` },

      /* ─────────────── 25 marks — Section B (diagram) and Section C ─────────────── */
      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s), whether free trade benefits all countries that take part in it.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagrams: two PPFs with differing slopes, showing specialisation according to comparative advantage and a consumption possibility line lying beyond each country\'s own frontier; and/or a tariff diagram in reverse, showing liberalisation lowering the domestic price, raising consumer surplus and removing the deadweight loss triangles.\n• The theoretical case: Ricardo\'s comparative advantage shows that if each country specialises where its opportunity cost is lower and trades at a rate between the two domestic ratios, world output rises and both countries can consume beyond their own PPF. Because the gain arises from differences in opportunity cost rather than from absolute productivity, even a country that is less efficient at everything gains.\n• Additional static and dynamic gains: greater variety and lower prices for consumers; economies of scale from access to larger markets; competition raising productivity and reducing X-inefficiency; and technology transfer through imported capital goods and through FDI. Export earnings relax the foreign exchange constraint on importing capital equipment, which matters most for low-income economies.\n• Evidence: liberalising economies have generally grown faster than closed ones, and the sharpest reductions in absolute poverty in history occurred in export-oriented Asian economies. The World Bank\'s revised international poverty line of $3.00 a day at 2021 prices, introduced in June 2025, puts global extreme poverty at 10.5% for 2022 — historically low despite the higher threshold.\n• The assumptions that weaken the case: the model assumes no transport costs, constant returns, full employment and perfect factor mobility within countries. In practice workers displaced by imports are not costlessly reabsorbed — adjustment costs are concentrated on particular industries, regions and age groups, and can persist for a generation.\n• Distribution within countries: trade raises the return to capital and to skills relative to low-skilled labour, so a country can gain in aggregate while substantial groups lose. The aggregate gain is a potential Pareto improvement — the winners could compensate the losers — but compensation is rarely paid, so the losses are real.\n• Distribution between countries: gains depend on the terms of trade, and a country exporting primary commodities faces the Prebisch-Singer problem of declining terms of trade against manufactures, so its share of the gains falls over time. Specialising according to current comparative advantage can lock a country into low value-added activity with little learning-by-doing.\n• Trade is not conducted on a level field: agricultural subsidies in rich economies depress world prices against developing producers, and tariff escalation on processed goods discourages moving up the value chain. Free trade in theory and the trading system in practice are different things — the WTO\'s Appellate Body has been non-functional for several years, with 61 members relying on an interim appeal arrangement as of 2026.\n• Other qualifications: infant industries may never develop if exposed too early; dependence on export demand increases vulnerability to external shocks; and environmental costs of transport and of production relocating to weakly regulated jurisdictions are not priced.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that free trade raises aggregate welfare in participating countries, so that in the potential sense all can gain, but that actual gains are unequally distributed both between and within countries and that significant groups lose absolutely. The policy implication is that the case for openness is strong but incomplete without adjustment support, redistribution and complementary investment in skills and infrastructure — and that the terms on which a country trades matter as much as whether it trades.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires the comparative advantage diagram used properly, the distinction between aggregate and distributional gains, and a conclusion that follows from the argument. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s), whether a government should intervene to influence its exchange rate.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: foreign exchange market with demand and supply for the currency, showing intervention — central bank purchases shifting demand right to support the currency, or sales shifting supply right to weaken it — and the resulting change in the equilibrium rate. Credit a second diagram showing the domestic consequences via AD/AS.\n• The case for intervention: exchange rates in floating regimes are driven substantially by speculative capital flows and interest differentials rather than by trade fundamentals, so they can overshoot and remain misaligned for years. Misalignment damages the real economy — an overvalued currency prices exporters out of world markets and can destroy productive capacity permanently through hysteresis.\n• Certainty and trade: volatility imposes hedging costs and deters exporting and long-term investment, particularly for small firms. Stability, whether through a peg or a managed float, can raise trade volumes and FDI, which matters most for small, very open economies.\n• Importing credibility: a country with a history of high inflation or a weak central bank can anchor expectations by pegging to a low-inflation currency, substituting an external commitment for a domestic one it cannot make credibly.\n• Competitiveness: a deliberately undervalued rate supports export-led growth and can be part of a development strategy, though it amounts to a transfer from consumers to exporters and invites accusations of currency manipulation.\n• The case against — the impossible trinity. A country cannot simultaneously have a fixed exchange rate, free capital movement and an independent monetary policy. Defending a rate means setting interest rates for the currency rather than for the domestic economy, which can force a central bank to raise rates into a recession — the clearest cost of intervention, and one that has produced severe unemployment in defending countries.\n• Intervention often fails. Foreign exchange markets turn over far more daily than most central banks hold in reserves, so sustained defence against a determined market is expensive and frequently unsuccessful; sterling\'s exit from the ERM in 1992 is the standard example. Reserves accumulated for defence are also a dead asset earning low returns.\n• Adjustment works better when left alone. A floating rate adjusts automatically to a current account imbalance, though imperfectly: the Marshall-Lerner condition must hold, and the J-curve delays improvement. Where a large share of export value consists of imported components, depreciation does less than the textbook case implies.\n• Intervention treats a symptom. A persistently weak currency usually reflects weak productivity and non-price competitiveness, which supply-side policy addresses and currency management does not. Repeated depreciation also imports inflation, reducing real incomes — a serious constraint when inflation is already above target, as UK CPI inflation was at 3.1% in the 12 months to August 2026.\n• Middle options: a managed or "dirty" float, in which the central bank leans against disorderly movements without defending a level, captures some stability without surrendering monetary independence; capital controls can reduce speculative pressure at the cost of deterring investment.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that intervention is justified to smooth disorderly short-run volatility and, for small open economies or those importing monetary credibility, to maintain a peg — but that defending a misaligned rate against market pressure is expensive, usually futile and costly in domestic output. For a large diversified economy with a credible central bank, monetary independence is worth more than exchange rate certainty, so the case for anything beyond occasional smoothing is weak.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires the forex diagram used to show intervention, application of the impossible trinity, and a conclusion that follows from the argument. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate, using an appropriate diagram(s), whether protectionism is ever in a country\'s economic interest.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate', diagram: true,
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Diagram: the standard tariff diagram with domestic supply and demand, world price Pw, the tariff raising price to Pw + t, and the four areas identified — loss of consumer surplus, gain in producer surplus, government revenue, and the two deadweight loss triangles. Credit a quota diagram showing the same effects with the revenue equivalent accruing to licence holders instead.\n• The general economic case against: the deadweight losses mean the loss to consumers exceeds the combined gain to producers and government, so protection reduces national welfare. It sustains high-cost production, raising input costs for downstream industries, reduces the competitive pressure that drives productivity, and invites retaliation that harms competitive export sectors.\n• Scale matters. When one major economy raised its overall average effective tariff from 2.5% in January 2025 to an estimated 27% by April 2025, the highest in over a century, the cost fell on consumers and on every firm using imported inputs; after courts struck most of the measures down in February 2026 the rate fell to 6.7% by July 2026, and the trade-weighted average fell from 15.3% to 8.3%. The reversal removed a substantial cost.\n• Arguments that can justify protection. The infant industry case: a new industry may not compete initially but could achieve economies of scale and learning-by-doing, so temporary protection can be a net gain if the industry becomes viable. The requirements are strict — the industry must eventually be competitive, the protection must be time-limited and credibly withdrawn, and the discounted future gain must exceed the present deadweight loss. Most historical attempts fail the second condition because protected producers lobby successfully to retain it.\n• Anti-dumping: if a foreign producer sells below cost to drive out domestic competitors and then raises prices, protection can prevent a monopoly outcome. In practice, distinguishing predatory dumping from genuine comparative advantage is very difficult, and anti-dumping duties are frequently used as ordinary protection.\n• Strategic and security arguments: maintaining domestic capacity in defence, energy, food, semiconductors or pharmaceuticals can be worth an efficiency cost if the alternative is dependence on a potentially hostile supplier — a non-economic objective pursued through economic means, and legitimate provided the cost is acknowledged.\n• Adjustment, distribution and the terms of trade: protection can slow the pace of structural change where adjustment costs are concentrated on particular regions, though retraining and regional support are cheaper instruments for the same objective. A large country can in principle improve its terms of trade with an optimal tariff, but only if others do not retaliate — and they generally do.\n• Balance of payments and revenue: tariffs can raise revenue where administrative capacity for other taxes is weak, which is a genuine consideration for some low-income economies; using them to correct a current account deficit invites retaliation and addresses a symptom rather than the underlying competitiveness problem.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that protectionism can be in a country\'s economic interest in narrow, specific and time-limited cases — a genuine infant industry with a credible sunset clause, genuine predatory dumping, and defined security needs — but that these justifications are far narrower than the uses made of them, and that the political economy of protection (concentrated organised beneficiaries, dispersed unorganised losers) means measures introduced as temporary rarely end. The strongest answers argue that the burden of proof should sit with protection, and that broad tariffs of the kind imposed in 2025 fail every one of the recognised tests.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires an accurate tariff diagram used in the analysis, serious testing of at least two justifications against their conditions, and a conclusion that follows. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate whether international aid is an effective way of promoting economic development.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• What aid is meant to do: official development assistance aims to fill the savings gap — where domestic saving is too low to finance the investment needed for growth, as in the Harrod-Domar framework — and the foreign exchange gap that limits imports of capital goods. It also funds public goods that markets will not supply.\n• The case for effectiveness: aid directed at health and education has some of the highest measured social returns of any spending — vaccination, malaria control, clean water and primary schooling raise human capital with effects compounding over decades. Humanitarian aid saves lives in famine, conflict and disaster where no market mechanism will act. Aid can also fund infrastructure that unlocks private investment, and can be counter-cyclical when private capital flees.\n• The case against: dependency, where sustained inflows weaken the incentive to build domestic tax capacity and to undertake politically difficult reform. Aid can prop up poor governments, be diverted through corruption, or be tied to donor procurement so that much of the value returns to the donor economy. Large inflows can appreciate the recipient\'s exchange rate, damaging export competitiveness — a form of Dutch disease. Donor priorities and reporting requirements can also fragment recipient administrative capacity across hundreds of separate projects.\n• Effectiveness is conditional on institutions, which is the central finding of the empirical literature. The same aid produces very different results depending on the rule of law, public financial management and corruption. Countries ranked near the bottom of corruption indices absorb aid far less productively than those with functioning institutions, which is why the debate has moved from how much to how — budget support versus projects, conditionality, results-based financing and independent evaluation.\n• Scale and alternatives. Aid is small relative to remittances, FDI and export earnings for most recipients, so treating it as the principal lever overstates its role. Debt relief may transfer more resources at lower administrative cost: in sub-Saharan Africa the ratio of external public debt service to government revenue doubled from 9% in 2017 to 18% in 2025, and more than a third of countries are at high risk of or already in debt distress, so released debt payments would exceed many aid flows. Improved market access, technology transfer and reform of agricultural subsidies in rich countries can also transfer more value than aid budgets.\n• Political fragility of aid: the UK announced a reduction in official development assistance from 0.5% of gross national income to 0.3% in 2027 to fund higher defence spending — around £9.2 billion against roughly £15.4 billion at 0.5%, a cut of about 40%, and the lowest share since 1999, having already been cut from 0.7% to 0.5% in 2021. Aid that can be halved twice in six years is an unreliable basis for multi-year development planning, which is itself an argument against building strategies around it.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that aid is effective for specific purposes — humanitarian response and high-return investments in health and education — and much weaker as a driver of long-run growth, where institutions, investment and trade matter far more. The strongest answers argue that the question is wrongly framed as aid versus no aid: the real determinants of effectiveness are the form aid takes, the institutional capacity to absorb it, and whether it substitutes for or complements domestic revenue-raising. They should also note that the counterfactual is unobservable, so confident claims in either direction outrun the evidence.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires the conditionality of aid effectiveness developed properly, comparison with at least one alternative transfer, and a conclusion that follows from the argument. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate whether globalisation has been beneficial for developing economies.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• What globalisation involves: the growing integration of economies through trade, capital flows, migration and technology transfer, driven by falling transport and communication costs and by policy liberalisation, and organised around global value chains in which production is fragmented across countries.\n• The case for benefit — poverty reduction. Export-led growth allowed low-income economies to specialise according to comparative advantage and to move labour from low-productivity agriculture into manufacturing, the Lewis dual-sector mechanism, producing the largest reduction in absolute poverty in recorded history, concentrated in East and South Asia. On the World Bank\'s revised international poverty line of $3.00 a day at 2021 purchasing power parity, introduced in June 2025, the global extreme poverty rate for 2022 was 10.5% — historically low despite the threshold being raised from $2.15.\n• Other benefits: FDI relaxes the savings constraint and transfers technology and management practice; access to world markets permits economies of scale a domestic market cannot support; imported capital goods embody technology; competition raises productivity; consumers gain from lower prices and greater variety; and remittances from migration provide a large, counter-cyclical, untied flow of foreign exchange.\n• Between-country convergence: fast-growing exporters have closed much of the gap with richer economies, so inequality between country averages has narrowed over recent decades.\n• The case against — the gains are conditional and uneven. Countries lacking infrastructure, institutions and human capital have been largely bypassed. A large share of those remaining in extreme poverty are in sub-Saharan Africa and in fragile and conflict-affected states, where growth forecast at 4.1% for 2026 falls well short of the 7% or more usually cited as necessary for substantial poverty reduction. Participation in global value chains requires reliable power, ports and contract enforcement that many economies do not have.\n• Vulnerability: integration transmits external shocks. Commodity exporters face volatile and, on the Prebisch-Singer argument, declining terms of trade; capital flows can reverse abruptly, producing sudden stops and currency crises; and dependence on demand in a few export markets exposes economies to recessions elsewhere. Openness also constrains policy — tax competition and the threat of relocation limit what governments can raise from mobile capital.\n• Distribution within countries: trade and FDI raise the return to capital and to skills, so within-country inequality has risen in many economies even as absolute poverty has fallen. Adjustment costs fall on identifiable regions and workers, and compensation is rarely paid.\n• Other costs: environmental damage from resource extraction and from production relocating to weakly regulated jurisdictions; exploitation where labour standards are weak; enclave FDI with high profit repatriation and few domestic linkages, which raises the level of output without raising the growth rate; and the loss of policy space for infant industry development.\n• The system is not neutral: agricultural subsidies in rich countries depress world prices against developing producers, tariff escalation on processed goods discourages moving up the value chain, and the WTO\'s Appellate Body has been non-functional for several years, with 61 members relying on an interim appeal arrangement as of 2026 — so the rules-based protection that most benefits small economies is weakened.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that globalisation has been strongly beneficial for economies positioned to participate and largely irrelevant or harmful for those that were not, so the honest answer distinguishes between developing economies rather than treating them as one group. The determinants of which group a country falls into — infrastructure, institutions, human capital and macroeconomic stability — are largely domestic, which means globalisation is better understood as an opportunity whose value depends on prior capacity than as a cause of development in its own right.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires differentiation between groups of developing economies, the distinction between absolute poverty and relative inequality, and a conclusion that follows from the argument. Indicative content is not a checklist.` },

      { q: 'Evaluate whether economic growth is sufficient to bring about economic development.', marks: 25, command: 'Evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The distinction: economic growth is an increase in real output, a quantitative measure; economic development is a broader, qualitative concept covering living standards, health, education, poverty, inequality, security and the freedom to make meaningful choices.\n• The case that growth is sufficient, or nearly so: growth raises the material resources available per person and generates the tax revenue that funds health, education, sanitation and infrastructure without raising tax rates. The correlation is strong — the relationship between GNI per head and life expectancy is positive across almost every dataset — and no country has achieved high human development without first achieving sustained growth. Growth also creates employment, and paid work is the principal route out of poverty for most households.\n• Growth is therefore necessary, and this is worth stating clearly: redistribution of a static or shrinking output cannot sustain rising living standards indefinitely, and economies that have stagnated have not developed.\n• The case that it is not sufficient — distribution. GDP is an aggregate, so growth concentrated in extractive industries, in particular regions or among capital owners can leave the median household no better off. Where the share of income going to the top decile rises alongside growth, poverty may fall slowly or not at all.\n• Composition matters. Growth in mineral extraction generates revenue but few jobs and few linkages, and can appreciate the currency and damage other tradable sectors (Dutch disease); it also invites rent-seeking, so resource wealth is associated with weaker institutions rather than stronger ones. Growth in labour-intensive manufacturing or in smallholder agricultural productivity transmits to incomes far more directly.\n• Institutions determine whether growth is converted into development. Without functioning public administration, growth generates revenue that is not spent on services; without secure property rights and rule of law, the gains accrue to those with power. The evidence that some countries invest heavily and grow slowly while others invest less and grow fast points to the same conclusion.\n• Sustainability: growth achieved by depleting natural capital or by generating emissions raises measured output while reducing the base available to future generations, so it is not development in any durable sense. GDP records the output from felling a forest but not the loss of the asset.\n• Measurement supports the distinction. The Human Development Index combines life expectancy, schooling and GNI per head; in the 2025 Human Development Report the United Kingdom scored 0.946 and ranked 13th of 193, while Iceland led at 0.972 — and several countries with higher GNI per head than the UK rank below it, which could not happen if income determined development. The inequality-adjusted HDI falls substantially in the most unequal economies, making the same point about distribution.\n• Growth without development can also be reversed: gains concentrated in one commodity sector disappear when prices fall, as they do repeatedly, whereas improvements in schooling and health are durable.\n• Evaluation: a defensible conclusion is that growth is necessary but not sufficient — it provides the resources for development but does not determine whether they are converted into health, education, reduced poverty or greater capability. What determines that conversion is the composition of growth, the distribution of its gains, the quality of institutions and the deliberate provision of public goods. The policy implication is that development strategy should target the conversion mechanisms rather than the growth rate alone, and that countries such as those with high growth and stagnant human development indicators are evidence that the two can move apart.\n\nLevels: L5 (21–25) L4 (16–20) L3 (11–15) L2 (6–10) L1 (1–5). Asterisked: quality of extended response is assessed. L5 requires the necessary-but-not-sufficient argument developed with attention to composition, distribution and institutions, supported by evidence, and a conclusion that follows from the argument. Indicative content is not a checklist.` }
    ]
  }
};
